const DAYS = {"1":{"day":1,"week":"01","themeRu":"Hello!","themeEn":"Приве́т!","intro":"Welcome — and congratulations on making it through Cyrillic. Today those letters start paying rent: you'll greet people, say your name, and introduce yourself in real Russian. And here's some genuinely happy news to start with: in the present tense, Russian has <b>no “am / is / are” and no “a / the” at all</b>. Fewer moving parts than English. Let's begin.","introAudio":"Welcome — and congratulations on making it through Cyrillic. Today those letters start paying rent: you'll greet people, say your name, and introduce yourself in real Russian. And here's some genuinely happy news to start with: in the present tense, Russian has no am, is, are — and no a or the at all. Fewer moving parts than English. Let's begin.","goals":["Greet anyone: <b>приве́т</b> to a friend, <b>здра́вствуйте</b> to everyone else","Say who you are with zero “to be”: <b>Я студе́нт / Я студе́нтка</b>","Give your name: <b>Меня́ зову́т…</b> — and ask for theirs","Tell apart <b>ты</b> and <b>вы</b> — the two Russian words behind one English “you”"],"learned":["Greeted people both ways: <b>приве́т</b> to a friend, <b>здра́вствуйте</b> to everyone else","Said who you are with zero “to be”: <b>Я студе́нт / Я студе́нтка</b>","Gave your name with <b>Меня́ зову́т…</b> and asked for theirs","Chose correctly between <b>ты</b> and <b>вы</b>"],"review":null,"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Greetings","title":"<em>Приве́т</em> or <em>Здра́вствуйте</em>?","paras":["Russian gives you two front doors. <b>Приве́т</b> [pree-VYET] is the casual one — friends, classmates, anyone you'd high-five. <b>Здра́вствуйте</b> [ZDRA-stvooy-tye] is the safe one — shops, teachers, strangers, your friend's mother. When in doubt, choose здра́вствуйте: nobody was ever offended by politeness.","Say здра́вствуйте slowly once: the first <b>в</b> in the middle cluster goes silent — [ZDRA-stvooy-tye], not “zdravstvooytye”. Russians swallow it too.","Leaving is just as easy: <b>пока́</b> [pa-KA] to a friend, <b>до свида́ния</b> [da svee-DA-nee-ya] to everyone else. Notice пока́: the unstressed <b>о</b> relaxes into “a” — that's the а́канье you met in the alphabet course."],"audio":"Russian gives you two front doors. Privet is the casual one — friends, classmates, anyone you'd high-five. Zdravstvuyte is the safe one — shops, teachers, strangers, your friend's mother. When in doubt, choose the polite one: nobody was ever offended by politeness. Say it slowly once: the first v in the middle goes silent. Leaving is just as easy: paka to a friend, do svidaniya to everyone else.","table":{"rows":[["Приве́т! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Привет!\">🔊</button>","Hi! (casual — friends)"],["Здра́вствуйте! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Здравствуйте!\">🔊</button>","Hello! (polite — everyone else)"],["Пока́! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Пока!\">🔊</button>","Bye! (casual)"],["До свида́ния! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"До свидания!\">🔊</button>","Goodbye! (polite)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Hi, Ivan!","en":"<b>Приве́т</b>, Ива́н!","say":"Привет, Иван!"},{"ru":"Hello! (to a shop assistant)","en":"<b>Здра́вствуйте</b>!","say":"Здравствуйте!"},{"ru":"Bye, Emma!","en":"<b>Пока́</b>, Э́мма!","say":"Пока, Эмма!"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the situation and tap the greeting that fits. Wrong taps are fine — that's how it sticks.","q":"You walk into a bakery in Moscow. The woman behind the counter looks up.","opts":["Приве́т!","Здра́вствуйте!","Пока́!"],"answer":1,"explain":"A stranger in a shop → the polite door: <b>здра́вствуйте</b>. Приве́т is for friends, пока́ is “bye”."},{"type":"choice","q":"Your friend Ива́н is leaving. What do you say?","opts":["До свида́ния!","Здра́вствуйте!","Пока́!"],"answer":2,"explain":"A friend → casual <b>пока́</b>. До свида́ния also works but sounds like saying goodbye to your bank manager."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say the phrase out loud. Listen to the sample first if you like.","target":"Приве́т!","sub":"[pree-VYET] — stress lands on the end","want":"привет"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · You and you","title":"One English “you” — two Russian: <em>ты</em> and <em>вы</em>","paras":["English has one “you” for everybody. Russian splits it in two. <b>Ты</b> [ty] — one person you're close to: a friend, a child, family. <b>Вы</b> [vy] — one person you're polite with, or any group of people.","The pairing is logical: приве́т goes with ты, здра́вствуйте goes with вы. If you greeted someone politely, stay on вы with them.","And <b>я</b> [ya] is simply “I” — with a bonus: Russian only capitalises it at the start of a sentence. No special treatment."],"audio":"English has one you for everybody. Russian splits it in two. Ty — one person you're close to: a friend, a child, family. Vy — one person you're polite with, or any group of people. The pairing is logical: privet goes with ty, zdravstvuyte goes with vy. And ya is simply I — Russian only capitalises it at the start of a sentence.","table":{"rows":[["я <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я\">🔊</button>","I"],["ты <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ты\">🔊</button>","you — one close person"],["вы <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вы\">🔊</button>","you — polite, or several people"]],"star":2},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Приве́т, профе́ссор!","right":"Здра́вствуйте, профе́ссор!","why":"A professor gets the polite register: здра́вствуйте + вы. Приве́т + ты would feel like slapping his shoulder."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"You're asking your taxi driver a question. Which “you”?","opts":["вы","ты"],"answer":0,"explain":"A stranger providing a service → polite <b>вы</b>. Ты would sound overly familiar."},{"type":"choice","q":"Ле́на talks to her little nephew. Which “you” does she use?","opts":["вы","ты"],"answer":1,"explain":"A child in the family → <b>ты</b>. Вы to a five-year-old would be comedy."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian word. Don't worry about the stress mark — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type “I” in Russian:","accept":["я","ya"],"placeholder":"one little letter…"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The vanished verb","title":"Я студе́нт — no “am”, no “a”","paras":["Here is the single most useful fact about Russian for an English speaker: in the present tense, the verb “to be” <b>doesn't exist</b>. “I am a student” becomes two words: <b>Я студе́нт</b> — literally “I student”. Nothing is missing; that IS the sentence.","Articles vanished too. Russian has no “a” and no “the” — кафе́ covers “a café”, “the café”, and plain “café”. Your English brain will keep reaching for “am” and “a” for a while. Every time it does, smile and put them down.","One person, one ending: a man says <b>Я студе́нт</b>, a woman says <b>Я студе́нтка</b> [stoo-DYENT-ka]. You'll meet this masculine/feminine pattern properly on day three — for now just pick your own form and own it."],"audio":"Here is the single most useful fact about Russian for an English speaker: in the present tense, the verb to be doesn't exist. I am a student becomes two words: ya studyent — literally, I student. Nothing is missing; that is the sentence. Articles vanished too: Russian has no a and no the. A man says ya studyent, a woman says ya studyentka. Pick your own form and own it.","mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я есть студе́нт.","right":"Я студе́нт.","why":"No “to be” in the present. Sticking есть in is the classic English-speaker move — Russians will understand and grin, but it's wrong."},{"wrong":"Ты есть студе́нт?","right":"Ты студе́нт?","why":"Questions too: same two words, just add a question mark — and raise your voice at the end."}],"examples":[{"ru":"I am a student. (a man speaking)","en":"Я <b>студе́нт</b>.","say":"Я студент."},{"ru":"I am a student. (a woman speaking)","en":"Я <b>студе́нтка</b>.","say":"Я студентка."},{"ru":"Are you a student?","en":"Ты студе́нт?","say":"Ты студент?","gloss":"no verb, no “do” — the rising voice makes it a question"},{"ru":"Emma is a student.","en":"Э́мма студе́нтка.","say":"Эмма студентка."}],"mnemonic":"English builds “I <b>am a</b> student” with scaffolding. Russian already moved in: <b>Я студе́нт</b>. Two words, done.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I am a student” in Russian is…","opts":["Я есть студе́нт.","Я студе́нт.","Я ам студе́нт."],"answer":1,"explain":"Drop “am”, drop “a”: <b>Я студе́нт</b>. Russian never had them here."},{"type":"type","q":"A woman introduces herself: “I am Emma. I am a student.” Type it in Russian (two tiny sentences):","accept":["я эмма. я студентка","я эмма я студентка","я эмма. я студе́нтка","ya emma ya studentka","я эмма, я студентка"],"placeholder":"Я … Я …","explain":"Я Э́мма. Я студе́нтка. — four words total. English needed eight."},{"type":"choice","q":"How do you turn «Ты студе́нт.» into a question?","opts":["Add до́ес: До́ес ты студе́нт?","Just raise your intonation: Ты студе́нт?","Add есть: Ты есть студе́нт?"],"answer":1,"explain":"No “do”, no “есть” — melody does the job. <b>Ты студе́нт?</b> with a rising voice is a full question."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Your name","title":"<em>Меня́ зову́т…</em> — “my name is…”","paras":["To give your name, Russian uses a ready-made chunk: <b>Меня́ зову́т</b> [mee-NYA za-VOOT] + your name. Literally it means “me they call” — but don't dissect it, just take the whole phrase as one brick: Меня́ зову́т Э́мма.","To ask back, flip it: <b>Как тебя́ зову́т?</b> — “what's your name?” (to a friend, ты-style). And when you've been introduced, seal it with <b>О́чень прия́тно!</b> [O-cheen pree-YAT-na] — “very nice to meet you”.","A shortcut also works: <b>Я Э́мма</b> — “I'm Emma”. Same vanished verb as in rule 3."],"audio":"To give your name, Russian uses a ready-made chunk: menya zovut plus your name. Literally it means me they call — but don't dissect it, just take the whole phrase as one brick. To ask back, flip it: kak tebya zovut? — what's your name? And when you've been introduced, seal it with ochen priyatno — very nice to meet you. A shortcut also works: ya Emma. Same vanished verb as before.","table":{"rows":[["Меня́ зову́т Э́мма. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Меня зовут Эмма.\">🔊</button>","My name is Emma."],["Как тебя́ зову́т? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Как тебя зовут?\">🔊</button>","What's your name?"],["О́чень прия́тно! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Очень приятно!\">🔊</button>","Very nice to meet you!"],["Я Э́мма. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я Эмма.\">🔊</button>","I'm Emma. (the shortcut)"]],"star":0},"tasks":[{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“My name is Ivan.”</b>","want":"Меня́ зову́т Ива́н","answer":"Меня́ зову́т Ива́н"},{"type":"choice","q":"Ле́на asks: «Как тебя́ зову́т?» Your best reply:","opts":["О́чень прия́тно!","Меня́ зову́т Том.","До свида́ния!"],"answer":1,"explain":"She asked for your name — give it: <b>Меня́ зову́т…</b> О́чень прия́тно comes right after the introductions."},{"type":"speak","target":"Меня́ зову́т Э́мма.","sub":"[mee-NYA za-VOOT] — then swap in your own name out loud","want":"меня зовут"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma has just landed in Moscow for a month of Russian. In the university corridor, a student with a coffee smiles at her. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Приве́т! Как тебя́ зову́т?","ru":"Hi! What's your name?"},{"who":"A","en":"Здра́вствуйте! Меня́ зову́т Э́мма.","ru":"Hello! My name is Emma."},{"who":"B","en":"Я Ива́н. О́чень прия́тно!","ru":"I'm Ivan. Very nice to meet you!"},{"who":"A","en":"О́чень прия́тно! Ты студе́нт?","ru":"Nice to meet you too! Are you a student?"},{"who":"B","en":"Да, я студе́нт. А ты?","ru":"Yes, I'm a student. And you?"},{"who":"A","en":"Я то́же студе́нтка.","ru":"I'm a student too. (то́же = too)"},{"who":"B","en":"Кла́ссно. Пока́, Э́мма!","ru":"Cool. Bye, Emma!"},{"who":"A","en":"Пока́!","ru":"Bye!"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words that already make you a person in Russian: greet, thank, agree, refuse, introduce yourself. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"приве́т","ru":"hi (casual)","ex":"[pree-VYET] — for friends and equals"},{"en":"здра́вствуйте","ru":"hello (polite)","ex":"[ZDRA-stvooy-tye] — the first в is silent; safe with everyone"},{"en":"пока́","ru":"bye (casual)","ex":"[pa-KA] — unstressed о relaxes to “a”"},{"en":"до свида́ния","ru":"goodbye (polite)","ex":"[da svee-DA-nee-ya] — literally “until the next meeting”"},{"en":"да","ru":"yes","ex":"[da] — short and firm"},{"en":"нет","ru":"no","ex":"[nyet] — the famous one"},{"en":"спаси́бо","ru":"thank you","ex":"[spa-SEE-ba] — final о sounds like “a”"},{"en":"пожа́луйста","ru":"please / you're welcome","ex":"[pa-ZHA-loo-sta] — the й hides in fast speech; one word, two jobs"},{"en":"я","ru":"I","ex":"[ya] — no capital letter mid-sentence"},{"en":"ты / вы","ru":"you (close) / you (polite or many)","ex":"[ty / vy] — that deep ы from the alphabet course"},{"en":"студе́нт / студе́нтка","ru":"student (m / f)","ex":"[stoo-DYENT / stoo-DYENT-ka] — pick your form"},{"en":"Меня́ зову́т…","ru":"My name is…","ex":"[mee-NYA za-VOOT] — one ready-made brick"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning on the right — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Приве́т!","Hi!"],["До свида́ния!","Goodbye!"],["Спаси́бо!","Thank you!"],["Меня́ зову́т Том.","My name is Tom."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — everything from today, nothing else. If one bites, peek back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"You walk into your friend's home and his mother opens the door.","opts":["Приве́т!","Здра́вствуйте!","Пока́!"],"answer":1},{"q":"“I am a student” in Russian:","opts":["Я есть студе́нт.","Я ам студе́нт.","Я студе́нт."],"answer":2},{"q":"«___ зову́т Ива́н.» — the missing word is:","opts":["Меня́","Я","Ты"],"answer":0},{"q":"Ле́на waves and says «Пока́!». She is…","opts":["greeting you","thanking you","saying goodbye"],"answer":2},{"q":"Which English words simply don't exist in «Я студе́нт»?","opts":["“am” and “a”","“I” and “student”","“yes” and “no”"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's first note home — read it aloud, slowly. You know every word.","title":"Приве́т, Москва́!","sentences":["До свида́ния, Ло́ндон!","Приве́т, Москва́!","Меня́ зову́т Э́мма. Я студе́нтка.","Ива́н студе́нт.","Спаси́бо, Ива́н!"],"translation":"Goodbye, London! Hello, Moscow! My name is Emma. I'm a student. Ivan is a student. Thank you, Ivan!"},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to exist in Russian — in writing.","prompt":"Introduce yourself in 2–3 short sentences: a greeting, your name, and who you are (student? just say «Я + your name» if not).","hint":"Three bricks you already own: Здра́вствуйте! → Меня́ зову́т… → Я студе́нт / Я студе́нтка. No “am”, no “a” — two-word sentences are perfect Russian 🙂","example":"Здра́вствуйте! Меня́ зову́т Са́ра. Я студе́нтка."}},"2":{"day":2,"week":"01","themeRu":"Where are you from?","themeEn":"Отку́да ты?","intro":"Yesterday you gave your name. The very next question anyone asks is «<b>Отку́да ты?</b>» — “where are you from?”. By tonight you'll answer it, name a few countries, say your nationality, and tell people which language you speak — all still with zero “to be”.","introAudio":"Yesterday you gave your name. The very next question anyone asks is otkuda ty — where are you from? By tonight you'll answer it, name a few countries, say your nationality, and tell people which language you speak — all still with zero to be.","goals":["Say where you're from: <b>Я из А́нглии</b> — and ask <b>Отку́да ты?</b>","Name your nationality in the right gender: <b>Я англича́нин / англича́нка</b>","Say which language you speak: <b>Я говорю́ по-англи́йски</b>","Keep choosing <b>ты</b> and <b>вы</b>, the two Russian “you”s"],"learned":["Said where you're from with <b>Я из…</b> and asked <b>Отку́да ты?</b>","Named your nationality in the right gender: <b>англича́нин / англича́нка</b>","Said which language you speak: <b>Я говорю́ по-англи́йски</b>","Kept <b>ты</b> and <b>вы</b> straight for one English “you”"],"review":{"intro":"Before we pack our bags for the world, a thirty-second look back at yesterday.","introAudio":"Before we pack our bags for the world, a thirty-second look back at yesterday.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the situation and tap the answer that fits. A wrong tap just teaches faster.","q":"Your friend's grandmother opens the door. You greet her:","opts":["Приве́т!","Здра́вствуйте!","Пока́!"],"answer":1,"explain":"An older person you don't know well → the polite door, <b>здра́вствуйте</b>. Приве́т is for friends; пока́ is “bye”."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. The stress mark is optional — plain letters are fine.","q":"A woman says “I am a student.” Type it in Russian:","accept":["я студентка","я студе́нтка","ya studentka"],"placeholder":"Я …"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which is correct Russian — remember, no “to be” in the present:","opts":["Я есть студе́нтка.","Я студе́нтка.","Я ам студе́нтка."],"answer":1,"explain":"Drop the “am”: just <b>Я студе́нтка</b>. Both «есть» and «ам» are the English verb trying to sneak back in."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Where from","title":"<em>Отку́да ты?</em> — “Where are you from?”","paras":["One breath after your name comes «<b>Отку́да ты?</b>» [at-KOO-da ty] — “where are you from?”. To answer, grab the ready phrase «<b>Я из…</b>» and add a country: <b>Я из А́нглии</b> — “I'm from England”.","Notice А́нглия lost its tail: <b>А́нглия</b> became <b>из А́нглии</b>. Russian reshapes a word after «из», the way English never touches “England”. Don't study the machinery today — just learn each country-after-«из» as one ready chunk. The pattern gets its proper day later.","And yesterday's «you» split still rules: «<b>Отку́да ты?</b>» to a friend, «<b>Отку́да вы?</b>» to a stranger or your teacher."],"audio":"One breath after your name comes otkuda ty — where are you from? To answer, grab the ready phrase ya eez and add a country: ya iz Anglii — I'm from England. Notice Angliya changed its tail to Anglii: Russian reshapes a word after eez, the way English never touches England. Just learn each country-after-eez as one chunk. And yesterday's you split still rules: otkuda ty to a friend, otkuda vy to a stranger.","table":{"rows":[["Отку́да ты? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Откуда ты?\">🔊</button>","Where are you from? (to a friend)"],["Отку́да вы? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Откуда вы?\">🔊</button>","Where are you from? (polite)"],["Я из А́нглии. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я из Англии.\">🔊</button>","I'm from England."],["Я из Росси́и. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я из России.\">🔊</button>","I'm from Russia."],["Я из Аме́рики. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я из Америки.\">🔊</button>","I'm from America."]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Where are you from? (to a friend)","en":"<b>Отку́да ты?</b>","say":"Откуда ты?"},{"ru":"I'm from England.","en":"Я <b>из А́нглии</b>.","say":"Я из Англии."},{"ru":"I'm from Canada.","en":"Я <b>из Кана́ды</b>.","say":"Я из Канады."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Отку́да ты, профе́ссор?","right":"Отку́да вы, профе́ссор?","why":"A professor is <b>вы</b> territory. One English “you”, but Russian makes you choose — and ты to your teacher lands like a shoulder-slap."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the situation and tap the phrase that fits.","q":"You want to ask a new friend your own age where she's from. You say:","opts":["Отку́да вы?","Отку́да ты?","Я из А́нглии."],"answer":1,"explain":"A new friend your age → informal «Отку́да <b>ты</b>?». «Отку́да вы?» is the polite version; «Я из А́нглии» is an answer, not a question."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“I'm from Russia.”</b>","want":"Я из Росси́и","answer":"Я из Росси́и"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Don't worry about the stress mark.","q":"Type in Russian: “I'm from England.”","accept":["я из англии","я из а́нглии","ya iz anglii"],"placeholder":"Я из…"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Your nationality","title":"<em>Англича́нин</em> or <em>англича́нка</em>?","paras":["Yesterday «студе́нт / студе́нтка» shifted with gender. Nationalities do the same. A man from England is an <b>англича́нин</b> [an-glee-CHA-neen]; a woman is an <b>англича́нка</b> [an-glee-CHAN-ka]. You pick the one that's you.","The word follows the person, not the country. So Emma — a woman — always says «Я <b>англича́нка</b>», never «англича́нин», even though England is one country. Same everywhere: <b>ру́сский / ру́сская</b>, <b>америка́нец / америка́нка</b>.","A small surprise: the country is <b>Росси́я</b>, but the person is <b>ру́сский</b> and the language is <b>по-ру́сски</b>. Three cousins, not triplets — learn them as a little family."],"audio":"Yesterday studyent and studyentka shifted with gender. Nationalities do the same. A man from England is an anglichanin; a woman is an anglichanka. You pick the one that's you. The word follows the person, not the country — so Emma, a woman, always says ya anglichanka, never anglichanin. Same everywhere: russkiy and russkaya, amerikanets and amerikanka. A small surprise: the country is Rossiya, but the person is russkiy and the language is pa-russki.","table":{"rows":[["А́нглия → англича́нин / англича́нка <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"англичанин, англичанка\">🔊</button>","England → Englishman / Englishwoman"],["Росси́я → ру́сский / ру́сская <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"русский, русская\">🔊</button>","Russia → Russian (m / f)"],["Аме́рика → америка́нец / америка́нка <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"американец, американка\">🔊</button>","America → American (m / f)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"I'm English. (a man)","en":"Я <b>англича́нин</b>.","say":"Я англичанин."},{"ru":"I'm English. (a woman)","en":"Я <b>англича́нка</b>.","say":"Я англичанка."},{"ru":"I'm Russian. (a woman)","en":"Я <b>ру́сская</b>.","say":"Я русская."},{"ru":"I'm American. (a man)","en":"Я <b>америка́нец</b>.","say":"Я американец."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я англича́нин. (Emma, a woman, speaking)","right":"Я англича́нка. (Emma, a woman, speaking)","why":"Emma is a woman, so her nationality takes the <b>-ка</b> ending: <b>англича́нка</b>. The word agrees with the speaker's gender, not with the country's name."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Ле́на is Russian. How does she state her nationality?","opts":["Я ру́сский.","Я ру́сская.","Я Росси́я."],"answer":1,"explain":"Ле́на is a woman → <b>ру́сская</b>. «Я ру́сский» is the man's form; «Я Росси́я» would mean “I am Russia”, the country."},{"type":"choice","q":"A man says where he's from and who he is. Pick the natural pair:","opts":["Я из Аме́рики. Я америка́нец.","Я из Аме́рики. Я америка́нка.","Я из америка́нец."],"answer":0,"explain":"From America → «из Аме́рики»; a man → «америка́нец». Option 2 borrows the woman's form; option 3 is scrambled."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I'm American.” (a man speaking)</b>","want":"Я америка́нец","answer":"Я америка́нец"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Your language","title":"<em>Я говорю́ по-англи́йски</em> — the language you speak","paras":["To say which language you speak, take the chunk «<b>Я говорю́</b>» [ya ga-va-RYOO] — “I speak” — and add a <b>по-</b> word: <b>Я говорю́ по-англи́йски</b> — “I speak English”, <b>Я говорю́ по-ру́сски</b> — “I speak Russian”.","That по-word isn't the name of the language — it's more like “englishly”, “in the English way”. So there's no “the” and no separate word for “English” to hunt for. And just like «Меня́ зову́т», treat «Я говорю́» as one ready brick for now; the verb machinery arrives in week 2."],"audio":"To say which language you speak, take the chunk ya govoryu — I speak — and add a po-word: ya govoryu pa-angliyski, I speak English; ya govoryu pa-russki, I speak Russian. That po-word isn't the name of the language — it's more like englishly, in the English way. So there's no the and no separate word to hunt for. And just like menya zovut, treat ya govoryu as one ready brick for now.","table":{"rows":[["Я говорю́ по-англи́йски. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я говорю по-английски.\">🔊</button>","I speak English."],["Я говорю́ по-ру́сски. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я говорю по-русски.\">🔊</button>","I speak Russian."]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I speak English.","en":"Я говорю́ <b>по-англи́йски</b>.","say":"Я говорю по-английски."},{"ru":"I speak Russian.","en":"Я говорю́ <b>по-ру́сски</b>.","say":"Я говорю по-русски."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я говорю́ англи́йски.","right":"Я говорю́ по-англи́йски.","why":"The little <b>по-</b> is not optional — it's what turns the word into “in English”. Without it, the phrase falls apart."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"How do you say “I speak Russian”?","opts":["Я ру́сская.","Я говорю́ по-ру́сски.","Я из Росси́и."],"answer":1,"explain":"«Я говорю́ по-ру́сски» = I speak Russian. «Я ру́сская» = I'm Russian (a woman); «Я из Росси́и» = I'm from Russia."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud. Listen to the sample first if you like.","target":"Я говорю́ по-англи́йски.","sub":"[ya ga-va-RYOO pa-an-GLEE-skee] — then swap in по-ру́сски","want":"Я говорю по-английски"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Your full intro","title":"Four bricks, one introduction","paras":["Look how far you've come: you can now stack four bricks into a real introduction — name, where from, nationality, language. You already own every brick.","<b>Меня́ зову́т Э́мма. Я из А́нглии. Я англича́нка. Я говорю́ по-англи́йски.</b> Four short sentences, and still not a single “am” or “a” among them. And to hand the conversation back, the tiny «<b>А ты?</b>» — “and you?” — does all the work."],"audio":"Look how far you've come: you can now stack four bricks into a real introduction — name, where from, nationality, language. You already own every brick. Menya zovut Emma. Ya iz Anglii. Ya anglichanka. Ya govoryu pa-angliyski. Four short sentences, and still not a single am or a among them. And to hand the conversation back, the tiny a ty — and you? — does all the work.","examples":[{"ru":"My name is Ivan. I'm from Russia.","en":"Меня́ зову́т Ива́н. Я <b>из Росси́и</b>.","say":"Меня зовут Иван. Я из России."},{"ru":"I'm Russian. I speak Russian and English.","en":"Я ру́сский. Я говорю́ <b>по-ру́сски</b> и <b>по-англи́йски</b>.","say":"Я русский. Я говорю по-русски и по-английски.","gloss":"и = and"}],"tasks":[{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“My name is Emma. I'm from England.”</b>","want":"Меня́ зову́т Э́мма Я из А́нглии","answer":"Меня́ зову́т Э́мма Я из А́нглии"},{"type":"type","q":"Type in Russian: “I speak Russian.”","accept":["я говорю по-русски","я говорю́ по-ру́сски","ya govoryu po-russki","я говорю по русски"],"placeholder":"Я говорю́…"},{"type":"choice","q":"Someone finishes their intro with «А ты?». What are they doing?","opts":["Saying goodbye","Handing the same question back to you","Telling you their name"],"answer":1,"explain":"«А ты?» = “and you?” — it bounces the same question over, so now it's your turn to introduce yourself."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"In the university café, Ivan sits down with Emma and the questions start. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Приве́т, Э́мма! Отку́да ты?","ru":"Hi, Emma! Where are you from?"},{"who":"A","en":"Я из А́нглии, из Ло́ндона. А ты?","ru":"I'm from England, from London. And you?"},{"who":"B","en":"Я из Росси́и, из Москвы́. Я ру́сский.","ru":"I'm from Russia, from Moscow. I'm Russian."},{"who":"A","en":"О́чень прия́тно! Я англича́нка.","ru":"Very nice! I'm English (a woman)."},{"who":"B","en":"Я говорю́ по-англи́йски и по-ру́сски. А ты?","ru":"I speak English and Russian. And you? (и = and)"},{"who":"A","en":"Я говорю́ по-англи́йски. По-ру́сски… немно́го!","ru":"I speak English. In Russian… a little! (немно́го = a little)"},{"who":"B","en":"Кла́ссно! Пока́, Э́мма!","ru":"Cool! Bye, Emma!"},{"who":"A","en":"Пока́, Ива́н!","ru":"Bye, Ivan!"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words that put you on the map: countries, nationalities, and the language you speak. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"отку́да","ru":"where from","ex":"[at-KOO-da] — «Отку́да ты?» = where are you from?"},{"en":"Я из…","ru":"I'm from…","ex":"[ya eez] — add a country, its ending shifts a little"},{"en":"А́нглия","ru":"England","ex":"[AN-glee-ya] — из А́нглии = from England"},{"en":"Росси́я","ru":"Russia","ex":"[ra-SEE-ya] — из Росси́и = from Russia"},{"en":"Аме́рика","ru":"America","ex":"[a-MYE-ree-ka] — из Аме́рики = from America"},{"en":"Кана́да","ru":"Canada","ex":"[ka-NA-da] — из Кана́ды = from Canada"},{"en":"англича́нин / англича́нка","ru":"Englishman / Englishwoman","ex":"[an-glee-CHA-neen / an-glee-CHAN-ka] — pick your gender"},{"en":"ру́сский / ру́сская","ru":"Russian person (m / f)","ex":"[ROOS-skee / ROOS-ska-ya] — the country is Росси́я, the person is ру́сский"},{"en":"америка́нец / америка́нка","ru":"American (m / f)","ex":"[a-mye-ree-KA-nyets / a-mye-ree-KAN-ka] — man / woman"},{"en":"по-англи́йски","ru":"(in) English","ex":"[pa-an-GLEE-skee] — Я говорю́ по-англи́йски"},{"en":"по-ру́сски","ru":"(in) Russian","ex":"[pa-ROOS-skee] — Я говорю́ по-ру́сски"},{"en":"Я говорю́…","ru":"I speak…","ex":"[ya ga-va-RYOO] — one ready chunk, for now"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Отку́да ты?","Where are you from?"],["Я из Росси́и.","I'm from Russia."],["Я англича́нка.","I'm English. (a woman)"],["Я говорю́ по-ру́сски.","I speak Russian."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — today's material only. If one bites, peek back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"A woman from England states her nationality:","opts":["Я англича́нка.","Я англича́нин.","Я А́нглия."],"answer":0},{"q":"You ask your new professor where she's from. Which fits?","opts":["Отку́да ты?","Отку́да вы?","Отку́да я?"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Я из ___.» — to say “from Russia”, fill the blank:","opts":["Росси́я","Росси́и","ру́сская"],"answer":1},{"q":"How do you say “I speak Russian”?","opts":["Я ру́сский.","Я из Росси́и.","Я говорю́ по-ру́сски."],"answer":2},{"q":"A man from America states his nationality:","opts":["Я америка́нец.","Я америка́нка.","Я Аме́рика."],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's first postcard home — read it aloud, slowly. You know every word.","title":"Приве́т из Москвы́!","sentences":["Приве́т! Меня́ зову́т Э́мма.","Я из А́нглии, из Ло́ндона.","Я англича́нка и студе́нтка.","Я говорю́ по-англи́йски. По-ру́сски немно́го.","Ива́н из Росси́и. Ива́н ру́сский.","Спаси́бо, Москва́!"],"translation":"Hi! My name is Emma. I'm from England, from London. I'm English and a student. I speak English. In Russian, a little. Ivan is from Russia. Ivan is Russian. Thank you, Moscow!"},"essay":{"intro":"Now you place yourself on the map — in writing.","prompt":"Write 3–4 short sentences: greet, give your name, say where you're from and your nationality, and which language you speak.","hint":"Bricks you own: Приве́т! → Меня́ зову́т… → Я из… → Я англича́нин / англича́нка → Я говорю́ по-англи́йски. Match the nationality to your own gender 🙂","example":"Приве́т! Меня́ зову́т Джек. Я из Аме́рики. Я америка́нец. Я говорю́ по-англи́йски."}},"3":{"day":3,"week":"01","themeRu":"He or she?","themeEn":"Он и́ли она́?","intro":"Here's something English lost centuries ago and Russian kept: every noun has a gender. Not just people — a table, a book, a window. Each one is secretly “he”, “she”, or “it”. Today you'll meet <b>он</b>, <b>она́</b>, <b>оно́</b> and learn a trick that tells you the gender at a glance.","introAudio":"Here's something English lost centuries ago and Russian kept: every noun has a gender. Not just people — a table, a book, a window. Each one is secretly he, she, or it. Today you'll meet on, ana, ano and learn a trick that tells you the gender at a glance.","goals":["Meet <b>он / она́ / оно́</b> — he, she, it — for people and for things","Read a noun's gender straight off its <b>ending</b>","Sort <b>стол</b>, <b>кни́га</b>, <b>окно́</b> into he / she / it","See why gender is about the word, not the meaning"],"learned":["Met <b>он / она́ / оно́</b> for people and for things alike","Read a noun's gender from its <b>ending</b>: consonant, -а/-я, -о/-е","Sorted <b>стол</b> → он, <b>кни́га</b> → она́, <b>окно́</b> → оно́","Saw that gender follows the word, not the meaning"],"review":{"intro":"Yesterday you crossed borders. Ten seconds to check it stuck, then we name the world around us.","introAudio":"Yesterday you crossed borders. Ten seconds to check it stuck, then we name the world around us.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A woman from Russia states her nationality:","opts":["Я ру́сский.","Я ру́сская.","Я Росси́я."],"answer":1,"explain":"A woman → <b>ру́сская</b>. «Я ру́сский» is the man's form; «Я Росси́я» would mean “I am Russia”, the country."},{"type":"type","q":"Type in Russian: “I'm from England.”","accept":["я из англии","я из а́нглии","ya iz anglii"],"placeholder":"Я из…"},{"type":"choice","q":"How do you say “I speak Russian”?","opts":["Я говорю́ по-ру́сски.","Я ру́сская.","Я из Росси́и."],"answer":0,"explain":"«Я говорю́ по-ру́сски» = I speak Russian. The other two say your nationality and your origin, not your language."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · He, she, it","title":"<em>Он</em>, <em>она́</em>, <em>оно́</em> — three little words","paras":["You already know я, ты, вы. Here are three more: <b>он</b> [on] = he, <b>она́</b> [a-NA] = she, <b>оно́</b> [a-NO] = it. For people they behave just like English: Ива́н is <b>он</b>, Ле́на is <b>она́</b>.","But Russian has a surprise waiting. These aren't only for people — every single <b>thing</b> is an “он”, an “она́”, or an “оно́” too. A table, a book, a window: each one is secretly he, she, or it. That's grammatical gender, and English quietly dropped it centuries ago."],"audio":"You already know ya, ty, vy. Here are three more: on is he, ana is she, ano is it. For people they behave just like English: Ivan is on, Lena is ana. But Russian has a surprise waiting. These aren't only for people — every single thing is an on, an ana, or an ano too. A table, a book, a window: each one is secretly he, she, or it. That's grammatical gender, and English quietly dropped it centuries ago.","table":{"rows":[["Ива́н → он","Ivan → he"],["Ле́на → она́","Lena → she"],["стол → он","a table → “he”"],["кни́га → она́","a book → “she”"],["окно́ → оно́","a window → “it”"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Ivan? He's a student.","en":"Ива́н? <b>Он</b> студе́нт.","say":"Иван? Он студент."},{"ru":"Lena? She's from Russia.","en":"Ле́на? <b>Она́</b> из Росси́и.","say":"Лена? Она из России."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the word that fits. A wrong tap just teaches faster.","q":"Ива́н is a man. Which word replaces his name?","opts":["она́","он","оно́"],"answer":1,"explain":"A man → <b>он</b> (he). Она́ is “she”, оно́ is “it”."},{"type":"choice","q":"Ле́на is a woman. Which word fits her?","opts":["он","она́","оно́"],"answer":1,"explain":"A woman → <b>она́</b> (she)."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The ending tells you","title":"How to know: look at the last letter","paras":["Here's the gift: you don't have to memorise the gender of every noun. The ending usually tells you. <b>Ends in a consonant</b> → он: стол, дом, го́род. <b>Ends in -а or -я</b> → она́: кни́га, Москва́, страна́. <b>Ends in -о or -е</b> → оно́: окно́, метро́, кафе́.","So the internationalisms you already read fall right into place: <b>метро́</b> and <b>кафе́</b> end in -о / -е, so they're <b>оно́</b>; <b>Москва́</b> ends in -а, so she's <b>она́</b>. Read the tail, know the gender."],"audio":"Here's the gift: you don't have to memorise the gender of every noun. The ending usually tells you. Ends in a consonant, it's on: stol, dom, gorat. Ends in -a or -ya, it's ana: kniga, Moskva, strana. Ends in -o or -e, it's ano: akno, metro, kafe. So the internationalisms you already read fall into place: metro and kafe end in -o and -e, so they're ano; Moskva ends in -a, so she's ana. Read the tail, know the gender.","table":{"rows":[["стол, дом, го́род <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"стол, дом, город\">🔊</button>","consonant → он (he)"],["кни́га, Москва́, страна́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"книга, Москва, страна\">🔊</button>","-а / -я → она́ (she)"],["окно́, метро́, кафе́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"окно, метро, кафе\">🔊</button>","-о / -е → оно́ (it)"]]},"examples":[{"ru":"a book — that's a “she” word","en":"<b>кни́га</b> — она́","say":"книга — она"},{"ru":"a window — that's an “it” word","en":"<b>окно́</b> — оно́","say":"окно — оно"},{"ru":"a city — that's a “he” word","en":"<b>го́род</b> — он","say":"город — он"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«кни́га» ends in -а. Which gender is it?","opts":["он","она́","оно́"],"answer":1,"explain":"-а ending → <b>она́</b>. The ending decides, not the meaning."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian word. The stress mark is optional.","q":"«метро́» ends in -о. Type its gender word (he / she / it in Russian):","accept":["оно","оно́","ono","ano"],"placeholder":"он / она́ / оно́"},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the two cards in order.","q":"Build: <b>“a book — she”</b>","want":"кни́га она́","answer":"кни́га она́"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Don't look for logic","title":"A table isn't manly — gender ≠ meaning","paras":["Resist the urge to find meaning in the gender. <b>Стол</b> (a table) is “он”, but there's nothing manly about a table. <b>Кни́га</b> (a book) is “она́”, but books aren't feminine. The gender lives in the <b>word's ending</b>, not in the object. Trust the tail, not your intuition.","And your friend from day one is still here: no “a”, no “the”. <b>Стол</b> is “a table”, “the table”, or just “table” — Russian never marks which, and never will."],"audio":"Resist the urge to find meaning in the gender. Stol, a table, is on, but there's nothing manly about a table. Kniga, a book, is ana, but books aren't feminine. The gender lives in the word's ending, not in the object. Trust the tail, not your intuition. And your friend from day one is still here: no a, no the. Stol is a table, the table, or just table — Russian never marks which.","mistakes":[{"wrong":"«стол» is “она́” because tables feel gentle.","right":"«стол» is “он” — it ends in a consonant.","why":"Meaning never decides gender. The ending does: стол ends in a consonant → <b>он</b>, full stop."}],"examples":[{"ru":"a table — “he”, a book — “she”","en":"<b>стол</b> — он, <b>кни́га</b> — она́","say":"стол — он, книга — она"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Why is «окно́» an “оно́”?","opts":["Because windows feel neutral.","Because it ends in -о.","Because it's a small thing."],"answer":1,"explain":"Only the ending counts: -о → <b>оно́</b>. Meaning and size are irrelevant."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Стол» means “a table”, “the table”, or just “table”. How do you say “the” in Russian?","opts":["Nothing — Russian has no articles.","You add «-то» to the noun.","You use «оно́»."],"answer":0,"explain":"Russian has no “a” and no “the” at all — «стол» is all three English versions at once."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Lena is a student. She's from Russia.”</b>","want":"Ле́на студе́нтка Она́ из Росси́и","answer":"Ле́на студе́нтка Она́ из Росси́и"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Pointing with он / она́ / оно́","title":"Swap the noun for <em>он</em>, <em>она́</em>, <em>оно́</em>","paras":["Once you know a noun's gender, you can point at it without repeating it. A table is «он», so instead of naming it again you just say <b>он</b>. Кни́га is <b>она́</b>. Окно́ is <b>оно́</b>. The little word carries the whole thing.","Two honest edges, so nothing surprises you later. A few -а words are “он” because they name males — <b>па́па</b> (dad) is “он”, not “она́”. And words ending in a soft sign <b>-ь</b> can go either way; you'll meet those one at a time. For every ordinary thing, though, the ending is your reliable map."],"audio":"Once you know a noun's gender, you can point at it without repeating it. A table is on, so instead of naming it again you just say on. Kniga is ana. Akno is ano. The little word carries the whole thing. Two honest edges, so nothing surprises you later. A few -a words are on because they name males — papa, dad, is on, not ana. And words ending in a soft sign can go either way; you'll meet those one at a time. For every ordinary thing, the ending is your reliable map.","mistakes":[{"wrong":"Па́па — она́ (ends in -а).","right":"Па́па — он.","why":"«Па́па» ends in -а, but it names a male, so it's <b>он</b>. This tiny group (па́па, де́душка) follows the person, not the ending."}],"examples":[{"ru":"Dad is Russian. He's from Moscow.","en":"Па́па ру́сский. <b>Он</b> из Москвы́.","say":"Папа русский. Он из Москвы."},{"ru":"America — “she” — is a country.","en":"Аме́рика — <b>она́</b>. Аме́рика — страна́.","say":"Америка — она. Америка — страна."}],"tasks":[{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Ivan is a student. He's from Russia.”</b>","want":"Ива́н студе́нт Он из Росси́и","answer":"Ива́н студе́нт Он из Росси́и"},{"type":"type","q":"About Ле́на, type in Russian: “She's from England.”","accept":["она из англии","она́ из а́нглии","ona iz anglii"],"placeholder":"Она́ …"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud. Feel how each ending points to its word.","target":"Кни́га — она́, окно́ — оно́.","sub":"[KNEE-ga a-NA, ak-NO a-NO]","want":"Книга она, окно оно"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ivan walks Emma through a campus classroom, naming things and sorting them into he, she, it. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Смотри́: стол — он, кни́га — она́.","ru":"Look: a table is “he”, a book is “she”. (смотри́ = look)"},{"who":"A","en":"А окно́?","ru":"And the window?"},{"who":"B","en":"Окно́ — оно́. Метро́ то́же оно́.","ru":"The window is “it”. The metro is “it” too."},{"who":"A","en":"Кла́ссно! А Москва́?","ru":"Cool! And Moscow?"},{"who":"B","en":"Москва́ — она́. Го́род — он.","ru":"Moscow is “she”. A city is “he”."},{"who":"A","en":"Стол — он? Стра́нно!","ru":"A table is “he”? Strange! (стра́нно = strange)"},{"who":"B","en":"Да! Окно́ — оно́, а стол — он.","ru":"Yes! A window is “it”, and a table is “he”."},{"who":"A","en":"Он, она́, оно́. Спаси́бо, Ива́н!","ru":"He, she, it. Thank you, Ivan!"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Ten things and three little words to sort them. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"он","ru":"he / it (a “он” word)","ex":"[on] — Ива́н → он; стол → он"},{"en":"она́","ru":"she / it (a “она́” word)","ex":"[a-NA] — Ле́на → она́; кни́га → она́"},{"en":"оно́","ru":"it (a neuter word)","ex":"[a-NO] — окно́ → оно́; метро́ → оно́"},{"en":"и́ли","ru":"or","ex":"[EE-lee] — он и́ли она́? = he or she?"},{"en":"стол","ru":"table","ex":"[stol] — ends in a consonant → он"},{"en":"дом","ru":"house / home","ex":"[dom] — ends in a consonant → он"},{"en":"кни́га","ru":"book","ex":"[KNEE-ga] — ends in -а → она́"},{"en":"окно́","ru":"window","ex":"[ak-NO] — ends in -о → оно́"},{"en":"го́род","ru":"city","ex":"[GO-rat] — он; the final д sounds like “t”"},{"en":"страна́","ru":"country","ex":"[stra-NA] — ends in -а → она́"},{"en":"телефо́н","ru":"telephone","ex":"[tye-lye-FON] — ends in a consonant → он"},{"en":"ма́ма","ru":"mum","ex":"[MA-ma] — ends in -а → она́"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["стол","table"],["кни́га","book"],["окно́","window"],["дом","house"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — today's material only. If one bites, peek back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"«окно́» is which gender?","opts":["он","она́","оно́"],"answer":2},{"q":"A table — «стол» — is…","opts":["“он”, because it ends in a consonant","“она́”, because tables are gentle","“оно́”, because it's furniture"],"answer":0},{"q":"Which word means “she”?","opts":["он","она́","оно́"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Москва́» ends in -а. So Moscow is…","opts":["он","оно́","она́"],"answer":2},{"q":"How many words does Russian have for “the”?","opts":["one","none — there are no articles","three, one per gender"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"A little gender tour, straight from Emma's notebook. Read it aloud and listen for the endings.","title":"Он, она́ и́ли оно́?","sentences":["Стол — он. Кни́га — она́. Окно́ — оно́.","Ива́н ру́сский. Он из Москвы́.","Ле́на то́же ру́сская. Она́ из Москвы́.","Метро́ — оно́. Кафе́ — оно́. Парк — он.","Кни́га — она́, а стол — он."],"translation":"A table is “he”. A book is “she”. A window is “it”. Ivan is Russian. He's from Moscow. Lena is Russian too. She's from Moscow. The metro is “it”. A café is “it”. A park is “he”. A book is “she”, and a table is “he”."},"essay":{"intro":"Look around the room you're in right now.","prompt":"Name three things you can see and give each its gender word — «стол — он», «кни́га — она́», «окно́ — оно́». Then add one line about a person (Ива́н — он / Ле́на — она́).","hint":"Read the last letter: consonant → он, -а/-я → она́, -о/-е → оно́. Don't hunt for meaning — trust the ending 🙂","example":"Стол — он. Кни́га — она́. Окно́ — оно́. Ива́н — он. Он студе́нт."}},"4":{"day":4,"week":"01","themeRu":"What is this?","themeEn":"Что э́то?","intro":"Picture Ivan pointing round his kitchen: “What's this? Who's that?” Today you get the two questions and their easy answers. The star is one tiny word, <b>э́то</b> — “this is / that is” — and it comes with more good news: it never changes shape, and it still isn't the word “is”.","introAudio":"Picture Ivan pointing round his kitchen: what's this? who's that? Today you get the two questions and their easy answers. The star is one tiny word, eta — this is, that is — and it comes with more good news: it never changes shape, and it still isn't the word is.","goals":["Name anything with <b>Э́то…</b> — “this is / that is”","Ask <b>Что э́то?</b> about things and <b>Кто э́то?</b> about people","Answer with <b>да</b> / <b>нет</b>, and deny with <b>Э́то не…</b>","See why <b>э́то</b> never changes and hides no “is”"],"learned":["Named things with <b>Э́то…</b> for “он”, “она́”, and “оно́” alike","Asked <b>Что э́то?</b> about things and <b>Кто э́то?</b> about people","Answered with <b>да</b> / <b>нет</b> and denied with <b>Э́то не…</b>","Kept <b>э́то</b> free of any hidden “is”"],"review":{"intro":"Yesterday everything had a gender. Ten seconds to prove it stuck, then we start pointing.","introAudio":"Yesterday everything had a gender. Ten seconds to prove it stuck, then we start pointing.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«окно́» ends in -о. Which gender word replaces it?","opts":["он","она́","оно́"],"answer":2,"explain":"-о ending → <b>оно́</b> (it). The ending decides the gender."},{"type":"choice","q":"Ле́на is a woman. Which pronoun replaces her name?","opts":["он","она́","оно́"],"answer":1,"explain":"A woman → <b>она́</b> (she)."},{"type":"type","q":"«кни́га» ends in -а. Type its gender word in Russian:","accept":["она","она́","ana","ona"],"placeholder":"он / она́ / оно́"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · This is…","title":"<em>Э́то</em> — “this is”, and it never changes","paras":["Point at anything and name it with one word: <b>э́то</b> [E-ta]. «<b>Э́то стол</b>» — “this is a table”. «<b>Э́то кни́га</b>» — “this is a book”. «<b>Э́то Ива́н</b>» — “this is Ivan”. One little word does “this is / that is / here is”.","And true to Russian, there's still no “is” hiding anywhere. «Э́то стол» is literally “this table” — the “is” lives only in the English translation. <b>Э́то</b> itself just means “this / that thing here”, not “is”.","Best of all: <b>э́то</b> never changes shape. Whether you point at a “он”, an “она́”, or an “оно́”, it stays <b>э́то</b>: Э́то стол, Э́то кни́га, Э́то окно́. One word, no exceptions."],"audio":"Point at anything and name it with one word: eta. Eta stol — this is a table. Eta kniga — this is a book. Eta Ivan — this is Ivan. One little word does this is, that is, here is. And true to Russian, there's still no is hiding anywhere. Eta stol is literally this table — the is lives only in the English. Eta itself just means this, that thing here, not is. Best of all, eta never changes shape: whether you point at an on, an ana, or an ano, it stays eta.","table":{"rows":[["Э́то стол. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Это стол.\">🔊</button>","This is a table. (он)"],["Э́то кни́га. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Это книга.\">🔊</button>","This is a book. (она́)"],["Э́то окно́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Это окно.\">🔊</button>","This is a window. (оно́)"],["Э́то Ива́н. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Это Иван.\">🔊</button>","This is Ivan. (a person)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"This is a pen.","en":"<b>Э́то</b> ру́чка.","say":"Это ручка."},{"ru":"That's Moscow.","en":"<b>Э́то</b> Москва́.","say":"Это Москва."},{"ru":"This is water.","en":"<b>Э́то</b> вода́.","say":"Это вода."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer that fits. A wrong tap just teaches faster.","q":"How do you say “This is a book”?","opts":["Э́то есть кни́га.","Э́то кни́га.","Кни́га есть э́то."],"answer":1,"explain":"«Э́то кни́га» — no “is”, no “a”. «Э́то» already carries “this is”."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“This is a car.”</b>","want":"Э́то маши́на","answer":"Э́то маши́на"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. The stress mark is optional.","q":"Type in Russian: “This is water.”","accept":["это вода","э́то вода́","eta voda","eto voda"],"placeholder":"Э́то…"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · What? Who?","title":"<em>Что э́то?</em> and <em>Кто э́то?</em>","paras":["To ask about a thing, put <b>что</b> [shto] in front: «<b>Что э́то?</b>» — “what is this?”. To ask about a person, use <b>кто</b> [kto]: «<b>Кто э́то?</b>» — “who is this?”. Same «э́то», different question word.","A spelling surprise worth a smile: <b>что</b> is written with ч but pronounced [shto], as if it began with ш. It's one of a tiny handful of words that do this — just know it and move on.","The answer reuses «э́то»: «Что э́то?» → «<b>Э́то ру́чка</b>». «Кто э́то?» → «<b>Э́то Ива́н</b>». Question and answer, both anchored on the same little word."],"audio":"To ask about a thing, put shto in front: shto eta — what is this? To ask about a person, use kto: kto eta — who is this? Same eta, different question word. A spelling surprise worth a smile: chto is written with the letter che but pronounced shto, as if it began with sha. It's one of a tiny handful of words that do this — just know it and move on. The answer reuses eta: shto eta becomes eta ruchka; kto eta becomes eta Ivan.","table":{"rows":[["Что э́то? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Что это?\">🔊</button>","What is this? (a thing)"],["Кто э́то? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Кто это?\">🔊</button>","Who is this? (a person)"],["Э́то ру́чка. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Это ручка.\">🔊</button>","It's a pen."],["Э́то Ле́на. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Это Лена.\">🔊</button>","It's Lena."]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"— What's this? — It's a bag.","en":"— <b>Что э́то?</b> — Э́то су́мка.","say":"Что это? Это сумка."},{"ru":"— Who's this? — It's a friend.","en":"— <b>Кто э́то?</b> — Э́то друг.","say":"Кто это? Это друг."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"You point at a pen and want its name. Which question?","opts":["Что э́то?","Кто э́то?","Отку́да э́то?"],"answer":0,"explain":"A thing → «<b>Что</b> э́то?». «Кто э́то?» asks about a person; «Отку́да э́то?» asks where it's from."},{"type":"choice","q":"You point at Ле́на and want to know who she is. Which question?","opts":["Что э́то?","Кто э́то?","Как э́то?"],"answer":1,"explain":"A person → «<b>Кто</b> э́то?». «Что э́то?» would treat Ле́на like an object; «Как э́то?» means “how's that?”."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“What is this?”</b>","want":"Что э́то","answer":"Что э́то"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Yes, no, and “not”","title":"<em>Да</em>, <em>нет</em>, and <em>Э́то не…</em>","paras":["Answer a yes-or-no question with <b>да</b> (yes) or <b>нет</b> (no). «Э́то ру́чка?» → «<b>Да</b>, э́то ру́чка». «Э́то стол?» → «<b>Нет</b>, э́то окно́».","To say something is <b>not</b> what you'd think, drop <b>не</b> right in front of the word: «<b>Э́то не стол</b>» — “this isn't a table”. Notice: still no “is”. «не» simply means “not”, and it stands guard before whatever it denies."],"audio":"Answer a yes-or-no question with da (yes) or nyet (no). Eta ruchka? Da, eta ruchka. Eta stol? Nyet, eta akno. To say something is not what you'd think, drop nye right in front of the word: eta nye stol — this isn't a table. Notice: still no is. Nye simply means not, and it stands guard before whatever it denies.","table":{"rows":[["Да, э́то ру́чка. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Да, это ручка.\">🔊</button>","Yes, it's a pen."],["Нет, э́то су́мка. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Нет, это сумка.\">🔊</button>","No, it's a bag."],["Э́то не стол. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Это не стол.\">🔊</button>","This isn't a table."]],"star":2},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Э́то есть не стол.","right":"Э́то не стол.","why":"There's no “is” to negate — Russian just puts <b>не</b> before the word. «Э́то не стол» is the whole sentence."}],"examples":[{"ru":"— Is this water? — No, it's tea.","en":"— Э́то вода́? — <b>Нет</b>, э́то чай.","say":"Это вода? Нет, это чай."},{"ru":"This isn't a pen, it's a bag.","en":"Э́то <b>не</b> ру́чка, э́то су́мка.","say":"Это не ручка, это сумка."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Э́то маши́на?» You point at a book and answer truthfully:","opts":["Нет, э́то кни́га.","Да, э́то маши́на.","Э́то не да."],"answer":0,"explain":"It's a book, not a car → «<b>Нет</b>, э́то кни́га». «Э́то не да» is nonsense."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“No, this is a bag.”</b>","want":"Нет э́то су́мка","answer":"Нет э́то су́мка"},{"type":"type","q":"Type in Russian: “This isn't a table.”","accept":["это не стол","э́то не стол","eta nye stol","eto ne stol"],"placeholder":"Э́то не…"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Two traps to sidestep","title":"<em>Э́то</em> isn't “is”, and isn't <em>оно́</em>","paras":["Two look-alikes to keep apart. First: <b>э́то</b> is not the word “is”. English “this <b>is</b> a book” has a verb; «Э́то кни́га» has none. «Э́то» means “this / that”, and the “is” is simply absent — as always in the present.","Second: don't mix up <b>э́то</b> (this is…) with <b>оно́</b> (it, yesterday's gender pronoun). «<b>Оно́</b>» replaces a specific “-о/-е” noun: окно́ → оно́. «<b>Э́то</b>» points at anything at all — a “он”, an “она́”, or an “оно́” — and always stays э́то. When you're introducing or pointing, you want <b>э́то</b>."],"audio":"Two look-alikes to keep apart. First: eta is not the word is. English this is a book has a verb; eta kniga has none. Eta means this or that, and the is is simply absent, as always in the present. Second: don't mix up eta, meaning this is, with ano, yesterday's gender pronoun. Ano replaces a specific -o or -e noun: akno becomes ano. Eta points at anything at all and always stays eta. When you're introducing or pointing, you want eta.","mistakes":[{"wrong":"Оно́ кни́га.","right":"Э́то кни́га.","why":"To point and name, use <b>э́то</b>, not the pronoun. «Оно́» is “it” for a neuter noun; «кни́га» is feminine anyway, so «оно́» is doubly wrong here."}],"examples":[{"ru":"This is a window. (pointing)","en":"<b>Э́то</b> окно́.","say":"Это окно."},{"ru":"The window? It's… (referring back)","en":"Окно́? <b>Оно́</b>…","say":"Окно? Оно...","gloss":"оно́ = it, standing in for окно́"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence has NO hidden “is”, like real Russian?","opts":["Э́то кни́га.","Э́то ис кни́га.","Э́то есть кни́га."],"answer":0,"explain":"«Э́то кни́га» — “this (is) a book”, zero verb. Both «ис» and «есть» are the English “is” sneaking back in."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud — first the question, then the answer.","target":"Что э́то? — Э́то кни́га.","sub":"[shto E-ta — E-ta KNEE-ga]","want":"Что это? Это книга."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ivan and Emma unpack a box in the kitchen. He points, she guesses. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Что э́то?","ru":"What's this?"},{"who":"A","en":"Э́то ру́чка. А э́то су́мка.","ru":"It's a pen. And this is a bag. (а = and)"},{"who":"B","en":"Э́то чай?","ru":"Is this tea?"},{"who":"A","en":"Нет, э́то не чай. Э́то вода́.","ru":"No, it's not tea. It's water."},{"who":"B","en":"А э́то? Кто э́то?","ru":"And this? Who's this?"},{"who":"A","en":"Э́то Ле́на. Она́ студе́нтка.","ru":"That's Lena. She's a student."},{"who":"B","en":"Да, Ле́на — ру́сская. Она́ из Москвы́.","ru":"Yes, Lena is Russian. She's from Moscow."},{"who":"A","en":"Кла́ссно! Спаси́бо, Ива́н.","ru":"Cool! Thanks, Ivan."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Two questions, two answers, and a boxful of things to point at. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"э́то","ru":"this is / that is","ex":"[E-ta] — points at anything; never changes"},{"en":"что","ru":"what","ex":"[shto] — «Что э́то?» = what's this? (ч sounds like “sh”)"},{"en":"кто","ru":"who","ex":"[kto] — «Кто э́то?» = who's this?"},{"en":"не","ru":"not","ex":"[nye] — «Э́то не стол» = this isn't a table"},{"en":"да","ru":"yes","ex":"[da] — «Да, э́то ру́чка»"},{"en":"нет","ru":"no","ex":"[nyet] — «Нет, э́то су́мка»"},{"en":"ру́чка","ru":"pen","ex":"[ROOCH-ka] — ends in -а → она́"},{"en":"су́мка","ru":"bag","ex":"[SOOM-ka] — ends in -а → она́"},{"en":"чай","ru":"tea","ex":"[chay] — ends in a consonant → он"},{"en":"вода́","ru":"water","ex":"[va-DA] — ends in -а → она́"},{"en":"друг","ru":"friend","ex":"[drook] — a person → он; final г sounds like “k”"},{"en":"маши́на","ru":"car","ex":"[ma-SHEE-na] — ends in -а → она́"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Что э́то?","What is this?"],["Кто э́то?","Who is this?"],["Э́то не стол.","This isn't a table."],["Да, э́то ру́чка.","Yes, this is a pen."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — today's material only. If one bites, peek back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"How do you say “This is a table”?","opts":["Стол есть э́то.","Э́то есть стол.","Э́то стол."],"answer":2},{"q":"You point at a person and want to know who it is. You ask:","opts":["Кто э́то?","Что э́то?","Отку́да э́то?"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Э́то не кни́га» means:","opts":["This is a book.","This isn't a book.","Is this a book?"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which word means “this is / that is”?","opts":["что","э́то","оно́"],"answer":1},{"q":"In «Э́то ру́чка», where is the word for “is”?","opts":["There is no word for “is”.","It's «э́то».","It's hidden in «ру́чка»."],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"A little pointing game from Emma's kitchen. Read it aloud and hear how «э́то» never changes.","title":"Что э́то?","sentences":["— Что э́то? — Э́то ру́чка.","— А э́то? — Э́то су́мка.","— Э́то чай? — Нет, э́то вода́.","— Кто э́то? — Э́то Ива́н. Он студе́нт.","Ива́н — друг. Он ру́сский."],"translation":"— What's this? — It's a pen. — And this? — It's a bag. — Is this tea? — No, it's water. — Who's this? — It's Ivan. He's a student. Ivan is a friend. He's Russian."},"essay":{"intro":"Give your camera a Russian voice.","prompt":"Point at four things near you and name each with «Э́то…». Then ask and answer one «Кто э́то?» about a person.","hint":"«Э́то» never changes and hides no “is”: «Э́то стол», «Э́то кни́га», «Э́то окно́». For a person: «Кто э́то? — Э́то…» 🙂","example":"Э́то стол. Э́то кни́га. Э́то ру́чка. Э́то окно́. — Кто э́то? — Э́то Ива́н."}},"5":{"day":5,"week":"01","themeRu":"My","themeEn":"Мой и моя́","intro":"Emma finally reaches her dorm room and starts unpacking: “my desk, my lamp, my window”. In English “my” never changes. In Russian it has three shapes, one per gender — and today you'll pick the right one every time. The twist worth remembering: it agrees with the <b>thing</b>, not with you.","introAudio":"Emma finally reaches her dorm room and starts unpacking: my desk, my lamp, my window. In English my never changes. In Russian it has three shapes, one per gender — and today you'll pick the right one every time. The twist worth remembering: it agrees with the thing, not with you.","goals":["Say “my” three ways: <b>мой</b>, <b>моя́</b>, <b>моё</b> — matched to the thing","Choose the right one from a noun's <b>gender</b>","See that “my” agrees with the <b>thing</b>, not with you","Meet <b>мои́</b> — “my” for several things"],"learned":["Said “my” three ways: <b>мой стол</b>, <b>моя́ кни́га</b>, <b>моё окно́</b>","Chose the right form from each noun's <b>gender</b>","Saw that “my” agrees with the <b>thing owned</b>, not the owner","Met <b>мои́</b> for several things"],"review":{"intro":"Yesterday you learned to point: «Э́то…». A quick warm-up, then we make everything yours.","introAudio":"Yesterday you learned to point: eta. A quick warm-up, then we make everything yours.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"How do you say “This is a book”?","opts":["Э́то есть кни́га.","Э́то кни́га.","Кни́га есть."],"answer":1,"explain":"«Э́то кни́га» — no “is”, no “a”. «Э́то» already means “this is”."},{"type":"choice","q":"«окно́» ends in -о. Its gender word is:","opts":["он","она́","оно́"],"answer":2,"explain":"-о ending → <b>оно́</b>. The ending decides the gender."},{"type":"type","q":"Type in Russian: “What is this?”","accept":["что это","что э́то","shto eta","chto eto"],"placeholder":"…?"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · My","title":"<em>Мой</em>, <em>моя́</em>, <em>моё</em> — “my”, three shapes","paras":["English “my” is one word for everything: my table, my book, my window. Russian “my” has three shapes, one per gender. <b>мой</b> for “он” things, <b>моя́</b> for “она́” things, <b>моё</b> for “оно́” things: <b>мой стол</b>, <b>моя́ кни́га</b>, <b>моё окно́</b>.","See the rhyme? The word for “my” echoes the noun's own gender ending: моя́ and кни́га both lean on -а; моё and окно́ both end in -о. Match the tune and you're right."],"audio":"English my is one word for everything: my table, my book, my window. Russian my has three shapes, one per gender. Moy for on things, maya for ana things, mayo for ano things: moy stol, maya kniga, mayo akno. See the rhyme? The word for my echoes the noun's own gender ending: maya and kniga both lean on -a; mayo and akno both end in -o. Match the tune and you're right.","table":{"rows":[["мой стол <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мой стол\">🔊</button>","my table (он)"],["моя́ кни́га <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"моя книга\">🔊</button>","my book (она́)"],["моё окно́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"моё окно\">🔊</button>","my window (оно́)"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"my phone","en":"<b>мой</b> телефо́н","say":"мой телефон"},{"ru":"my bag","en":"<b>моя́</b> су́мка","say":"моя сумка"},{"ru":"my window","en":"<b>моё</b> окно́","say":"моё окно"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer that fits. A wrong tap just teaches faster.","q":"«стол» is a “он” word. How do you say “my table”?","opts":["моя́ стол","мой стол","моё стол"],"answer":1,"explain":"«стол» is он → <b>мой стол</b>. The “my” matches the noun's gender."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the two cards in order.","q":"Build: <b>“my book”</b>","want":"моя́ кни́га","answer":"моя́ кни́га"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. The stress mark is optional.","q":"Type in Russian: “my window”.","accept":["моё окно","моё окно́","мое окно","mayo akno","moyo okno"],"placeholder":"… окно́"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Which shape?","title":"First find the gender, then pick <em>мой / моя́ / моё</em>","paras":["The recipe is two steps. <b>Step 1:</b> find the noun's gender from its ending — consonant → он, -а/-я → она́, -о/-е → оно́ (exactly yesterday's map). <b>Step 2:</b> pick the matching “my”: он → <b>мой</b>, она́ → <b>моя́</b>, оно́ → <b>моё</b>.","So «ла́мпа» (a lamp) ends in -а → она́ → <b>моя́ ла́мпа</b>. «телефо́н» ends in a consonant → он → <b>мой телефо́н</b>. One glance at the tail decides everything.","One friendly warning for later: a few nouns hide their gender behind a soft sign -ь. «крова́ть» (a bed) looks like it could go either way, but it's “она́” — so <b>моя́ крова́ть</b>. You'll collect these one by one."],"audio":"The recipe is two steps. Step one: find the noun's gender from its ending — consonant is on, -a or -ya is ana, -o or -e is ano, exactly yesterday's map. Step two: pick the matching my: on takes moy, ana takes maya, ano takes mayo. So lampa, a lamp, ends in -a, so ana, so maya lampa. Telefon ends in a consonant, so on, so moy telefon. One glance at the tail decides everything. One friendly warning for later: a few nouns hide their gender behind a soft sign. Krovat, a bed, is ana, so maya krovat. You'll collect these one by one.","table":{"rows":[["телефо́н → он → мой телефо́н <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мой телефон\">🔊</button>","phone → он → my phone"],["ла́мпа → она́ → моя́ ла́мпа <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"моя лампа\">🔊</button>","lamp → она́ → my lamp"],["окно́ → оно́ → моё окно́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"моё окно\">🔊</button>","window → оно́ → my window"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"my room","en":"<b>моя́</b> ко́мната","say":"моя комната"},{"ru":"my key","en":"<b>мой</b> ключ","say":"мой ключ"},{"ru":"my photo","en":"<b>моя́</b> фотогра́фия","say":"моя фотография"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«ла́мпа» ends in -а. Say “my lamp”:","opts":["моя́ ла́мпа","мой ла́мпа","моё ла́мпа"],"answer":0,"explain":"-а → она́ → <b>моя́ ла́мпа</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which pair matches correctly?","opts":["мой ключ","моё стол","моя́ окно́"],"answer":0,"explain":"«ключ» is он → <b>мой ключ</b>. «стол» needs мой (not моё); «окно́» needs моё (not моя́)."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“my room”</b>","want":"моя́ ко́мната","answer":"моя́ ко́мната"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The famous trap","title":"“My” agrees with the thing, not with you","paras":["Here's the mistake almost every English speaker makes, so let's disarm it now. In English, a man and a woman both say “my book” — “my” never bends. In Russian, «my» bends to the <b>book</b>, not to the speaker. «кни́га» is “она́”, so <b>everyone</b> says «<b>моя́</b> кни́га» — Ива́н too, even though he's a man.","Flip it: «стол» is “он”, so everyone says «<b>мой</b> стол», a woman included. Your own gender never enters the room. The only question that matters is: what gender is the <b>thing</b> you own?"],"audio":"Here's the mistake almost every English speaker makes, so let's disarm it now. In English, a man and a woman both say my book — my never bends. In Russian, my bends to the book, not to the speaker. Kniga is ana, so everyone says maya kniga — Ivan too, even though he's a man. Flip it: stol is on, so everyone says moy stol, a woman included. Your own gender never enters the room. The only question that matters is: what gender is the thing you own?","mistakes":[{"wrong":"Ива́н: «мой кни́га».","right":"Ива́н: «моя́ кни́га».","why":"«кни́га» is feminine, so it takes <b>моя́</b> — even from a man. The possessive follows the thing owned, never the owner."}],"examples":[{"ru":"my table (said by a woman)","en":"<b>мой</b> стол","say":"мой стол","gloss":"стол is он, so мой — her own gender doesn't matter"},{"ru":"my book (said by a man)","en":"<b>моя́</b> кни́га","say":"моя книга","gloss":"кни́га is она́, so моя́ — his own gender doesn't matter"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Ива́н (a man) talks about his book. He says:","opts":["мой кни́га","моя́ кни́га","моё кни́га"],"answer":1,"explain":"«кни́га» is feminine → <b>моя́</b>, whoever is speaking. Ivan's own gender is irrelevant."},{"type":"choice","q":"Ле́на (a woman) points at her desk («стол»). She says:","opts":["моя́ стол","моё стол","мой стол"],"answer":2,"explain":"«стол» is masculine → <b>мой стол</b>, even from Lena. The thing decides, not the owner."},{"type":"type","q":"A woman talks about her book. Type “my book” in Russian:","accept":["моя книга","моя́ кни́га","maya kniga","moya kniga"],"placeholder":"… кни́га"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · And when there are several","title":"<em>Мои́</em> — “my” for more than one","paras":["One last shape, just so it doesn't surprise you: when you own <b>several</b> things, “my” becomes <b>мои́</b> [ma-EE]. «<b>мои́ друзья́</b>» — “my friends”. You'll use it properly next week, once we start making plurals; for now, just recognise it.","Put it all together and you can lay claim to your whole room: <b>мой</b> стол, <b>моя́</b> ла́мпа, <b>моё</b> окно́, <b>мои́</b> друзья́. Four shapes of one little word — and each one simply echoes what it's holding."],"audio":"One last shape, just so it doesn't surprise you: when you own several things, my becomes maee. Maee druzya — my friends. You'll use it properly next week, once we start making plurals; for now, just recognise it. Put it all together and you can lay claim to your whole room: moy stol, maya lampa, mayo akno, maee druzya. Four shapes of one little word — and each one simply echoes what it's holding.","table":{"rows":[["мой стол <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мой стол\">🔊</button>","my table (он)"],["моя́ ла́мпа <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"моя лампа\">🔊</button>","my lamp (она́)"],["моё окно́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"моё окно\">🔊</button>","my window (оно́)"],["мои́ друзья́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мои друзья\">🔊</button>","my friends (several)"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"This is my room.","en":"<b>Э́то моя́</b> ко́мната.","say":"Это моя комната."},{"ru":"This is my key.","en":"<b>Э́то мой</b> ключ.","say":"Это мой ключ."}],"tasks":[{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“This is my room.”</b>","want":"Э́то моя́ ко́мната","answer":"Э́то моя́ ко́мната"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud — claim your room.","target":"Э́то моя́ ко́мната.","sub":"[E-ta ma-YA KOM-na-ta]","want":"Это моя комната."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Lena helps Emma settle into her dorm room. Emma names each thing as it comes out of the box. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Кла́ссно! Что э́то?","ru":"Cool! What's this?"},{"who":"A","en":"Э́то моя́ ко́мната. Э́то мой стол.","ru":"This is my room. This is my desk."},{"who":"B","en":"А э́то? Ла́мпа?","ru":"And this? A lamp?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да, моя́ ла́мпа. А э́то моё окно́.","ru":"Yes, my lamp. And this is my window. (а = and)"},{"who":"B","en":"Москва́! Кла́ссно!","ru":"Moscow! Cool!"},{"who":"A","en":"А э́то мой телефо́н и мой ключ.","ru":"And this is my phone and my key. (и = and)"},{"who":"B","en":"А э́то кто?","ru":"And who are these?"},{"who":"A","en":"Э́то мои́ друзья́: Ива́н и ты!","ru":"These are my friends: Ivan and you! (мои́ друзья́ = my friends)"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Four shapes of “my” and a roomful of things to own. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"мой","ru":"my (for a “он” thing)","ex":"[moy] — мой стол, мой ключ"},{"en":"моя́","ru":"my (for a “она́” thing)","ex":"[ma-YA] — моя́ кни́га, моя́ ла́мпа"},{"en":"моё","ru":"my (for a “оно́” thing)","ex":"[ma-YO] — моё окно́"},{"en":"мои́","ru":"my (for several things)","ex":"[ma-EE] — мои́ друзья́ = my friends"},{"en":"ко́мната","ru":"room","ex":"[KOM-na-ta] — -а → она́ → моя́ ко́мната"},{"en":"крова́ть","ru":"bed","ex":"[kra-VAT] — a soft-sign “она́” → моя́ крова́ть"},{"en":"ла́мпа","ru":"lamp","ex":"[LAM-pa] — -а → она́ → моя́ ла́мпа"},{"en":"окно́","ru":"window","ex":"[ak-NO] — -о → оно́ → моё окно́"},{"en":"стол","ru":"table / desk","ex":"[stol] — consonant → он → мой стол"},{"en":"телефо́н","ru":"phone","ex":"[tye-lye-FON] — consonant → он → мой телефо́н"},{"en":"ключ","ru":"key","ex":"[klyooch] — consonant → он → мой ключ"},{"en":"фотогра́фия","ru":"photo","ex":"[fa-ta-GRA-fee-ya] — -я → она́ → моя́ фотогра́фия"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["мой телефо́н","my phone"],["моя́ су́мка","my bag"],["моё окно́","my window"],["моя́ крова́ть","my bed"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — today's material only. If one bites, peek back at the rule above.","items":[{"q":"«стол» is “он”. “My table” is:","opts":["мой стол","моя́ стол","моё стол"],"answer":0},{"q":"«окно́» is neuter. “My window” is:","opts":["мой окно́","моя́ окно́","моё окно́"],"answer":2},{"q":"Ива́н (a man) talks about his book («кни́га»). He says:","opts":["моя́ кни́га","мой кни́га","моё кни́га"],"answer":0},{"q":"In Russian, “my” changes to match…","opts":["the speaker's gender","the gender of the thing owned","nothing — it never changes"],"answer":1},{"q":"You own several things. “My” becomes:","opts":["мой","мои́","моё"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note above her desk — her whole room in six lines. Read it aloud and match each «my» to its thing.","title":"Моя́ ко́мната","sentences":["Э́то моя́ ко́мната.","Э́то мой стол. Э́то моя́ ла́мпа.","Э́то моё окно́. Москва́!","Э́то мой телефо́н и мой ключ.","А э́то — мои́ друзья́: Ива́н и Ле́на."],"translation":"This is my room. This is my desk. This is my lamp. This is my window. Moscow! This is my phone and my key. And these are my friends: Ivan and Lena."},"essay":{"intro":"Move into your Russian room.","prompt":"Describe your room in 3–4 sentences: name four things and put the right “my” on each — «мой стол», «моя́ ла́мпа», «моё окно́»…","hint":"Two steps: find the thing's gender from its ending, then match — он→мой, она́→моя́, оно́→моё. Your own gender never matters, only the thing's 🙂","example":"Э́то моя́ ко́мната. Э́то мой стол. Э́то моя́ ла́мпа. Э́то моё окно́."}},"6":{"day":6,"week":"01","themeRu":"How old are you?","themeEn":"Ско́лько тебе́ лет?","intro":"It's <b>Ле́на</b>'s birthday, and her little flat is full of candles, tea and people. Someone will ask your age within the first minute — so today you'll count from <b>ноль</b> to <b>два́дцать</b> and answer the question every Russian asks: <b>Ско́лько тебе́ лет?</b> The twist for an English speaker: you won't say “I am twenty”. You'll say, quite literally, “to-me — twenty years”.","introAudio":"It's Lena's birthday, and her little flat is full of candles, tea and people. Someone will ask your age within the first minute — so today you'll count from nol to dvatsat and answer the question every Russian asks: skolka tebye lyet? The twist for an English speaker: you won't say I am twenty. You'll say, quite literally, to-me, twenty years.","goals":["Count from <b>ноль</b> to <b>два́дцать</b> — every number 0–20","Ask and answer <b>Ско́лько тебе́ лет?</b> → <b>Мне два́дцать лет</b>","Pick <b>год</b>, <b>го́да</b> or <b>лет</b> to match the number","Take a first taste of “having”: <b>У меня́ есть…</b>"],"learned":["Counted from <b>ноль</b> to <b>два́дцать</b> — the whole 0–20","Asked and answered <b>Ско́лько тебе́ лет?</b> with <b>Мне… лет</b>","Matched <b>год / го́да / лет</b> to the number","Took a first taste of <b>У меня́ есть…</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Before the numbers, a quick look back at week one — names, origins, and the gender that hides in every word.","introAudio":"Before the numbers, a quick look back at week one — names, origins, and the gender that hides in every word.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the option that fits. A wrong tap costs nothing — it's a warm-up.","q":"Ива́н points at his desk: «Э́то ___ стол.»","opts":["моя́","мой","мои́"],"answer":1,"explain":"Стол is masculine (он), so the possessive matches it: <b>мой</b>. The owner's gender never counts — the thing's does."},{"type":"choice","q":"Emma is from England. She introduces herself:","opts":["Я англича́нин.","Я англича́нка.","Я ру́сская."],"answer":1,"explain":"A woman from England → <b>англича́нка</b>; the -ка marks the feminine. Англича́нин is the man's form, ру́сская is the wrong country."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“What's your name?”</b> (to a friend)","answer":"Как тебя́ зову́т"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Numbers","title":"From <em>ноль</em> to <em>де́сять</em>","paras":["Here are your first eleven numbers — the ones you'll count candles, cups and years with. Say each one out loud as you meet it; most are short and blunt, exactly the way Russians fire them off.","Two little sound notes. <b>Оди́н</b> [a-DEEN] opens with an unstressed о that relaxes into “a” — the а́канье you already know. And the soft <b>ь</b> at the end of <b>пять</b>, <b>шесть</b>, <b>во́семь</b> softens the last consonant: пять is “pyat'”, not a hard “pyat”.","You don't need them in a strict row yet — but by the end of tonight, <b>Ско́лько?</b> (“how many?”) won't scare you."],"audio":"Here are your first eleven numbers — the ones you'll count candles, cups and years with. Say each one out loud as you meet it; most are short and blunt. Two little sound notes. Adeen opens with an unstressed o that relaxes into ah — the akanye you already know. And the soft sign at the end of pyat, shest, vosem softens the last consonant. You don't need them in a strict row yet, but by the end of tonight skolka — how many — won't scare you.","table":{"rows":[["ноль <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ноль\">🔊</button>","0"],["оди́н <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"один\">🔊</button>","1"],["два · три <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"два. три\">🔊</button>","2 · 3"],["четы́ре · пять <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"четыре. пять\">🔊</button>","4 · 5"],["шесть · семь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"шесть. семь\">🔊</button>","6 · 7"],["во́семь · де́вять · де́сять <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"восемь. девять. десять\">🔊</button>","8 · 9 · 10"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"How many? — Three.","en":"Ско́лько? — Три.","say":"Сколько? Три."},{"ru":"One, two, three…","en":"Оди́н, два, три…","say":"Один, два, три."},{"ru":"Two coffees, please.","en":"Два ко́фе, пожа́луйста.","say":"Два кофе, пожалуйста.","gloss":"ко́фе never changes — “two coffees”, not «два ко́феа»"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which one is “four”?","opts":["четы́ре","четы́рнадцать","три"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Четы́ре</b> = 4. Четы́рнадцать is 14 — the -надцать turns it into a teen. Три is 3."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian word. Don't worry about the stress mark — plain letters are fine.","q":"Type the number “seven” in Russian:","accept":["семь","syem","sem"],"placeholder":"a short one…"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and count out loud. Listen to the sample first if you like.","target":"Оди́н, два, три.","sub":"[a-DEEN, dva, tree] — steady and clear","want":"один два три"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Teens","title":"The <em>-надцать</em> family: 11–20","paras":["The teens are a gift: nearly all of them just glue <b>-надцать</b> onto a smaller number. <b>Оди́ннадцать</b> (11), <b>двена́дцать</b> (12), <b>трина́дцать</b> (13)… — that “-надцать” is a worn-down “on ten”, a cousin of the English “-teen”.","Most teens put the stress on <b>-на́д-</b>: двена́дцать, семна́дцать, девятна́дцать. Two keep their base-number stress up front instead — <b>оди́ннадцать</b> (11) and <b>четы́рнадцать</b> (14) — so listen for those. And the т in the middle goes quiet — say “-natsat”, not “-nadtsat”.","Then <b>два́дцать</b> (20) breaks the mould and jumps its stress to the front: <b>ДВА́-дцать</b>. It's the number you'll hear most at this party."],"audio":"The teens are a gift: nearly all of them just glue natsat onto a smaller number. Adinnatsat, eleven; dvenatsat, twelve; trinatsat, thirteen — that natsat is a worn-down on ten, a cousin of the English teen. Most teens stress the nad: dvenatsat, semnatsat, devyatnatsat. Two are different — adinnatsat and chetyrnatsat keep their stress up front. And the t in the middle goes quiet — say natsat, not nadtsat. Then dvatsat, twenty, breaks the mould and jumps its stress to the front. It's the number you'll hear most at this party.","table":{"rows":[["оди́ннадцать <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"одиннадцать\">🔊</button>","11"],["двена́дцать · трина́дцать <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"двенадцать. тринадцать\">🔊</button>","12 · 13"],["четы́рнадцать · пятна́дцать <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"четырнадцать. пятнадцать\">🔊</button>","14 · 15"],["шестна́дцать · семна́дцать <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"шестнадцать. семнадцать\">🔊</button>","16 · 17"],["восемна́дцать · девятна́дцать <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"восемнадцать. девятнадцать\">🔊</button>","18 · 19"],["два́дцать <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"двадцать\">🔊</button>","20"]],"star":5},"examples":[{"ru":"Fifteen — say it slowly.","en":"Пятна́дцать.","say":"Пятнадцать."},{"ru":"Ten, eleven, twelve…","en":"Де́сять, оди́ннадцать, двена́дцать…","say":"Десять, одиннадцать, двенадцать."},{"ru":"Twenty! Happy birthday, Lena!","en":"Два́дцать! С днём рожде́ния, Ле́на!","say":"Двадцать! С днём рождения, Лена!","gloss":"С днём рожде́ния! = “Happy birthday!” — a fixed phrase, take it whole"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Ле́на turns 20 today. “Twenty” is…","opts":["два́дцать","двена́дцать","де́сять"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Два́дцать</b> = 20, stress right at the front. Двена́дцать is 12, де́сять is 10."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order.","q":"Build the count: <b>“eleven, twelve, thirteen”</b>","answer":"оди́ннадцать двена́дцать трина́дцать"},{"type":"choice","q":"Which number is NOT a teen (not 11–19)?","opts":["семна́дцать","два́дцать","четы́рнадцать"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Два́дцать</b> is 20 — a round number, not a -надцать teen. Семна́дцать (17) and четы́рнадцать (14) both are."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Your age","title":"<em>Ско́лько тебе́ лет?</em> — and the missing “I am”","paras":["Now the sentence of the day. To ask a friend's age: <b>Ско́лько тебе́ лет?</b> — literally “how many to-you years?”. To answer, don't reach for “I am”. Russian gives the age <b>to</b> you: <b>Мне два́дцать лет</b> — “to-me, twenty years”. Take <b>Мне… лет</b> as one ready brick and drop your number inside.","The word for “year(s)” shifts with the number, and it's worth getting right. After <b>1</b>: <b>год</b>. After <b>2, 3, 4</b>: <b>го́да</b>. After <b>5</b> and up — all the way through the teens and to 20 — <b>лет</b>: пять лет, оди́ннадцать лет, два́дцать лет.","So: <b>оди́н год</b>, <b>четы́ре го́да</b>, <b>два́дцать лет</b> — the number picks the word. Watch the teens: 11–14 quietly take <b>лет</b>, not го́да. And for someone you'd вы, ask <b>Ско́лько вам лет?</b>"],"audio":"Now the sentence of the day. To ask a friend's age: skolka tebye lyet — literally, how many to-you years. To answer, don't reach for I am. Russian gives the age to you: mnye dvatsat lyet — to-me, twenty years. Take mnye lyet as one ready brick and drop your number inside. The word for year shifts with the number. After one: got. After two, three, four: goda. After five and up, all the way through the teens and to twenty: lyet. So, adeen got, chetyre goda, dvatsat lyet — the number picks the word. Watch the teens: eleven to fourteen quietly take lyet, not goda. And for someone you'd address politely, ask skolka vam lyet.","table":{"rows":[["оди́н <b>год</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"один год\">🔊</button>","1 → “year”"],["два · три · четы́ре <b>го́да</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"два, три, четыре года\">🔊</button>","2–4 → “years”"],["пять … два́дцать <b>лет</b> <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пять лет, двадцать лет\">🔊</button>","5–20 → “years”"],["Мне два́дцать <b>лет</b>. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мне двадцать лет.\">🔊</button>","I'm 20 years old."]],"star":3},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я два́дцать лет.","right":"Мне два́дцать лет.","why":"No “I am” with age. Russian gives the years <b>to</b> you: start with <b>Мне</b>, not Я. «Я два́дцать» lands like “I twenty”."},{"wrong":"Мне два́дцать го́да.","right":"Мне два́дцать лет.","why":"From 5 upward — 20 included — it's <b>лет</b>, never го́да. Го́да belongs to 2, 3, 4 only."}],"examples":[{"ru":"How old are you? (to a friend)","en":"Ско́лько тебе́ лет?","say":"Сколько тебе лет?"},{"ru":"I'm twenty.","en":"Мне два́дцать лет.","say":"Мне двадцать лет."},{"ru":"I'm four! (a proud little cousin)","en":"Мне четы́ре го́да!","say":"Мне четыре года!"},{"ru":"How old are you? (polite)","en":"Ско́лько вам лет?","say":"Сколько вам лет?","gloss":"вам = the polite “you”, from вы"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I am twenty (years old)” in Russian:","opts":["Я два́дцать лет.","Мне два́дцать лет.","Я есть два́дцать."],"answer":1,"explain":"Age goes “to me”: <b>Мне два́дцать лет</b> — never «Я…». And there's still no «есть»."},{"type":"type","q":"Answer «Ско́лько тебе́ лет?» — say you're 18:","accept":["мне восемнадцать лет","mne vosemnadtsat let","мне восемна́дцать лет","мне 18 лет"],"placeholder":"Мне … лет","explain":"Мне восемна́дцать лет. — 18 is above 5 (and a teen), so it takes лет."},{"type":"choice","q":"Ма́ша is 3. “Three years” is…","opts":["три год","три го́да","три лет"],"answer":1,"explain":"2, 3, 4 → <b>го́да</b>: три го́да. Год is for 1; лет starts at 5."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Having","title":"A first taste: <em>У меня́ есть…</em>","paras":["One more brick for the party — “I have”. Russian doesn't lean on a verb like English “have”. It says, literally, “by me there-is…”: <b>У меня́ есть брат</b> — “I have a brother”. <b>У меня́ есть сестра́</b> — “I have a sister”.","For now, keep <b>У меня́ есть…</b> whole and simply add what you've got. (And yes — this <b>есть</b> means “there is / I've got”, a different есть from the “to be” we happily threw away on day one.)","You'll meet the full machinery on day 11. Tonight it's enough to say what — and who — you have."],"audio":"One more brick for the party — I have. Russian doesn't lean on a verb like the English have. It says, literally, by me there is: oo menya yest brat — I have a brother. Oo menya yest sestra — I have a sister. For now, keep oo menya yest whole and simply add what you've got. And yes, this yest means there is, or I've got — a different yest from the to-be we happily threw away on day one. You'll meet the full machinery on day eleven. Tonight it's enough to say what, and who, you have.","table":{"rows":[["У меня́ есть брат. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"У меня есть брат.\">🔊</button>","I have a brother."],["У меня́ есть сестра́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"У меня есть сестра.\">🔊</button>","I have a sister."],["У меня́ есть вопро́с. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"У меня есть вопрос.\">🔊</button>","I have a question."]],"star":0},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я име́ю брат.","right":"У меня́ есть брат.","why":"There is a verb «име́ть» (to have), but almost nobody talks that way day-to-day. Real, warm Russian says <b>У меня́ есть…</b> — “by me there is…”."}],"examples":[{"ru":"I have a brother and a sister.","en":"У меня́ есть брат и сестра́.","say":"У меня есть брат и сестра."},{"ru":"Do you have a question?","en":"У тебя́ есть вопро́с?","say":"У тебя есть вопрос?","gloss":"у тебя́ = “do you have”, the ты-version of у меня́"},{"ru":"I've got coffee. Great!","en":"У меня́ есть ко́фе. Кла́ссно!","say":"У меня есть кофе. Классно!","gloss":"кла́ссно = “cool / great”, very casual"}],"tasks":[{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I have a sister.”</b>","answer":"У меня́ есть сестра́"},{"type":"choice","q":"How does everyday Russian say “I have a brother”?","opts":["Я име́ю брат.","У меня́ есть брат.","Мне брат."],"answer":1,"explain":"The living phrase is <b>У меня́ есть…</b> — literally “by me there is a brother”. «Я име́ю» is technically a verb but sounds stiff and bookish."},{"type":"type","q":"Type “I have a question” in Russian:","accept":["у меня есть вопрос","u menya est vopros","у меня́ есть вопро́с"],"placeholder":"У меня́ есть…"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"It's Ле́на's birthday. Emma arrives with Ива́н; there's tea, a small cake, and Ле́на's little cousin Ма́ша buzzing around the table. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, э́то моя́ сестра́ Ле́на. Её день рожде́ния!","ru":"Emma, this is my sister Lena. It's her birthday! (её = her)"},{"who":"A","en":"Приве́т, Ле́на! С днём рожде́ния! Ско́лько тебе́ лет?","ru":"Hi, Lena! Happy birthday! How old are you?"},{"who":"B","en":"Спаси́бо! Мне два́дцать лет.","ru":"Thank you! I'm twenty."},{"who":"A","en":"Мне то́же два́дцать!","ru":"I'm twenty too!"},{"who":"B","en":"А мне четы́ре го́да!","ru":"And I'm four! (little Masha, proudly)"},{"who":"A","en":"Кла́ссно! А э́то кто?","ru":"Cool! And who's this?"},{"who":"B","en":"Э́то мой брат. У меня́ есть брат и сестра́.","ru":"This is my brother. I have a brother and a sister."},{"who":"A","en":"У меня́ то́же есть сестра́! О́чень прия́тно, Ма́ша.","ru":"I have a sister too! Nice to meet you, Masha."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Numbers 0–20, the age question, and your first “I have”. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"ноль, оди́н, два, три","ru":"0, 1, 2, 3","ex":"[nol, a-DEEN, dva, tree] — the start of the count"},{"en":"четы́ре, пять, шесть","ru":"4, 5, 6","ex":"[chee-TY-rye, pyat, shest] — пять and шесть end on a soft ь"},{"en":"семь, во́семь, де́вять, де́сять","ru":"7, 8, 9, 10","ex":"[syem, VO-syem, DYE-vyat, DYE-syat] — the soft -мь closes во́семь"},{"en":"оди́ннадцать, двена́дцать, трина́дцать","ru":"11, 12, 13","ex":"[a-DEE-na-tsat, dvee-NA-tsat, tree-NA-tsat] — glue -надцать on"},{"en":"четы́рнадцать, пятна́дцать, шестна́дцать","ru":"14, 15, 16","ex":"[chee-TYR-na-tsat, pit-NA-tsat, shees-NA-tsat] — 14 keeps четы́р- stress; 15 & 16 stress -на́д-"},{"en":"семна́дцать, восемна́дцать","ru":"17, 18","ex":"[seem-NA-tsat, va-seem-NA-tsat] — teens keep rolling"},{"en":"девятна́дцать, два́дцать","ru":"19, 20","ex":"[dee-veet-NA-tsat, DVA-tsat] — 20 jumps its stress to the front"},{"en":"Ско́лько тебе́ лет?","ru":"How old are you? (to a friend)","ex":"[SKOL-ka tee-BYE lyet] — literally “how much to-you years”"},{"en":"Мне … лет","ru":"I am … years old","ex":"[mnye … lyet] — a ready brick; drop your number in"},{"en":"год · го́да · лет","ru":"year(s) — chosen by the number","ex":"[got · GO-da · lyet] — 1 → год, 2–4 → го́да, 5+ → лет"},{"en":"день рожде́ния","ru":"birthday","ex":"[dyen razh-DYE-nee-ya] — «С днём рожде́ния!» = Happy birthday!"},{"en":"У меня́ есть…","ru":"I have… (lit. “by me there is”)","ex":"[oo mee-NYA yest] — keep it whole; add what you've got"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Мне два́дцать лет.","I'm twenty."],["Ско́лько тебе́ лет?","How old are you?"],["У меня́ есть сестра́.","I have a sister."],["три го́да","three years"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick checks on numbers and age — if one bites, the rules above are a scroll away.","items":[{"q":"«Ско́лько тебе́ лет?» is asking about your…","opts":["name","age","city"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which is the number 12?","opts":["два́дцать","двена́дцать","де́сять"],"answer":1},{"q":"“I am eight years old” — the right Russian:","opts":["Я во́семь лет.","Мне во́семь год.","Мне во́семь лет."],"answer":2},{"q":"Ма́ша is 3 today. Choose the correct pair:","opts":["три го́да","три лет","три год"],"answer":0},{"q":"Which phrase means “I have a brother”?","opts":["Мне брат.","У меня́ есть брат.","Я брат."],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's little card, pinned to Ле́на's fridge. Read it aloud — you know every word.","title":"Ско́лько тебе́ лет?","sentences":["Приве́т! Меня́ зову́т Э́мма.","Я студе́нтка. Мне два́дцать лет.","У меня́ есть брат и сестра́.","Мой брат — студе́нт. Моя́ сестра́ — шко́льница.","А ты? Ско́лько тебе́ лет?"],"translation":"Hi! My name is Emma. I'm a student. I'm twenty years old. I have a brother and a sister. My brother is a student; my sister is a schoolgirl (шко́льница). And you? How old are you?"},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — put a number into your own introduction.","prompt":"Write 2–3 short sentences: your name, whether you're a student, and your age. If you like, add one person you have (У меня́ есть…).","hint":"The age brick is <b>Мне … лет</b> — never «Я…». Choose the year-word by your number: 5–20 → <b>лет</b>, 2–4 → <b>го́да</b>, 1 → <b>год</b> 🙂","example":"Здра́вствуйте! Меня́ зову́т Джон. Я студе́нт. Мне два́дцать лет. У меня́ есть сестра́."}},"7":{"day":7,"week":"01","themeRu":"Review 1","themeEn":"Повторе́ние 1","intro":"Six days in — and look how much is already yours. You can greet anyone, say who you are with no “to be” in sight, name where you're from, and count to twenty. Today we add nothing new: we take a slow lap through week one, tighten the bolts, and let it all settle. This is the day the pieces click together.","introAudio":"Six days in — and look how much is already yours. You can greet anyone, say who you are with no to be in sight, name where you're from, and count to twenty. Today we add nothing new: we take a slow lap through week one, tighten the bolts, and let it all settle. This is the day the pieces click together.","goals":["Greet, name yourself, and tell <b>ты</b> from <b>вы</b> — smoothly","Say who you are with <b>zero “to be”</b> and match gender in <b>мой / моя́</b>","Give your origin and nationality: <b>Я из… / Я англича́нка</b>","Count 0–20 and answer <b>Ско́лько тебе́ лет?</b>"],"learned":["Greeted, named yourself, and chose <b>ты</b> vs <b>вы</b> with ease","Said who you are with <b>zero “to be”</b> and matched gender in <b>мой / моя́</b>","Gave your origin and nationality: <b>Я из… / Я англича́нка</b>","Counted 0–20 and answered <b>Ско́лько тебе́ лет?</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Warm up with a lap through the whole week — a little of everything before we lock it in.","introAudio":"Warm up with a lap through the whole week — a little of everything before we lock it in.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the option that fits. Wrong taps cost nothing — it's a warm-up.","q":"You walk into a pharmacy and the pharmacist looks up. You say…","opts":["Приве́т!","Здра́вствуйте!","Пока́!"],"answer":1,"explain":"A stranger at work → the polite door: <b>здра́вствуйте</b>. Приве́т is for friends, пока́ is “bye”."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Э́то ___ кни́га.» (my book)","opts":["мой","моя́","моё"],"answer":1,"explain":"Кни́га ends in -а → she → <b>моя́</b>. The book's gender decides, not yours."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“My name is Emma.”</b>","answer":"Меня́ зову́т Э́мма"},{"type":"choice","q":"A man from Russia introduces himself:","opts":["Я ру́сская.","Я ру́сский.","Я англича́нин."],"answer":1,"explain":"A man → <b>ру́сский</b>. Ру́сская is the feminine form; англича́нин is the wrong country."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — no stress mark needed.","q":"Type “I'm twenty (years old)” in Russian:","accept":["мне двадцать лет","mne dvadtsat let","мне два́дцать лет","мне 20 лет"],"placeholder":"Мне … лет","explain":"Мне два́дцать лет — age goes “to me”, and 20 takes лет."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · No “to be”","title":"Say who you are — with nothing extra","paras":["The happiest fact of week one: in the present, Russian has <b>no “am / is / are”</b> and <b>no “a / the”</b>. “I am a student” is just <b>Я студе́нт</b> — two words. A woman says <b>Я студе́нтка</b>.","Names come as a ready brick: <b>Меня́ зову́т Э́мма</b> (“my name is Emma”), and you ask a friend back with <b>Как тебя́ зову́т?</b> Point at things with <b>э́то</b>: «<b>Э́то</b> кни́га», «<b>Кто э́то?</b>», «<b>Что э́то?</b>» — and э́то never changes and never means “is”.","Keep the two “you”s straight: <b>ты</b> for a friend (with приве́т), <b>вы</b> for politeness or a group (with здра́вствуйте)."],"audio":"The happiest fact of week one: in the present, Russian has no am, is, are — and no a or the. I am a student is just ya studyent, two words. A woman says ya studyentka. Names come as a ready brick: menya zovut Emma, my name is Emma, and you ask a friend back with kak tebya zovut. Point at things with eta: eta kniga, kto eta, shto eta — and eta never changes and never means is. Keep the two yous straight: ty for a friend, vy for politeness or a group.","table":{"rows":[["Я студе́нт / Я студе́нтка <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я студент. Я студентка.\">🔊</button>","I'm a student (m / f) — no “am”, no “a”"],["Меня́ зову́т Э́мма. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Меня зовут Эмма.\">🔊</button>","My name is Emma."],["Э́то кни́га. Что э́то? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Это книга. Что это?\">🔊</button>","This is a book. What is this?"],["ты / вы <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ты. вы.\">🔊</button>","you — close / polite or many"]],"star":0},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я есть студе́нт.","right":"Я студе́нт.","why":"No “to be” in the present — «есть» has no place here. Two words already say it all."},{"wrong":"Приве́т, профе́ссор!","right":"Здра́вствуйте, профе́ссор!","why":"A professor gets the polite door: <b>здра́вствуйте</b> + вы. Приве́т would feel like a slap on the back."}],"examples":[{"ru":"Hello! My name is Ivan.","en":"Здра́вствуйте! Меня́ зову́т Ива́н.","say":"Здравствуйте! Меня зовут Иван."},{"ru":"What's your name? (to a friend)","en":"Как тебя́ зову́т?","say":"Как тебя зовут?"},{"ru":"Is this a café? — Yes.","en":"Э́то кафе́? — Да.","say":"Это кафе? Да.","gloss":"no “is”, no “a” — just э́то and the word"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Your friend's grandmother opens the door. You say…","opts":["Приве́т!","Здра́вствуйте!","Пока́!"],"answer":1,"explain":"An older person you're meeting → the polite <b>здра́вствуйте</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I am a student”, with nothing extra:","opts":["Я студе́нт.","Я есть студе́нт.","Я а студе́нт."],"answer":0,"explain":"Drop “am”, drop “a”: <b>Я студе́нт</b>. Russian never had them here."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“What is this?”</b>","answer":"Что э́то"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Gender","title":"He, she, it — and the words that follow","paras":["Every Russian thing has a gender you can hear in its ending. Ends in a consonant → <b>он</b> (стол, па́спорт). Ends in <b>-а/-я</b> → <b>она́</b> (кни́га, Москва́). Ends in <b>-о/-е</b> → <b>оно́</b> (окно́, кафе́).","Gender then ripples outward. Your <b>possessive</b> matches the thing, not the owner: <b>мой</b> стол, <b>моя́</b> кни́га, <b>моё</b> окно́, <b>мои́</b> for several. And a <b>nationality</b> matches the person: a man is <b>ру́сский / англича́нин</b>, a woman is <b>ру́сская / англича́нка</b>.","Origins recap: <b>Отку́да ты?</b> — “where are you from?” → <b>Я из А́нглии</b>, <b>Я из Москвы́</b>. And <b>Я говорю́ по-англи́йски / по-ру́сски</b>."],"audio":"Every Russian thing has a gender you can hear in its ending. Ends in a consonant, it's on, he: stol, pasport. Ends in ah or ya, it's ana, she: kniga, Moskva. Ends in oh or yeh, it's ano, it: akno, kafe. Gender then ripples outward. Your possessive matches the thing, not the owner: moy stol, maya kniga, mayo akno. And a nationality matches the person: a man is russkiy or anglichanin, a woman is russkaya or anglichanka. Origins: atkuda ty, where are you from, ya iz Anglii, ya iz Moskvy. And ya gavaryu pa-angliyski or pa-russki.","table":{"rows":[["стол → он, мой <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"стол. он. мой.\">🔊</button>","consonant → “he”, мой"],["кни́га → она́, моя́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"книга. она. моя.\">🔊</button>","-а/-я → “she”, моя́"],["окно́ → оно́, моё <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"окно. оно. моё.\">🔊</button>","-о/-е → “it”, моё"],["ру́сский / ру́сская <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"русский. русская.\">🔊</button>","Russian (m / f)"]],"star":1},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"мой кни́га","right":"моя́ кни́га","why":"Кни́га ends in -а → she → <b>моя́</b>. The book's gender rules, never the owner's."},{"wrong":"Я ру́сский. (a woman speaking)","right":"Я ру́сская.","why":"A woman takes the -ая form: <b>ру́сская</b>. Nationality follows the person's own gender."}],"examples":[{"ru":"This is my book. (owner's gender doesn't matter)","en":"Э́то моя́ кни́га.","say":"Это моя книга.","gloss":"моя́ because кни́га is “she”, whoever owns it"},{"ru":"Where are you from? — I'm from Moscow.","en":"Отку́да ты? — Я из Москвы́.","say":"Откуда ты? Я из Москвы."},{"ru":"He's Russian, she's English.","en":"Он ру́сский, она́ англича́нка.","say":"Он русский, она англичанка."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Э́то ___ окно́.» (my window)","opts":["мой","моя́","моё"],"answer":2,"explain":"Окно́ ends in -о → оно́ → <b>моё</b>. The window's gender picks the possessive."},{"type":"type","q":"A woman says she is Russian. Type it:","accept":["я русская","ya russkaya","я ру́сская"],"placeholder":"Я …","explain":"Я ру́сская — the feminine -ая ending."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Отку́да ты?» is asking about your…","opts":["age","origin (where you're from)","name"],"answer":1,"explain":"Отку́да = “from where”. Answer with <b>Я из…</b> + the place."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Numbers & age","title":"0–20, and “to-me… years”","paras":["You can now count <b>ноль</b>–<b>два́дцать</b>. The teens glue on <b>-надцать</b> (оди́ннадцать, пятна́дцать), and <b>два́дцать</b> (20) puts its stress up front.","Age has its own shape — the one English speakers trip on. Not “I am 20”, but <b>Мне два́дцать лет</b>, literally “to-me twenty years”. Ask a friend: <b>Ско́лько тебе́ лет?</b>","And the year-word bends with the number: <b>1 → год</b>, <b>2–4 → го́да</b>, <b>5–20 → лет</b>."],"audio":"You can now count nol to dvatsat. The teens glue on natsat — adinnatsat, pyatnatsat — and dvatsat, twenty, puts its stress up front. Age has its own shape, the one English speakers trip on. Not I am twenty, but mnye dvatsat lyet, literally to-me twenty years. Ask a friend: skolka tebye lyet. And the year-word bends with the number: one, got; two to four, goda; five to twenty, lyet.","table":{"rows":[["Ско́лько тебе́ лет? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Сколько тебе лет?\">🔊</button>","How old are you? (to a friend)"],["Мне два́дцать лет. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мне двадцать лет.\">🔊</button>","I'm 20."],["оди́н год · три го́да · пять лет <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"один год. три года. пять лет.\">🔊</button>","1 / 3 / 5 — the year-word bends"],["ноль … два́дцать <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ноль. двадцать.\">🔊</button>","0 … 20"]],"star":1},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я два́дцать лет.","right":"Мне два́дцать лет.","why":"Age starts with <b>Мне</b>, not Я — “to-me, twenty years”. This is THE age slip for English speakers."},{"wrong":"Мне пять го́да.","right":"Мне пять лет.","why":"From 5 up it's <b>лет</b>. Го́да is only for 2, 3, 4."}],"examples":[{"ru":"How old are you? — I'm nineteen.","en":"Ско́лько тебе́ лет? — Мне девятна́дцать лет.","say":"Сколько тебе лет? Мне девятнадцать лет."},{"ru":"I'm four years old!","en":"Мне четы́ре го́да!","say":"Мне четыре года!"},{"ru":"One, two, three… twenty!","en":"Оди́н, два, три… два́дцать!","say":"Один, два, три, двадцать!"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I'm fifteen” — the right Russian:","opts":["Я пятна́дцать.","Мне пятна́дцать лет.","Мне пятна́дцать го́да."],"answer":1,"explain":"Age = <b>Мне… лет</b>; 15 is above 5, so лет."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“How old are you?”</b> (to a friend)","answer":"Ско́лько тебе́ лет"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say your own age out loud. Listen to the sample first if you like.","target":"Мне два́дцать лет.","sub":"[mnye DVA-tsat lyet] — then swap in your real number","want":"мне двадцать лет"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"After the cake, everyone settles in with tea. Emma realises she can follow the whole conversation now — a week ago these were just shapes on a page. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, э́то моя́ ма́ма.","ru":"Emma, this is my mum. (ма́ма = mum)"},{"who":"A","en":"Здра́вствуйте! Меня́ зову́т Э́мма. О́чень прия́тно.","ru":"Hello! My name is Emma. Nice to meet you."},{"who":"B","en":"О́чень прия́тно, Э́мма! Ты студе́нтка?","ru":"Nice to meet you, Emma! Are you a student?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да, я студе́нтка. Я из А́нглии.","ru":"Yes, I'm a student. I'm from England."},{"who":"B","en":"Ско́лько тебе́ лет, Э́мма?","ru":"How old are you, Emma?"},{"who":"A","en":"Мне два́дцать лет. Ой, а кто э́то?","ru":"I'm twenty. Oh, and who's this?"},{"who":"B","en":"Э́то мой кот Ба́рсик!","ru":"This is my cat, Barsik! (кот = cat)"},{"who":"A","en":"Приве́т, Ба́рсик! Кла́ссно.","ru":"Hi, Barsik! Cool."},{"who":"B","en":"Ура́! Э́мма, ты ру́сская!","ru":"Hooray! Emma, you're Russian (basically)!"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Week one, all on twelve cards — the words that already make you a person in Russian. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"приве́т / здра́вствуйте","ru":"hi (casual) / hello (polite)","ex":"[pree-VYET / ZDRA-stvooy-tye] — friend vs everyone else"},{"en":"пока́ / до свида́ния","ru":"bye / goodbye","ex":"[pa-KA / da-svee-DA-nee-ya] — casual vs polite"},{"en":"спаси́бо / пожа́луйста","ru":"thank you / please","ex":"[spa-SEE-ba / pa-ZHA-loo-sta] — the everyday pair"},{"en":"Меня́ зову́т…","ru":"my name is…","ex":"[mee-NYA za-VOOT] — one ready brick"},{"en":"я / ты / вы","ru":"I / you (close) / you (polite or many)","ex":"[ya / ty / vy] — one English “you”, two Russian"},{"en":"он / она́ / оно́","ru":"he / she / it","ex":"[on / a-NA / a-NO] — every thing has one of these"},{"en":"мой / моя́ / моё / мои́","ru":"my — by the thing's gender","ex":"[moy / ma-YA / ma-YO / ma-EE] — matches the thing, not you"},{"en":"ру́сский / ру́сская","ru":"Russian (m / f)","ex":"[ROO-skee / ROO-ska-ya] — nationality follows the person"},{"en":"Отку́да ты?","ru":"where are you from?","ex":"[at-KOO-da ty] — answer: Я из…"},{"en":"Ско́лько тебе́ лет?","ru":"how old are you?","ex":"[SKOL-ka tee-BYE lyet] — answer: Мне… лет"},{"en":"Мне … лет","ru":"I am … years old","ex":"[mnye … lyet] — never «Я…»"},{"en":"У меня́ есть…","ru":"I have…","ex":"[oo mee-NYA yest] — a different есть from “to be”"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Здра́вствуйте!","Hello! (polite)"],["Меня́ зову́т Ива́н.","My name is Ivan."],["Мне два́дцать лет.","I'm twenty."],["Отку́да ты?","Where are you from?"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Eight questions across the whole week — your first checkpoint. If one bites, the rule above is one scroll away.","items":[{"q":"Your friend Ива́н is leaving. You say…","opts":["Здра́вствуйте!","Пока́!","Спаси́бо!"],"answer":1},{"q":"“I am a student” (a woman speaking):","opts":["Я студе́нтка.","Я есть студе́нтка.","Я студе́нт."],"answer":0},{"q":"«___ зову́т Ле́на.» — the missing word is:","opts":["Я","Меня́","Ты"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Э́то ___ окно́.» (my)","opts":["мой","моя́","моё"],"answer":2},{"q":"A woman from England is…","opts":["англича́нин","англича́нка","ру́сская"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which is the number 14?","opts":["четы́рнадцать","четы́ре","два́дцать"],"answer":0},{"q":"“I'm eighteen” — the right Russian:","opts":["Я восемна́дцать.","Мне восемна́дцать лет.","Мне восемна́дцать год."],"answer":1},{"q":"Ма́ша is 3. Pick the correct pair:","opts":["три лет","три го́да","три год"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's message home at the end of week one. Read it aloud — you truly know every word now.","title":"Приве́т из Москвы́!","sentences":["Здра́вствуйте! Меня́ зову́т Э́мма.","Я англича́нка, я из А́нглии.","Я студе́нтка. Мне два́дцать лет.","Э́то Ива́н. Он ру́сский.","У меня́ есть вопро́с: ско́лько тебе́ лет?"],"translation":"Hello! My name is Emma. I'm English, I'm from England. I'm a student. I'm twenty years old. This is Ivan. He's Russian. I have a question: how old are you?"},"essay":{"intro":"Everything from week one, in your own words.","prompt":"Write a short introduction of 3–4 sentences: greet, give your name, say where you're from, and how old you are. Add one thing or person you have.","hint":"Your week-one bricks: <b>Здра́вствуйте / Меня́ зову́т… / Я из… / Мне … лет / У меня́ есть…</b>. Remember: no «есть» for “I am”, but «есть» is welcome for “I have” 🙂","example":"Здра́вствуйте! Меня́ зову́т Мари́я. Я из Москвы́. Я студе́нтка. Мне девятна́дцать лет. У меня́ есть брат."}},"8":{"day":8,"week":"02","themeRu":"I know, I work","themeEn":"Я зна́ю, рабо́таю","intro":"Classes start today, and suddenly everyone is <em>doing</em> things — reading, working, understanding. Until now your sentences have stood still: “I am a student”. Today they start to move. You'll meet your first real verbs, and a small piece of good news comes with them — the verb ending already tells you <b>who</b> is doing the action.","introAudio":"Classes start today, and suddenly everyone is doing things — reading, working, understanding. Until now your sentences have stood still: I am a student. Today they start to move. You'll meet your first real verbs, and a small piece of good news comes with them — the verb ending already tells you who is doing the action.","goals":["Conjugate your first verb: <b>я зна́ю, ты зна́ешь, он зна́ет</b>","Use the full set: <b>мы зна́ем, вы зна́ете, они́ зна́ют</b>","Say what you do: <b>рабо́таю, чита́ю, понима́ю</b>","Trust the <b>ending</b> to show the person — and keep dropping “to be”"],"learned":["Conjugated your first verb: <b>я зна́ю, ты зна́ешь, он зна́ет</b>","Used the full set: <b>мы зна́ем, вы зна́ете, они́ зна́ют</b>","Said what you do: <b>рабо́таю, чита́ю, понима́ю</b>","Let the <b>ending</b> mark the person — with no “to be” in sight"],"review":{"intro":"Yesterday you closed week one. Before the verbs, a quick pull on three threads: gender, age, and origins.","introAudio":"Yesterday you closed week one. Before the verbs, a quick pull on three threads: gender, age, and origins.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the option that fits — a warm-up, so wrong taps cost nothing.","q":"«Э́то ___ кафе́.» (my café)","opts":["мой","моё","моя́"],"answer":1,"explain":"Кафе́ ends in -е → оно́ → <b>моё</b>. The thing's gender picks the possessive."},{"type":"choice","q":"Ма́ша is 4. Choose the correct pair:","opts":["четы́ре го́да","четы́ре лет","четы́ре год"],"answer":0,"explain":"2, 3, 4 → <b>го́да</b>: четы́ре го́да. Год is for 1; лет starts at 5."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“I'm from Moscow.”</b>","answer":"Я из Москвы́"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · One verb, six people","title":"The ending already says <em>who</em>","paras":["English needs a pronoun to know who's acting: “I know, you know, he knows” — the verb barely changes. Russian flips the effort onto the <b>ending</b>. From <b>знать</b> (“to know”) you get <b>я зна́ю</b>, <b>ты зна́ешь</b>, <b>он зна́ет</b> — a different tail for each person.","Because the tail already points at the person, Russian can even drop the pronoun in quick speech: <b>Зна́ю!</b> = “(I) know!”. For now keep the pronoun — it's clearer — but notice the ending doing the real work.","And the old good news still holds: there's <b>no “to be”</b> hiding in here. <b>Я рабо́таю</b> is the whole of “I am working” — no «есть», no “am”."],"audio":"English needs a pronoun to know who's acting: I know, you know, he knows — the verb barely changes. Russian flips the effort onto the ending. From znat, to know, you get ya znayu, ty znayesh, on znayet — a different tail for each person. Because the tail already points at the person, Russian can even drop the pronoun in quick speech: znayu, I know. For now keep the pronoun, it's clearer, but notice the ending doing the real work. And the old good news still holds: there's no to be hiding in here. Ya rabotayu is the whole of I am working — no yest, no am.","table":{"rows":[["я зна́ю <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я знаю\">🔊</button>","I know"],["ты зна́ешь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ты знаешь\">🔊</button>","you know (a friend)"],["он / она́ зна́ет <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"он знает. она знает.\">🔊</button>","he / she knows"],["мы зна́ем <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мы знаем\">🔊</button>","we know"],["вы зна́ете <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вы знаете\">🔊</button>","you know (polite / many)"],["они́ зна́ют <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"они знают\">🔊</button>","they know"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"I know! (pronoun dropped)","en":"Зна́ю!","say":"Знаю!"},{"ru":"Do you know who this is? — Yes, I know.","en":"Ты зна́ешь, кто э́то? — Да, зна́ю.","say":"Ты знаешь, кто это? Да, знаю."},{"ru":"I don't know. Sorry!","en":"Я не зна́ю. Извини́!","say":"Я не знаю. Извини!","gloss":"извини́ = “sorry”, to a friend"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Ты ___, кто э́то?» — pick the verb form:","opts":["зна́ю","зна́ешь","зна́ет"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ты → <b>зна́ешь</b>: the -ешь ending is “you” (a friend)."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — no stress mark needed.","q":"Type “we know” in Russian:","accept":["мы знаем","my znaem","мы зна́ем"],"placeholder":"Мы …","explain":"Мы → -ем: <b>мы зна́ем</b>."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say both forms out loud. Listen to the sample first if you like.","target":"Я зна́ю. Ты зна́ешь.","sub":"[ya ZNA-yoo · ty ZNA-yesh] — feel the ending change","want":"я знаю ты знаешь"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The -е- family","title":"Six endings you'll reuse forever: <em>-ю, -ешь, -ет, -ем, -ете, -ют</em>","paras":["Pull the tails off знать: <b>-ю, -ешь, -ет, -ем, -ете, -ют</b>. This is the first of Russia's two big verb families — call it the <b>-е-</b> family, because that <b>е</b> hums through the middle endings (зна́<b>е</b>шь, зна́<b>е</b>т, зна́<b>е</b>м, зна́<b>е</b>те).","The recipe is friendly: take the verb, drop <b>-ть</b>, add the ending. <b>Чита́</b>ть → чита́<b>ю</b>, чита́<b>ешь</b>… — the same six tails, every time.","Only the <b>я</b> and <b>они́</b> forms use -ю and -ют; the four in the middle all carry that <b>е</b>. Learn the shape once, and it repeats for a whole family of verbs."],"audio":"Pull the tails off znat: yu, esh, et, em, ete, yut. This is the first of Russia's two big verb families — call it the e family, because that e hums through the middle endings: znayesh, znayet, znayem, znayete. The recipe is friendly: take the verb, drop t, add the ending. Chitat becomes chitayu, chitayesh — the same six tails, every time. Only the ya and oni forms use yu and yut; the four in the middle all carry that e. Learn the shape once, and it repeats for a whole family of verbs.","table":{"rows":[["я чита́ю <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я читаю\">🔊</button>","-ю"],["ты чита́ешь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ты читаешь\">🔊</button>","-ешь"],["он чита́ет <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"он читает\">🔊</button>","-ет"],["мы чита́ем <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мы читаем\">🔊</button>","-ем"],["вы чита́ете <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вы читаете\">🔊</button>","-ете"],["они́ чита́ют <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"они читают\">🔊</button>","-ют"]],"star":0},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я чита́ет.","right":"Я чита́ю.","why":"«-ет» is the “he/she” tail. For <b>я</b> you need <b>-ю</b>: чита́ю. Match the ending to the person."},{"wrong":"Я есть чита́ю.","right":"Я чита́ю.","why":"No «есть», no “am”. The verb alone is the whole present: <b>чита́ю</b> = “I read” and “I am reading”."}],"examples":[{"ru":"I read a little in Russian.","en":"Я немно́го чита́ю по-ру́сски.","say":"Я немного читаю по-русски.","gloss":"немно́го = “a little”"},{"ru":"Are you reading? — Yes, I am.","en":"Ты чита́ешь? — Да, чита́ю.","say":"Ты читаешь? Да, читаю."},{"ru":"They read and understand.","en":"Они́ чита́ют и понима́ют.","say":"Они читают и понимают."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which ending goes with <b>я</b>?","opts":["-ю","-ешь","-ет"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>я</b> → -ю: я чита́ю, я зна́ю."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“We read.”</b>","answer":"Мы чита́ем"},{"type":"choice","q":"«Они́ ___ по-ру́сски.» (read)","opts":["чита́ет","чита́ют","чита́ешь"],"answer":1,"explain":"Они́ → -ют: <b>чита́ют</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The family grows","title":"<em>рабо́тать, чита́ть, понима́ть</em> — same six tails","paras":["Here's the payoff: once you own the six tails, you own a whole family. <b>Рабо́тать</b> (“to work”), <b>чита́ть</b> (“to read”), <b>понима́ть</b> (“to understand”) all conjugate exactly like знать: рабо́таю, рабо́таешь…; понима́ю, понима́ешь…","So you can already say a lot: <b>Я рабо́таю</b>, <b>Ты хорошо́ понима́ешь</b>, <b>Мы чита́ем по-ру́сски</b>. Add <b>хорошо́</b> (“well”), <b>пло́хо</b> (“badly”), <b>немно́го</b> (“a little”) to colour it.","One honest note: not <em>every</em> Russian verb joins this family — tomorrow you'll meet a second one. But these four, and many like them, are all -е-."],"audio":"Here's the payoff: once you own the six tails, you own a whole family. Rabotat, to work; chitat, to read; ponimat, to understand — all conjugate exactly like znat: rabotayu, rabotayesh; ponimayu, ponimayesh. So you can already say a lot: ya rabotayu, ty harasho ponimayesh, my chitayem pa-russki. Add harasho, well; ploha, badly; nemnoga, a little, to colour it. One honest note: not every Russian verb joins this family — tomorrow you'll meet a second one. But these four, and many like them, are all e verbs.","table":{"rows":[["рабо́тать → я рабо́таю <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"работать. я работаю.\">🔊</button>","to work → I work"],["чита́ть → ты чита́ешь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читать. ты читаешь.\">🔊</button>","to read → you read"],["понима́ть → он понима́ет <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"понимать. он понимает.\">🔊</button>","to understand → he understands"],["мы понима́ем <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мы понимаем\">🔊</button>","we understand"],["вы рабо́таете <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вы работаете\">🔊</button>","you (all) work"],["они́ чита́ют <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"они читают\">🔊</button>","they read"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"I work, and I'm a student too.","en":"Я рабо́таю, и я то́же студе́нт.","say":"Я работаю, и я тоже студент."},{"ru":"Do you understand Russian well? — A little.","en":"Ты хорошо́ понима́ешь по-ру́сски? — Немно́го.","say":"Ты хорошо понимаешь по-русски? Немного."},{"ru":"We read a little in Russian.","en":"Мы немно́го чита́ем по-ру́сски.","say":"Мы немного читаем по-русски."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Ива́н ___ по-ру́сски.» (understands)","opts":["понима́ю","понима́ет","понима́ешь"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ива́н = he → <b>понима́ет</b>: the -ет ending is “he/she”."},{"type":"type","q":"Type “I work” in Russian:","accept":["я работаю","ya rabotayu","я рабо́таю"],"placeholder":"Я …","explain":"рабо́тать → <b>я рабо́таю</b> (-ю for я)."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“You understand well.”</b> (to a friend)","answer":"Ты хорошо́ понима́ешь"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Word order","title":"Freer than English — and still no “to be”","paras":["Because the ending pins down the person, Russian doesn't cling to word order the way English does. <b>Я чита́ю по-ру́сски</b> and <b>По-ру́сски я чита́ю</b> both mean “I read Russian” — the second just leans on “in Russian”. English can't shuffle like that without sounding odd.","Two habits to keep from English, though. First: still <b>no “to be”</b> — «Я рабо́таю», never «Я есть рабо́таю». Second: Russian has <b>one</b> present tense where English has two — <b>чита́ю</b> covers both “I read” and “I am reading”. Don't hunt for a separate “am …-ing”.","Keep the pronoun for now (it's clearer), and let the verb ending quietly confirm who's speaking."],"audio":"Because the ending pins down the person, Russian doesn't cling to word order the way English does. Ya chitayu pa-russki and pa-russki ya chitayu both mean I read Russian — the second just leans on in Russian. English can't shuffle like that without sounding odd. Two habits to keep from English, though. First: still no to be — ya rabotayu, never ya yest rabotayu. Second: Russian has one present tense where English has two — chitayu covers both I read and I am reading. Don't hunt for a separate am-ing. Keep the pronoun for now, and let the verb ending quietly confirm who's speaking.","mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я есть рабо́таю.","right":"Я рабо́таю.","why":"Still no «есть». The verb alone is the present tense — <b>рабо́таю</b> is already “I work / I am working”."},{"wrong":"Я ам чита́ю.","right":"Я чита́ю.","why":"There's no “am” to translate. One Russian verb form does the work of English “read” and “am reading”."}],"examples":[{"ru":"I'm reading in Russian.","en":"Я чита́ю по-ру́сски.","say":"Я читаю по-русски."},{"ru":"In Russian, I understand a little.","en":"По-ру́сски я понима́ю немно́го.","say":"По-русски я понимаю немного."}],"mnemonic":"English drags along “am / is / are” like extra luggage. Russian travels light: one verb, one form — <b>чита́ю</b> is “I read” and “I'm reading” at once.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I am working” in Russian is…","opts":["Я есть рабо́таю.","Я рабо́таю.","Я ам рабо́таю."],"answer":1,"explain":"One verb, no “am”: <b>Я рабо́таю</b> = “I work” and “I am working”."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Чита́ю» with no pronoun can mean…","opts":["“(I) read / am reading”","“to read”","“a reader”"],"answer":0,"explain":"The -ю ending points at <b>я</b>, so <b>чита́ю</b> alone = “(I) read / am reading”."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"First day of classes. In the corridor, Emma, Ива́н and Ле́на compare what they do all day — and Emma discovers she follows more than she thought. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Приве́т, Э́мма! Ты чита́ешь по-ру́сски?","ru":"Hi, Emma! Do you read Russian?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да, немно́го чита́ю. И немно́го понима́ю!","ru":"Yes, I read a little. And I understand a little!"},{"who":"B","en":"Кла́ссно! Я то́же чита́ю по-англи́йски.","ru":"Cool! I read English too."},{"who":"A","en":"А Ле́на? Она́ рабо́тает?","ru":"And Lena? Does she work?"},{"who":"B","en":"Да, она́ рабо́тает. Она́ бари́ста.","ru":"Yes, she works. She's a barista. (бари́ста = barista)"},{"who":"A","en":"Я студе́нтка, а Ле́на рабо́тает. Кла́ссно.","ru":"I'm a student, and Lena works. Nice."},{"who":"B","en":"Приве́т! Вы чита́ете и́ли рабо́таете?","ru":"Hi! Are you (two) reading or working? (и́ли = or)"},{"who":"A","en":"Мы чита́ем и понима́ем! Спаси́бо, Ива́н.","ru":"We're reading — and understanding! Thanks, Ivan."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Four verbs that run your whole day, plus the people and adverbs to go with them. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"знать","ru":"to know","ex":"[ZNAT] — я зна́ю, ты зна́ешь, они́ зна́ют"},{"en":"рабо́тать","ru":"to work","ex":"[ra-BO-tat] — я рабо́таю, ты рабо́таешь"},{"en":"чита́ть","ru":"to read","ex":"[chee-TAT] — я чита́ю, мы чита́ем"},{"en":"понима́ть","ru":"to understand","ex":"[pa-nee-MAT] — я понима́ю, вы понима́ете"},{"en":"гуля́ть","ru":"to stroll, take a walk","ex":"[goo-LYAT] — same -е- family: я гуля́ю"},{"en":"отдыха́ть","ru":"to rest, relax","ex":"[at-dy-KHAT] — я отдыха́ю, они́ отдыха́ют"},{"en":"мы","ru":"we","ex":"[my] — мы зна́ем, мы чита́ем"},{"en":"они́","ru":"they","ex":"[a-NEE] — они́ рабо́тают, они́ зна́ют"},{"en":"хорошо́","ru":"well","ex":"[kha-ra-SHO] — Я хорошо́ понима́ю."},{"en":"пло́хо","ru":"badly","ex":"[PLO-kha] — the opposite of хорошо́"},{"en":"немно́го","ru":"a little","ex":"[neem-NO-ga] — Я немно́го чита́ю."},{"en":"то́же","ru":"too, also","ex":"[TO-zhe] — Я то́же рабо́таю."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English — watch the endings.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Я чита́ю по-ру́сски.","I read Russian."],["Ты рабо́таешь?","Do you work?"],["Мы понима́ем.","We understand."],["Они́ зна́ют.","They know."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five checks on today's verbs — if one bites, the rules above are one scroll away.","items":[{"q":"Which ending goes with <b>они́</b> (“they”)?","opts":["-ет","-ешь","-ют"],"answer":2},{"q":"«Ты ___ по-ру́сски?» — pick the verb form:","opts":["чита́ешь","чита́ю","чита́ет"],"answer":0},{"q":"“I am working” in Russian:","opts":["Я есть рабо́таю.","Я рабо́таю.","Я рабо́таешь."],"answer":1},{"q":"«Мы ___ немно́го.» (understand)","opts":["понима́ю","понима́ешь","понима́ем"],"answer":2},{"q":"«Чита́ю» (with no pronoun) means…","opts":["you read","(I) read / am reading","they read"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note after her first day of classes. Read it aloud — the verbs are all doing their job.","title":"Я чита́ю и понима́ю","sentences":["Приве́т! Меня́ зову́т Э́мма.","Я студе́нтка. Я немно́го чита́ю по-ру́сски.","Ива́н то́же чита́ет по-англи́йски.","Ле́на не студе́нтка. Она́ рабо́тает.","Мы чита́ем и понима́ем. Кла́ссно!"],"translation":"Hi! My name is Emma. I'm a student. I read a little in Russian. Ivan reads English too. Lena isn't a student — she works. We read and understand. Cool!"},"essay":{"intro":"Now say what you do.","prompt":"Write 3–4 sentences about what you and people you know do: read, work, understand. Use at least three different persons (я, ты/он, мы…).","hint":"Same six tails on every verb: <b>-ю, -ешь, -ет, -ем, -ете, -ют</b>. Match the ending to the person — and remember, no «есть» for “am” 🙂","example":"Я студе́нтка. Я чита́ю и немно́го понима́ю по-ру́сски. Ива́н рабо́тает. Мы хорошо́ рабо́таем."}},"9":{"day":9,"week":"02","themeRu":"I speak Russian","themeEn":"Я говорю́ по-ру́сски","intro":"The international faculty is a little Babel: down one corridor you'll hear English, French, Chinese, Russian. Perfect timing — today you learn to say which languages you speak, starting with the sentence you've been waiting for: <b>Я говорю́ по-ру́сски</b>. There's a catch, though — Russian has a <b>second</b> verb family, and today you meet it.","introAudio":"The international faculty is a little Babel: down one corridor you'll hear English, French, Chinese, Russian. Perfect timing — today you learn to say which languages you speak, starting with the sentence you've been waiting for: ya govoryu po-russki. There's a catch, though — Russian has a second verb family, and today you meet it.","goals":["Meet the second verb family — the <b>-и-</b> type: <b>говорю́, говори́шь, говори́т</b>","Say which languages you speak: <b>Я говорю́ по-ру́сски</b>","Handle a stress that hops: <b>люблю́ → лю́бишь</b>","Tell the two families apart — and spot the soft sign <b>ь</b>"],"learned":["Met the second verb family — the <b>-и-</b> type: <b>говорю́, говори́шь, говори́т</b>","Said which languages you speak: <b>Я говорю́ по-ру́сски</b>","Handled a hopping stress: <b>люблю́ → лю́бишь</b>","Told the two families apart and spotted the soft sign <b>ь</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Before the second family, a look back at the first — plus a number and a gender, as always.","introAudio":"Before the second family, a look back at the first — plus a number and a gender, as always.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the option that fits — a warm-up, so wrong taps cost nothing.","q":"«Мы ___ по-ру́сски.» (read)","opts":["чита́ю","чита́ем","чита́ешь"],"answer":1,"explain":"Мы → -ем: <b>чита́ем</b>, the -е- family from yesterday."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Э́то ___ окно́.» (my)","opts":["мой","моя́","моё"],"answer":2,"explain":"Окно́ ends in -о → оно́ → <b>моё</b>. The thing's gender picks the possessive — always-on."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“I'm twenty (years old).”</b>","answer":"Мне два́дцать лет"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The -и- family","title":"<em>говори́ть</em> → я говорю́, ты говори́шь","paras":["Yesterday's verbs hummed on <b>е</b>. Today's second family hums on <b>и</b>. Take <b>говори́ть</b> (“to speak”): drop <b>-ить</b> and add the new endings — <b>я говорю́</b>, <b>ты говори́шь</b>, <b>он говори́т</b>, <b>мы говори́м</b>, <b>вы говори́те</b>, <b>они́ говоря́т</b>.","Line them up against the -е- family and the difference is one vowel: чита́<b>е</b>шь vs говор<b>и́</b>шь, чита́<b>е</b>т vs говор<b>и́</b>т. The <b>я</b> form still takes -ю (говор<b>ю́</b>), and <b>они́</b> takes <b>-ят</b> here (говор<b>я́т</b>), not -ют.","Notice the soft sign <b>ь</b> in говори́<b>ть</b> and in every <b>-ишь</b> form — it just softens the consonant before it, the way it did back in пять and шесть."],"audio":"Yesterday's verbs hummed on e. Today's second family hums on i. Take govorit, to speak: drop it and add the new endings — ya govoryu, ty govorish, on govorit, my govorim, vy govorite, oni govoryat. Line them up against the e family and the difference is one vowel: chitayesh versus govorish, chitayet versus govorit. The ya form still takes yu, govoryu, and oni takes yat here, govoryat, not yut. Notice the soft sign in govorit and in every ish form — it just softens the consonant before it, the way it did back in pyat and shest.","table":{"rows":[["я говорю́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я говорю\">🔊</button>","I speak"],["ты говори́шь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ты говоришь\">🔊</button>","you speak (a friend)"],["он / она́ говори́т <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"он говорит. она говорит.\">🔊</button>","he / she speaks"],["мы говори́м <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мы говорим\">🔊</button>","we speak"],["вы говори́те <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вы говорите\">🔊</button>","you speak (polite / many)"],["они́ говоря́т <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"они говорят\">🔊</button>","they speak"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Do you speak Russian? — A little.","en":"Ты говори́шь по-ру́сски? — Немно́го.","say":"Ты говоришь по-русски? Немного."},{"ru":"He speaks Russian well.","en":"Он хорошо́ говори́т по-ру́сски.","say":"Он хорошо говорит по-русски."},{"ru":"We speak English and Russian.","en":"Мы говори́м по-англи́йски и по-ру́сски.","say":"Мы говорим по-английски и по-русски."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Она́ ___ по-англи́йски.» (speaks)","opts":["говорю́","говори́т","говори́шь"],"answer":1,"explain":"Она́ = she → <b>говори́т</b>: the -ит ending is “he/she”."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — no stress mark needed.","q":"Type “I speak Russian” in Russian:","accept":["я говорю по-русски","ya govoryu po-russki","я говорю́ по-ру́сски","я говорю по русски"],"placeholder":"Я говорю́ …","explain":"Я говорю́ по-ру́сски — the sentence of the day."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud. Listen to the sample first if you like.","target":"Я говорю́ по-ру́сски.","sub":"[ya ga-va-RYOO pa-ROO-skee] — proud and clear","want":"я говорю по-русски"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Languages","title":"<em>по-ру́сски, по-англи́йски</em> — naming a language","paras":["To name the language you speak, Russian uses a neat little adverb: <b>по-</b> + the language, ending in <b>-ски</b>. <b>по-ру́сски</b> (in Russian), <b>по-англи́йски</b> (in English), <b>по-неме́цки</b> (in German), <b>по-францу́зски</b> (in French), <b>по-испа́нски</b> (in Spanish).","Two things to love here. There's <b>no “in”</b> to add — по-ру́сски already means “in Russian”. And these words never change: <b>Я говорю́ по-ру́сски</b>, <b>Мы чита́ем по-ру́сски</b> — the same form every time.","A small spelling note: they're written with a hyphen and a lowercase letter — <b>по-ру́сски</b>, not «По Ру́сски». The language name inside isn't capitalised the way English capitalises “Russian”."],"audio":"To name the language you speak, Russian uses a neat little adverb: po plus the language, ending in ski. Pa-russki, in Russian; pa-angliyski, in English; pa-nyemetski, in German; pa-frantsuzski, in French; pa-ispanski, in Spanish. Two things to love here. There's no in to add — pa-russki already means in Russian. And these words never change: ya govoryu pa-russki, my chitayem pa-russki, the same form every time. A small spelling note: they're written with a hyphen and a lowercase letter, and the language name inside isn't capitalised the way English capitalises Russian.","table":{"rows":[["по-ру́сски <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"по-русски\">🔊</button>","in Russian"],["по-англи́йски <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"по-английски\">🔊</button>","in English"],["по-неме́цки <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"по-немецки\">🔊</button>","in German"],["по-францу́зски <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"по-французски\">🔊</button>","in French"],["по-испа́нски <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"по-испански\">🔊</button>","in Spanish"],["по-кита́йски <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"по-китайски\">🔊</button>","in Chinese"]],"star":0},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я говорю́ в ру́сский.","right":"Я говорю́ по-ру́сски.","why":"No separate “in”, no adjective. The single word <b>по-ру́сски</b> already carries “in Russian”."}],"examples":[{"ru":"I speak English and a little Russian.","en":"Я говорю́ по-англи́йски и немно́го по-ру́сски.","say":"Я говорю по-английски и немного по-русски."},{"ru":"Do you speak French? — No, only English.","en":"Ты говори́шь по-францу́зски? — Нет, то́лько по-англи́йски.","say":"Ты говоришь по-французски? Нет, только по-английски.","gloss":"то́лько = “only”"},{"ru":"They speak German.","en":"Они́ говоря́т по-неме́цки.","say":"Они говорят по-немецки."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"How do you say “in Russian”?","opts":["в ру́сский","по-ру́сски","ру́сская"],"answer":1,"explain":"One word, hyphen, lowercase: <b>по-ру́сски</b>. No “in” to bolt on."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I speak English.”</b>","answer":"Я говорю́ по-англи́йски"},{"type":"type","q":"Type “We speak Russian” in Russian:","accept":["мы говорим по-русски","my govorim po-russki","мы говори́м по-ру́сски","мы говорим по русски"],"placeholder":"Мы говори́м …","explain":"Мы → -им: <b>говори́м</b>, plus the never-changing по-ру́сски."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Hopping stress","title":"<em>люблю́ → лю́бишь</em>: same verb, moving stress","paras":["Two more -и- verbs, and they carry a twist. <b>Люби́ть</b> (“to love / to like”) and <b>смотре́ть</b> (“to watch / to look”) put the stress on the <b>ending</b> in the <b>я</b> form, then hop it back onto the stem for everyone else: <b>люблю́</b> → <b>лю́бишь</b>, <b>лю́бит</b>…; <b>смотрю́</b> → <b>смо́тришь</b>, <b>смо́трит</b>…","And <b>люби́ть</b> has one more quirk: the <b>я</b> form grows an extra <b>л</b> — <b>люблю́</b>, not «любю́». Take it as a one-off you'll get used to fast.","Handy pairing: <b>люби́ть + a verb</b> = “to like doing”. <b>Я люблю́ чита́ть</b> — “I like to read”. (Not every -и- verb hops, though — <b>говори́ть</b> keeps its stress on the ending all the way: говорю́, говори́шь, говори́т.)"],"audio":"Two more i verbs, and they carry a twist. Lyubit, to love or like, and smotret, to watch or look, put the stress on the ending in the ya form, then hop it back onto the stem for everyone else: lyublyu becomes lyubish, lyubit; smotryu becomes smotrish, smotrit. And lyubit has one more quirk: the ya form grows an extra l — lyublyu, not lyubyu. Take it as a one-off you'll get used to fast. Handy pairing: lyubit plus a verb means to like doing. Ya lyublyu chitat, I like to read. Not every i verb hops, though — govorit keeps its stress on the ending all the way: govoryu, govorish, govorit.","table":{"rows":[["я люблю́ · ты лю́бишь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я люблю. ты любишь.\">🔊</button>","I love · you love (stress hops back)"],["я смотрю́ · ты смо́тришь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я смотрю. ты смотришь.\">🔊</button>","I watch · you watch (stress hops back)"],["я говорю́ · ты говори́шь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я говорю. ты говоришь.\">🔊</button>","I speak · you speak (no hop)"],["Я люблю́ чита́ть. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я люблю читать.\">🔊</button>","I like to read."]],"star":3},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я любю́.","right":"Я люблю́.","why":"The <b>я</b> form of люби́ть grows an extra <b>л</b>: <b>люблю́</b>. It's a small, famous exception."},{"wrong":"Ты любю́шь.","right":"Ты лю́бишь.","why":"The extra л and the end-stress belong only to the <b>я</b> form. For ты it's plain <b>лю́бишь</b> — stress on the stem, no extra л."}],"examples":[{"ru":"I like to read in Russian.","en":"Я люблю́ чита́ть по-ру́сски.","say":"Я люблю читать по-русски."},{"ru":"Do you like to read? (to a friend)","en":"Ты лю́бишь чита́ть?","say":"Ты любишь читать?"},{"ru":"She speaks Russian and understands English.","en":"Она́ говори́т по-ру́сски и понима́ет по-англи́йски.","say":"Она говорит по-русски и понимает по-английски."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Ты ___ чита́ть?» (like)","opts":["люблю́","лю́бишь","лю́бит"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ты → <b>лю́бишь</b>: stress on the stem, no extra л. Люблю́ is the я form, лю́бит is “he/she”."},{"type":"choice","q":"The <b>я</b> form of люби́ть is…","opts":["любю́","люблю́","лю́бишь"],"answer":1,"explain":"It grows an extra л and stresses the end: <b>люблю́</b>."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I like to read.”</b>","answer":"Я люблю́ чита́ть"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Two families","title":"Which family? The <em>-и-</em> vs <em>-е-</em> question","paras":["So now there are two. How do you know which endings a verb takes? A useful rule of thumb: verbs ending in <b>-ить</b> usually join the <b>-и-</b> family (говори́ть, люби́ть). Many verbs in <b>-ать</b> join the <b>-е-</b> family (чита́ть, знать, рабо́тать).","But it's a <em>hint</em>, not a law — and <b>смотре́ть</b> is the honest catch: it ends in <b>-еть</b> yet belongs to the <b>-и-</b> family (смотрю́, смо́тришь). When a verb surprises you, just learn its <b>я</b> and <b>ты</b> forms and the rest falls into place.","The dividing question is simple: does <b>ты</b> end in <b>-ешь</b> or <b>-ишь</b>? That one vowel tells you the whole family: чита́<b>ешь</b> (-е-) vs говори́<b>шь</b>, лю́б<b>ишь</b>, смо́тр<b>ишь</b> (-и-)."],"audio":"So now there are two families. How do you know which endings a verb takes? A useful rule of thumb: verbs ending in it usually join the i family, govorit, lyubit. Many verbs in at join the e family, chitat, znat, rabotat. But it's a hint, not a law — and smotret is the honest catch: it ends in et yet belongs to the i family, smotryu, smotrish. When a verb surprises you, just learn its ya and ty forms and the rest falls into place. The dividing question is simple: does ty end in esh or ish? That one vowel tells you the whole family: chitayesh, the e family, versus govorish, lyubish, smotrish, the i family.","table":{"rows":[["чита́ть → ты чита́ешь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читать. ты читаешь.\">🔊</button>","-е- family (-ешь)"],["знать → ты зна́ешь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"знать. ты знаешь.\">🔊</button>","-е- family (-ешь)"],["говори́ть → ты говори́шь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"говорить. ты говоришь.\">🔊</button>","-и- family (-ишь)"],["смотре́ть → ты смо́тришь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"смотреть. ты смотришь.\">🔊</button>","-и- family (-ишь, surprise!)"]],"star":3},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Ты смотре́ешь.","right":"Ты смо́тришь.","why":"Despite its -еть spelling, смотре́ть is an <b>-и-</b> verb: ты <b>смо́тришь</b>, not «смотре́ешь». The infinitive fooled you — trust the ты form."}],"examples":[{"ru":"I read (an е-verb) and I speak (an и-verb).","en":"Я чита́ю и говорю́.","say":"Я читаю и говорю."},{"ru":"You understand and you're watching.","en":"Ты понима́ешь и смо́тришь.","say":"Ты понимаешь и смотришь."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Ты смо́тришь» belongs to which family?","opts":["the -е- family","the -и- family"],"answer":1,"explain":"Its ты form ends in <b>-ишь</b> → the -и- family, even though the infinitive смотре́ть looks like -еть."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which form is from the <b>-е-</b> family?","opts":["говори́шь","чита́ешь","лю́бишь"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>-ешь</b> = the -е- family: чита́ешь. Говори́шь and лю́бишь both take -ишь (-и- family)."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Between classes, students from all over gather in the faculty lounge. Someone always ends up asking the fun question: how many languages do you speak? Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, ты говори́шь по-ру́сски?","ru":"Emma, do you speak Russian?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да, немно́го! Я говорю́ по-англи́йски и немно́го по-ру́сски.","ru":"Yes, a little! I speak English and a little Russian."},{"who":"B","en":"Кла́ссно! Я говорю́ по-ру́сски и по-англи́йски.","ru":"Cool! I speak Russian and English."},{"who":"A","en":"А э́то кто? Он то́же студе́нт?","ru":"And who's this? Is he a student too?"},{"who":"B","en":"Э́то Пьер. Он говори́т по-францу́зски.","ru":"This is Pierre. He speaks French."},{"who":"A","en":"О́чень прия́тно, Пьер! Ты понима́ешь по-ру́сски?","ru":"Nice to meet you, Pierre! Do you understand Russian?"},{"who":"B","en":"Немно́го понима́ю, но говорю́ пло́хо.","ru":"I understand a little, but I speak badly. (но = but)"},{"who":"A","en":"Хорошо́! Я то́же люблю́ говори́ть по-ру́сски.","ru":"Great! I like speaking Russian too."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"The second verb family and the languages to use it on. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"говори́ть","ru":"to speak","ex":"[ga-va-REET] — я говорю́, ты говори́шь, они́ говоря́т"},{"en":"люби́ть","ru":"to love, to like","ex":"[lyoo-BEET] — я люблю́ (extra л!), ты лю́бишь"},{"en":"смотре́ть","ru":"to watch, to look","ex":"[sma-TRYET] — я смотрю́, ты смо́тришь"},{"en":"язы́к","ru":"language","ex":"[ya-ZYK] — ру́сский язы́к = the Russian language"},{"en":"по-ру́сски","ru":"in Russian","ex":"[pa-ROO-skee] — Я говорю́ по-ру́сски."},{"en":"по-англи́йски","ru":"in English","ex":"[pa-an-GLEE-skee] — Я чита́ю по-англи́йски."},{"en":"по-неме́цки","ru":"in German","ex":"[pa-nee-MYETS-kee] — the -ски language adverb"},{"en":"по-францу́зски","ru":"in French","ex":"[pa-fran-TSOOS-kee] — never changes"},{"en":"по-испа́нски","ru":"in Spanish","ex":"[pa-ees-PAN-skee] — по- + language + -ски"},{"en":"по-кита́йски","ru":"in Chinese","ex":"[pa-kee-TAY-skee] — same tidy pattern"},{"en":"то́лько","ru":"only","ex":"[TOL-ka] — Я говорю́ то́лько по-англи́йски."},{"en":"о́чень","ru":"very, really","ex":"[O-chen] — Я о́чень люблю́ чита́ть."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English — mind the endings.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Я говорю́ по-ру́сски.","I speak Russian."],["Ты лю́бишь чита́ть?","Do you like to read?"],["Он смо́трит.","He is watching."],["Мы говори́м по-англи́йски.","We speak English."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five checks on the second family and the languages — if one bites, the rules above are a scroll away.","items":[{"q":"«Я ___ по-ру́сски.» (speak)","opts":["говорю́","говори́шь","говори́т"],"answer":0},{"q":"How do you say “in English”?","opts":["в англи́йский","по-англи́йски","англича́нка"],"answer":1},{"q":"The <b>я</b> form of люби́ть is…","opts":["любю́","лю́бишь","люблю́"],"answer":2},{"q":"«Ты смо́тришь» is from which family?","opts":["the -и- family","the -е- family"],"answer":0},{"q":"Which one is the odd one out — NOT the -и- family?","opts":["говори́т","чита́ет","лю́бит"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note from the language table. Read it aloud — you can say all of this now.","title":"Я говорю́ по-ру́сски","sentences":["Приве́т! Меня́ зову́т Э́мма.","Я англича́нка. Я говорю́ по-англи́йски.","Я то́же немно́го говорю́ по-ру́сски.","Ива́н говори́т по-ру́сски и по-англи́йски.","Я о́чень люблю́ говори́ть по-ру́сски!"],"translation":"Hi! My name is Emma. I'm English. I speak English. I also speak a little Russian. Ivan speaks Russian and English. I really love speaking Russian!"},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — take the microphone at the language table.","prompt":"Write 3–4 sentences about the languages you and others speak. Use говори́ть with at least two persons, and add one thing you like (Я люблю́…).","hint":"Say the language with one word: <b>по-ру́сски, по-англи́йски</b> — no “in” needed. And watch the endings: <b>говорю́, говори́шь, говори́т</b> 🙂","example":"Приве́т! Я студе́нтка. Я говорю́ по-англи́йски и немно́го по-ру́сски. Ива́н говори́т по-ру́сски. Я о́чень люблю́ говори́ть по-ру́сски!"}},"10":{"day":10,"week":"02","themeRu":"I read a book","themeEn":"Я чита́ю кни́гу","intro":"Look at these two English sentences: “Emma reads a book” and “A book reads Emma”. Same four words — word order alone decides who does what. Russian plays a different game entirely: the <b>ending</b> of the word carries the role, and today you learn your first case — the accusative. Emma is off to the campus bookshop, so the timing couldn't be better.","introAudio":"Look at these two English sentences: Emma reads a book, and, a book reads Emma. Same four words — word order alone decides who does what. Russian plays a different game entirely: the ending of the word carries the role, and today you learn your first case — the accusative. Emma is off to the campus bookshop, so the timing couldn't be better.","goals":["Say what you read, watch and love: <b>Я чита́ю кни́гу</b>","Swap feminine <b>-а</b> for <b>-у</b> on the object: кни́га → <b>кни́гу</b>","Leave masculine and neuter things alone: <b>Я люблю́ ко́фе</b>","Trust the <b>ending</b>, not the word order — the core Russian habit"],"learned":["Said what you read, watch and love: <b>Я чита́ю кни́гу</b>","Swapped feminine <b>-а</b> for <b>-у</b> on the object: кни́га → <b>кни́гу</b>","Left masculine and neuter things alone: <b>Я люблю́ ко́фе</b>","Started trusting the <b>ending</b>, not the word order"],"review":{"intro":"Quick warm-up before the new case: yesterday's verb family and the genders that never stop mattering.","introAudio":"Quick warm-up before the new case: yesterday's verb family and the genders that never stop mattering.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the option that fits. A wrong tap costs nothing — it's a warm-up.","q":"Ле́на ___ по-ру́сски и по-англи́йски.","opts":["говори́т","говорю́","говори́шь"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ле́на = she → <b>говори́т</b>. The -и- family from yesterday: говорю́, говори́шь, говори́т."},{"type":"choice","q":"Emma points at her book: «Э́то ___ кни́га.»","opts":["мой","моя́","моё"],"answer":1,"explain":"Кни́га is feminine, so the possessive matches HER: <b>моя́</b>. The owner's gender never matters — the thing's does."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“I speak Russian.”</b>","answer":"Я говорю́ по-ру́сски"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Who does what","title":"Endings do the job of word order","paras":["English marks the doer and the done-to by <b>position</b>: whoever stands before the verb is the subject. Russian marks them by <b>ending</b>. In <b>Э́мма чита́ет кни́гу</b> the -у on кни́гу quietly says: “I'm the thing being read.”","The grammar names, once and gently: the doer stands in the <b>nominative</b> — the dictionary form you've used all along. The direct object stands in the <b>accusative</b> — and for some words that means a new ending.","The good news for today: only feminine <b>-а</b> words actually change. Everything else — журна́л, фильм, ко́фе — stays exactly as it is. One small swap, and you're speaking in cases."],"audio":"English marks the doer and the done-to by position: whoever stands before the verb is the subject. Russian marks them by ending. In Emma chitayet knigu, the oo sound at the end of knigu quietly says: I'm the thing being read. The doer stands in the nominative — the dictionary form you know. The object stands in the accusative. And the good news: only feminine words ending in ah actually change. Everything else stays exactly as it is.","examples":[{"ru":"Emma is reading a book.","en":"Э́мма чита́ет <b>кни́гу</b>.","say":"Эмма читает книгу."},{"ru":"I love music.","en":"Я люблю́ <b>му́зыку</b>.","say":"Я люблю музыку."},{"ru":"Ivan is buying a pizza.","en":"Ива́н покупа́ет <b>пи́ццу</b>.","say":"Иван покупает пиццу.","gloss":"покупа́ть [pa-koo-PAT] = to buy — the -е- family, like чита́ть"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"In «Э́мма чита́ет кни́гу», which word wears the “I'm the object” signal?","opts":["Э́мма","чита́ет","кни́гу"],"answer":2,"explain":"The -у on <b>кни́гу</b> is the accusative flag: it's the thing being read. Э́мма keeps her dictionary form — she's the doer."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I'm listening to music.”</b> (слу́шать = to listen; no “to” needed in Russian)","answer":"Я слу́шаю му́зыку"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say the sentence out loud. Listen to the sample first if you like.","target":"Я чита́ю кни́гу.","sub":"[ya chee-TA-yoo KNEE-goo] — let the final -у land clearly","want":"я читаю книгу"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The -а → -у swap","title":"Feminine objects: кни́га → <em>кни́гу</em>","paras":["Here is the whole mechanism: a feminine word ends in <b>-а</b> in the dictionary; the moment it becomes the direct object, that -а turns into <b>-у</b>. Кни́га → кни́гу, газе́та → газе́ту, пи́цца → пи́ццу.","Names play too — including cities. <b>Ты зна́ешь Ле́ну?</b> — “Do you know Lena?”. <b>Я люблю́ Москву́</b> — “I love Moscow”. If it ends in -а and it's on the receiving end, swap.","The stress almost always stays home: кни́га → кни́гу, му́зыка → му́зыку. One famous hopper is вода́ (water) → <b>во́ду</b> — you'll spot it in the word cards below."],"audio":"Here is the whole mechanism: a feminine word ends in ah in the dictionary. The moment it becomes the direct object, that ah turns into oo. Kniga becomes knigu, gazeta becomes gazetu, pizza becomes pizzu. Names play too, including cities: ty znayesh Lenu? — do you know Lena? Ya lyublyu Moskvu — I love Moscow. If it ends in ah and it's on the receiving end, swap.","table":{"rows":[["кни́га → кни́гу <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"книга — книгу\">🔊</button>","book → a book (as object)"],["газе́та → газе́ту <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"газета — газету\">🔊</button>","newspaper → a newspaper (as object)"],["му́зыка → му́зыку <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"музыка — музыку\">🔊</button>","music → music (as object)"],["Москва́ → Москву́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Москва — Москву\">🔊</button>","Moscow → Moscow (as object)"]],"star":0},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я чита́ю кни́га.","right":"Я чита́ю кни́гу.","why":"Кни́га isn't doing anything here — it's being read. Object duty = accusative: -а becomes -у. This is THE ending English speakers forget first."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Ива́н asks about your new friend: «Ты зна́ешь ___?»","opts":["Ле́на","Ле́ну","Ле́не"],"answer":1,"explain":"Lena is the one being known — the object. Her -а swaps for -у: <b>Ле́ну</b>."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — no stress mark needed.","q":"Translate: <b>“I love Moscow.”</b>","accept":["я люблю москву","я люблю́ москву́","ya lyublyu moskvu","ya lublu moskvu"],"placeholder":"Я … Москв…","explain":"Я люблю́ Москву́. — the city takes the object seat, so -а becomes -у."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Emma is buying a newspaper.”</b>","answer":"Э́мма покупа́ет газе́ту"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The lazy ones","title":"Masculine and neuter things don't budge","paras":["Things that end in a consonant (masculine) or in -о / -е (neuter) keep their dictionary form as objects: <b>Я чита́ю журна́л. Я смотрю́ фильм. Я люблю́ ко́фе.</b> Nothing to swap — the verb and common sense mark the object.","So today's rule of thumb is small and honest: <b>see -а, write -у; see anything else, leave it alone.</b> That covers every THING you'll want to read, watch, buy or love this week.","One honest border: this is the rule for things. Words for people and animals play by different rules — we'll meet those later, when you need them. (Ле́на and Э́мма already work because of their -а.)"],"audio":"Things that end in a consonant — masculine — or in oh or yeh — neuter — keep their dictionary form as objects. Ya chitayu zhurnal. Ya smotryu film. Ya lyublyu kofye. Nothing to swap. So today's rule of thumb is small and honest: see ah, write oo. See anything else, leave it alone. One honest border: this is the rule for things. Words for people and animals play by different rules — we'll meet those later.","examples":[{"ru":"I'm watching a film.","en":"Я смотрю́ <b>фильм</b>.","say":"Я смотрю фильм."},{"ru":"Ivan really loves coffee.","en":"Ива́н о́чень лю́бит <b>ко́фе</b>.","say":"Иван очень любит кофе."},{"ru":"Are you reading a magazine?","en":"Ты чита́ешь <b>журна́л</b>?","say":"Ты читаешь журнал?","gloss":"журна́л [zhoor-NAL] = magazine — the real one; a shop is магази́н"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I love tea” in Russian:","opts":["Я люблю́ чай.","Я люблю́ ча́йу.","Я люблю́ ча́я."],"answer":0,"explain":"Чай is a masculine thing — it doesn't change as an object. Only feminine -а words swap."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which object will keep its dictionary form untouched?","opts":["кни́га — in “I'm reading (a book)”","му́зыка — in “I'm listening to (music)”","фильм — in “I'm watching (a film)”"],"answer":2,"explain":"Фильм ends in a consonant — masculine, stays put. Кни́га and му́зыка both end in -а, so they'd become кни́гу and му́зыку."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Free word order","title":"<em>Кни́гу чита́ет Э́мма</em> — still Emma reading","paras":["Because endings carry the roles, Russian can shuffle its words for emphasis. <b>Кни́гу чита́ет Э́мма</b> means “it's EMMA who's reading the book” — the meaning survives the shuffle, only the spotlight moves.","Your new habit: when a feminine word wears <b>-у</b>, it's the object — no matter where it stands in the sentence. Position lies; the ending doesn't.","And relax: the neutral, everyday order is still doer–verb–object, just like English. Speak in that order — but when a Russian shuffles, you won't be fooled."],"audio":"Because endings carry the roles, Russian can shuffle its words for emphasis. Knigu chitayet Emma means: it's EMMA who's reading the book. The meaning survives the shuffle — only the spotlight moves. Your new habit: when a feminine word wears the oo ending, it's the object, no matter where it stands. Position lies; the ending doesn't. And relax: the neutral everyday order is still doer, verb, object, just like English.","examples":[{"ru":"Emma is reading the book. (neutral order)","en":"Э́мма чита́ет кни́гу.","say":"Эмма читает книгу."},{"ru":"It's Emma who's reading the book. (the object steps forward)","en":"<b>Кни́гу</b> чита́ет Э́мма.","say":"Книгу читает Эмма."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"«Му́зыку слу́шает Ле́на.» = “The music is listening to Lena.”","right":"«Му́зыку слу́шает Ле́на.» = “Lena is listening to the music.”","why":"English decodes by position, Russian by ending: -у on му́зыку marks the object wherever it stands. Ле́на, in her dictionary form, is the doer."}],"mnemonic":"In English the words stand in line; in Russian they wear <b>name tags</b>. Read the tag (-у = object), not the queue.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Пи́ццу лю́бит Ива́н.» Who loves what?","opts":["Ivan loves pizza","The pizza loves Ivan","Ivan is buying a pizza"],"answer":0,"explain":"Пи́ццу wears the object tag -у, so Ива́н is the lover, pizza the loved — even though the pizza stands first. And лю́бит is “loves”, not “is buying”."},{"type":"type","q":"Translate into English: «Му́зыку слу́шает Ле́на.»","accept":["lena is listening to music","lena listens to music","lena is listening to the music","lena listens to the music","lena is listening to music."],"placeholder":"Lena…","explain":"The -у on му́зыку tells you it's the object: Lena is listening to the music."},{"type":"build","q":"Build (neutral order): <b>“He is buying a pizza.”</b>","answer":"Он покупа́ет пи́ццу"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ива́н takes Emma to the campus bookshop. She wants a real Russian book — and Ива́н, as usual, wants coffee. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, вот магази́н «Кни́га».","ru":"Emma, here's the “Book” shop. (вот = here is)"},{"who":"A","en":"Спаси́бо, Ива́н! Я покупа́ю кни́гу.","ru":"Thanks, Ivan! I'm buying a book."},{"who":"B","en":"Что ты чита́ешь, Э́мма?","ru":"What do you read, Emma?"},{"who":"A","en":"Я чита́ю журна́л «Мо́да» и газе́ту «Метро́». По-ру́сски!","ru":"I read “Moda” magazine and the “Metro” newspaper. In Russian!"},{"who":"B","en":"А кни́гу? Ты хорошо́ понима́ешь по-ру́сски.","ru":"And a book? You understand Russian well. (хорошо́ = well)"},{"who":"A","en":"Да! Вот кни́га «Москва́». Я покупа́ю кни́гу и журна́л.","ru":"Yes! Here's the book “Moscow”. I'm buying the book and a magazine."},{"who":"B","en":"А я покупа́ю ко́фе. Я о́чень люблю́ ко́фе.","ru":"And I'm buying coffee. I really love coffee."},{"who":"A","en":"Ива́н, ты студе́нт и́ли бари́ста?","ru":"Ivan, are you a student or a barista?"},{"who":"B","en":"Я студе́нт. Но ко́фе — э́то жизнь!","ru":"I'm a student. But coffee is life! (но = but, жизнь = life)"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for reading, buying and loving things — today's endings need objects to practise on. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"магази́н","ru":"shop","ex":"[ma-ga-ZEEN] — a shop, NOT a magazine; the famous false friend"},{"en":"журна́л","ru":"magazine","ex":"[zhoor-NAL] — the real magazine; masculine, never changes as object"},{"en":"газе́та","ru":"newspaper","ex":"[ga-ZYE-ta] — as an object: газе́ту"},{"en":"му́зыка","ru":"music","ex":"[MOO-zy-ka] — Я слу́шаю му́зыку."},{"en":"фильм","ru":"film, movie","ex":"[feelm] — Я смотрю́ фильм."},{"en":"ко́фе","ru":"coffee","ex":"[KO-fye] — never changes; and it's “he” — a famous masculine exception"},{"en":"чай","ru":"tea","ex":"[chai] — masculine, stays put: Я люблю́ чай."},{"en":"вода́","ru":"water","ex":"[va-DA] — as an object the stress hops home: во́ду [VO-doo]"},{"en":"пи́цца","ru":"pizza","ex":"[PEET-tsa] — object form: пи́ццу"},{"en":"слу́шать","ru":"to listen (to)","ex":"[SLOO-shat] — -е- family: я слу́шаю, ты слу́шаешь; no “to” needed"},{"en":"покупа́ть","ru":"to buy","ex":"[pa-koo-PAT] — -е- family: я покупа́ю кни́гу"},{"en":"сейча́с","ru":"now","ex":"[see-CHAS] — Я сейча́с чита́ю. = I'm reading right now."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English — watch the endings.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning on the right — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Я чита́ю кни́гу.","I'm reading a book."],["Я люблю́ ко́фе.","I love coffee."],["Он покупа́ет газе́ту.","He's buying a newspaper."],["Ты слу́шаешь му́зыку?","Are you listening to music?"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions on today's case — if one bites, the rules above are one scroll away.","items":[{"q":"“I'm reading a book” — pick the Russian:","opts":["Я чита́ю кни́га.","Я чита́ю кни́гу.","Я чита́ет кни́гу."],"answer":1},{"q":"Which object word will NOT change after «Я люблю́…»?","opts":["кни́га","му́зыка","ко́фе"],"answer":2},{"q":"«Газе́ту чита́ет Ле́на.» means…","opts":["Lena is reading the newspaper.","The newspaper is reading Lena.","Lena is buying a newspaper."],"answer":0},{"q":"Emma sighs happily: «Я о́чень люблю́ Москву́!» The dictionary form of the city is…","opts":["Москву́","Москва́","Москво́"],"answer":1},{"q":"The word магази́н means…","opts":["magazine","shop","newspaper"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's diary after the bookshop — read it aloud. Every ending is doing its job.","title":"Кни́га и ко́фе","sentences":["Э́то магази́н «Кни́га».","Я покупа́ю кни́гу и журна́л.","Ива́н покупа́ет ко́фе — он о́чень лю́бит ко́фе.","Сейча́с я чита́ю по-ру́сски!","Я люблю́ Москву́."],"translation":"This is the “Book” shop. I'm buying a book and a magazine. Ivan is buying coffee — he really loves coffee. Right now I'm reading in Russian! I love Moscow."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — put the object tags on yourself.","prompt":"Write 3–4 short sentences about what you read, watch, listen to and love. Use at least one feminine word with its -у ending.","hint":"Verbs you own: чита́ю, смотрю́, слу́шаю, люблю́, покупа́ю. Feminine words swap -а for -у: му́зыка → му́зыку 📖","example":"Я чита́ю кни́гу и журна́л. Я слу́шаю му́зыку. Я о́чень люблю́ ко́фе и пи́ццу. Сейча́с я чита́ю по-ру́сски!"}},"11":{"day":11,"week":"02","themeRu":"I have","themeEn":"У меня́ есть","intro":"Emma unzips her backpack at Ле́на's café: passport, keys, pen, phone… but where is her bank card? Today you'll say what you have — and Russian does it without any verb “to have”: <b>У меня́ есть…</b>, literally “by me there is…”. And for what's missing, a second case quietly steps in.","introAudio":"Emma unzips her backpack at Lena's café: passport, keys, pen, phone… but where is her bank card? Today you'll say what you have — and Russian does it without any verb to have: oo menya yest — literally, by me there is. And for what's missing, a second case quietly steps in.","goals":["Say what you own the Russian way: <b>У меня́ есть телефо́н</b>","Ask around: <b>У тебя́ есть ру́чка?</b> — and answer <b>Да, есть / Нет</b>","Say what's missing with the new ending: <b>У меня́ нет ка́рты</b>","Drop the ready bricks: <b>нет вре́мени, нет де́нег, нет пробле́м</b>"],"learned":["Said what you own the Russian way: <b>У меня́ есть телефо́н</b>","Asked around: <b>У тебя́ есть ру́чка?</b> — and answered <b>Да, есть / Нет</b>","Said what's missing with the genitive: <b>У меня́ нет ка́рты</b>","Learned the ready bricks: <b>нет вре́мени, нет де́нег, нет пробле́м</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Before we talk about having things — yesterday's endings and the genders that never take a day off.","introAudio":"Before we talk about having things — yesterday's endings and the genders that never take a day off.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the option that fits. Wrong taps are free — it's a warm-up.","q":"Я чита́ю ___.","opts":["кни́га","кни́гу","кни́ге"],"answer":1,"explain":"The book is being read — object duty, so -а becomes -у: <b>кни́гу</b>. Yesterday's swap, today's habit."},{"type":"choice","q":"Э́то ___ окно́.","opts":["мой","моя́","моё"],"answer":2,"explain":"Окно́ ends in -о — neuter, so the possessive matches: <b>моё</b>. The thing's gender rules, always."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“Ivan is buying a pizza.”</b>","answer":"Ива́н покупа́ет пи́ццу"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · By me there is","title":"<em>У меня́ есть…</em> — having, without “to have”","paras":["English owns things with a verb: “I <b>have</b> a phone.” Russian doesn't own — it locates: <b>У меня́ есть телефо́н</b>, literally “by me there is a phone”. That whole opening, <b>у меня́ есть</b>, is one ready brick — you met у меня́ back on day 6, and today it's official.","The thing you have stays in its dictionary form: У меня́ есть рюкза́к. У меня́ есть ка́рта. У меня́ есть иде́я! Nothing to bend — yet.","Dictionaries do list a verb “to have” (име́ть), but Russians don't use it for everyday things. <b>Я име́ю телефо́н</b> sounds like a clause in a legal contract. Resist the word-for-word translation — this is the day's trap."],"audio":"English owns things with a verb: I have a phone. Russian doesn't own — it locates: oo menya yest telefon, literally, by me there is a phone. That whole opening — oo menya yest — is one ready brick. The thing you have stays in its dictionary form. Dictionaries do list a verb to have — imet — but Russians don't use it for everyday things. Ya imeyu telefon sounds like a clause in a legal contract. Resist the word-for-word translation — this is the day's trap.","examples":[{"ru":"I have a phone.","en":"<b>У меня́ есть</b> телефо́н.","say":"У меня есть телефон."},{"ru":"I have an idea!","en":"<b>У меня́ есть</b> иде́я!","say":"У меня есть идея!"},{"ru":"I have a ticket and a passport.","en":"У меня́ есть биле́т и па́спорт.","say":"У меня есть билет и паспорт."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я име́ю телефо́н.","right":"У меня́ есть телефо́н.","why":"Word-for-word “I have” imports an English habit. Everyday Russian possession is location: у меня́ есть — by me there is."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I have a phone” — the way Russians actually say it:","opts":["Я име́ю телефо́н.","У меня́ есть телефо́н.","Я есть телефо́н."],"answer":1,"explain":"Possession = location: <b>у меня́ есть</b>. Име́ть lives in contracts; я есть телефо́н would claim you ARE a phone."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I have a backpack.”</b>","answer":"У меня́ есть рюкза́к"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it like you mean it — Emma-style.","target":"У меня́ есть иде́я!","sub":"[oo mee-NYA yest ee-DYE-ya] — one smooth brick, then the idea","want":"у меня есть идея"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Asking around","title":"У тебя́ есть…? У вас есть…?","paras":["To ask, swap the person under <b>у</b>: <b>у тебя́</b> — your friend, <b>у вас</b> — someone polite, or several people. <b>У тебя́ есть ру́чка?</b> — “Do you have a pen?”","The answers are beautifully short: <b>Да, есть.</b> — got one. <b>Нет.</b> — nope. Russian happily drops everything obvious.","In a café or shop, <b>У вас есть…?</b> is THE question to own: У вас есть чай? Day 1's rule still runs the show — вы to strangers, ты to friends."],"audio":"To ask, swap the person under oo: oo tebya — your friend; oo vas — someone polite, or several people. Oo tebya yest ruchka? — do you have a pen? The answers are beautifully short: da, yest — got one. Nyet — nope. Russian happily drops everything obvious. In a café or shop, oo vas yest is THE question to own. Day one's rule still runs the show — vy to strangers, ty to friends.","table":{"rows":[["у меня́ есть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"у меня есть\">🔊</button>","I have"],["у тебя́ есть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"у тебя есть\">🔊</button>","you have (a friend)"],["у вас есть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"у вас есть\">🔊</button>","you have (polite / several people)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Do you have a pen? (to a friend)","en":"<b>У тебя́ есть</b> ру́чка?","say":"У тебя есть ручка?"},{"ru":"Do you have tea? (in a café)","en":"<b>У вас есть</b> чай?","say":"У вас есть чай?"},{"ru":"— Yes, we do. — No.","en":"— Да, есть. — Нет.","say":"Да, есть. Нет."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"You're at the counter of a little bakery, asking about tea. You say…","opts":["У тебя́ есть чай?","У вас есть чай?","Ты есть чай?"],"answer":1,"explain":"A stranger behind a counter → polite <b>у вас</b>. У тебя́ is for friends; ты есть чай would ask whether the person IS tea."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Do you have a pen?”</b> (asking a friend)","answer":"У тебя́ есть ру́чка"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The case of absence","title":"У меня́ нет + genitive: нет ка́рты","paras":["Now the flip side. “I don't have…” is <b>У меня́ нет</b> + the thing — but here the thing must change its ending. Meet your second case, the <b>genitive</b>: the case of absence.","The recipe for things: masculine words <b>add -а</b> — нет телефо́на, нет биле́та, нет зонта́ (the stress runs to the end on that one). Feminine <b>-а</b> turns into <b>-ы</b> — нет ка́рты, нет газе́ты, нет пи́ццы.","Why the change? Russian literally says “by me there is none <b>of</b> a card”. English keeps a shadow of this — “none of it” — Russian just builds the “of” into the ending. Нет with a dictionary form sounds amputated to a Russian ear."],"audio":"Now the flip side. I don't have is oo menya nyet plus the thing — but here the thing must change its ending. Meet your second case, the genitive: the case of absence. Masculine words add ah: nyet telefona, nyet bileta, nyet zonta. Feminine ah turns into y: nyet karty, nyet gazety, nyet pizzy. Why? Russian literally says: by me there is none OF a card. English keeps a shadow of this — none of it. Russian builds the OF into the ending.","table":{"rows":[["есть телефо́н → нет телефо́на <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"есть телефон — нет телефона\">🔊</button>","have a phone → no phone"],["есть ка́рта → нет ка́рты <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"есть карта — нет карты\">🔊</button>","have a card → no card"],["есть биле́т → нет биле́та <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"есть билет — нет билета\">🔊</button>","have a ticket → no ticket"],["есть пи́цца → нет пи́ццы <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"есть пицца — нет пиццы\">🔊</button>","have pizza → no pizza"]],"star":1},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"У меня́ нет ка́рта.","right":"У меня́ нет ка́рты.","why":"Нет is a genitive magnet: -а → -ы. In English no ending changes after “no” — which is exactly why this slip is the classic one."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Emma digs through the backpack: «У меня́ нет ___!» (her phone is missing)","opts":["телефо́н","телефо́на","телефо́ну"],"answer":1,"explain":"After нет a masculine thing adds -а: <b>нет телефо́на</b>. The dictionary form can't follow нет."},{"type":"choice","q":"The café has run out of pizza. Ле́на announces…","opts":["Пи́ццы нет.","Пи́цца нет.","Нет пи́цца."],"answer":0,"explain":"Word order can flex, the genitive can't: пи́цца → <b>пи́ццы</b> after нет, wherever нет stands."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — no stress mark needed.","q":"Translate: <b>“I don't have a card.”</b>","accept":["у меня нет карты","у меня́ нет ка́рты","oo menya nyet karty","u menya net karty"],"placeholder":"У меня…","explain":"У меня́ нет ка́рты. — нет + genitive: the -а bows out, -ы steps in."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · The ready bricks","title":"Нет вре́мени, нет де́нег, нет пробле́м","paras":["Three everyday absences refuse the recipe — so take them as whole bricks: <b>нет вре́мени</b> (no time), <b>нет де́нег</b> (no money), <b>Нет пробле́м!</b> (no problem!).","Вре́мя is a neuter oddball whose genitive grows into вре́мени; де́ньги exists only in the plural. Don't build these — just say them. English does the same with its own bricks: nobody assembles “no worries” from rules.","Between them, these three cover half of student life. <b>У меня́ нет вре́мени</b> — running to class. <b>Нет де́нег</b> — end of the month. <b>Нет пробле́м</b> — the answer to almost everything else."],"audio":"Three everyday absences refuse the recipe — so take them as whole bricks: nyet vryemeni — no time; nyet dyenyek — no money; nyet problyem — no problem! Vremya is a neuter oddball; dyengi exists only in the plural. Don't build these — just say them. English does the same: nobody assembles no worries from rules. Between them, these three cover half of student life.","examples":[{"ru":"I have no time!","en":"У меня́ нет <b>вре́мени</b>!","say":"У меня нет времени!"},{"ru":"I have no money.","en":"У меня́ нет <b>де́нег</b>.","say":"У меня нет денег."},{"ru":"No problem!","en":"Нет пробле́м!","say":"Нет проблем!"}],"mnemonic":"Time, money, problems — the three things students run out of first. Russian pre-packed them for you: <b>вре́мени, де́нег, пробле́м</b>.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Ива́н suggests a film, but your class starts in five minutes. You say…","opts":["У меня́ нет вре́мени!","У меня́ есть вре́мя!","У меня́ нет де́нег!"],"answer":0,"explain":"Class in five minutes = no time: <b>нет вре́мени</b>. Money isn't the issue, and having time is the opposite of your problem."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I have no money.”</b>","answer":"У меня́ нет де́нег"},{"type":"type","q":"Ле́на thanks you for helping. Reply with the two-word brick — “No problem!”:","accept":["нет проблем","нет пробле́м","nyet problyem","net problem","нет проблем!"],"placeholder":"Нет…","explain":"Нет пробле́м! — a ready brick. You'll say it ten times a day in Russia."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ле́на's café, morning rush. Emma orders coffee — and her backpack turns into a lost-and-found. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Приве́т, Э́мма! Ко́фе?","ru":"Hi, Emma! Coffee?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да, пожа́луйста! Так… ка́рта, ка́рта…","ru":"Yes, please! So… card, card… (так = so, right)"},{"who":"B","en":"Пробле́ма?","ru":"A problem?"},{"who":"A","en":"У меня́ есть па́спорт, ключ и зонт. Но ка́рты нет!","ru":"I have a passport, a key and an umbrella. But no card! (но = but)"},{"who":"B","en":"Нет пробле́м! Ко́фе — пода́рок.","ru":"No problem! The coffee's a gift. (пода́рок = a gift)"},{"who":"A","en":"Спаси́бо, Ле́на!.. О! Вот ка́рта!","ru":"Thank you, Lena!.. Oh! Here's the card!"},{"who":"B","en":"Ну вот. Ка́рта есть — пробле́мы нет.","ru":"There you go. Card found — problem gone. (ну вот = there you go)"},{"who":"A","en":"Да! Ка́рта есть, ко́фе есть!","ru":"Yes! I've got my card, I've got my coffee!"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve things to have — or to run out of. Everything Emma carries, plus the three great absences. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"рюкза́к","ru":"backpack","ex":"[ryook-ZAK] — У меня́ есть рюкза́к."},{"en":"телефо́н","ru":"phone","ex":"[tee-lee-FON] — missing one? нет телефо́на"},{"en":"ка́рта","ru":"card; map","ex":"[KAR-ta] — bank card or city map; нет ка́рты"},{"en":"ключ","ru":"key","ex":"[klyooch] — one syllable, no stress mark needed"},{"en":"ру́чка","ru":"pen","ex":"[ROOCH-ka] — У тебя́ есть ру́чка?"},{"en":"па́спорт","ru":"passport","ex":"[PAS-part] — the unstressed о relaxes to “a”"},{"en":"биле́т","ru":"ticket","ex":"[bee-LYET] — нет биле́та = no ticket"},{"en":"де́ньги","ru":"money","ex":"[DYEN-gee] — always plural; нет де́нег [DYE-nyek]"},{"en":"вре́мя","ru":"time","ex":"[VRYE-mya] — neuter oddball; нет вре́мени"},{"en":"зонт","ru":"umbrella","ex":"[zont] — after нет the stress runs off: нет зонта́ [zan-TA]"},{"en":"иде́я","ru":"idea","ex":"[ee-DYE-ya] — У меня́ есть иде́я!"},{"en":"пробле́ма","ru":"problem","ex":"[pra-BLYE-ma] — and the brick: Нет пробле́м!"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English — mind what's there and what's missing.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning on the right — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["У меня́ есть телефо́н.","I have a phone."],["У меня́ нет ка́рты.","I don't have a card."],["У тебя́ есть ру́чка?","Do you have a pen?"],["У меня́ нет вре́мени.","I have no time."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions about having and not having. The genitive is watching.","items":[{"q":"“I have an umbrella” in Russian:","opts":["Я име́ю зонт.","У меня́ есть зонт.","Я есть зонт."],"answer":1},{"q":"Ива́н is broke this week. He says…","opts":["У меня́ нет де́нег.","У меня́ есть де́ньги.","У меня́ нет де́ньги."],"answer":0},{"q":"«У меня́ нет ___.» — pick the correct form of ка́рта:","opts":["ка́рта","ка́рту","ка́рты"],"answer":2},{"q":"How do you ask a friend whether they have a pen?","opts":["Ты есть ру́чка?","У тебя́ есть ру́чка?","У тебя́ есть ру́чку?"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Нет вре́мени!» means…","opts":["No problem!","No time!","No money!"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma inventories her backpack before a day out. Read it aloud — every есть and нет in its place.","title":"Мой рюкза́к","sentences":["Э́то мой рюкза́к.","У меня́ есть па́спорт, ключ, ру́чка и телефо́н.","У меня́ есть биле́т «Москва́ — Ло́ндон».","А зонта́ нет. И вре́мени нет!","Но у меня́ есть иде́я и ко́фе. Нет пробле́м!"],"translation":"This is my backpack. I have a passport, a key, a pen and a phone. I have a Moscow–London ticket. But no umbrella. And no time! Still, I have an idea and coffee. No problem!"},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — empty your own bag onto the page.","prompt":"What's in your bag? Write 3–4 sentences: two things you have (У меня́ есть…) and at least one thing you don't (У меня́ нет + the changed ending).","hint":"Masculine things grow -а after нет (нет телефо́на), feminine -а turns to -ы (нет ка́рты). And the bricks are always ready: нет вре́мени, нет де́нег 🎒","example":"У меня́ есть рюкза́к. У меня́ есть телефо́н, ключ и ру́чка. Но у меня́ нет зонта́. И де́нег нет!"}},"12":{"day":12,"week":"02","themeRu":"Many things","themeEn":"Мно́го всего́","intro":"How do you turn one banana into many? English adds -s and moves on. Russian adds <b>-ы</b> — most of the time — and today you'll also meet the spelling law every Russian first-grader chants out loud, plus one word that went completely wild. Emma is off to the farmers' market, where everything comes in plurals.","introAudio":"How do you turn one banana into many? English adds s and moves on. Russian adds y — most of the time — and today you'll also meet the spelling law every Russian first-grader chants out loud, plus one word that went completely wild. Emma is off to the farmers' market, where everything comes in plurals.","goals":["Turn one into many: бана́н → <b>бана́ны</b>, газе́та → <b>газе́ты</b>","Obey the spelling law: <b>и</b> after г, к, х, ж, ш — кни́ги, ру́чки","Tame the rebel: друг → <b>друзья́</b>","Shop like a local: <b>Здесь мно́го всего́! Ско́лько сто́ит?</b>"],"learned":["Turned one into many: бана́н → <b>бана́ны</b>, газе́та → <b>газе́ты</b>","Obeyed the spelling law: <b>и</b> after г, к, х, ж, ш — кни́ги, ру́чки","Tamed the rebel: друг → <b>друзья́</b>","Shopped like a local: <b>Здесь мно́го всего́! Ско́лько сто́ит?</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Market day ahead — but first, yesterday's absences and the endings that mark them.","introAudio":"Market day ahead — but first, yesterday's absences and the endings that mark them.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the option that fits. Wrong taps cost nothing — it's a warm-up.","q":"Emma pats her pockets: «У меня́ нет ___!» (the card again)","opts":["ка́рта","ка́рту","ка́рты"],"answer":2,"explain":"Нет is a genitive magnet: -а → -ы, so <b>нет ка́рты</b>. The dictionary form never follows нет."},{"type":"choice","q":"The word газе́та is…","opts":["он","она́","оно́"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ends in -а → feminine, so газе́та is <b>она́</b>. Gender never takes a day off."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“I'm buying water.”</b> (remember where the stress hops)","answer":"Я покупа́ю во́ду"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · One → many","title":"The -ы plural: бана́н → бана́ны","paras":["The default recipe: masculine words ending in a consonant <b>add -ы</b> — бана́н → бана́ны, студе́нт → студе́нты, телефо́н → телефо́ны. Feminine words <b>swap -а for -ы</b> — газе́та → газе́ты, пи́цца → пи́ццы.","Still no articles and no “some”: <b>бана́ны</b> covers “bananas”, “the bananas” and “some bananas” all at once. One form, three English meanings — Russian is being generous again.","Honest border: neuter -о words make their plural differently — we'll park them until you need them. Today belongs to the -ы/-и crowd, which is most of what's on Emma's shopping list."],"audio":"The default recipe: masculine words ending in a consonant add y — banan, banany; studyent, studyenty. Feminine words swap ah for y — gazeta, gazety. Still no articles and no some: banany covers bananas, the bananas, and some bananas all at once. Honest border: neuter words make their plural differently — we'll park them until you need them. Today belongs to the y crowd, which is most of what's on Emma's shopping list.","table":{"rows":[["бана́н → бана́ны <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"банан — бананы\">🔊</button>","banana → bananas"],["телефо́н → телефо́ны <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"телефон — телефоны\">🔊</button>","phone → phones"],["газе́та → газе́ты <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"газета — газеты\">🔊</button>","newspaper → newspapers"],["студе́нт → студе́нты <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"студент — студенты\">🔊</button>","student → students"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Here are tomatoes and oranges.","en":"Вот <b>помидо́ры</b> и <b>апельси́ны</b>.","say":"Вот помидоры и апельсины."},{"ru":"Ivan and Lena are students.","en":"Ива́н и Ле́на — <b>студе́нты</b>.","say":"Иван и Лена — студенты."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The plural of телефо́н:","opts":["телефо́ны","телефо́на","телефо́н"],"answer":0,"explain":"Consonant ending → add -ы: <b>телефо́ны</b>. (Телефо́на is yesterday's «нет телефо́на» form — a different job.)"},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I'm buying bananas and oranges.”</b>","answer":"Я покупа́ю бана́ны и апельси́ны"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — no stress mark needed.","q":"Type the plural of газе́та:","accept":["газеты","газе́ты","gazety","gazyety"],"placeholder":"газе́та → …","explain":"Feminine -а swaps for -ы: <b>газе́ты</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The spelling law","title":"Жи-ши: write <em>и</em>, never ы","paras":["Seven letters refuse to stand next to <b>ы</b>: the throat trio <b>г, к, х</b> and the hushers <b>ж, ч, ш, щ</b>. After any of them, Russian writes <b>и</b> instead.","So: кни́га → <b>кни́ги</b> (never «книгы»), ру́чка → <b>ру́чки</b>, студе́нтка → <b>студе́нтки</b>, ба́бушка → <b>ба́бушки</b>. The sound barely changes — the spelling law is absolute.","Russian first-graders chant it in class: <b>жи-ши пиши́ с и</b> — “write жи-ши with и”. After ж and ш it even still SOUNDS like [ы] — you write и anyway. Chant it once and the rule is yours forever."],"audio":"Seven letters refuse to stand next to y: the throat trio — g, k, kh — and the hushers — zh, ch, sh, shch. After any of them, Russian writes ee instead. So kniga becomes knigi, never knigy. Ruchka — ruchki. Studyentka — studyentki. Russian first-graders chant it in class: zhi shi — write with ee. After zh and sh it even still sounds like y — you write ee anyway. Chant it once and the rule is yours forever.","mistakes":[{"wrong":"кни́гы","right":"кни́ги","why":"Г belongs to the seven letters that never take ы. After г, к, х, ж, ч, ш, щ — write и. English has spelling laws too (“i before e…”); this one at least has no exceptions."}],"examples":[{"ru":"Here are the books and the pens.","en":"Вот <b>кни́ги</b> и <b>ру́чки</b>.","say":"Вот книги и ручки."},{"ru":"Emma and Lena are students.","en":"Э́мма и Ле́на — <b>студе́нтки</b>.","say":"Эмма и Лена — студентки."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Emma writes «книгы» in her notebook. What does Ива́н tell her?","opts":["Nothing — it's correct","After г you must write и: кни́ги","The plural of кни́га is кни́га"],"answer":1,"explain":"Г is one of the seven anti-ы letters: <b>кни́ги</b>. The chant: жи-ши (and г-к-х) — с и."},{"type":"choice","q":"Ле́на и Э́мма — ___.","opts":["студе́нтки","студе́нткы","студе́нток"],"answer":0,"explain":"Two women, plain plural: студе́нтка → <b>студе́нтки</b> — к calls for и, never ы."},{"type":"type","q":"Type the plural of ру́чка (mind the spelling law):","accept":["ручки","ру́чки","ruchki"],"placeholder":"ру́чка → …","explain":"К is in the anti-ы club: <b>ру́чки</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · The rebel","title":"друг → <em>друзья́</em>","paras":["Some plurals refuse every recipe. Today's rebel is the one you'll use most: <b>друг → друзья́</b> [droo-ZYA] — “friend → friends”. It's such a beloved word that Russian let it go wild centuries ago and never called it back.","The female friend behaves: <b>подру́га → подру́ги</b> — regular, plus the spelling law (г → и). And <b>де́ньги</b> from yesterday is a word that never had a singular at all.","When you meet a wild plural, learn it as its own word — Russians did exactly that as children. English speakers can hardly complain: child → children, mouse → mice."],"audio":"Some plurals refuse every recipe. Today's rebel is the one you'll use most: drook — droozya. Friend — friends. It's such a beloved word that Russian let it go wild centuries ago and never called it back. The female friend behaves: padrooga, padroogi — regular, plus the spelling law. And dyengi from yesterday never had a singular at all. When you meet a wild plural, learn it as its own word. English speakers can hardly complain: child — children, mouse — mice.","examples":[{"ru":"Ivan is a friend.","en":"Ива́н — <b>друг</b>.","say":"Иван — друг."},{"ru":"Ivan and Lena are friends.","en":"Ива́н и Ле́на — <b>друзья́</b>.","say":"Иван и Лена — друзья."},{"ru":"Lena is my (female) friend.","en":"Ле́на — моя́ <b>подру́га</b>.","say":"Лена — моя подруга."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Ива́н и Ле́на — дру́ги.","right":"Ива́н и Ле́на — друзья́.","why":"Друг ignores the -и recipe — its plural is the wild <b>друзья́</b>. Learn it as its own word, like “children”."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“Ivan and Lena are friends” — pick the Russian:","opts":["Ива́н и Ле́на — дру́ги.","Ива́н и Ле́на — друзья́.","Ива́н и Ле́на — друг."],"answer":1,"explain":"The rebel plural: <b>друзья́</b>. Дру́ги doesn't exist in modern Russian, and друг is just one friend."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“These are my friends.”</b>","answer":"Э́то мои́ друзья́"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · At the market","title":"Здесь мно́го всего́! Ско́лько сто́ит?","paras":["Markets are plural heaven, so take the market chunk: <b>Здесь мно́го всего́!</b> [MNO-ga fsye-VO] — “there's a lot of everything here”. Здесь = here; and in всего́ the г says “v” — the -его ending you met in the alphabet course.","Plural things as objects keep their plural form — no new endings today: <b>Я покупа́ю бана́ны. Я люблю́ фру́кты.</b> The -а → -у swap from day 10 was for singular feminine words only.","And the question that runs every market: <b>Ско́лько сто́ит?</b> — “how much is it?” Point at anything and ask. The answers are numbers — and you've owned 0–20 since day 6."],"audio":"Markets are plural heaven, so take the market chunk: zdyes mnoga fsyevo — there's a lot of everything here. Zdyes means here; and in fsyevo the g says v — the ending you met in the alphabet course. Plural things as objects keep their plural form — no new endings today: ya pakupayu banany; ya lyublyu frookty. And the question that runs every market: skolka stoit? — how much is it? Point at anything and ask. The answers are numbers — and you've owned zero to twenty since day six.","examples":[{"ru":"There's a lot of everything here!","en":"Здесь <b>мно́го всего́</b>!","say":"Здесь много всего!"},{"ru":"How much is the cheese?","en":"<b>Ско́лько сто́ит</b> сыр?","say":"Сколько стоит сыр?"},{"ru":"I'm buying tomatoes and bread.","en":"Я покупа́ю <b>помидо́ры</b> и хлеб.","say":"Я покупаю помидоры и хлеб."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Я о́чень люблю́ ___. (fruit)","opts":["фру́кты","фру́кта","фру́ктах"],"answer":0,"explain":"Plural things don't change as objects: <b>люблю́ фру́кты</b>. The -у swap was for singular -а words."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“There's a lot of everything here!”</b>","answer":"Здесь мно́го всего́"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and ask like you're already at the stall.","target":"Ско́лько сто́ит?","sub":"[SKOL-ka STO-eet] — point at anything and ask","want":"сколько стоит"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Saturday. Ива́н has drifted to the honey stall, so Emma faces the tomato queen alone — ба́бушка Мари́на, who sells like it's high art. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Фру́кты! Помидо́ры! Бана́ны!","ru":"Fruit! Tomatoes! Bananas!"},{"who":"A","en":"Здра́вствуйте! Ско́лько сто́ит сыр?","ru":"Hello! How much is the cheese?"},{"who":"B","en":"Два́дцать. А для тебя́ — де́сять!","ru":"Twenty. But for you — ten! (для тебя́ = for you)"},{"who":"A","en":"Спаси́бо! И бана́ны, пожа́луйста.","ru":"Thank you! And bananas, please."},{"who":"B","en":"Вот бана́ны, вот сыр. Ты студе́нтка?","ru":"Here are the bananas, here's the cheese. Are you a student?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да, я студе́нтка. Я из А́нглии.","ru":"Yes, I'm a student. I'm from England."},{"who":"B","en":"О! А по-ру́сски говори́шь хорошо́!","ru":"Oh! And you speak Russian well! (хорошо́ = well)"},{"who":"A","en":"Спаси́бо! Здесь мно́го всего́ — я люблю́ ры́нок!","ru":"Thank you! There's a lot of everything here — I love the market!"},{"who":"B","en":"Ры́нок — э́то жизнь! Вот апельси́н — пода́рок.","ru":"The market is life! Here's an orange — a gift. (пода́рок = a gift)"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve market words — things that come in manys, plus the two phrases that make you sound local. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"ры́нок","ru":"market","ex":"[RY-nak] — the farmers' market; unstressed о relaxes to “a”"},{"en":"фру́кты","ru":"fruit","ex":"[FROOK-ty] — usually plural, just like in English shopping lists"},{"en":"бана́н","ru":"banana","ex":"[ba-NAN] — plural бана́ны; the friendliest word today"},{"en":"апельси́н","ru":"orange (fruit)","ex":"[a-peel-SEEN] — plural апельси́ны"},{"en":"помидо́р","ru":"tomato","ex":"[pa-mee-DOR] — plural помидо́ры"},{"en":"сыр","ru":"cheese","ex":"[syr] — that deep ы from the alphabet course"},{"en":"хлеб","ru":"bread","ex":"[khlyep] — the final б whispers into “p”"},{"en":"друг","ru":"friend","ex":"[drook] — plural: the rebel друзья́ [droo-ZYA]"},{"en":"подру́га","ru":"female friend","ex":"[pa-DROO-ga] — plural подру́ги: г calls for и"},{"en":"ба́бушка","ru":"grandmother","ex":"[BA-boosh-ka] — plural ба́бушки; the true rulers of any market"},{"en":"здесь","ru":"here","ex":"[zdyes] — Здесь мно́го всего́!"},{"en":"Ско́лько сто́ит?","ru":"How much is it?","ex":"[SKOL-ka STO-eet] — point and ask; answers are numbers you know"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English — plurals everywhere.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning on the right — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Э́то мои́ друзья́.","These are my friends."],["Здесь мно́го всего́!","There's a lot of everything here!"],["Ско́лько сто́ит сыр?","How much is the cheese?"],["Я покупа́ю бана́ны.","I'm buying bananas."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions from the market — plurals, the spelling law and one rebel.","items":[{"q":"The plural of ба́бушка:","opts":["ба́бушкы","ба́бушки","ба́бушка"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which letters can NEVER be followed by ы?","opts":["б, в, д","г, к, х, ж, ш","н, м, л"],"answer":1},{"q":"“Friends” in Russian:","opts":["друзья́","дру́ги","дру́гы"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Здесь мно́го всего́!» means…","opts":["Everything is expensive here.","Everyone is here.","There's a lot of everything here."],"answer":2},{"q":"At the market you point at the cheese and ask…","opts":["Ско́лько сто́ит?","Кто э́то?","Отку́да ты?"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's market diary — read it aloud and let the plurals roll.","title":"Ры́нок","sentences":["Э́то ры́нок. Здесь мно́го всего́!","Вот фру́кты: бана́ны и апельси́ны.","Я покупа́ю сыр, хлеб и помидо́ры.","Ба́бушка Мари́на говори́т: «Ты студе́нтка? Вот пода́рок!»","Я люблю́ ры́нок. Мои́ друзья́ то́же."],"translation":"This is the market. There's a lot of everything here! Here's the fruit: bananas and oranges. I'm buying cheese, bread and tomatoes. Grandma Marina says: “You're a student? Here's a gift!” I love the market. My friends do too."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — set up a market stall of your own.","prompt":"Your dream market stall, in 3–4 sentences: what's here (Здесь…), which plurals are on the table, and what do you buy?","hint":"The recipe: -ы after most letters, but и after г, к, х, ж, ш (кни́ги, ру́чки). And друг goes wild: друзья́ 🍅","example":"Э́то мой ры́нок. Здесь фру́кты: бана́ны и апельси́ны. Я покупа́ю помидо́ры, сыр и хлеб. Здесь мно́го всего́!"}},"13":{"day":13,"week":"02","themeRu":"New and good","themeEn":"Но́вый и хоро́ший","intro":"Yesterday you filled Emma's bag with plurals; today you'll paint everything you own. One English word — “new” — never changes: new phone, new car, new café. Its Russian mirror <b>но́вый</b> dresses differently for every gender and for the plural, and when it describes, it stands before its noun. Welcome to agreement — the habit that makes Russian sound Russian.","introAudio":"Yesterday you filled Emma's bag with plurals; today you'll paint everything you own. One English word — new — never changes: new phone, new car, new café. Its Russian mirror — novy — dresses differently for every gender and for the plural, and when it describes, it stands before its noun. Welcome to agreement — the habit that makes Russian sound Russian.","goals":["Dress a noun: <b>но́вый телефо́н, но́вая маши́на, но́вое кафе́, но́вые кни́ги</b>","Handle the good one: <b>хоро́ший / хоро́шая / хоро́шее / хоро́шие</b>","Meet the stressed tails: <b>большо́й, плохо́й</b>","Make the whole chain agree: <b>Э́то моя́ но́вая кни́га</b>"],"learned":["Dressed nouns: <b>но́вый телефо́н, но́вая маши́на, но́вое кафе́, но́вые кни́ги</b>","Handled the good one: <b>хоро́ший / хоро́шая / хоро́шее / хоро́шие</b>","Met the stressed tails: <b>большо́й, плохо́й</b>","Made the whole chain agree: <b>Э́то моя́ но́вая кни́га</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Adjectives arrive today — but first, a quick sweep of plurals, absences and the endings you've banked.","introAudio":"Adjectives arrive today — but first, a quick sweep of plurals, absences and the endings you've banked.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the option that fits. Wrong taps are free — it's a warm-up.","q":"Ива́н checks his watch: «У меня́ нет ___!»","opts":["вре́мя","вре́мени","вре́мю"],"answer":1,"explain":"The ready brick from day 11: <b>нет вре́мени</b>. Вре́мя is the neuter oddball — don't build it, say it."},{"type":"choice","q":"The plural of подру́га:","opts":["подру́ги","подру́гы","подру́га"],"answer":0,"explain":"Г belongs to the anti-ы seven: <b>подру́ги</b>. Yesterday's spelling law, already earning its keep."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“I'm buying cheese and bread.”</b>","answer":"Я покупа́ю сыр и хлеб"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Dressing the noun","title":"но́вый / но́вая / но́вое / но́вые","paras":["In English “new” is frozen. A Russian adjective dresses for its noun: <b>но́вый</b> телефо́н (he), <b>но́вая</b> маши́на (she), <b>но́вое</b> кафе́ (it), <b>но́вые</b> кни́ги (many). Four outfits: <b>-ый, -ая, -ое, -ые</b>.","When it describes, the adjective stands <b>before</b> its noun, just like English: но́вая маши́на — a new car. Ста́рый (old) wears the same four outfits: ста́рый ры́нок, ста́рая кни́га.","And day 1 pays off again: to STATE it, put the adjective after — with zero “is”. <b>Маши́на но́вая.</b> = “The car is new.” Describe → before; state → after. Two positions, two jobs."],"audio":"In English, new is frozen. A Russian adjective dresses for its noun: novy telefon — he; novaya mashina — she; novoye kafe — it; novye knigi — many. Four outfits. When it describes, the adjective stands before its noun, just like English: novaya mashina, a new car. And day one pays off again: to state it, put the adjective after — with zero is. Mashina novaya: the car is new. Describe — before; state — after. Two positions, two jobs.","table":{"rows":[["но́вый телефо́н <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"новый телефон\">🔊</button>","a new phone (he)"],["но́вая маши́на <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"новая машина\">🔊</button>","a new car (she)"],["но́вое кафе́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"новое кафе\">🔊</button>","a new café (it)"],["но́вые кни́ги <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"новые книги\">🔊</button>","new books (many)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"This is a new park.","en":"Э́то <b>но́вый</b> парк.","say":"Это новый парк."},{"ru":"The car is new.","en":"Маши́на <b>но́вая</b>.","say":"Машина новая.","gloss":"adjective after the noun + zero “is” = a full sentence"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"но́вый маши́на","right":"но́вая маши́на","why":"Маши́на is feminine — the adjective must dress to match: -ая. In English nothing ever agrees, so this is the habit to build first."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Ле́на shows off her brother's car: «Э́то ___ маши́на!»","opts":["но́вый","но́вая","но́вое"],"answer":1,"explain":"Маши́на is “she”, so the adjective wears -ая: <b>но́вая</b>. The noun picks the outfit."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“This is a new car.”</b>","answer":"Э́то но́вая маши́на"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — no stress mark needed.","q":"Type: <b>“a new café”</b> (кафе́ is “it”)","accept":["новое кафе","но́вое кафе́","novoye kafe","novoe kafe"],"placeholder":"но́в… кафе́","explain":"Кафе́ is neuter, so the -ое outfit: <b>но́вое кафе́</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The good one","title":"хоро́ший — with yesterday's law inside","paras":["<b>Хоро́ший</b> (good) sings the same tune with one twist — the spelling law lives inside it: <b>хоро́ший, хоро́шая, хоро́шее, хоро́шие</b>. After ш you never write ы, so the plural is хоро́шие.","Same family: <b>ма́ленький</b> (small) → ма́ленькие — the к calls for и. Yesterday you learned the law for nouns; today it quietly runs through adjectives too.","One form to copy rather than build: the neuter is <b>хоро́шее</b> — with е. After the hushers, Russian avoids that -ое ending here. Don't engineer it; just take the four forms as a set."],"audio":"Khoroshy — good — sings the same tune with one twist: the spelling law lives inside it. Khoroshy, khoroshaya, khorosheye, khoroshiye. After sh you never write y, so the plural is khoroshiye. Same family: malenky — small — malenkiye; the k calls for ee. One form to copy rather than build: the neuter is khorosheye, with yeh. Don't engineer it; just take the four forms as a set.","table":{"rows":[["хоро́ший друг <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"хороший друг\">🔊</button>","a good friend (he)"],["хоро́шая иде́я <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"хорошая идея\">🔊</button>","a good idea (she)"],["хоро́шее кафе́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"хорошее кафе\">🔊</button>","a good café (it)"],["хоро́шие друзья́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"хорошие друзья\">🔊</button>","good friends (many)"]],"star":3},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"хоро́шые друзья́","right":"хоро́шие друзья́","why":"Ш never takes ы — write и. The market law from yesterday runs through adjectives too."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“Good friends” in Russian:","opts":["хоро́шие друзья́","хоро́шые друзья́","хоро́ший друзья́"],"answer":0,"explain":"Plural + spelling law: <b>хоро́шие</b>. Хоро́шые breaks the ш-и rule; хоро́ший is singular."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it with feeling — you'll use this line daily.","target":"Э́то хоро́шая иде́я!","sub":"[EH-ta kha-RO-sha-ya ee-DYE-ya] — a compliment that fits everything","want":"это хорошая идея"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Stressed tails","title":"большо́й и плохо́й","paras":["Some adjectives put the stress on the tail — and then the masculine ends in <b>-о́й</b>, not -ый: <b>большо́й</b> (big), <b>плохо́й</b> (bad). Большо́й го́род — a big city.","The rest of the family is familiar: <b>больша́я, большо́е, больши́е</b> — and there's the law again: больши́е with и, because ш.","Key habit: the adjective agrees with <b>its own noun</b>, not with the star of the sentence. <b>Москва́ — большо́й го́род.</b> “Big” dresses for го́род (he), even though the sentence is about Москва́ (she)."],"audio":"Some adjectives put the stress on the tail — and then the masculine ends in oy, not y: balshoy — big; plakhoy — bad. Balshoy gorat — a big city. The rest of the family is familiar: balshaya, balshoye, balshiye — and there's the law again: balshiye with ee, because of sh. Key habit: the adjective agrees with its own noun, not with the star of the sentence. Moskva — balshoy gorat. Big dresses for gorat — he — even though the sentence is about Moscow.","examples":[{"ru":"Moscow is a big city.","en":"Москва́ — <b>большо́й</b> го́род.","say":"Москва — большой город."},{"ru":"The pizza is big!","en":"Пи́цца <b>больша́я</b>!","say":"Пицца большая!"},{"ru":"The coffee here isn't bad.","en":"Ко́фе здесь не <b>плохо́й</b>.","say":"Кофе здесь не плохой."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Москва́ — больша́я го́род.","right":"Москва́ — большо́й го́род.","why":"The adjective dresses for го́род — masculine — not for Москва́. Find the noun it describes, then pick the ending."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Москва́ — ___ го́род.","opts":["большо́й","больша́я","большо́е"],"answer":0,"explain":"Big describes го́род (he), so <b>большо́й</b> — even though we're talking about her, Москва́."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“This is a big city.”</b>","answer":"Э́то большо́й го́род"},{"type":"type","q":"Type: <b>“a big pizza”</b> — mind the stressed tail:","accept":["большая пицца","больша́я пи́цца","bolshaya pizza","balshaya pitsa","bolshaya pitsa"],"placeholder":"больш… пи́цца","explain":"Пи́цца is “she”, and the stress rides the tail: <b>больша́я пи́цца</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Everything agrees","title":"Э́то моя́ но́вая кни́га","paras":["Now stack the chain: possessive + adjective + noun, all matching. <b>Э́то моя́ но́вая кни́га. Мои́ но́вые друзья́.</b> English sends zero gender signals; Russian sends the same signal through every link.","One celebrity exception on the masculine list: <b>ко́фе</b> looks neuter but is “he” — so it's <b>вку́сный ко́фе</b>, delicious coffee. Day 10's word card warned you; today it matters.","Watch the week snap together: У меня́ есть но́вая кни́га. Здесь хоро́ший ры́нок и вку́сная пи́цца. Every piece — having, plurals, agreement — is now one machine."],"audio":"Now stack the chain: possessive plus adjective plus noun, all matching. Eta maya novaya kniga. Mai novye droozya. English sends zero gender signals; Russian sends the same signal through every link. One celebrity exception: kofye looks neuter but is he — so it's fkoosny kofye, delicious coffee. Day ten's word card warned you; today it matters. Watch the week snap together: having, plurals, agreement — one machine now.","examples":[{"ru":"This is my new book.","en":"Э́то моя́ <b>но́вая</b> кни́га.","say":"Это моя новая книга."},{"ru":"My new friends are students.","en":"Мои́ <b>но́вые</b> друзья́ — студе́нты.","say":"Мои новые друзья — студенты."},{"ru":"Delicious coffee!","en":"<b>Вку́сный</b> ко́фе!","say":"Вкусный кофе!"}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"вку́сное ко́фе","right":"вку́сный ко́фе","why":"Ко́фе is the famous masculine exception — Russians test each other on it. Вку́сный, always."}],"mnemonic":"The adjective checks the noun's gender tag before getting dressed: но́в<b>ая</b> for кни́га, но́в<b>ое</b> for кафе́, но́в<b>ые</b> for the crowd.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Ле́на pours you a cup: «Ко́фе о́чень ___!»","opts":["вку́сное","вку́сный","вку́сная"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ко́фе is “he” despite its looks: <b>вку́сный</b>. The most beloved exception in Russian."},{"type":"choice","q":"“My new friends” in Russian:","opts":["мои́ но́вый друзья́","мои́ но́вые друзья́","мой но́вые друг"],"answer":1,"explain":"The whole chain dresses plural: <b>мои́ но́вые друзья́</b>. One link out of uniform breaks the sentence."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“This is delicious pizza.”</b>","answer":"Э́то вку́сная пи́цца"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Evening. Emma scrolls through her photos with Ива́н — two weeks of Moscow finds, now with adjectives. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Ива́н, вот мои́ фо́то! Э́то мой люби́мый парк.","ru":"Ivan, here are my photos! This is my favourite park. (фо́то = photo)"},{"who":"B","en":"Краси́вый парк! А э́то что?","ru":"A beautiful park! And what's this?"},{"who":"A","en":"Э́то кафе́ «Ла́мпа». Ма́ленькое, но о́чень хоро́шее.","ru":"That's the café “Lampa”. Small, but very good. (но = but)"},{"who":"B","en":"Ле́на рабо́тает здесь?","ru":"Does Lena work here?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да! Ко́фе здесь о́чень вку́сный.","ru":"Yes! The coffee here is delicious."},{"who":"B","en":"А э́то но́вая кни́га? Интере́сная?","ru":"And is that a new book? Interesting?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да, о́чень интере́сная. И вот ста́рый ры́нок — мой люби́мый.","ru":"Yes, very interesting. And here's the old market — my favourite."},{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, ты о́чень лю́бишь Москву́!","ru":"Emma, you really love Moscow!"},{"who":"A","en":"Да. Москва́ — большо́й и краси́вый го́род!","ru":"I do. Moscow is a big and beautiful city!"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Ten adjectives and two nouns to hang them on — the painter's kit for your new city. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"но́вый","ru":"new","ex":"[NO-vy] — но́вая маши́на, но́вое кафе́"},{"en":"ста́рый","ru":"old","ex":"[STA-ry] — ста́рый го́род = the old town"},{"en":"хоро́ший","ru":"good","ex":"[kha-RO-shy] — plural хоро́шие: the spelling law inside"},{"en":"плохо́й","ru":"bad","ex":"[pla-KHOY] — stress on the tail, like большо́й"},{"en":"большо́й","ru":"big","ex":"[bal-SHOY] — больша́я, большо́е, больши́е"},{"en":"ма́ленький","ru":"small","ex":"[MA-leen-kee] — plural ма́ленькие: к calls for и"},{"en":"краси́вый","ru":"beautiful","ex":"[kra-SEE-vy] — краси́вый го́род, краси́вая му́зыка"},{"en":"вку́сный","ru":"tasty, delicious","ex":"[FKOOS-ny] — вку́сный ко́фе: remember, ко́фе is “he”"},{"en":"интере́сный","ru":"interesting","ex":"[een-tee-RYES-ny] — интере́сная кни́га"},{"en":"люби́мый","ru":"favourite","ex":"[lyoo-BEE-my] — мой люби́мый ры́нок"},{"en":"маши́на","ru":"car","ex":"[ma-SHY-na] — spelled with и: yesterday's law mid-word"},{"en":"го́род","ru":"city, town","ex":"[GO-rat] — the final д whispers into “t”"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English — check every ending before you drop.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning on the right — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["но́вая маши́на","a new car"],["хоро́ший друг","a good friend"],["вку́сный ко́фе","delicious coffee"],["большо́й го́род","a big city"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions of pure agreement — the endings are watching each other.","items":[{"q":"“A new phone” in Russian:","opts":["но́вый телефо́н","но́вая телефо́н","но́вое телефо́н"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Э́то ___ кафе́.» (good)","opts":["хоро́ший","хоро́шая","хоро́шее"],"answer":2},{"q":"Pick the correct pair:","opts":["больши́е кни́ги","большы́е кни́ги","большо́й кни́ги"],"answer":0},{"q":"Emma sums up her city. Which sentence is correct?","opts":["Москва́ — большо́е го́род.","Москва́ — большо́й го́род.","Москва́ — больша́я го́род."],"answer":1},{"q":"«Мои́ но́вые друзья́» means…","opts":["my new friend","my new friends","my good friends"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's postcard home, now in full colour — read it aloud and hear the endings agree.","title":"Мой но́вый го́род","sentences":["Москва́ — большо́й и краси́вый го́род.","Вот моё люби́мое кафе́. Оно́ ма́ленькое, но хоро́шее.","Здесь рабо́тает Ле́на. Ко́фе здесь о́чень вку́сный!","А э́то ста́рый ры́нок и мои́ но́вые кни́ги.","Кни́ги но́вые и о́чень интере́сные."],"translation":"Moscow is a big and beautiful city. Here's my favourite café. It's small but good. Lena works here. The coffee here is delicious! And this is the old market and my new books. The books are new and very interesting."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn — paint your own town.","prompt":"Describe three favourite finds in your town in 3–4 sentences. Use но́вый/но́вая/но́вое/но́вые at least once and one form of хоро́ший. Keep each adjective in front of its noun — or after it, if you're stating.","hint":"Match the ending to the thing: но́вый парк, но́вая кни́га, но́вое кафе́, но́вые друзья́. The spelling law still holds: хоро́шие, ма́ленькие 🏙","example":"Мой го́род ма́ленький, но краси́вый. Вот моё люби́мое кафе́ — о́чень хоро́шее. Здесь вку́сный ко́фе. А э́то но́вый парк и ста́рый ры́нок."}},"14":{"day":14,"week":"02","themeRu":"Review 2","themeEn":"Повторе́ние 2","intro":"One week ago you couldn't say a single Russian verb. Today you conjugate two whole families, aim the accusative at objects, own things with <b>у меня́ есть</b>, and make adjectives agree. Ле́на has put the teapot on the table at her café — today is a victory lap, not a lesson. Nothing new: we polish what you already have.","introAudio":"One week ago you couldn't say a single Russian verb. Today you conjugate two whole families, aim the accusative at objects, own things with u menya yest, and make adjectives agree. Lena has put the teapot on the table at her café — today is a victory lap, not a lesson. Nothing new: we polish what you already have.","goals":["Run both present-tense families without notes: <b>я зна́ю — ты зна́ешь</b>, <b>я говорю́ — ты говори́шь</b>","Aim the accusative at any object: <b>Я чита́ю кни́гу</b>, <b>Я люблю́ Москву́</b>","Say what you have — and what you don't: <b>У меня́ есть друг</b> / <b>У меня́ нет ка́рты</b>","Match adjectives to anything: <b>но́вый дом, но́вая маши́на, но́вые кни́ги</b>"],"learned":["Ran both present-tense families without notes: <b>я зна́ю — ты зна́ешь</b>, <b>я говорю́ — ты говори́шь</b>","Aimed the accusative at objects: <b>Я чита́ю кни́гу</b>, <b>Я люблю́ Москву́</b>","Said what you have and what you don't: <b>У меня́ есть друг</b> / <b>У меня́ нет ка́рты</b>","Matched adjectives to their nouns: <b>но́вый дом, но́вая маши́на, но́вые кни́ги</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Before the tea: five quick throwbacks — some go all the way back to week one.","introAudio":"Before the tea: five quick throwbacks — some go all the way back to week one.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the situation and tap the right answer. A wrong tap costs nothing — it's how memory sets.","q":"Whose is it? Pick the right “my” for маши́на (car):","opts":["мой","моя́","моё"],"answer":1,"explain":"Маши́на ends in -а — she's feminine, so <b>моя́</b>. Agreement follows the thing's gender, never the owner's."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Ско́лько тебе́ лет?» — you're nineteen (девятна́дцать). Your answer:","opts":["Я девятна́дцать лет.","У меня́ девятна́дцать лет.","Мне девятна́дцать лет."],"answer":2,"explain":"Age arrives <em>to</em> you: <b>Мне… лет</b> — the dative from day six. “I am 19” translated word-for-word is the classic slip."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — no stress marks needed.","q":"Type the Russian for “This is a café” — remember, no “is”:","accept":["это кафе","eto kafe","eto cafe"],"placeholder":"Э́то …"},{"type":"choice","q":"Э́мма is from England. She says:","opts":["Я англича́нка.","Я англича́нин."],"answer":0,"explain":"Nationality words change with the speaker's gender: Emma is <b>англича́нка</b>. Ива́н would be ру́сский, Ле́на — ру́сская."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“I am from England.”</b>","want":"Я из Англии","answer":"Я из А́нглии"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Recap 1 · Two verb families","title":"Present tense: the <em>-е-</em> team and the <em>-и-</em> team","paras":["All your week-two verbs sort into two families. The <b>-е-</b> team — знать, рабо́тать, чита́ть, понима́ть — glues on -ю, -ешь, -ет: <b>я зна́ю, ты зна́ешь, он зна́ет</b>.","The <b>-и-</b> team — говори́ть, люби́ть, смотре́ть — answers with -ю́, -и́шь, -и́т: <b>я говорю́, ты говори́шь, она́ говори́т</b>. Remember the two little dramas of люби́ть: люблю́ keeps its extra л, and after it the stress runs home — люблю́ → лю́бишь → лю́бит.","The ending already announces who acts — that's why Russian needs no “do / does” and no extra scaffolding. One word, full information."],"audio":"All your week-two verbs sort into two families. The ye-team — znat, rabotat, chitat, ponimat — glues on yu, yesh, yet: ya znayu, ty znayesh, on znayet. The ee-team — govorit, lyubit, smotret — answers with yu, ish, it: ya govoryu, ty govorish, ona govorit. Remember the two little dramas: lyublyu keeps its extra L, and after it the stress runs home. The ending already announces who acts — one word, full information.","table":{"rows":[["я зна́ю · я говорю́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я знаю, я говорю\">🔊</button>","I know · I speak"],["ты зна́ешь · ты говори́шь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ты знаешь, ты говоришь\">🔊</button>","you know · you speak"],["он/она́ зна́ет · говори́т <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"он знает, она говорит\">🔊</button>","he/she knows · speaks"],["мы зна́ем · говори́м <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мы знаем, мы говорим\">🔊</button>","we know · we speak"],["вы зна́ете · говори́те <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вы знаете, вы говорите\">🔊</button>","you (polite/many) know · speak"],["они́ зна́ют · говоря́т <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"они знают, они говорят\">🔊</button>","they know · they speak"]],"star":2},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Он зна́ю Москву́.","right":"Он зна́ет Москву́.","why":"The ending must match the doer: он → зна́ет. In English only “he knows” changes; in Russian every person has its own ending."},{"wrong":"Я любю́ чай.","right":"Я люблю́ чай.","why":"Люблю́ keeps its extra л — only in the я-form. Ты лю́бишь, он лю́бит go back to normal."}],"examples":[{"ru":"Emma understands Russian well.","en":"Э́мма хорошо́ <b>понима́ет</b> по-ру́сски.","say":"Эмма хорошо понимает по-русски."},{"ru":"They speak Russian.","en":"Они́ <b>говоря́т</b> по-ру́сски.","say":"Они говорят по-русски.","gloss":"-ят — the и-team's “they” ending"},{"ru":"Do you watch films? (no “do” needed)","en":"Ты <b>смо́тришь</b> фи́льмы?","say":"Ты смотришь фильмы?"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Ле́на says about her brother: «Он ___ по-ру́сски и по-англи́йски.»","opts":["говори́т","говорю́","говори́шь"],"answer":0,"explain":"Он → <b>говори́т</b>. Говорю́ is “I speak”, говори́шь is “you speak” — the ending is the name tag."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“You speak Russian well.”</b> (to a friend)","want":"Ты хорошо говоришь по-русски","answer":"Ты хорошо́ говори́шь по-ру́сски"}]},{"eyebrow":"Recap 2 · Aim and own","title":"Accusative for the object, <em>у меня́ есть / нет</em> for having","paras":["English marks the object by position: <em>I read the book</em>. Russian marks it with an ending: feminine <b>-а</b> flips to <b>-у</b> — <b>Я чита́ю кни́гу</b>, <b>Я люблю́ Москву́</b>. Masculine and neuter things don't change: Я смотрю́ фильм, Я люблю́ ко́фе.","Having is a location trick: <b>У меня́ есть друг</b> — literally “by me there is a friend”. The thing you have stays in its dictionary form — есть does no damage.","Not having flips the case: <b>нет</b> demands the genitive — <b>У меня́ нет ка́рты</b>, <b>нет вре́мени</b>. One little word, one case change. Say «нет ка́рта» and a Russian hears the sentence limp."],"audio":"English marks the object by position: I read the book. Russian marks it with an ending: feminine a flips to u — ya chitayu knigu, ya lyublyu Moskvu. Masculine and neuter things don't change. Having is a location trick: u menya yest droog — literally, by me there is a friend — and the thing you have stays in its dictionary form. Not having flips the case: nyet demands the genitive — u menya nyet karty, nyet vryemeni.","table":{"rows":[["Э́то кни́га. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Это книга.\">🔊</button>","This is a book. — nominative: naming"],["Я чита́ю кни́гу. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я читаю книгу.\">🔊</button>","I'm reading the book. — accusative: the object"],["У меня́ есть кни́га. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"У меня есть книга.\">🔊</button>","I have a book. — есть + dictionary form"],["У меня́ нет кни́ги. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"У меня нет книги.\">🔊</button>","I don't have a book. — нет + genitive"]],"star":3},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я люблю́ Москва́.","right":"Я люблю́ Москву́.","why":"Москва́ is the object of your love — feminine -а becomes -у. English shows the object by word order; Russian writes it on the word itself."},{"wrong":"У меня́ нет кни́га.","right":"У меня́ нет кни́ги.","why":"After нет the genitive is non-negotiable: кни́ги. Keep «кни́га» for when you have it: У меня́ есть кни́га."}],"examples":[{"ru":"I'm watching a film.","en":"Я смотрю́ <b>фильм</b>.","say":"Я смотрю фильм.","gloss":"masculine thing — no change"},{"ru":"I have no time.","en":"У меня́ нет <b>вре́мени</b>.","say":"У меня нет времени.","gloss":"the most Moscow sentence there is"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Ле́на checks her bag — the map is gone. She sighs:","opts":["У меня́ нет ка́рта.","У меня́ нет ка́рты.","У меня́ не ка́рта."],"answer":1,"explain":"Нет + genitive: <b>нет ка́рты</b>. Plain «не» can't own things — it only negates what follows it."},{"type":"type","q":"Type: “I love Moscow.” — aim the ending:","accept":["я люблю москву","люблю москву","ya lyublyu moskvu","ya lublu moskvu","ya lyublyu moskvoo"],"placeholder":"Я люблю́ …","explain":"Я люблю́ Москву́ — the -а of Москва́ bends to -у under the verb."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I have a new friend.”</b>","want":"У меня есть новый друг","answer":"У меня́ есть но́вый друг"}]},{"eyebrow":"Recap 3 · Everything agrees","title":"Plurals in <em>-ы/-и</em>, adjectives in step","paras":["More than one: add <b>-ы</b> — маши́на → маши́ны, стол → столы́. But after г, к, х, ж, ш the letter ы is banned: write <b>-и</b> — кни́га → кни́ги, ру́чка → ру́чки. That's the жи/ши law from day twelve doing its quiet work.","A few friends refuse the system entirely: друг → <b>друзья́</b>. Russian keeps its irregulars exactly where you talk the most.","Adjectives copy their noun's gender and number like a shadow, and stand before it, English-style: <b>но́вый дом, но́вая маши́на, но́вые кни́ги</b>. Хоро́ший obeys the spelling law too: <b>хоро́шие</b>, never «хорошые»."],"audio":"More than one: add y — mashina, mashiny. But after the letters g, k, kh, zh and sh, y is banned: write ee — kniga, knigi. A few friends refuse the system entirely: droog becomes droozya. Adjectives copy their noun's gender and number like a shadow, and stand before it, English-style: novy dom, novaya mashina, novye knigi. Khoroshy obeys the spelling law too: khoroshiye.","mistakes":[{"wrong":"но́вый маши́на","right":"но́вая маши́на","why":"Маши́на is feminine, so the adjective wears -ая. Same logic as мой/моя́ — agreement is with the thing, not with you."},{"wrong":"кни́гы","right":"кни́ги","why":"After г you may not write ы. Г, к, х, ж, ш always take и."}],"examples":[{"ru":"New books, new friends.","en":"<b>Но́вые</b> кни́ги, <b>но́вые</b> друзья́.","say":"Новые книги, новые друзья."},{"ru":"Ivan is a good friend.","en":"Ива́н — <b>хоро́ший</b> друг.","say":"Иван — хороший друг."},{"ru":"Is that a new film?","en":"Э́то <b>но́вый</b> фильм?","say":"Это новый фильм?"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Pick the pair that agrees:","opts":["но́вые друзья́","но́вый друзья́","но́вая друзья́"],"answer":0,"explain":"Друзья́ is plural, so the adjective goes plural too: <b>но́вые</b>. Gender retires in the plural — number takes over."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud. Match the stress you see.","target":"У меня́ есть но́вые друзья́.","sub":"[oo mee-NYA yest NO-vy-ye droo-ZYA]","want":"У меня есть новые друзья"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Week two is done. Ле́на pours tea at her café and inspects Emma's progress. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, чай?","ru":"Emma, tea? (чай = tea)"},{"who":"A","en":"Да, спаси́бо! Я люблю́ чай.","ru":"Yes, thanks! I love tea."},{"who":"B","en":"Ты хорошо́ говори́шь по-ру́сски!","ru":"You speak Russian well!"},{"who":"A","en":"Спаси́бо. Я мно́го чита́ю и мно́го понима́ю.","ru":"Thanks. I read a lot and I understand a lot."},{"who":"B","en":"А что э́то?","ru":"And what's this?"},{"who":"A","en":"Э́то моя́ но́вая кни́га. Ру́сская кни́га!","ru":"This is my new book. A Russian book!"},{"who":"B","en":"Ты чита́ешь ру́сские кни́ги? Хорошо́!","ru":"You read Russian books? Well done!"},{"who":"A","en":"У меня́ есть хоро́шие друзья́. Они́ говоря́т по-ру́сски — и я говорю́!","ru":"I have good friends. They speak Russian — so I speak!"},{"who":"B","en":"А у меня́ есть но́вая подру́га — э́то ты!","ru":"And I have a new friend — it's you! (подру́га = a female friend)"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"No new words today — twelve of week two's best, back on the table. If a card feels soft, that's your homework.","items":[{"en":"знать","ru":"to know","ex":"[znat] — я зна́ю, ты зна́ешь"},{"en":"рабо́тать","ru":"to work","ex":"[ra-BO-tat] — я рабо́таю, она́ рабо́тает"},{"en":"чита́ть","ru":"to read","ex":"[chee-TAT] — я чита́ю кни́гу"},{"en":"понима́ть","ru":"to understand","ex":"[pa-nee-MAT] — а́канье in action: о sounds like a"},{"en":"говори́ть","ru":"to speak","ex":"[ga-va-REET] — я говорю́ по-ру́сски"},{"en":"люби́ть","ru":"to love","ex":"[lyoo-BEET] — я люблю́ (extra л!), ты лю́бишь"},{"en":"смотре́ть","ru":"to watch","ex":"[sma-TRYET] — я смотрю́ фильм"},{"en":"кни́га","ru":"book","ex":"[KNEE-ga] — чита́ю кни́гу, нет кни́ги, кни́ги (plural)"},{"en":"друг / друзья́","ru":"friend / friends","ex":"[drook / droo-ZYA] — the famous irregular plural"},{"en":"но́вый","ru":"new","ex":"[NO-vy] — но́вая маши́на, но́вые кни́ги"},{"en":"хоро́ший","ru":"good","ex":"[kha-RO-shy] — хоро́шие: ши takes и"},{"en":"у меня́ есть / у меня́ нет","ru":"I have / I don't have","ex":"[oo mee-NYA yest / nyet] — нет wants the genitive: нет ка́рты"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning on the right — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Я чита́ю кни́гу.","I'm reading a book."],["У меня́ нет вре́мени.","I have no time."],["но́вая маши́на","a new car"],["Они́ говоря́т по-ру́сски.","They speak Russian."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Eight questions — the whole of week two on one tray. If one bites, the rule above is one scroll away.","items":[{"q":"«Ты ___ по-ру́сски?» — asks Ива́н. Fill it in:","opts":["говори́шь","говори́т","говорю́"],"answer":0},{"q":"“I'm reading a book” is:","opts":["Я чита́ю кни́га.","Я чита́ю кни́гу.","Я чита́ешь кни́гу."],"answer":1},{"q":"You have no map. In Russian:","opts":["У меня́ нет ка́рта.","У меня́ есть ка́рта.","У меня́ нет ка́рты."],"answer":2},{"q":"Pick the correct pair:","opts":["но́вый кни́га","но́вая кни́га","но́вое кни́га"],"answer":1},{"q":"The plural of кни́га is…","opts":["кни́гы","кни́ги"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Он ___ фильм.» (He is watching a film.)","opts":["смотрю́","смо́тришь","смо́трит"],"answer":2},{"q":"“I love Moscow”:","opts":["Я люблю́ Москва́.","Я люблю́ Москву́.","Я лю́бишь Москву́."],"answer":1},{"q":"Друг in the plural:","opts":["друзья́","дру́ги","дру́гы"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's week-two postcard — six sentences, every one of them yours. Read it aloud.","title":"Я говорю́ по-ру́сски!","sentences":["Я Э́мма. Я студе́нтка.","Я мно́го чита́ю и мно́го понима́ю.","У меня́ есть но́вые кни́ги и но́вые друзья́.","Я люблю́ Москву́.","Ива́н говори́т: «Э́мма, ты хорошо́ говори́шь по-ру́сски!»","Спаси́бо, Москва́!"],"translation":"I'm Emma. I'm a student. I read a lot and I understand a lot. I have new books and new friends. I love Moscow. Ivan says: “Emma, you speak Russian well!” Thank you, Moscow!"},"essay":{"intro":"Your victory lap, in writing.","prompt":"Write 3–4 sentences about your week: one thing you do (a verb), one thing you have (у меня́ есть…), one thing you love (я люблю́…) — and make at least one adjective agree.","hint":"Lean on the frames you own: Я чита́ю / рабо́таю… · У меня́ есть но́вый / но́вая… · Я люблю́… — and if what you love ends in -а, bend it to -у 🙂","example":"Я мно́го рабо́таю и чита́ю. У меня́ есть но́вая кни́га. Я люблю́ ко́фе и Москву́. Мои́ но́вые друзья́ о́чень хоро́шие."}},"15":{"day":15,"week":"03","themeRu":"Where?","themeEn":"Где ты?","intro":"Emma's phone buzzes: «Ты где?» — two words, and she suddenly realises she can't answer. Today you unlock the answer: the <b>prepositional case</b>, Russian's way of saying where anything is. One tiny ending, two tiny prepositions — and the whole map of Moscow opens up.","introAudio":"Emma's phone buzzes: ty gdye? — where are you? Two words, and she suddenly realises she can't answer. Today you unlock the answer: the prepositional case, Russian's way of saying where anything is. One tiny ending, two tiny prepositions — and the whole map of Moscow opens up.","goals":["Ask <b>Где?</b> — where? — and understand the answer","Say where things and people are: <b>в Москве́, в па́рке</b> — the prepositional case","Choose between <b>в</b> and <b>на</b> — and memorise the stubborn <b>на рабо́те</b>","Say you're home: <b>Я до́ма</b> — one word, no preposition"],"learned":["Asked <b>Где?</b> and understood the answers","Said where things and people are: <b>в Москве́, в па́рке</b> — the prepositional case","Chose between <b>в</b> and <b>на</b> — and memorised <b>на рабо́те</b>","Said you're home: <b>Я до́ма</b> — one word, no preposition"],"review":{"intro":"Warm hands first: three throwbacks before the new case walks in.","introAudio":"Warm hands first: three throwbacks before the new case walks in.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the right answer. Yesterday's tea is still warm — this should feel easy.","q":"«Я люблю́ ___» — you love Moscow. Which form?","opts":["Москва́","Москву́","Москвы́"],"answer":1,"explain":"The object of люблю́ takes the accusative: <b>Москву́</b>. Москва́ is the naming form, Москвы́ is the after-нет form."},{"type":"choice","q":"Pick the pair that agrees:","opts":["но́вый окно́","но́вая окно́","но́вое окно́"],"answer":2,"explain":"Окно́ ends in -о — neuter, «оно́». Its adjective wears -ое: <b>но́вое окно́</b>."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“She is watching a new film.”</b>","want":"Она смотрит новый фильм","answer":"Она́ смо́трит но́вый фильм"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The question","title":"<em>Где?</em> — where is everything?","paras":["<b>Где</b> [gdye] is “where”. Russian happily drops it anywhere in the sentence: <b>Где Ива́н?</b> and <b>Ты где?</b> are both perfectly normal. And no “is”, as always — Где Ле́на? is literally “Where Lena?”","The answer needs two things where English needs one: a preposition (<b>в</b> — in, <b>на</b> — on/at) <em>plus</em> a new ending on the place word. English says “in Moscow” and leaves Moscow alone; Russian says <b>в Москве́</b> — the place itself dresses for the occasion.","That costume is the <b>prepositional case</b> — the only Russian case that never appears without a preposition. You already own three case ideas: nominative to name, accusative to aim, genitive after нет. This is number four — and it's the friendliest of them all."],"audio":"Gdye is where. Russian happily drops it anywhere in the sentence: gdye Ivan? ty gdye? — both are normal. And no is, as always. The answer needs two things where English needs one: a preposition — v, in, or na, on — plus a new ending on the place word. English says in Moscow and leaves Moscow alone; Russian says v Maskvye — the place itself dresses for the occasion. That costume is the prepositional case — the only Russian case that never appears without a preposition, and the friendliest of them all.","examples":[{"ru":"Where is Ivan?","en":"<b>Где</b> Ива́н?","say":"Где Иван?"},{"ru":"Where are you? (texting a friend)","en":"Ты <b>где</b>?","say":"Ты где?","gloss":"word order relaxes — both orders are fine"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the situation and tap the phrase that fits it.","q":"Your phone rings. Ива́н wants to know where you are. His two words:","opts":["Ты где?","Ты кто?","Что э́то?"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Ты где?</b> — “where are you?”. Ты кто? asks who you are, Что э́то? asks what something is."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Where is my book?”</b>","want":"Где моя книга","answer":"Где моя́ кни́га"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The -е ending","title":"в Москве́, в па́рке — the place changes its ending","paras":["The recipe is one line: <b>swap the ending for -е</b>. Feminine -а steps aside: Москва́ → <b>в Москве́</b>, шко́ла → <b>в шко́ле</b>. Masculine words simply add it: парк → <b>в па́рке</b>, о́фис → <b>в о́фисе</b>. (A few word-shapes take -и instead — you'll meet them later; everything this week ends in -е.)","Watch the stress in в Москве́ — it stays out on the ending: [v mask-VYE]. Say it aloud once.","And some guests never change clothes: foreign words ending in -о́ or -е́ — кафе́, метро́, такси́ — stay exactly as they are: <b>в кафе́, в метро́</b>. The easiest members of the club."],"audio":"The recipe is one line: swap the ending for ye. Feminine a steps aside: Maskva — v Maskvye; shkola — v shkolye. Masculine words simply add it: park — v parkye; ofis — v ofisye. A few word-shapes take a different ending — you'll meet them later; everything this week ends in ye. Watch the stress: it stays out on the ending in v Maskvye. And some guests never change clothes: foreign words like kafe, metro, taxi stay exactly as they are.","table":{"rows":[["Москва́ → в Москве́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Москва — в Москве\">🔊</button>","Moscow → in Moscow"],["шко́ла → в шко́ле <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"школа — в школе\">🔊</button>","school → at school"],["парк → в па́рке <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"парк — в парке\">🔊</button>","park → in the park"],["о́фис → в о́фисе <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"офис — в офисе\">🔊</button>","office → in the office"],["кафе́ → в кафе́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"кафе — в кафе\">🔊</button>","café → in the café (no change)"]],"star":0},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я в Москва́.","right":"Я в Москве́.","why":"After в the place itself must change: -а → -е. English leaves “Moscow” alone; Russian never does."}],"examples":[{"ru":"Emma is in the park.","en":"Э́мма <b>в па́рке</b>.","say":"Эмма в парке.","gloss":"no “is” — name, в, place"},{"ru":"Ivan is in the metro.","en":"Ива́н <b>в метро́</b>.","say":"Иван в метро.","gloss":"метро́ never changes"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Ле́на's little nephew is at school. In Russian:","opts":["Он в шко́ла.","Он в шко́ле.","Он шко́ла."],"answer":1,"explain":"Place after в → ending -е: <b>в шко́ле</b>. «Он шко́ла» would mean “he is a school” — a career move nobody wants."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — no stress marks needed.","q":"Type: “I am in Moscow.”","accept":["я в москве","ya v moskve","ya v maskvye","ya v moskvye"],"placeholder":"Я в …","explain":"Я в Москве́ — three words, one new ending. Москва́ dressed for the occasion."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · в or на?","title":"<em>в</em> the box, <em>на</em> the surface — mostly","paras":["<b>в</b> means inside something with walls: в до́ме, в о́фисе, в кафе́. <b>на</b> means on a surface or in an open space: <b>на столе́</b> (on the table), <b>на у́лице</b> (in the street — Russians see the street as an open surface).","But a stubborn list of words takes <b>на</b> for no visible reason — and the star of that list is рабо́та: <b>на рабо́те</b>, never «в рабо́те». English “at” maps to both в and на, so it won't warn you. Don't hunt for logic; Russians memorised these as children, and you'll memorise them in a week.","The practical habit: learn each new place word together with its preposition, like a first name and surname — на рабо́те, в шко́ле, в па́рке."],"audio":"V means inside something with walls: v domye, v ofisye, v kafe. Na means on a surface or in an open space: na stolye — on the table; na oolitse — in the street: Russians see the street as an open surface. But a stubborn list of words takes na for no visible reason — and the star of that list is rabota, work: na rabotye, never v rabotye. English at maps to both, so it won't warn you. The practical habit: learn each new place word together with its preposition, like a first name and surname.","mistakes":[{"wrong":"Ива́н в рабо́те.","right":"Ива́н на рабо́те.","why":"Рабо́та is on the memorise-me list: always <b>на рабо́те</b>. English “at work” gives you no hint — this one is pure memory."}],"examples":[{"ru":"Lena is at work.","en":"Ле́на <b>на рабо́те</b>.","say":"Лена на работе."},{"ru":"The book is on the table.","en":"Кни́га <b>на столе́</b>.","say":"Книга на столе.","gloss":"surface → на"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Ле́на is at work — her café shift. In Russian:","opts":["Ле́на в рабо́те.","Ле́на на рабо́те."],"answer":1,"explain":"Рабо́та takes <b>на</b> — no logic, just the list. Say на рабо́те out loud three times and it's yours."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“The book is on the table.”</b>","want":"Книга на столе","answer":"Кни́га на столе́"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Home","title":"<em>Я до́ма</em> — home needs no preposition","paras":["“Home” is special in Russian, just as it is in English. English says “I'm home” with no “at” — Russian has one dedicated word: <b>до́ма</b>. No в, no на, no ending: <b>Я до́ма. Ты до́ма?</b>","Keep the pair apart: <b>дом</b> is the building — a house; <b>до́ма</b> is the state of being home. Э́то мой дом — this is my house. Я до́ма — I'm home.","And now your answer to «Ты где?» can be anything: Я до́ма. Я в па́рке. Я на рабо́те. Я в метро́. Emma's texting life has officially begun."],"audio":"Home is special in Russian, just as it is in English. English says I'm home with no at — Russian has one dedicated word: doma. No preposition, no ending: ya doma — I'm home. Keep the pair apart: dom is the building, a house; doma is the state of being home. And now your answer to ty gdye can be anything: ya doma, ya v parkye, ya na rabotye, ya v metro.","mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я в до́ма.","right":"Я до́ма.","why":"До́ма already carries the whole “at home” idea — it's a ready adverb. No preposition, no ending."}],"examples":[{"ru":"I'm home.","en":"Я <b>до́ма</b>.","say":"Я дома."},{"ru":"Is Lena home? — No, she's at work.","en":"Ле́на до́ма? — Нет, она́ <b>на рабо́те</b>.","say":"Лена дома? — Нет, она на работе."}],"tasks":[{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say the phrase out loud. Listen to the sample first if you like.","target":"Я до́ма.","sub":"[ya DO-ma] — stress at the front","want":"Я дома"},{"type":"choice","q":"Ива́н texts: «Ты где?» You're at home. Reply:","opts":["Я дом.","Я в до́ме.","Я до́ма."],"answer":2,"explain":"Home = <b>до́ма</b>, no preposition. «Я в до́ме» sounds like you're inside some random building, and «Я дом» claims you ARE one."},{"type":"type","q":"«Где Э́мма?» — she's in the park. Type the answer:","accept":["эмма в парке","она в парке","в парке","emma v parke","ona v parke"],"placeholder":"Она́ …","explain":"Она́ в па́рке — в plus the -е ending, and no “is” anywhere."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Evening. Emma's phone rings — it's Ива́н, and for the first time she can answer every where-question. Tap a line to listen.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Приве́т! Ты где?","ru":"Hi! Where are you?"},{"who":"A","en":"Приве́т! Я в кафе́, в це́нтре.","ru":"Hi! I'm in a café, in the centre."},{"who":"B","en":"А где Ле́на? Она́ до́ма?","ru":"And where's Lena? Is she home?"},{"who":"A","en":"Нет, она́ на рабо́те.","ru":"No, she's at work."},{"who":"B","en":"А я в метро́. Где кафе́?","ru":"And I'm in the metro. Where's the café?"},{"who":"A","en":"На у́лице Арба́т.","ru":"In Arbat Street. (Арба́т — Moscow's famous walking street)"},{"who":"B","en":"О, я зна́ю, где э́то!","ru":"Oh, I know where that is!"},{"who":"A","en":"Хорошо́. Чай на столе́!","ru":"Good. The tea is on the table!"},{"who":"B","en":"Пока́!","ru":"See you!"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve places to be. Each card comes with its preposition — learn them as a pair, like a first name and surname.","items":[{"en":"где","ru":"where","ex":"[gdye] — Где Ива́н? Ты где?"},{"en":"дом","ru":"house, home (the building)","ex":"[dom] — э́то мой дом"},{"en":"до́ма","ru":"at home","ex":"[DO-ma] — я до́ма: no preposition needed"},{"en":"го́род","ru":"city","ex":"[GO-rat] — в го́роде; the final д sounds like т"},{"en":"у́лица","ru":"street","ex":"[OO-lee-tsa] — на у́лице: streets take на"},{"en":"шко́ла","ru":"school","ex":"[SHKO-la] — в шко́ле"},{"en":"университе́т","ru":"university","ex":"[oo-nee-veer-see-TYET] — в университе́те"},{"en":"рабо́та","ru":"work, job","ex":"[ra-BO-ta] — на рабо́те, never «в рабо́те»"},{"en":"о́фис","ru":"office","ex":"[O-fees] — в о́фисе… but на рабо́те!"},{"en":"центр","ru":"centre, downtown","ex":"[tsentr] — в це́нтре"},{"en":"теа́тр","ru":"theatre","ex":"[tee-ATR] — в теа́тре"},{"en":"рестора́н","ru":"restaurant","ex":"[ree-sta-RAN] — в рестора́не"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning on the right — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Я в Москве́.","I'm in Moscow."],["Ле́на на рабо́те.","Lena is at work."],["Я до́ма.","I'm home."],["Кни́га на столе́.","The book is on the table."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — today's case and nothing else. One scroll up if anything bites.","items":[{"q":"«Я в ___» — you're in Moscow:","opts":["Москва́","Москве́","Москву́"],"answer":1},{"q":"Ива́н is at work. In Russian:","opts":["Ива́н в рабо́те.","Ива́н на рабо́та.","Ива́н на рабо́те."],"answer":2},{"q":"You're at home. Your text reads:","opts":["Я до́ма.","Я в до́ма.","Я дом."],"answer":0},{"q":"Which place word never changes its form?","opts":["шко́ла","метро́","парк"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Где кни́га?» — it's in the office:","opts":["Кни́га в о́фис.","Кни́га в о́фисе.","Кни́га о́фис."],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"A tiny where-is-everyone snapshot. Read it aloud — and answer the last question yourself.","title":"Где все?","sentences":["Э́то Москва́.","Э́мма в це́нтре, в кафе́.","Ива́н в метро́.","Ле́на на рабо́те.","Чай на столе́.","А где ты?"],"translation":"This is Moscow. Emma is in the centre, in a café. Ivan is in the metro. Lena is at work. The tea is on the table. And where are you? (все = everyone)"},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to put your world on the map.","prompt":"Write 3–4 sentences: where you are right now, where one person you know is, and where one thing of yours is. Try to use в, на and до́ма at least once each.","hint":"Frames: Я в/на …е · Он до́ма / Она́ на рабо́те · Мой/Моя́ … на столе́. Remember the stubborn one: рабо́та takes на 🙂","example":"Я до́ма. Мой друг Ива́н в университе́те. Моя́ кни́га на столе́. Ка́рта то́же на столе́."}},"16":{"day":16,"week":"03","themeRu":"I like it","themeEn":"Мне нра́вится","intro":"Yesterday you put Moscow on the map — today you fall in love with it, grammatically. English says “I like the park”: you are the subject. Russian turns the telescope around: <b>Мне нра́вится парк</b> — “to me, the park is pleasing”. The park does the verb-work; you just receive the pleasure. Strange for one morning, natural for life.","introAudio":"Yesterday you put Moscow on the map — today you fall in love with it, grammatically. English says I like the park: you are the subject. Russian turns the telescope around: mnye NRA-veet-sa park — to me, the park is pleasing. The park does the verb-work; you just receive the pleasure. Strange for one morning, natural for life.","goals":["Say what you like: <b>Мне нра́вится Москва́</b>","Talk about others' taste: <b>тебе́, ему́, ей</b> — to you, to him, to her","Switch to <b>нра́вятся</b> for several things: <b>Мне нра́вятся па́рки</b>","Keep <b>я люблю́</b> and <b>мне нра́вится</b> on their separate rails"],"learned":["Said what you like: <b>Мне нра́вится Москва́</b>","Talked about others' taste with <b>тебе́, ему́, ей</b>","Switched to <b>нра́вятся</b> for several things: <b>Мне нра́вятся па́рки</b>","Kept <b>я люблю́ + accusative</b> and <b>мне нра́вится + nominative</b> on their separate rails"],"review":{"intro":"Three quick ones — yesterday's places, plus two old friends that never really leave.","introAudio":"Three quick ones — yesterday's places, plus two old friends that never really leave.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the right answer. Wrong taps teach too.","q":"«Ива́н в ___.» (Ivan is in the park.)","opts":["парк","па́рке","па́рку"],"answer":1,"explain":"Place after в → the -е ending: <b>в па́рке</b>. Yesterday's case, today's warm-up."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — no stress marks needed.","q":"Type: “I'm home.”","accept":["я дома","ya doma"],"placeholder":"Я …"},{"type":"choice","q":"«Я смотрю́ ___ фильм.» — pick the agreeing adjective:","opts":["но́вая","но́вое","но́вый"],"answer":2,"explain":"Фильм is masculine, and masculine things don't change as objects: <b>но́вый фильм</b>."}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The flip","title":"<em>Мне нра́вится парк</em> — the park pleases me","paras":["Here is the flip in slow motion. English: <em>I like the park</em> — “I” is the boss of the sentence. Russian: <b>Мне нра́вится парк</b> — literally “to-me is-pleasing the-park”. The boss is <b>парк</b>: it stands in the nominative, the naming case, and the verb belongs to it.","<b>Мне</b> is “to me” — you've already met it in <b>Мне два́дцать лет</b>. Same word, same job: the receiver of the situation.","So to like something: park the liked thing in its dictionary form, put yourself in the dative. <b>Мне нра́вится Москва́. Мне нра́вится метро́.</b>"],"audio":"Here is the flip in slow motion. English: I like the park — I is the boss of the sentence. Russian: mnye nravitsa park — literally, to me is pleasing the park. The boss is the park: it stands in the nominative, the naming case, and the verb belongs to it. Mnye is to me — you've already met it in mnye dvadtsat lyet. Same word, same job: the receiver of the situation. So to like something: park the liked thing in its dictionary form, put yourself in the dative.","mnemonic":"Think “<b>to me</b> it's pleasing” — English even keeps the old echo: “it pleases me”. Мне = to me; the liked thing = the subject.","mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я нра́вится парк.","right":"Мне нра́вится парк.","why":"The liker is not the subject — the liked thing is. You go in the dative: <b>мне</b>."}],"examples":[{"ru":"I like Moscow.","en":"<b>Мне нра́вится</b> Москва́.","say":"Мне нравится Москва.","gloss":"Москва́ stays nominative — she is the subject"},{"ru":"I like the metro. (the Moscow metro deserves it)","en":"<b>Мне нра́вится</b> метро́.","say":"Мне нравится метро."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the sentence that's built correctly.","q":"“I like the park” in Russian:","opts":["Я нра́вится парк.","Мне нра́вится парк.","Мне нра́вится па́рке."],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Мне</b> (to me) + <b>нра́вится</b> + <b>парк</b> in its dictionary form. Па́рке is yesterday's place-ending — it has no business here."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“I like Moscow.”</b>","want":"Мне нравится Москва","answer":"Мне нра́вится Москва́"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · To you, to him, to her","title":"тебе́, ему́, ей — sharing the liking","paras":["Swap мне for another receiver and the same frame carries anyone's taste: <b>Тебе́ нра́вится Москва́?</b> — do you like Moscow? <b>Ему́ нра́вится метро́</b> — he likes the metro. <b>Ей нра́вится му́зыка</b> — she likes music.","These are the dative pronouns — the “to-” family: <b>мне, тебе́, ему́, ей</b>. You already use two of them for age: Мне два́дцать лет. Ско́лько тебе́ лет? Age and liking live in the same case.","Asking costs nothing — no “do”, as ever: <b>Тебе́ нра́вится?</b> with the melody going up."],"audio":"Swap mnye for another receiver and the same frame carries anyone's taste. Tebye nravitsa Maskva? — do you like Moscow? Yemoo nravitsa metro — he likes the metro. Yey nravitsa moozyka — she likes music. These are the dative pronouns, the to-family: mnye, tebye, yemoo, yey. You already use two of them for age. And asking costs nothing — no do, as ever: tebye nravitsa? — with the melody going up.","table":{"rows":[["мне <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мне\">🔊</button>","to me"],["тебе́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"тебе\">🔊</button>","to you (a friend)"],["ему́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ему\">🔊</button>","to him"],["ей <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ей\">🔊</button>","to her"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Do you like Moscow? — Yes, very much!","en":"<b>Тебе́</b> нра́вится Москва́? — Да, о́чень!","say":"Тебе нравится Москва? — Да, очень!"},{"ru":"He likes the film, she likes the music.","en":"<b>Ему́</b> нра́вится фильм, <b>ей</b> нра́вится му́зыка.","say":"Ему нравится фильм, ей нравится музыка."}],"tasks":[{"type":"build","q":"Build the question: <b>“Do you like Moscow?”</b> (to a friend)","want":"Тебе нравится Москва","answer":"Тебе́ нра́вится Москва́"},{"type":"type","q":"Type: “She likes music.”","accept":["ей нравится музыка","yey nravitsya muzyka","ei nravitsya muzyka","ey nravitsya muzyka"],"placeholder":"Ей …","explain":"Ей нра́вится му́зыка — she receives (ей), му́зыка is the subject."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · One or many","title":"нра́вится / нра́вятся — the verb counts the liked things","paras":["Since the liked thing is the subject, the verb obeys <em>it</em> — not you. One thing: <b>нра́вится</b>. Several things: <b>нра́вятся</b>. Мне нра́вится парк → <b>Мне нра́вятся па́рки</b>.","In fast speech the two sound nearly identical, and Russians lean on the noun to tell them apart. In writing the difference matters: <b>-ится</b> for one, <b>-ятся</b> for many.","This is the trap of the day: English “I like” never changes, so your instinct whispers that the verb is about you. It isn't. Count the liked things, conjugate for them."],"audio":"Since the liked thing is the subject, the verb obeys it — not you. One thing: nravitsa. Several things: nravyatsa. Mnye nravitsa park — mnye nravyatsa parki. In fast speech the two sound nearly identical, and Russians lean on the noun. In writing the difference matters. This is the trap of the day: English I like never changes, so your instinct whispers that the verb is about you. It isn't. Count the liked things, conjugate for them.","mistakes":[{"wrong":"Мне нра́вится сы́рники.","right":"Мне нра́вятся сы́рники.","why":"Сы́рники is plural — the verb follows the cheese pancakes, not you. <em>They</em> please me: нра́вятся."}],"examples":[{"ru":"I like Moscow's parks.","en":"Мне <b>нра́вятся</b> па́рки.","say":"Мне нравятся парки."},{"ru":"I like sirniki — the little cheese pancakes.","en":"Мне <b>нра́вятся</b> сы́рники.","say":"Мне нравятся сырники.","gloss":"[SYR-nee-kee] — Russia's breakfast gold"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Мне ___ ста́нции метро́.» (I like the metro stations.)","opts":["нра́вится","нра́вятся"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ста́нции is plural → <b>нра́вятся</b>. The verb counts the stations, not you."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I like blini.”</b>","want":"Мне нравятся блины","answer":"Мне нра́вятся блины́"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud. Listen to the sample first if you like.","target":"Мне нра́вятся сы́рники.","sub":"[mnye NRA-vyat-sa SYR-nee-kee]","want":"Мне нравятся сырники"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Love vs like","title":"<em>я люблю́</em> aims, <em>мне нра́вится</em> receives","paras":["You now own two ways to be positive, and they run on different rails. <b>Я люблю́ + accusative</b> — you act on the thing: Я люблю́ <b>Москву́</b> (Москва́ bends to -у). <b>Мне нра́вится + nominative</b> — the thing acts on you: Мне нра́вится <b>Москва́</b> (she stays as she is).","Meaning-wise, люблю́ is the strong word — love, passion, always. Нра́вится is lighter — it pleases me. A Russian says нра́вится about a film after one evening, and люблю́ about their city after a lifetime.","The classic mix-up is crossing the rails: «Мне нра́вится Москву́» drags the accusative onto the wrong train. If мне opens the sentence, the thing stays nominative."],"audio":"You now own two ways to be positive, and they run on different rails. Ya lyublyu plus the accusative — you act on the thing: ya lyublyu Moskvu. Mnye nravitsa plus the nominative — the thing acts on you: mnye nravitsa Maskva. Meaning-wise, lyublyu is the strong word — love. Nravitsa is lighter — it pleases me. The classic mix-up is crossing the rails and dragging the accusative into the nravitsa sentence. If mnye opens the sentence, the thing stays nominative.","mistakes":[{"wrong":"Мне нра́вится Москву́.","right":"Мне нра́вится Москва́.","why":"After мне нра́вится the liked thing is the subject — nominative. Save Москву́ for я люблю́ Москву́."}],"examples":[{"ru":"I love Moscow — and I like the parks.","en":"Я люблю́ <b>Москву́</b> — и мне нра́вятся <b>па́рки</b>.","say":"Я люблю Москву — и мне нравятся парки.","gloss":"two rails in one sentence"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Pick the sentence where everything is correct:","opts":["Мне нра́вится Москву́.","Я люблю́ Москва́.","Мне нра́вится Москва́."],"answer":2,"explain":"Нра́вится keeps the nominative: <b>Мне нра́вится Москва́</b>. The other two cross the rails in opposite directions."},{"type":"choice","q":"You've adored tea your whole life. The stronger, truer sentence:","opts":["Я люблю́ чай.","Мне нра́вится чай."],"answer":0,"explain":"Both are correct Russian — but a lifetime of devotion is <b>люблю́</b>. Нра́вится is for a pleasant first impression."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Sunday. Ива́н shows Emma парк Го́рького — Gorky Park, pronounced [GOR-ka-va]: that -ого says “va”. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, тебе́ нра́вится Москва́?","ru":"Emma, do you like Moscow?"},{"who":"A","en":"О́чень! Мне нра́вятся па́рки и метро́.","ru":"Very much! I like the parks and the metro."},{"who":"B","en":"Метро́? Ста́нции?","ru":"The metro? The stations?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да! Ста́нции — как теа́тр.","ru":"Yes! The stations are like a theatre. (как = like)"},{"who":"B","en":"А сы́рники? Тебе́ нра́вятся сы́рники?","ru":"And sirniki? Do you like sirniki?"},{"who":"A","en":"Нет. Я люблю́ сы́рники!","ru":"No. I LOVE sirniki!"},{"who":"B","en":"Ха-ха! Ле́на на рабо́те. Сы́рники — в кафе́.","ru":"Ha ha! Lena's at work. The sirniki are at the café."},{"who":"A","en":"Ива́н, я люблю́ Москву́!","ru":"Ivan, I love Moscow!"},{"who":"B","en":"Я зна́ю.","ru":"I know."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve cards: the to-family, and things worth liking. Tap to flip, tap 🔊 to hear.","items":[{"en":"мне нра́вится","ru":"I like (one thing)","ex":"[mnye NRA-veet-sa] — мне нра́вится парк"},{"en":"мне нра́вятся","ru":"I like (several things)","ex":"[mnye NRA-vyat-sa] — мне нра́вятся па́рки"},{"en":"тебе́","ru":"to you (a friend)","ex":"[tee-BYE] — тебе́ нра́вится?"},{"en":"ему́","ru":"to him","ex":"[yee-MOO] — ему́ нра́вится фильм"},{"en":"ей","ru":"to her","ex":"[yey] — ей нра́вится му́зыка"},{"en":"сы́рники","ru":"sirniki (cheese pancakes)","ex":"[SYR-nee-kee] — that deep ы from the alphabet course"},{"en":"блины́","ru":"blini, thin pancakes","ex":"[blee-NY] — мне нра́вятся блины́"},{"en":"чай","ru":"tea","ex":"[chai] — я люблю́ чай"},{"en":"ста́нция","ru":"station","ex":"[STAN-tsee-ya] — ста́нция метро́"},{"en":"му́зыка","ru":"music","ex":"[MOO-zy-ka] — stress at the front"},{"en":"спорт","ru":"sport","ex":"[sport] — ему́ нра́вится спорт"},{"en":"язы́к","ru":"language; tongue","ex":"[yee-ZYK] — ру́сский язы́к"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning on the right — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Мне нра́вится Москва́.","I like Moscow."],["Мне нра́вятся па́рки.","I like the parks."],["Ей нра́вится му́зыка.","She likes music."],["Я люблю́ сы́рники.","I love sirniki."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions on the day's flip. The verb follows the liked thing — hold that and you're through.","items":[{"q":"“I like the metro”:","opts":["Я нра́вится метро́.","Мне нра́вится метро́.","Мне нра́вятся метро́."],"answer":1},{"q":"«Мне ___ блины́.» Fill in:","opts":["нра́вится","нра́вятся"],"answer":1},{"q":"“He likes sport”:","opts":["Ему́ нра́вится спорт.","Он нра́вится спорт.","Ему́ нра́вятся спорт."],"answer":0},{"q":"Which is correct?","opts":["Я люблю́ Москва́.","Мне нра́вится Москву́.","Я люблю́ Москву́."],"answer":2},{"q":"Ле́на tells you about her brother: «___ нра́вится му́зыка.» (He likes music.)","opts":["Ему́","Ей","Тебе́"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's love-list, two weeks in. Read it aloud — with feeling.","title":"Что мне нра́вится","sentences":["Мне о́чень нра́вится Москва́.","Мне нра́вятся па́рки и ста́нции метро́.","Ста́нция «Маяко́вская» — как теа́тр.","Я люблю́ сы́рники и чай.","И мне нра́вится ру́сский язы́к!"],"translation":"I really like Moscow. I like the parks and the metro stations. Mayakovskaya station is like a theatre. I love sirniki and tea. And I like the Russian language!"},"essay":{"intro":"Now your list.","prompt":"Write 3–4 sentences about what you like: one thing (мне нра́вится…), several things (мне нра́вятся…), and one thing you truly love (я люблю́ + accusative!).","hint":"The liked thing stays nominative after нра́вится — but after люблю́, a feminine -а bends to -у. Two rails, don't cross them 🙂","example":"Мне нра́вится ру́сский язы́к. Мне нра́вятся па́рки и му́зыка. Я люблю́ чай и блины́. И я о́чень люблю́ Москву́!"}},"17":{"day":17,"week":"03","themeRu":"Days and time","themeEn":"Дни и вре́мя","intro":"Ле́на wants Emma at the café on Saturday, Ива́н votes for the park on Sunday — and Emma realises she can't name a single day of the week. Today we fix that: seven days, the clock question <b>Кото́рый час?</b>, and the little trick English never warns you about — “on Monday” is <b>в</b> plus the accusative.","introAudio":"Lena wants Emma at the café on Saturday, Ivan votes for the park on Sunday — and Emma realises she can't name a single day of the week. Today we fix that: seven days, the clock question katory chas, and the little trick English never warns you about — on Monday is v plus the accusative.","goals":["Name all seven days — no capital letters needed","Say when: <b>в понеде́льник, в сре́ду, в суббо́ту</b> — в + accusative","Ask and tell the time: <b>Кото́рый час? — Сейча́с два часа́</b>","Plan a week out loud with <b>сего́дня</b> and <b>за́втра</b>"],"learned":["Named all seven days — no capital letters needed","Said when: <b>в понеде́льник, в сре́ду, в суббо́ту</b> — в + accusative","Asked and told the time: <b>Кото́рый час? — Сейча́с два часа́</b>","Planned a week out loud with <b>сего́дня</b> and <b>за́втра</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Three quick ones before the calendar opens — liking, places, and the language you're falling for.","introAudio":"Three quick ones before the calendar opens — liking, places, and the language you're falling for.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the right answer. Small stumbles now save big ones later.","q":"Ива́н tells you about Ле́на: «___ нра́вятся блины́.» (She likes blini.)","opts":["Ей","Ему́","Тебе́"],"answer":0,"explain":"She receives the liking: <b>ей</b>. Ему́ is “to him”, тебе́ is “to you”."},{"type":"choice","q":"Where's Emma? At the university:","opts":["Э́мма в университе́т.","Э́мма в университе́те.","Э́мма на университе́те."],"answer":1,"explain":"Place → в + the -е ending: <b>в университе́те</b>. And университе́т isn't on the stubborn на-list."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“I like the Russian language.”</b>","want":"Мне нравится русский язык","answer":"Мне нра́вится ру́сский язы́к"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Seven days","title":"From <em>понеде́льник</em> to <em>воскресе́нье</em>","paras":["Here's the week, Monday first — Russian calendars start there. Small pleasure: day names never take a capital letter unless they open the sentence.","Several names carry their own logic: <b>вто́рник</b> hides “second”, <b>четве́рг</b> — four, <b>пя́тница</b> — пять, five. <b>Среда́</b> is the “middle” of the week. Listen for the а́канье everywhere: понеде́льник opens with [pa-], not “po-”.","The weekend pair: <b>суббо́та</b> [soo-BO-ta] — “sabbath” in Russian clothing — and <b>воскресе́нье</b> [vas-kree-SYEN-ye], which literally means “resurrection”. Sunday kept its church name."],"audio":"Here's the week, Monday first — Russian calendars start there, and day names never take a capital letter. Several names carry their own logic: ftornik hides the word second, chetvyerk — four, pyatnitsa — pyat, five. Sreda is the middle of the week. Listen for the akanye everywhere: panedyelnik opens with pa. The weekend pair: soobbota — sabbath in Russian clothing — and vaskresyenye, which literally means resurrection. Sunday kept its church name.","table":{"rows":[["понеде́льник <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"понедельник\">🔊</button>","Monday"],["вто́рник <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вторник\">🔊</button>","Tuesday"],["среда́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"среда\">🔊</button>","Wednesday"],["четве́рг <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"четверг\">🔊</button>","Thursday"],["пя́тница <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пятница\">🔊</button>","Friday"]],"star":4},"examples":[{"ru":"Today is Monday. (sigh)","en":"Сего́дня <b>понеде́льник</b>.","say":"Сегодня понедельник.","gloss":"сего́дня = today; the г says [v]: [see-VOD-nya]"},{"ru":"Tomorrow is Saturday!","en":"За́втра <b>суббо́та</b>!","say":"Завтра суббота!","gloss":"за́втра = tomorrow"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the right answer — the day names carry clues.","q":"Ле́на says: «Сего́дня пя́тница.» What day is it?","opts":["Wednesday","Friday","Sunday"],"answer":1,"explain":"Пя́тница hides <b>пять</b> — five — the fifth day. Friday."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian word. Plain letters are fine — no stress marks needed.","q":"Type the day that hides the number “two”:","accept":["вторник","vtornik"],"placeholder":"The second day…"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · On Monday","title":"<em>в сре́ду</em> — how days answer “when?”","paras":["English says <em>on</em> Monday; Russian says <b>в</b> + the day in the accusative. Masculine days show no visible change: <b>в понеде́льник, в четве́рг</b>. But the feminine -а days show the case you learned in week two: среда́ → <b>в сре́ду</b>, пя́тница → <b>в пя́тницу</b>, суббо́та → <b>в суббо́ту</b>. Notice среда́'s stress running home: в сре́ду.","One cosmetic detail: before вто́рник, в becomes <b>во</b> — <b>во вто́рник</b> — purely so your mouth survives the в-вт pileup.","Don't hunt for an “on”: it's the same little в you used for places, in a different case. A place takes the prepositional — в па́рке. A day takes the accusative — в сре́ду. One preposition, two jobs."],"audio":"English says on Monday; Russian says v plus the day in the accusative. Masculine days show no visible change: v panedyelnik, v chetvyerk. But the feminine a-days show the case you learned in week two: sreda becomes v sryedu — and the stress runs home. Pyatnitsa — v pyatnitsu. Soobbota — v soobbotu. One cosmetic detail: before ftornik, v becomes vo — va ftornik — purely so your mouth survives. And don't hunt for an on: same little v, different case. A place takes the prepositional; a day takes the accusative.","table":{"rows":[["в понеде́льник <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в понедельник\">🔊</button>","on Monday (no change)"],["во вто́рник <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"во вторник\">🔊</button>","on Tuesday (в → во)"],["в сре́ду <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в среду\">🔊</button>","on Wednesday (среда́ → сре́ду!)"],["в пя́тницу <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в пятницу\">🔊</button>","on Friday"],["в суббо́ту <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в субботу\">🔊</button>","on Saturday"],["в воскресе́нье <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в воскресенье\">🔊</button>","on Sunday (no change)"]],"star":2},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я рабо́таю в среда́.","right":"Я рабо́таю в сре́ду.","why":"A day after в goes to the accusative: -а → -у. And the stress walks back to the front: в сре́ду."},{"wrong":"На понеде́льник я в университе́те.","right":"В понеде́льник я в университе́те.","why":"English “on Monday” tempts you toward на — but days take <b>в</b>. There is no one-to-one “on” here."}],"examples":[{"ru":"On Saturday I'm at the café.","en":"<b>В суббо́ту</b> я в кафе́.","say":"В субботу я в кафе."},{"ru":"He works on Wednesday.","en":"Он рабо́тает <b>в сре́ду</b>.","say":"Он работает в среду."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“On Friday” in Russian:","opts":["в пя́тница","на пя́тницу","в пя́тницу"],"answer":2,"explain":"В + accusative: <b>в пя́тницу</b>. Пя́тница unbent is the calendar name; на is the English-shaped trap."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“On Saturday I'm home.”</b>","want":"В субботу я дома","answer":"В суббо́ту я до́ма"},{"type":"type","q":"Type: “on Tuesday” — watch the little о:","accept":["во вторник","vo vtornik"],"placeholder":"… вто́рник"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · What time is it?","title":"<em>Кото́рый час?</em> — the clock in round hours","paras":["The classic question is <b>Кото́рый час?</b> — literally “which hour?”. On the street you'll also hear the everyday <b>Ско́лько вре́мени?</b> — “how much time?” — the same ско́лько as in Ско́лько тебе́ лет, and вре́мени is the form you know from нет вре́мени. Both questions are fine; answer them the same way.","The answer opens with <b>сейча́с</b> (now) and a number — and час changes after numbers exactly like год: <b>час</b> alone for one o'clock, <b>часа́</b> after 2–4, <b>часо́в</b> after 5 and up. You met this dance in два́дцать лет.","So: Сейча́с час. Сейча́с три часа́. Сейча́с семь часо́в. Round hours are all you need this week — real Russians round too."],"audio":"The classic question is katory chas — literally, which hour. On the street you'll also hear the everyday skolka vryemeni — how much time. Both are fine; answer them the same way. The answer opens with seychas — now — and a number, and the word chas changes after numbers exactly like god in ages: chas alone for one o'clock, chasa after two to four, chasov after five and up. Round hours are all you need this week — real Russians round too.","table":{"rows":[["Кото́рый час? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Который час?\">🔊</button>","What time is it?"],["Сейча́с час. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Сейчас час.\">🔊</button>","It's one o'clock."],["Сейча́с два часа́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Сейчас два часа.\">🔊</button>","It's two o'clock."],["Сейча́с пять часо́в. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Сейчас пять часов.\">🔊</button>","It's five o'clock."]],"star":0},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Сейча́с пять часа́.","right":"Сейча́с пять часо́в.","why":"After 5 and up — <b>часо́в</b>, the same shelf as два́дцать лет. Часа́ belongs to 2–4 only."}],"examples":[{"ru":"What time is it? — It's ten.","en":"Кото́рый час? — Сейча́с де́сять <b>часо́в</b>.","say":"Который час? — Сейчас десять часов."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Сейча́с четы́ре ___.»","opts":["час","часа́","часо́в"],"answer":1,"explain":"2–4 take <b>часа́</b>: четы́ре часа́. Час alone means one o'clock; часо́в starts at five."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and ask the time out loud. Listen to the sample first if you like.","target":"Кото́рый час?","sub":"[ka-TO-ry chas]","want":"Который час"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Making plans","title":"Сего́дня, за́втра — and at what time","paras":["Two anchor words carry every plan: <b>сего́дня</b> [see-VOD-nya] — today (that г really says [v]) — and <b>за́втра</b> [ZAF-tra] — tomorrow. No case, no changes: Сего́дня я рабо́таю. За́втра я до́ма.","“At what time?” is <b>Во ско́лько?</b> The answer re-uses в with the hour: <b>в два часа́</b> — at two, <b>в семь часо́в</b> — at seven.","Now watch a full plan snap together from parts you own: <b>В суббо́ту, в два часа́, я в кафе́.</b> Day (в + accusative), time (в + hour), place (в + prepositional). Three little в's, three jobs — Russian time-space in one line."],"audio":"Two anchor words carry every plan: sevodnya — today — yes, that letter g really says v — and zaftra, tomorrow. No case, no changes. At what time is va skolka? The answer re-uses v with the hour: v dva chasa — at two; v syem chasov — at seven. Now watch a full plan snap together: v soobbotu, v dva chasa, ya v kafe. Day, time, place — three little v's, three jobs. Russian time-space in one line.","examples":[{"ru":"Today I'm working, tomorrow I'm home.","en":"<b>Сего́дня</b> я рабо́таю, <b>за́втра</b> я до́ма.","say":"Сегодня я работаю, завтра я дома."},{"ru":"At what time? — At three.","en":"<b>Во ско́лько?</b> — В три часа́.","say":"Во сколько? — В три часа."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Ива́н suggests the park “tomorrow at five”. In Russian:","opts":["За́втра, в пять часо́в.","Сего́дня, в пять часа́.","За́втра, во пять часо́в."],"answer":0,"explain":"За́втра = tomorrow, and 5+ takes <b>часо́в</b>. Во appears only before awkward clusters like вт- — пять doesn't need it."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“On Sunday I'm in the park.”</b>","want":"В воскресенье я в парке","answer":"В воскресе́нье я в па́рке"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Evening after Gorky Park. Ива́н and Emma plan the week — she now owns all seven days. Tap to listen.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, где ты за́втра?","ru":"Emma, where are you tomorrow?"},{"who":"A","en":"За́втра понеде́льник. Я в университе́те.","ru":"Tomorrow is Monday. I'm at the university."},{"who":"B","en":"А во вто́рник?","ru":"And on Tuesday?"},{"who":"A","en":"Во вто́рник и в сре́ду я то́же в университе́те. Я мно́го рабо́таю!","ru":"On Tuesday and Wednesday I'm at the university too. I work a lot!"},{"who":"B","en":"В пя́тницу — фильм?","ru":"A film on Friday?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да! Во ско́лько?","ru":"Yes! At what time?"},{"who":"B","en":"В семь часо́в.","ru":"At seven."},{"who":"A","en":"Хорошо́. А в суббо́ту — кафе́ и сы́рники?","ru":"Good. And on Saturday — the café and sirniki?"},{"who":"B","en":"Коне́чно! В два часа́.","ru":"Of course! At two. (коне́чно — the ч sounds like ш: [ka-NYESH-na])"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve cards: the seven days plus the time words that glue a week together.","items":[{"en":"понеде́льник","ru":"Monday","ex":"[pa-nee-DYEL-neek] — в понеде́льник"},{"en":"вто́рник","ru":"Tuesday","ex":"[FTOR-neek] — во вто́рник; the в sounds like f"},{"en":"среда́","ru":"Wednesday","ex":"[sree-DA] — but: в сре́ду"},{"en":"четве́рг","ru":"Thursday","ex":"[cheet-VYERK] — the final г sounds like к"},{"en":"пя́тница","ru":"Friday","ex":"[PYAT-nee-tsa] — пять inside: day five"},{"en":"суббо́та","ru":"Saturday","ex":"[soo-BO-ta] — “sabbath” in Russian clothes"},{"en":"воскресе́нье","ru":"Sunday","ex":"[vas-kree-SYEN-ye] — the longest day name"},{"en":"сего́дня","ru":"today","ex":"[see-VOD-nya] — the г says [v]!"},{"en":"за́втра","ru":"tomorrow","ex":"[ZAF-tra] — за́втра суббо́та!"},{"en":"час","ru":"hour; one o'clock","ex":"[chas] — час, два часа́, пять часо́в"},{"en":"сейча́с","ru":"now","ex":"[see-CHAS] — Сейча́с два часа́."},{"en":"неде́ля","ru":"week","ex":"[nee-DYE-lya] — seven days, one week"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning on the right — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["в понеде́льник","on Monday"],["в сре́ду","on Wednesday"],["за́втра","tomorrow"],["Сейча́с два часа́.","It's two o'clock."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions — days, hours, and the в that does two jobs.","items":[{"q":"“On Wednesday” is:","opts":["в среда́","на сре́ду","в сре́ду"],"answer":2},{"q":"«Сейча́с семь ___.»","opts":["часо́в","часа́","час"],"answer":0},{"q":"Пя́тница is…","opts":["Thursday","Friday","Saturday"],"answer":1},{"q":"“Tomorrow I'm at work”:","opts":["Сего́дня я на рабо́те.","За́втра я на рабо́те.","За́втра я в рабо́те."],"answer":1},{"q":"Which day needs «во», not «в»?","opts":["___ вто́рник","___ суббо́ту","___ понеде́льник"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma pins her week to the fridge. Read it aloud — your week probably rhymes with it.","title":"Моя́ неде́ля","sentences":["Сего́дня воскресе́нье.","В понеде́льник, во вто́рник и в сре́ду я в университе́те.","В четве́рг я до́ма: я мно́го чита́ю.","В пя́тницу — фильм, в семь часо́в.","В суббо́ту, в два часа́, я в кафе́: чай и сы́рники.","Хоро́шая неде́ля!"],"translation":"Today is Sunday. On Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday I'm at the university. On Thursday I'm home: I read a lot. On Friday — a film, at seven. On Saturday, at two, I'm at the café: tea and sirniki. A good week!"},"essay":{"intro":"Your week, in Russian.","prompt":"Write a 3–4 line plan of your week: name at least two days (в + day), one time (в … часа́ / часо́в), and one place (в/на + -е, or до́ма).","hint":"The full frame: day, time, place — «В сре́ду, в пять часо́в, я в па́рке.» Feminine days bend: сре́ду, пя́тницу, суббо́ту 🙂","example":"Сего́дня понеде́льник, и я мно́го рабо́таю. В сре́ду я в университе́те. В пя́тницу, в семь часо́в, я в теа́тре. В суббо́ту я до́ма: чай, кни́га и му́зыка."}},"18":{"day":18,"week":"03","themeRu":"I’m going","themeEn":"Я иду́","intro":"Yesterday Emma could say where she is — <b>в па́рке</b>, <b>на рабо́те</b>. Today she starts moving. You'll learn Russian's motion verbs: <b>идти́</b> for walking somewhere right now, <b>е́хать</b> for anything on wheels, and <b>ходи́ть</b> for the trips you make again and again. One catch English never warns you about: the place you're heading to changes case — <b>в парк</b>, not <b>в па́рке</b>.","introAudio":"Yesterday Emma could say where she is — v parkye, na rabotye. Today she starts moving. You'll learn Russian's motion verbs: idti for walking somewhere right now, yekhat for anything on wheels, and khodit for the trips you make again and again. One catch English never warns you about: the place you're heading to changes case — v park, not v parkye.","goals":["Say you're walking somewhere now: <b>Я иду́ в парк</b>","Switch to wheels: <b>Я е́ду в Москву́</b> — by metro, bus or car","Talk about your routine: <b>Я хожу́ в университе́т ка́ждый день</b>","Answer <b>куда́?</b> — where to — with the accusative: <b>в парк</b>, not <b>в па́рке</b>"],"learned":["Said you're walking somewhere now: <b>Я иду́ в парк</b>","Switched to wheels: <b>Я е́ду в Москву́</b>","Talked about your routine: <b>Я хожу́ в университе́т ка́ждый день</b>","Answered <b>куда́?</b> with the accusative — <b>в парк</b>, not <b>в па́рке</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Warm hands first: three throwbacks before the city starts moving.","introAudio":"Warm hands first: three throwbacks before the city starts moving.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the situation and tap the right answer. A wrong tap costs nothing — it's how memory sets.","q":"«Ле́на ___.» (Lena is at work.)","opts":["на рабо́ту","в рабо́те","на рабо́те"],"answer":2,"explain":"Where someone is → the prepositional, and рабо́та takes на: <b>на рабо́те</b>. На рабо́ту would mean “to work”."},{"type":"choice","q":"Ива́н tells you about his sister: «___ нра́вится ко́фе.» (She likes coffee.)","opts":["Ему́","Ей","Тебе́"],"answer":1,"explain":"She receives the liking → <b>ей</b>. Ему́ is “to him”, тебе́ is “to you”."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“I love Moscow.”</b>","want":"Я люблю Москву","answer":"Я люблю́ Москву́"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · On foot, now","title":"<em>Я иду́</em> — walking there, this minute","paras":["<b>Идти́</b> [ee-TEE] is the verb for walking somewhere <em>right now</em>, in one direction. It's a little irregular, so learn the shape once: <b>я иду́, ты идёшь, он идёт, мы идём, вы идёте, они́ иду́т</b>. Hear the ё ring out in идёшь, идёт — it's always stressed.","Where are you walking <em>to</em>? That question is <b>куда́</b> [koo-DA] — the moving cousin of где. And the answer puts the destination in the accusative, the case you already aim at objects: <b>Я иду́ в парк</b>, <b>Я иду́ в кафе́</b>.","Masculine places like парк and neuter кафе́ don't change their look — в парк, в кафе́. A feminine -а place bends: шко́ла → <b>в шко́лу</b>, рабо́та → <b>на рабо́ту</b>. Same -у you met in Москву́."],"audio":"Idti is the verb for walking somewhere right now, in one direction. It's a little irregular, so learn the shape once: ya idoo, ty idyosh, on idyot, my idyom, vy idyotye, oni idoot. Hear the yo ring out in idyosh, idyot — it's always stressed. Where are you walking to? That question is kooda — the moving cousin of gdye. And the answer puts the destination in the accusative: ya idoo v park, ya idoo v kafe. Masculine and neuter places don't change their look. A feminine a-place bends: shkola becomes v shkoloo, rabota becomes na rabotoo — the same oo you met in Moskvoo.","table":{"rows":[["я иду́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я иду\">🔊</button>","I'm going (on foot)"],["ты идёшь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ты идёшь\">🔊</button>","you're going"],["он/она́ идёт <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"он идёт, она идёт\">🔊</button>","he/she is going"],["мы идём <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мы идём\">🔊</button>","we're going"],["они́ иду́т <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"они идут\">🔊</button>","they're going"]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Where are you going? — To the park.","en":"<b>Куда́</b> ты идёшь? — В парк.","say":"Куда ты идёшь? — В парк."},{"ru":"I'm walking to the café.","en":"Я <b>иду́</b> в кафе́.","say":"Я иду в кафе."},{"ru":"She's going to school.","en":"Она́ <b>идёт</b> в шко́лу.","say":"Она идёт в школу.","gloss":"шко́ла → в шко́лу, the -у of direction"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the situation and tap the sentence that fits.","q":"You're heading to the park on foot, right now. You say:","opts":["Я иду́ в па́рке.","Я иду́ парк.","Я иду́ в парк."],"answer":2,"explain":"Motion to a place → в + accusative: <b>в парк</b>. В па́рке is where you already are; «в парк» without в isn't Russian at all."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“I'm walking to the café.”</b>","want":"Я иду в кафе","answer":"Я иду́ в кафе́"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · By wheels","title":"<em>Я е́ду</em> — anything with wheels","paras":["The moment there are wheels under you — metro, bus, car, train — Russian trades идти́ for <b>е́хать</b> [YE-khat]. Same job, different verb: <b>я е́ду, ты е́дешь, он е́дет, мы е́дем, вы е́дете, они́ е́дут</b>. The stress stays home on е́- all the way through.","The destination works exactly as before — куда́ + accusative: <b>Я е́ду в Москву́</b>, <b>Я е́ду домо́й</b>. To name the vehicle, reuse <b>на</b> + the transport: <b>на метро́, на авто́бусе, на маши́не</b>.","So the choice is only about your feet. Walking there yourself? идти́. Being carried by an engine? е́хать. English blurs both into one word, “go” — Russian makes you pick."],"audio":"The moment there are wheels under you — metro, bus, car, train — Russian trades idti for yekhat. Same job, different verb: ya yedoo, ty yedyesh, on yedyet, my yedyem, vy yedyetye, oni yedoot. The stress stays home all the way through. The destination works exactly as before: ya yedoo v Moskvoo, ya yedoo damoy. To name the vehicle, reuse na plus the transport: na metro, na avtobusye, na mashinye. So the choice is only about your feet. Walking there yourself? idti. Carried by an engine? yekhat.","table":{"rows":[["я е́ду <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я еду\">🔊</button>","I'm going (riding)"],["ты е́дешь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ты едешь\">🔊</button>","you're going"],["он/она́ е́дет <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"он едет, она едет\">🔊</button>","he/she is going"],["мы е́дем <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мы едем\">🔊</button>","we're going"],["они́ е́дут <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"они едут\">🔊</button>","they're going"]],"star":0},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я иду́ на авто́бусе.","right":"Я е́ду на авто́бусе.","why":"On a bus you're riding, not walking — so <b>е́ду</b>. Save идти́ for your own two feet."}],"examples":[{"ru":"I'm going to Moscow by train.","en":"Я <b>е́ду</b> в Москву́.","say":"Я еду в Москву.","gloss":"far away → wheels → е́хать"},{"ru":"We're going home by metro.","en":"Мы <b>е́дем</b> домо́й на метро́.","say":"Мы едем домой на метро."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Emma is on the metro, heading downtown. She texts:","opts":["Я иду́ в центр.","Я е́ду в центр.","Я е́ду в це́нтре."],"answer":1,"explain":"On the metro = riding → <b>е́ду</b>; heading to the centre → в центр (accusative). В це́нтре would mean standing there already."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — no stress marks needed.","q":"Type: “I'm going home by car.” (wheels!)","accept":["я еду домой на машине","ya edu domoy na mashine","ya yedu domoy na mashine"],"placeholder":"Я е́ду …","explain":"Я е́ду домо́й на маши́не — wheels → е́ду, and домо́й for “to home”."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Every day","title":"<em>Я хожу́</em> — the trips you always make","paras":["<b>Идти́</b> is this one walk, happening now. For a walk you make <em>regularly</em> — a routine, a habit — Russian uses <b>ходи́ть</b> [kha-DEET]: <b>я хожу́, ты хо́дишь, он хо́дит, мы хо́дим, вы хо́дите, они́ хо́дят</b>.","Two things to notice. The я-form swaps in a ж: <b>хожу́</b>, not «ходю́». And the stress runs home after it — <b>хожу́ → хо́дишь</b> — exactly the trick you saw in люблю́ → лю́бишь.","So: <b>Я хожу́ в университе́т ка́ждый день</b> — every day, a habit. But <b>Я иду́ в университе́т</b> — right now, out the door. Same feet, two different stories."],"audio":"Idti is this one walk, happening now. For a walk you make regularly — a routine, a habit — Russian uses khodit: ya khazhoo, ty khodish, on khodit, my khodim, vy khodite, oni khodyat. Two things to notice. The ya-form swaps in a zh: khazhoo, not khodyoo. And the stress runs home after it — khazhoo, khodish — exactly the trick you saw in lyublyu, lyubish. So: ya khazhoo v universitet kazhdy dyen — every day, a habit. But ya idoo v universitet — right now, out the door.","table":{"rows":[["я хожу́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"я хожу\">🔊</button>","I go (regularly)"],["ты хо́дишь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ты ходишь\">🔊</button>","you go"],["он/она́ хо́дит <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"он ходит, она ходит\">🔊</button>","he/she goes"],["мы хо́дим <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мы ходим\">🔊</button>","we go"],["они́ хо́дят <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"они ходят\">🔊</button>","they go"]],"star":0},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Ка́ждый день я иду́ в шко́лу.","right":"Ка́ждый день я хожу́ в шко́лу.","why":"«Ка́ждый день» signals a habit — that's <b>хожу́</b> (ходи́ть). Иду́ is only for the trip you're on right now."}],"examples":[{"ru":"I go to the café every day.","en":"Я <b>хожу́</b> в кафе́ ка́ждый день.","say":"Я хожу в кафе каждый день."},{"ru":"Do you go to the theatre?","en":"Ты <b>хо́дишь</b> в теа́тр?","say":"Ты ходишь в театр?","gloss":"a habit, not right now"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Ка́ждый день я ___ в университе́т.» Which verb?","opts":["иду́","хожу́","е́ду"],"answer":1,"explain":"«Ка́ждый день» = a habit → <b>хожу́</b>. Иду́ is one trip right now; е́ду would be by transport."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I walk to the park every day.”</b>","want":"Я хожу в парк каждый день","answer":"Я хожу́ в парк ка́ждый день"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud. Listen to the sample first if you like.","target":"Я хожу́ в университе́т ка́ждый день.","sub":"[ya kha-ZHOO v oo-nee-veer-see-TYET KA-zhdy dyen]","want":"Я хожу в университет каждый день"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Куда́ or где?","title":"<em>в парк</em> (going) vs <em>в па́рке</em> (there)","paras":["Here's the pattern that trips up every English speaker, because English hides it. One little <b>в</b>, two different jobs. Ask <b>где?</b> — where? — and the place stands still, in the prepositional: <b>Я в па́рке</b>. Ask <b>куда́?</b> — where to? — and the place is a destination, in the accusative: <b>Я иду́ в парк</b>.","Movement chooses the accusative; staying put chooses the prepositional. <b>на рабо́те</b> (at work) vs <b>на рабо́ту</b> (off to work). And home keeps its own private pair: <b>до́ма</b> (at home) vs <b>домо́й</b> (heading home).","This is just a first look — the full face-off is coming next week. For now, let the verb tip you off: a motion verb (иду́, е́ду, хожу́) pulls the accusative behind it."],"audio":"Here's the pattern that trips up every English speaker, because English hides it. One little v, two different jobs. Ask gdye — where? — and the place stands still, in the prepositional: ya v parkye. Ask kooda — where to? — and the place is a destination, in the accusative: ya idoo v park. Movement chooses the accusative; staying put chooses the prepositional. Na rabotye, at work, versus na rabotoo, off to work. And home keeps its own pair: doma, at home, versus damoy, heading home. This is a first look — the full face-off is coming next week. For now, let the verb tip you off: a motion verb pulls the accusative behind it.","table":{"rows":[["Где? — Я в па́рке. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Где? Я в парке.\">🔊</button>","Where? — I'm in the park. (prepositional)"],["Куда́? — Я иду́ в парк. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Куда? Я иду в парк.\">🔊</button>","Where to? — I'm going to the park. (accusative)"],["на рабо́те / на рабо́ту <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"на работе, на работу\">🔊</button>","at work / to work"],["до́ма / домо́й <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дома, домой\">🔊</button>","at home / home(ward)"]],"star":1},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я иду́ в па́рке.","right":"Я иду́ в парк.","why":"Иду́ is motion, so the destination goes accusative: <b>в парк</b>. В па́рке (prepositional) is for standing still — «Я в па́рке»."}],"examples":[{"ru":"I'm at work. — I'm going to work.","en":"Я <b>на рабо́те</b>. — Я иду́ <b>на рабо́ту</b>.","say":"Я на работе. Я иду на работу."},{"ru":"Are you home? — No, I'm heading home.","en":"Ты <b>до́ма</b>? — Нет, я е́ду <b>домо́й</b>.","say":"Ты дома? Нет, я еду домой."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Я иду́ в ___.» (I'm going to the theatre.)","opts":["теа́тре","теа́тр","теа́тра"],"answer":1,"explain":"Motion → accusative. Теа́тр is masculine, so it looks unchanged: <b>в теа́тр</b>. Теа́тре is the “where” form (в теа́тре)."},{"type":"type","q":"«Где Ле́на?» — she's at work. Type the answer:","accept":["она на работе","лена на работе","на работе","ona na rabote","lena na rabote"],"placeholder":"Она́ …","explain":"Где → the place stands still → prepositional: на рабо́те. (To work would be на рабо́ту.)"},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I'm going to work.”</b>","want":"Я иду на работу","answer":"Я иду́ на рабо́ту"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Saturday morning. Ива́н texts Emma to meet — and for the first time she navigates Moscow on her own, half on foot, half by metro. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Приве́т! Куда́ ты идёшь?","ru":"Hi! Where are you off to?"},{"who":"A","en":"Я иду́ на бульва́р, пешко́м.","ru":"I'm walking to the boulevard, on foot. (бульва́р = boulevard)"},{"who":"B","en":"А в кафе́? Как ты е́дешь?","ru":"And to the café? How will you get there?"},{"who":"A","en":"Я е́ду на метро́. Я о́чень люблю́ метро́!","ru":"By metro. I really love the metro!"},{"who":"B","en":"Куда́ ты хо́дишь ка́ждый день?","ru":"Where do you go every day?"},{"who":"A","en":"Ка́ждый день я хожу́ в университе́т.","ru":"Every day I go to the university."},{"who":"B","en":"А сейча́с ты где?","ru":"And right now — where are you?"},{"who":"A","en":"Я на бульва́ре, в це́нтре. Москва́ краси́вая!","ru":"I'm on the boulevard, in the centre. Moscow is beautiful! (краси́вая = beautiful)"},{"who":"B","en":"Пока́, Э́мма!","ru":"Bye, Emma!"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve ways to move through Moscow. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"идти́","ru":"to go (on foot, right now)","ex":"[ee-TEE] — я иду́, ты идёшь"},{"en":"ходи́ть","ru":"to go (on foot, regularly)","ex":"[kha-DEET] — я хожу́, ты хо́дишь"},{"en":"е́хать","ru":"to go (by transport)","ex":"[YE-khat] — я е́ду, ты е́дешь"},{"en":"куда́","ru":"where to","ex":"[koo-DA] — Куда́ ты идёшь?"},{"en":"домо́й","ru":"home(ward), to home","ex":"[da-MOY] — я иду́ домо́й"},{"en":"пешко́м","ru":"on foot","ex":"[peesh-KOM] — я иду́ пешко́м"},{"en":"авто́бус","ru":"bus","ex":"[af-TO-boos] — я е́ду на авто́бусе"},{"en":"на маши́не","ru":"by car","ex":"[na ma-SHY-nye] — е́хать на маши́не"},{"en":"на метро́","ru":"by metro","ex":"[na mee-TRO] — я е́ду на метро́"},{"en":"ка́ждый день","ru":"every day","ex":"[KA-zhdy dyen] — я хожу́ в парк ка́ждый день"},{"en":"бульва́р","ru":"boulevard","ex":"[bool-VAR] — я иду́ на бульва́р"},{"en":"музе́й","ru":"museum","ex":"[moo-ZYEY] — я иду́ в музе́й"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning on the right — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Я иду́ в парк.","I'm going to the park."],["Я е́ду на метро́.","I'm going by metro."],["Я хожу́ в университе́т.","I go to the university."],["Я иду́ домо́й.","I'm going home."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — today's verbs of motion, nothing else. If one bites, the rule is one scroll up.","items":[{"q":"Emma is walking to the café right now. Which is right?","opts":["Я е́ду в кафе́.","Я иду́ в кафе́.","Я хожу́ в кафе́."],"answer":1},{"q":"You're on the bus to Moscow. In Russian:","opts":["Я иду́ в Москву́.","Я е́ду в Москве́.","Я е́ду в Москву́."],"answer":2},{"q":"«Ка́ждый день я ___ в университе́т.»","opts":["хожу́","иду́","е́ду"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Я иду́ в ___.» — to the park:","opts":["па́рке","парк","па́рка"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which question means “where to?”","opts":["Где?","Куда́?","Кто?"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note to a friend back home — how she gets around Moscow. Read it aloud.","title":"Пешко́м и на метро́","sentences":["Я о́чень люблю́ Москву́.","Ка́ждый день я хожу́ в университе́т пешко́м.","А в центр я е́ду на метро́.","Сего́дня суббо́та, и я иду́ на бульва́р.","Москва́ — краси́вый го́род!"],"translation":"I really love Moscow. Every day I walk to the university on foot. But to the centre I go by metro. Today is Saturday, and I'm walking to the boulevard. Moscow is a beautiful city! (краси́вый = beautiful)"},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to move through your own city.","prompt":"Write 3–4 sentences: where you go every day (я хожу́…), how you get somewhere (пешко́м or на метро́ / на маши́не), and where you're heading right now (я иду́ / е́ду в…). Mind куда́ — the destination takes the accusative.","hint":"Frames: Ка́ждый день я хожу́ в… · Я е́ду в… на метро́ · Сейча́с я иду́ в… Motion pulls the accusative: в парк, not в па́рке 🙂","example":"Ка́ждый день я хожу́ в университе́т пешко́м. А в центр я е́ду на метро́. Сейча́с я иду́ в кафе́. Я о́чень люблю́ Москву́!"}},"19":{"day":19,"week":"03","themeRu":"At the café","themeEn":"В кафе́","intro":"This is the day Emma has been quietly waiting for. She walks into Ле́на's café, and instead of pointing and smiling, she orders — out loud, in Russian. Today you get the two magic keys to any café or shop: <b>Я хочу́…</b> (I want…) and <b>Да́йте, пожа́луйста…</b> (Give me, please…). Plus a plate of new words and one sly false friend.","introAudio":"This is the day Emma has been quietly waiting for. She walks into Lena's café, and instead of pointing and smiling, she orders — out loud, in Russian. Today you get the two magic keys to any café or shop: ya khachoo — I want — and dayte pazhalusta — give me, please. Plus a plate of new words and one sly false friend.","goals":["Ask for what you want: <b>Я хочу́ ко́фе</b>, <b>Я хочу́ во́ду</b>","Order politely: <b>Да́йте, пожа́луйста, сала́т</b>","Name food and drink — and read a Russian <b>меню́</b>","Dodge the false friend: <b>магази́н</b> is a shop, not a magazine"],"learned":["Asked for what you want: <b>Я хочу́ ко́фе</b>, <b>Я хочу́ во́ду</b>","Ordered politely: <b>Да́йте, пожа́луйста, сала́т</b>","Named food and drink and read a <b>меню́</b>","Remembered <b>магази́н</b> = shop, not magazine"],"review":{"intro":"Before we order: three warm-ups, drawn from this whole week.","introAudio":"Before we order: three warm-ups, drawn from this whole week.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the right answer. A wrong tap costs nothing — it's how memory sets.","q":"Emma is walking to the park, right now:","opts":["Я е́ду в па́рке.","Я иду́ в парк.","Я хожу́ в парк."],"answer":1,"explain":"On foot, right now → <b>иду́</b>; destination → в парк (accusative). Хожу́ is a habit; е́ду is by transport."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Мне ___ блины́.» (I like blini.)","opts":["нра́вятся","нра́вится"],"answer":0,"explain":"Блины́ is plural → the verb follows it: <b>нра́вятся</b>. The verb counts the liked things, not you."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“Lena is at work.”</b>","want":"Лена на работе","answer":"Ле́на на рабо́те"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · I want","title":"<em>Я хочу́…</em> — and the object bends","paras":["<b>Хоте́ть</b> — to want — is a small rebel, so just pocket the forms you'll actually use: <b>я хочу́, ты хо́чешь, он/она́ хо́чет</b>. Notice the stress hop you already know from люблю́: <b>хочу́ → хо́чешь</b>.","After хочу́ the thing you want lands in the accusative. Masculine and neuter words don't flinch: <b>Я хочу́ ко́фе, чай, суп, сала́т</b>. But a feminine -а bends to -у: вода́ → <b>во́ду</b>, пи́цца → <b>пи́ццу</b>.","Watch вода́: the stress jumps to the front when it bends — <b>во́ду</b> — exactly like среда́ → сре́ду last week. Say «Я хочу́ во́ду» and you'll never go thirsty in Moscow."],"audio":"Khotet — to want — is a small rebel, so just pocket the forms you'll use: ya khachoo, ty khochesh, on khochet. Notice the stress hop you know from lyublyu: khachoo, khochesh. After khachoo the thing you want lands in the accusative. Masculine and neuter words don't flinch: ya khachoo kofye, chai, soop, salat. But a feminine a bends to u: vada becomes vodoo, pitsa becomes pitsoo. Watch vada: the stress jumps to the front when it bends — vodoo — exactly like sreda became sryedoo last week. Say ya khachoo vodoo and you'll never go thirsty in Moscow.","table":{"rows":[["Я хочу́ ко́фе. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я хочу кофе.\">🔊</button>","I want a coffee. (no change)"],["Я хочу́ во́ду. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я хочу воду.\">🔊</button>","I want water. (вода́ → во́ду)"],["Ты хо́чешь чай? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Ты хочешь чай?\">🔊</button>","Do you want tea?"],["Он хо́чет суп. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Он хочет суп.\">🔊</button>","He wants soup."]],"star":1},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я хочу́ вода́.","right":"Я хочу́ во́ду.","why":"Whatever you want is an object → accusative. Feminine вода́ bends to <b>во́ду</b>, with the stress hopping to the front."}],"examples":[{"ru":"I want a coffee and water.","en":"Я <b>хочу́</b> ко́фе и <b>во́ду</b>.","say":"Я хочу кофе и воду."},{"ru":"Do you want pizza? — Yes, please!","en":"Ты <b>хо́чешь</b> пи́ццу? — Да, пожа́луйста!","say":"Ты хочешь пиццу? — Да, пожалуйста!","gloss":"пи́цца → пи́ццу"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the situation and tap the right sentence.","q":"Emma is thirsty. She says:","opts":["Я хочу́ вода́.","Я хо́чешь во́ду.","Я хочу́ во́ду."],"answer":2,"explain":"Want + object → accusative: <b>во́ду</b>. And “I” takes хочу́, not хо́чешь."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“I want soup and salad.”</b>","want":"Я хочу суп и салат","answer":"Я хочу́ суп и сала́т"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Ordering out loud","title":"<em>Да́йте, пожа́луйста</em> — the polite order","paras":["To order like a local, reach for a ready-made polite chunk: <b>Да́йте, пожа́луйста</b> — “give me, please” — plus the thing (accusative, same as after хочу́). <b>Да́йте, пожа́луйста, ко́фе.</b> Take it whole, exactly as you took Здра́вствуйте.","To catch the server's attention first, use <b>Извини́те!</b> — “excuse me”. And to close the bill: <b>Счёт, пожа́луйста</b> — “the check, please”. These are learned phrases; you don't build them, you just say them.","Хочу́ states your wish; Да́йте makes the request softer and more polite. In a café, either works — but Да́йте, пожа́луйста is the one that earns a smile."],"audio":"To order like a local, reach for a ready-made polite chunk: dayte pazhalusta — give me, please — plus the thing in the accusative, same as after khachoo. Dayte pazhalusta kofye. Take it whole, exactly as you took zdravstvuyte. To catch the server's attention first, use izvinitye — excuse me. And to close the bill: shchot pazhalusta — the check, please. These are learned phrases; you don't build them, you just say them. Khachoo states your wish; dayte makes the request softer and more polite. Either works — but dayte pazhalusta earns a smile.","table":{"rows":[["Извини́те! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Извините!\">🔊</button>","Excuse me!"],["Да́йте, пожа́луйста, ко́фе. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Дайте, пожалуйста, кофе.\">🔊</button>","A coffee, please."],["Да́йте, пожа́луйста, меню́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Дайте, пожалуйста, меню.\">🔊</button>","The menu, please."],["Счёт, пожа́луйста. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Счёт, пожалуйста.\">🔊</button>","The check, please."]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Give me a tea, please.","en":"<b>Да́йте, пожа́луйста</b>, чай.","say":"Дайте, пожалуйста, чай."},{"ru":"Excuse me! The menu, please.","en":"<b>Извини́те</b>! Да́йте, пожа́луйста, меню́.","say":"Извините! Дайте, пожалуйста, меню."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"You want to order a coffee politely. Best line:","opts":["Я ко́фе.","Да́йте, пожа́луйста, ко́фе.","Ко́фе есть?"],"answer":1,"explain":"The polite order is <b>Да́йте, пожа́луйста</b> + the item. «Я ко́фе» is just “I coffee”; «Ко́фе есть?» asks whether coffee exists."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — «е» for «ё» is OK too.","q":"Type: “The check, please.”","accept":["счёт пожалуйста","счет пожалуйста","счёт, пожалуйста","счет, пожалуйста","schyot pozhaluysta","schet pozhaluysta"],"placeholder":"Счёт, …","explain":"Счёт, пожа́луйста — the two words that end every Russian meal."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · On the menu","title":"Drinks, food, and one neuter surprise","paras":["Here's a café's worth of words. Drinks: <b>вода́, ко́фе, чай, сок</b>. Food: <b>суп, сала́т, пи́цца, хлеб</b>. And the dessert everyone orders: <b>моро́женое</b> — ice cream.","Stack an order with и (and), like any list: <b>Я хочу́ суп, сала́т и сок.</b> Everything after хочу́ is an object, so feminine words still bend — <b>Да́йте, пожа́луйста, пи́ццу и во́ду.</b>","One word looks like an adjective, and it is one: <b>моро́женое</b> ends in -ое, so it's neuter (“the frozen thing”). As an object it doesn't change: Я хочу́ моро́женое."],"audio":"Here's a café's worth of words. Drinks: vada, kofye, chai, sok. Food: soop, salat, pitsa, khlyeb. And the dessert everyone orders: marozhenaye — ice cream. Stack an order with i, and, like any list: ya khachoo soop, salat i sok. Everything after khachoo is an object, so feminine words still bend: dayte pazhalusta pitsoo i vodoo. One word looks like an adjective, and it is one: marozhenaye ends in oye, so it's neuter — the frozen thing — and as an object it doesn't change.","table":{"rows":[["вода́ · сок <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вода, сок\">🔊</button>","water · juice"],["суп · сала́т <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"суп, салат\">🔊</button>","soup · salad"],["пи́цца · хлеб <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пицца, хлеб\">🔊</button>","pizza · bread"],["моро́женое <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мороженое\">🔊</button>","ice cream (neuter)"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"I want soup, salad and juice.","en":"Я хочу́ <b>суп</b>, <b>сала́т</b> и <b>сок</b>.","say":"Я хочу суп, салат и сок."},{"ru":"Give me pizza and water, please.","en":"Да́йте, пожа́луйста, <b>пи́ццу</b> и <b>во́ду</b>.","say":"Дайте, пожалуйста, пиццу и воду."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which one is a drink?","opts":["сала́т","сок","хлеб"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Сок</b> is juice — a drink. Сала́т (salad) and хлеб (bread) you eat."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and order out loud. Listen to the sample first if you like.","target":"Да́йте, пожа́луйста, ко́фе и моро́женое.","sub":"[DAY-tye pa-ZHA-loo-sta KO-fye ee ma-RO-zhe-na-ye]","want":"Дайте пожалуйста кофе и мороженое"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · False friend","title":"<em>магази́н</em> is a shop, not a magazine","paras":["Some Russian words look like English words but mean something else — false friends. Today's is <b>магази́н</b> [ma-ga-ZEEN]. Your ear shouts “magazine”, but магази́н is a <b>shop, a store</b> — where you buy your bread and water.","A magazine you read is a completely different word: <b>журна́л</b>. So «Я чита́ю магази́н» would mean “I'm reading a shop” — the sentence you want is <b>Я чита́ю журна́л</b>.","The café and the магази́н are cousins: in one you order (Да́йте, пожа́луйста), in the other you buy. Both take your polite chunk and your accusative."],"audio":"Some Russian words look like English words but mean something else — false friends. Today's is magazin. Your ear shouts magazine, but magazin is a shop, a store — where you buy your bread and water. A magazine you read is a completely different word: zhurnal. So ya chitayu magazin would mean I'm reading a shop — the sentence you want is ya chitayu zhurnal. The café and the shop are cousins: in one you order, in the other you buy. Both take your polite chunk and your accusative.","mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я чита́ю магази́н.","right":"Я чита́ю журна́л.","why":"<b>Магази́н</b> is a shop, not a magazine — you can't read it. A magazine is <b>журна́л</b>."}],"examples":[{"ru":"The water is in the shop.","en":"Вода́ в <b>магази́не</b>.","say":"Вода в магазине.","gloss":"в магази́не — where: prepositional"},{"ru":"I'm reading a good magazine.","en":"Я чита́ю хоро́ший <b>журна́л</b>.","say":"Я читаю хороший журнал."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Магази́н» means:","opts":["a magazine","a shop","a café"],"answer":1,"explain":"False friend alert: <b>магази́н</b> is a shop. A magazine is журна́л."},{"type":"type","q":"Translate: “a shop” — one Russian word:","accept":["магазин","magazin"],"placeholder":"a store…","explain":"Магази́н — a shop. Don't let it trick you into “magazine”."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ле́на's café, lunchtime. For the first time, Emma orders everything herself — no pointing, no English. Ле́на pretends not to notice how proud she is. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"A","en":"Здра́вствуйте! Да́йте, пожа́луйста, меню́.","ru":"Hello! The menu, please."},{"who":"B","en":"Коне́чно. Что ты хо́чешь?","ru":"Of course. What would you like?"},{"who":"A","en":"Я хочу́ суп, сала́т и хлеб.","ru":"I want soup, salad and bread."},{"who":"B","en":"А пить? Чай, ко́фе, сок?","ru":"And to drink? Tea, coffee, juice? (пить = to drink)"},{"who":"A","en":"Да́йте, пожа́луйста, во́ду и ко́фе.","ru":"Water and a coffee, please."},{"who":"B","en":"А моро́женое? Ты лю́бишь моро́женое.","ru":"And ice cream? You love ice cream."},{"who":"A","en":"Да! Я о́чень люблю́ моро́женое.","ru":"Yes! I really love ice cream."},{"who":"B","en":"Хорошо́! Ты отли́чно говори́шь по-ру́сски.","ru":"Good! You speak Russian brilliantly. (отли́чно = brilliantly)"},{"who":"A","en":"Спаси́бо! Счёт, пожа́луйста.","ru":"Thank you! The check, please."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words to eat and drink your way through Moscow. Tap to flip, tap 🔊 to hear.","items":[{"en":"хоте́ть","ru":"to want","ex":"[kha-TYET] — я хочу́, ты хо́чешь"},{"en":"Да́йте, пожа́луйста","ru":"give (me), please","ex":"[DAY-tye pa-ZHA-loo-sta] — the polite order"},{"en":"вода́","ru":"water","ex":"[va-DA] — я хочу́ во́ду"},{"en":"ко́фе","ru":"coffee","ex":"[KO-fye] — Да́йте, пожа́луйста, ко́фе"},{"en":"сок","ru":"juice","ex":"[sok] — я хочу́ сок"},{"en":"сала́т","ru":"salad","ex":"[sa-LAT] — сала́т и суп"},{"en":"суп","ru":"soup","ex":"[soop] — я хочу́ суп"},{"en":"пи́цца","ru":"pizza","ex":"[PEE-tsa] — я хочу́ пи́ццу"},{"en":"хлеб","ru":"bread","ex":"[khlyep] — the final б sounds like п"},{"en":"моро́женое","ru":"ice cream","ex":"[ma-RO-zhe-na-ye] — neuter, shaped like an adjective"},{"en":"меню́","ru":"menu","ex":"[mee-NYU] — never changes"},{"en":"магази́н","ru":"shop, store (not magazine!)","ex":"[ma-ga-ZEEN] — a magazine is журна́л"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning on the right — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Я хочу́ ко́фе.","I want a coffee."],["Да́йте, пожа́луйста, во́ду.","Water, please."],["Я люблю́ моро́женое.","I love ice cream."],["магази́н","a shop"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions — ordering, food, and one false friend. If one bites, the rule is one scroll up.","items":[{"q":"Emma is thirsty and wants water. She says:","opts":["Я хочу́ чай.","Я хочу́ вода́.","Я хочу́ во́ду."],"answer":2},{"q":"The polite way to order a salad:","opts":["Да́йте, пожа́луйста, сала́т.","Я сала́т.","Сала́т?"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Магази́н» is:","opts":["a magazine","a shop","a menu"],"answer":1},{"q":"Which word is a dessert?","opts":["суп","хлеб","моро́женое"],"answer":2},{"q":"«Ты ___ ко́фе?» (Do you want coffee?)","opts":["хо́чешь","хочу́","хо́чет"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma writes down her proudest sentence yet — her whole café order. Read it aloud.","title":"В кафе́ у Ле́ны","sentences":["Сего́дня я в кафе́ у Ле́ны.","Я хочу́ суп, сала́т и хлеб.","И я о́чень хочу́ ко́фе и моро́женое!","«Да́йте, пожа́луйста, во́ду», — говорю́ я.","Ле́на говори́т: «Ты отли́чно говори́шь по-ру́сски!»","Спаси́бо, Ле́на! Всё о́чень вку́сно."],"translation":"Today I'm at Lena's café. I want soup, salad and bread. And I really want coffee and ice cream! “Water, please,” I say. Lena says: “You speak Russian brilliantly!” Thank you, Lena! Everything is delicious. (вку́сно = tasty)"},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to order — and to eat well.","prompt":"Imagine you're at a café. Write 3–4 sentences: greet, ask for the menu, and order two or three things with <b>Я хочу́…</b> or <b>Да́йте, пожа́луйста…</b>. Remember: after both, a feminine -а word bends to -у.","hint":"Frames: Здра́вствуйте! Да́йте, пожа́луйста, меню́. · Я хочу́ суп и сала́т. · Да́йте, пожа́луйста, во́ду. Feminine bends: вода́ → во́ду, пи́цца → пи́ццу 🙂","example":"Здра́вствуйте! Да́йте, пожа́луйста, меню́. Я хочу́ суп, сала́т и хлеб. И да́йте, пожа́луйста, во́ду и ко́фе. Спаси́бо!"}},"20":{"day":20,"week":"03","themeRu":"Questions","themeEn":"Вопро́сы","intro":"Ива́н has friends, and tonight Emma meets them over tea. They play a game: everyone asks each other questions — fast. Good news for Emma: Russian questions are simpler than English ones. There's no “do” or “does” to bolt on, and often nothing changes at all — you just lift your voice. Today you gather the whole question toolkit: <b>где, куда́, когда́, почему́, како́й, ско́лько</b>.","introAudio":"Ivan has friends, and tonight Emma meets them over tea. They play a game: everyone asks each other questions — fast. Good news for Emma: Russian questions are simpler than English ones. There's no do or does to bolt on, and often nothing changes at all — you just lift your voice. Today you gather the whole question toolkit: gdye, kooda, kagda, pachemoo, kakoy, skolka.","goals":["Ask yes/no questions with pure melody — no “do” needed: <b>Ты лю́бишь ко́фе?</b>","Deploy the question words: <b>где, куда́, когда́, почему́</b>","Ask “what kind?” with agreement: <b>Како́й фильм? Кака́я му́зыка?</b>","Give a reason with <b>потому́ что</b> — because"],"learned":["Asked yes/no questions with pure melody — no “do” needed","Deployed the question words <b>где, куда́, когда́, почему́</b>","Asked “what kind?” with agreement: <b>Како́й фильм? Кака́я му́зыка?</b>","Gave reasons with <b>потому́ что</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Three quick warm-ups before the questions game — café, movement, and a plan.","introAudio":"Three quick warm-ups before the questions game — café, movement, and a plan.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the right answer. A wrong tap costs nothing.","q":"Emma orders water: «Я хочу́ ___.»","opts":["во́ду","вода́","воды́"],"answer":0,"explain":"Want + object → accusative: <b>во́ду</b>, with the stress on the front."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Ка́ждый день я ___ в университе́т.» (a habit)","opts":["иду́","хожу́","е́ду"],"answer":1,"explain":"Every day = a habit → <b>хожу́</b>. Иду́ is one trip now; е́ду is by transport."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“On Saturday I'm at the café.”</b>","want":"В субботу я в кафе","answer":"В суббо́ту я в кафе́"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · No “do” needed","title":"A question is just a statement, lifted","paras":["In English, a question grows extra machinery: “<em>Do</em> you like coffee?”, “<em>Does</em> she work?”. Russian throws all of that away. Take the plain sentence and simply raise your voice at the end: <b>Ты лю́бишь ко́фе?</b> <b>Она́ рабо́тает?</b> Same words, rising melody — that's the whole question.","Because nothing is bolted on, word order can relax too. You can front the word you care about: <b>Ко́фе ты лю́бишь?</b> (coffee — is that what you like?). The melody, not the order, marks the question.","So never reach for a Russian “do” — there isn't one. If your English brain wants «До ты…», smile and drop the «до»."],"audio":"In English, a question grows extra machinery: do you like coffee, does she work. Russian throws all of that away. Take the plain sentence and simply raise your voice at the end: ty lyubish kofye? ona rabotayet? Same words, rising melody — that's the whole question. Because nothing is bolted on, word order can relax too: you can front the word you care about — kofye ty lyubish? The melody, not the order, marks the question. So never reach for a Russian do — there isn't one.","table":{"rows":[["Ты студе́нт. → Ты студе́нт? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Ты студент. Ты студент?\">🔊</button>","You're a student. → Are you a student?"],["Она́ до́ма. → Она́ до́ма? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Она дома. Она дома?\">🔊</button>","She's home. → Is she home?"],["Ты лю́бишь ко́фе? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Ты любишь кофе?\">🔊</button>","Do you like coffee?"],["Ты хо́чешь чай? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Ты хочешь чай?\">🔊</button>","Do you want tea?"]],"star":2},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"До ты лю́бишь ко́фе?","right":"Ты лю́бишь ко́фе?","why":"There is no Russian “do”. The rising melody alone turns <b>Ты лю́бишь ко́фе</b> into a question."}],"examples":[{"ru":"Do you work on Saturday?","en":"Ты <b>рабо́таешь</b> в суббо́ту?","say":"Ты работаешь в субботу?","gloss":"no “do” — just the melody"},{"ru":"Is this a café?","en":"Э́то <b>кафе́</b>?","say":"Это кафе?"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read and tap the correct option. A wrong tap teaches too.","q":"How do you turn «Ты рабо́таешь» into a question?","opts":["Add «до»: До ты рабо́таешь?","Just raise your voice: Ты рабо́таешь?","Change the verb: Ты рабо́тать?"],"answer":1,"explain":"No “do”, no verb change — melody does it: <b>Ты рабо́таешь?</b>"},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — no stress marks needed.","q":"Ask a friend “Do you like tea?” in Russian:","accept":["ты любишь чай","ты любишь чай?","ty lyubish chai","ty lubish chai"],"placeholder":"Ты …?","explain":"Ты лю́бишь чай? — statement words, rising melody, no “do”."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · The -да trio","title":"<em>Где, куда́, когда́</em> — place, direction, time","paras":["Three rhyming question words, easy to keep as a set. <b>Где?</b> — where (a place): answer with the prepositional, <b>в па́рке</b>. <b>Куда́?</b> — where to (a direction): answer with the accusative, <b>в парк</b>. <b>Когда́?</b> — when (a time): answer with a day or an hour, <b>в суббо́ту, в два часа́</b>.","You already own all three answers — this just hands you the questions that fetch them: <b>Где Ле́на? Куда́ ты идёшь? Когда́ фильм?</b>","Keep где and куда́ apart by the verb: standing still → где → в па́рке; moving → куда́ → в парк. The same в, two cases — the split you previewed yesterday."],"audio":"Three rhyming question words, easy to keep as a set. Gdye — where, a place: answer with the prepositional, v parkye. Kooda — where to, a direction: answer with the accusative, v park. Kagda — when, a time: answer with a day or an hour, v soobbotu, v dva chasa. You already own all three answers — this just hands you the questions that fetch them: gdye Lena? kooda ty idyosh? kagda film? Keep gdye and kooda apart by the verb: standing still, gdye, v parkye; moving, kooda, v park.","table":{"rows":[["Где? — в па́рке <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Где? В парке.\">🔊</button>","Where? — in the park"],["Куда́? — в парк <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Куда? В парк.\">🔊</button>","Where to? — to the park"],["Когда́? — в суббо́ту <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Когда? В субботу.\">🔊</button>","When? — on Saturday"],["Когда́? — в два часа́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Когда? В два часа.\">🔊</button>","When? — at two o'clock"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Where are you going? — To the café.","en":"<b>Куда́</b> ты идёшь? — В кафе́.","say":"Куда ты идёшь? — В кафе."},{"ru":"When is the film? — On Friday, at seven.","en":"<b>Когда́</b> фильм? — В пя́тницу, в семь часо́в.","say":"Когда фильм? — В пятницу, в семь часов."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Ива́н asks «Куда́ ты идёшь?» He wants to know:","opts":["where you are","where you're going","when you're going"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>Куда́</b> = where to — your destination. Где asks where you are; когда́ asks when."},{"type":"build","q":"Build the question: <b>“When is the film?”</b>","want":"Когда фильм","answer":"Когда́ фильм"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Why and how much","title":"<em>Почему́?</em> and <em>ско́лько?</em>","paras":["<b>Почему́?</b> — why? And the answer starts with its partner <b>потому́ что</b> — because: <b>Почему́ ты до́ма? — Потому́ что суббо́та.</b>","<b>Ско́лько?</b> — how much / how many — is an old friend: you've used it in <b>Ско́лько тебе́ лет?</b> and <b>Ско́лько вре́мени?</b>. In a shop it becomes gold: <b>Ско́лько сто́ит ко́фе?</b> — how much is a coffee?","Both questions, like all the others, need no “do” — just the question word and a curious tone. Your voice lands on the question word and settles down after it: <b>Почему́?</b> The rising melody stays with yes/no questions."],"audio":"Pachemoo — why? And the answer starts with its partner patamoo shta — because: pachemoo ty doma? patamoo shta soobbota. Skolka — how much or how many — is an old friend: you've used it in skolka tebye lyet and skolka vryemeni. In a shop it becomes gold: skolka stoit kofye — how much is a coffee? Both questions, like all the others, need no do — just the question word and a curious tone. Your voice lands on the question word and settles down after it. The rising melody stays with yes or no questions.","table":{"rows":[["Почему́? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Почему?\">🔊</button>","Why?"],["Потому́ что… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Потому что…\">🔊</button>","Because…"],["Ско́лько тебе́ лет? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Сколько тебе лет?\">🔊</button>","How old are you?"],["Ско́лько сто́ит ко́фе? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Сколько стоит кофе?\">🔊</button>","How much is a coffee?"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"Why are you home? — Because it's Sunday.","en":"<b>Почему́</b> ты до́ма? — <b>Потому́ что</b> воскресе́нье.","say":"Почему ты дома? — Потому что воскресенье."},{"ru":"How much does a coffee cost?","en":"<b>Ско́лько</b> сто́ит ко́фе?","say":"Сколько стоит кофе?","gloss":"сто́ит = costs"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Почему́ ты до́ма?» The natural answer starts with:","opts":["Потому́ что…","Куда́…","Ско́лько…"],"answer":0,"explain":"Почему́ (why) is answered by <b>потому́ что</b> (because)."},{"type":"type","q":"Type the Russian for “why”:","accept":["почему","pochemu","pachemu"],"placeholder":"Why…?","explain":"Почему́ — why. Its answer-partner is потому́ что."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · What kind?","title":"<em>Како́й?</em> — the question that agrees","paras":["<b>Како́й?</b> asks “what kind of?” or “which?”. Because it leans on a noun, it dresses for that noun's gender, just like an adjective: <b>како́й</b> (m), <b>кака́я</b> (f), <b>како́е</b> (n), <b>каки́е</b> (plural).","So: <b>Како́й фильм?</b> (which film?), <b>Кака́я му́зыка?</b> (what music?), <b>Како́е кафе́?</b> (which café?), <b>Каки́е кни́ги?</b> (which books?). Match the ending to the thing, exactly as with но́вый / но́вая / но́вое.","And a handy frozen one for every morning: <b>Како́й сего́дня день?</b> — what day is it today?"],"audio":"Kakoy asks what kind of, or which. Because it leans on a noun, it dresses for that noun's gender, just like an adjective: kakoy for masculine, kakaya for feminine, kakoye for neuter, kakiye for plural. So: kakoy film? kakaya moozyka? kakoye kafe? kakiye knigi? Match the ending to the thing, exactly as with novy, novaya, novoye. And a handy frozen one for every morning: kakoy sevodnya dyen — what day is it today?","table":{"rows":[["како́й фильм? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"какой фильм?\">🔊</button>","which film? (m)"],["кака́я му́зыка? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"какая музыка?\">🔊</button>","what music? (f)"],["како́е кафе́? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"какое кафе?\">🔊</button>","which café? (n)"],["каки́е кни́ги? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"какие книги?\">🔊</button>","which books? (pl)"]],"star":0},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Кака́я фильм?","right":"Како́й фильм?","why":"Фильм is masculine, so the question word agrees: <b>како́й</b>. Кака́я is for feminine things — кака́я му́зыка."}],"examples":[{"ru":"What music do you like?","en":"<b>Кака́я</b> му́зыка тебе́ нра́вится?","say":"Какая музыка тебе нравится?"},{"ru":"What day is it today? — Wednesday.","en":"<b>Како́й</b> сего́дня день? — Среда́.","say":"Какой сегодня день? — Среда."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Fill in: «___ му́зыка тебе́ нра́вится?»","opts":["Како́й","Кака́я","Каки́е"],"answer":1,"explain":"Му́зыка is feminine → <b>кака́я</b>. Како́й is masculine, каки́е is plural."},{"type":"build","q":"Build the question: <b>“Which film?”</b>","want":"Какой фильм","answer":"Како́й фильм"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"At Ива́н's place, his friend Ка́тя challenges Emma to a rapid-fire question game — only questions allowed. Emma doesn't miss one. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Приве́т! Ты отку́да?","ru":"Hi! Where are you from?"},{"who":"A","en":"Я из А́нглии. А ты?","ru":"I'm from England. And you?"},{"who":"B","en":"Я из Москвы́. Кака́я му́зыка тебе́ нра́вится?","ru":"I'm from Moscow. What music do you like?"},{"who":"A","en":"Ру́сская му́зыка! А куда́ ты идёшь в суббо́ту?","ru":"Russian music! And where are you going on Saturday?"},{"who":"B","en":"В теа́тр. Почему́ ты в Москве́?","ru":"To the theatre. Why are you in Moscow?"},{"who":"A","en":"Потому́ что я люблю́ ру́сский язы́к!","ru":"Because I love the Russian language!"},{"who":"B","en":"Ско́лько тебе́ лет?","ru":"How old are you?"},{"who":"A","en":"Мне два́дцать лет. А кото́рый час?","ru":"I'm twenty. And what time is it?"},{"who":"B","en":"Ха-ха! Уже́ по́здно!","ru":"Ha ha! It's late already! (уже́ = already, по́здно = late)"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Your complete question toolkit — twelve cards. Tap to flip, tap 🔊 to hear.","items":[{"en":"вопро́с","ru":"question","ex":"[va-PROS] — a question"},{"en":"кто","ru":"who","ex":"[kto] — Кто э́то?"},{"en":"что","ru":"what","ex":"[shto] — Что э́то?"},{"en":"как","ru":"how","ex":"[kak] — Как тебя́ зову́т?"},{"en":"где","ru":"where","ex":"[gdye] — Где ты?"},{"en":"куда́","ru":"where to","ex":"[koo-DA] — Куда́ ты идёшь?"},{"en":"когда́","ru":"when","ex":"[kag-DA] — Когда́ фильм?"},{"en":"почему́","ru":"why","ex":"[pa-chee-MOO] — Почему́ ты до́ма?"},{"en":"потому́ что","ru":"because","ex":"[pa-ta-MOO shta] — потому́ что суббо́та"},{"en":"како́й","ru":"what kind, which","ex":"[ka-KOY] — Како́й фильм?"},{"en":"ско́лько","ru":"how much, how many","ex":"[SKOL-ka] — Ско́лько тебе́ лет?"},{"en":"Ско́лько сто́ит?","ru":"how much does it cost?","ex":"[SKOL-ka STO-eet] — in a shop or café"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning on the right — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Куда́ ты идёшь?","Where are you going?"],["Когда́ фильм?","When is the film?"],["Почему́ ты до́ма?","Why are you home?"],["Кака́я му́зыка?","What music?"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions about questions. Melody makes them — no “do” anywhere.","items":[{"q":"How do you ask “Do you like coffee?” in Russian?","opts":["До ты лю́бишь ко́фе?","Ты лю́бишь ко́фе?","Ты ко́фе лю́бишь до?"],"answer":1},{"q":"«___ ты идёшь?» — you want to ask where someone is heading:","opts":["Где","Куда́","Когда́"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Почему́?» is answered by:","opts":["Потому́ что…","Пока́…","Пожа́луйста…"],"answer":0},{"q":"Fill in: «___ фильм тебе́ нра́вится?»","opts":["Кака́я","Каки́е","Како́й"],"answer":2},{"q":"Which sentence is a correct Russian question?","opts":["До она́ рабо́тает?","Она́ рабо́тает?","Она́ рабо́тать?"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Ка́тя's little questionnaire about Emma, pinned to the café wall. Read it aloud — every line is a question and its answer.","title":"Пять вопро́сов","sentences":["Как тебя́ зову́т? — Меня́ зову́т Э́мма.","Отку́да ты? — Я из А́нглии.","Почему́ ты в Москве́? — Потому́ что я люблю́ ру́сский язы́к.","Кака́я му́зыка тебе́ нра́вится? — Ру́сская и класси́ческая.","Куда́ ты идёшь в суббо́ту? — В кафе́ и в парк."],"translation":"What's your name? — My name is Emma. Where are you from? — I'm from England. Why are you in Moscow? — Because I love the Russian language. What music do you like? — Russian and classical. Where are you going on Saturday? — To the café and the park. (класси́ческая = classical)"},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to be curious.","prompt":"Write 4 questions you'd ask a new Russian friend — use a different question word in each: try <b>как, отку́да, где / куда́, когда́, почему́</b> or <b>како́й</b>. No “do” anywhere — just the word and a question mark.","hint":"Frames: Как тебя́ зову́т? · Отку́да ты? · Куда́ ты идёшь? · Кака́я му́зыка тебе́ нра́вится? Pick a different word each time 🙂","example":"Как тебя́ зову́т? Отку́да ты? Кака́я му́зыка тебе́ нра́вится? Почему́ ты в Москве́?"}},"21":{"day":21,"week":"03","themeRu":"Review 3","themeEn":"Повторе́ние 3","intro":"Three weeks in Moscow, and look how far Emma's come: she finds any place on the map, says what she likes, plans her week, moves through the city on foot and by metro, orders in cafés, and fires off questions like a local. Today is the week-three victory lap — nothing new, just everything you've built since day fifteen, polished until it shines.","introAudio":"Three weeks in Moscow, and look how far Emma's come: she finds any place on the map, says what she likes, plans her week, moves through the city on foot and by metro, orders in cafés, and fires off questions like a local. Today is the week-three victory lap — nothing new, just everything you've built since day fifteen, polished until it shines.","goals":["Place anything on the map: <b>в Москве́, на рабо́те, до́ма</b> — and ask <b>где?</b>","Say what pleases you: <b>Мне нра́вится Москва́</b>, <b>Мне нра́вятся па́рки</b>","Move and aim: <b>Я иду́ в парк</b>, <b>Я е́ду домо́й</b> — куда́ + accusative","Order and ask: <b>Да́йте, пожа́луйста, ко́фе</b>; <b>Куда́? Когда́? Почему́?</b>"],"learned":["Placed things on the map with <b>в / на + -е</b> and <b>до́ма</b>","Said what pleases you: <b>Мне нра́вится / нра́вятся</b> + dative","Moved and aimed: <b>Я иду́ в парк</b>, <b>Я е́ду домо́й</b>","Ordered and asked questions with the whole toolkit"],"review":{"intro":"Five throwbacks before the lap — some run all the way back to week one.","introAudio":"Five throwbacks before the lap — some run all the way back to week one.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the right answer. A wrong tap costs nothing — it's how memory sets.","q":"Pick the right “my” for кни́га (book):","opts":["моя́","мой","моё"],"answer":0,"explain":"Кни́га ends in -а → feminine → <b>моя́</b>. Agreement follows the thing, not the owner."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Ско́лько тебе́ лет?» — you're eighteen (восемна́дцать):","opts":["Я восемна́дцать лет.","Мне восемна́дцать лет.","У меня́ восемна́дцать лет."],"answer":1,"explain":"Age arrives <em>to</em> you: <b>Мне… лет</b> — the dative from week one."},{"type":"choice","q":"“I'm reading a book”:","opts":["Я чита́ешь кни́гу.","Я чита́ю кни́га.","Я чита́ю кни́гу."],"answer":2,"explain":"Object of чита́ю → accusative: <b>кни́гу</b>. And “I” takes чита́ю, not чита́ешь."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — no stress marks needed.","q":"Type: “They speak Russian.”","accept":["они говорят по-русски","они говорят по русски","oni govoryat po-russki","oni govoryat po russki"],"placeholder":"Они́ …","explain":"Они́ говоря́т по-ру́сски — the -ят ending is the и-team's “they”."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“I have a new friend.”</b>","want":"У меня есть новый друг","answer":"У меня́ есть но́вый друг"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Recap 1 · Where?","title":"Place: <em>в Москве́, на рабо́те, до́ма</em>","paras":["To say where something is, dress the place in the prepositional — swap the ending for <b>-е</b> after <b>в</b> or <b>на</b>: <b>в Москве́, в па́рке, в о́фисе, на столе́</b>. The question is <b>где?</b>","Two things to hold onto: the stubborn <b>на</b>-words, star of the list <b>на рабо́те</b>; and the guests that never change — кафе́, метро́: <b>в кафе́, в метро́</b>.","And home has its own word, no preposition: <b>Я до́ма</b>. Building vs state: <b>дом</b> is the house, <b>до́ма</b> is being home."],"audio":"To say where something is, dress the place in the prepositional — swap the ending for ye after v or na: v Maskvye, v parkye, v ofisye, na stolye. The question is gdye. Two things to hold onto: the stubborn na-words, star of the list na rabotye; and the guests that never change — kafe, metro. And home has its own word, no preposition: ya doma. Dom is the house, doma is being home.","table":{"rows":[["Где Ива́н? — в па́рке <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Где Иван? В парке.\">🔊</button>","Where's Ivan? — in the park"],["на рабо́те <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"на работе\">🔊</button>","at work (memorise the на)"],["в кафе́, в метро́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"в кафе, в метро\">🔊</button>","in the café, in the metro (no change)"],["Я до́ма. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я дома.\">🔊</button>","I'm home. (no preposition)"]],"star":1},"examples":[{"ru":"Lena is at work, Emma is in a café.","en":"Ле́на <b>на рабо́те</b>, Э́мма <b>в кафе́</b>.","say":"Лена на работе, Эмма в кафе."},{"ru":"Where's the book? — On the table.","en":"Где кни́га? — <b>На столе́</b>.","say":"Где книга? — На столе."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the right answer. It's all familiar — three weeks of it behind you.","q":"«Ива́н ___.» (Ivan is at work.)","opts":["на рабо́ту","в рабо́те","на рабо́те"],"answer":2,"explain":"Where → prepositional, and рабо́та takes на: <b>на рабо́те</b>."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — no stress marks needed.","q":"Type: “I'm in Moscow.”","accept":["я в москве","ya v moskve","ya v maskve"],"placeholder":"Я в …","explain":"Я в Москве́ — в plus the -е ending."}]},{"eyebrow":"Recap 2 · Taste, time, café","title":"<em>Мне нра́вится</em>, days, and ordering","paras":["Liking flips the sentence: <b>Мне нра́вится Москва́</b> — the liked thing is the subject, you sit in the dative (<b>мне, тебе́, ему́, ей</b>). Several things → <b>нра́вятся</b>: <b>Мне нра́вятся па́рки.</b>","Plans run on little в's: a day takes в + accusative (<b>в суббо́ту</b>), an hour takes в + the number (<b>в два часа́</b>), a place takes в + prepositional (<b>в кафе́</b>). <b>В суббо́ту, в два часа́, я в кафе́.</b>","And in that café you order: <b>Я хочу́ ко́фе</b> or the politer <b>Да́йте, пожа́луйста, во́ду</b> — the wanted thing in the accusative (вода́ → во́ду)."],"audio":"Liking flips the sentence: mnye nravitsa Maskva — the liked thing is the subject, you sit in the dative: mnye, tebye, yemoo, yey. Several things, nravyatsa: mnye nravyatsa parki. Plans run on little v's: a day takes v plus accusative, v soobbotu; an hour takes v plus the number, v dva chasa; a place takes v plus prepositional, v kafe. V soobbotu, v dva chasa, ya v kafe. And in that café you order: ya khachoo kofye, or the politer dayte pazhalusta vodoo — the wanted thing in the accusative: vada becomes vodoo.","table":{"rows":[["Мне нра́вится Москва́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мне нравится Москва.\">🔊</button>","I like Moscow."],["Мне нра́вятся па́рки. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мне нравятся парки.\">🔊</button>","I like the parks. (plural)"],["В суббо́ту, в два часа́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"В субботу, в два часа.\">🔊</button>","On Saturday, at two."],["Да́йте, пожа́луйста, во́ду. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Дайте, пожалуйста, воду.\">🔊</button>","Water, please."]],"star":0},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я нра́вится Москва́.","right":"Мне нра́вится Москва́.","why":"The liker goes in the dative: <b>мне</b>, not я. Москва́ is the subject that pleases you."}],"examples":[{"ru":"She likes music; he likes sport.","en":"<b>Ей</b> нра́вится му́зыка, <b>ему́</b> нра́вится спорт.","say":"Ей нравится музыка, ему нравится спорт."},{"ru":"On Friday at seven I'm at the theatre.","en":"<b>В пя́тницу</b>, <b>в семь часо́в</b>, я <b>в теа́тре</b>.","say":"В пятницу, в семь часов, я в театре."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Мне ___ блины́.» (I like blini.)","opts":["нра́вится","нра́вятся"],"answer":1,"explain":"Блины́ is plural → the verb follows it: <b>нра́вятся</b>."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I like Moscow.”</b>","want":"Мне нравится Москва","answer":"Мне нра́вится Москва́"}]},{"eyebrow":"Recap 3 · Going & asking","title":"<em>куда́?</em> — going, and the question toolkit","paras":["Moving? On foot right now → <b>иду́</b>; a habit → <b>хожу́</b>; on wheels → <b>е́ду</b>. And the destination takes the accusative — <b>куда́?</b> → <b>в парк, домо́й</b> — while a place you're standing in takes the prepositional — <b>где?</b> → <b>в па́рке, до́ма</b>.","The question toolkit is complete: <b>где</b> (where), <b>куда́</b> (where to), <b>когда́</b> (when), <b>почему́</b> (why), <b>како́й</b> (what kind — it agrees), <b>ско́лько</b> (how much).","And every question skips the English “do” — just the words and a rising voice: <b>Ты лю́бишь ко́фе? Куда́ ты идёшь?</b>"],"audio":"Moving? On foot right now, idoo; a habit, khazhoo; on wheels, yedoo. And the destination takes the accusative — kooda — v park, damoy — while a place you're standing in takes the prepositional — gdye — v parkye, doma. The question toolkit is complete: gdye, kooda, kagda, pachemoo, kakoy — which agrees — and skolka. And every question skips the English do — just the words and a rising voice: ty lyubish kofye? kooda ty idyosh?","table":{"rows":[["Куда́? — в парк <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Куда? В парк.\">🔊</button>","Where to? — to the park (accusative)"],["Где? — в па́рке <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Где? В парке.\">🔊</button>","Where? — in the park (prepositional)"],["Ка́ждый день я хожу́… <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Каждый день я хожу…\">🔊</button>","Every day I go… (habit)"],["Ты лю́бишь ко́фе? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Ты любишь кофе?\">🔊</button>","Do you like coffee? (no “do”)"]],"star":0},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я иду́ в па́рке.","right":"Я иду́ в парк.","why":"Motion → destination in the accusative: <b>в парк</b>. В па́рке (prepositional) is for standing still."}],"examples":[{"ru":"Where are you going? — Home.","en":"<b>Куда́</b> ты идёшь? — <b>Домо́й</b>.","say":"Куда ты идёшь? — Домой."},{"ru":"Why are you home? — Because it's Sunday.","en":"<b>Почему́</b> ты до́ма? — <b>Потому́ что</b> воскресе́нье.","say":"Почему ты дома? — Потому что воскресенье."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Я е́ду в ___.» — you're on the metro to Moscow:","opts":["Москве́","Москву́","Москва́"],"answer":1,"explain":"Motion → куда́ → accusative: <b>в Москву́</b>. В Москве́ is “in Moscow” (standing still)."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and ask it out loud. Listen to the sample first if you like.","target":"Куда́ ты идёшь?","sub":"[koo-DA ty ee-DYOSH]","want":"Куда ты идёшь"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Sunday evening, three weeks in. Ива́н quizzes Emma on her whole Moscow life — and she answers every question without a stumble. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, где ты сейча́с?","ru":"Emma, where are you right now?"},{"who":"A","en":"Я в кафе́ у Ле́ны, в це́нтре.","ru":"I'm at Lena's café, in the centre."},{"who":"B","en":"Тебе́ нра́вится Москва́?","ru":"Do you like Moscow?"},{"who":"A","en":"О́чень! Мне нра́вятся па́рки, метро́ и сы́рники.","ru":"Very much! I like the parks, the metro and sirniki."},{"who":"B","en":"Куда́ ты идёшь в суббо́ту?","ru":"Where are you going on Saturday?"},{"who":"A","en":"В теа́тр, в семь часо́в. Я е́ду на метро́.","ru":"To the theatre, at seven. I'm going by metro."},{"who":"B","en":"Почему́ ты лю́бишь Москву́?","ru":"Why do you love Moscow?"},{"who":"A","en":"Потому́ что здесь мои́ друзья́!","ru":"Because my friends are here! (здесь = here)"},{"who":"B","en":"Отли́чно, Э́мма! За́втра — но́вая неде́ля.","ru":"Brilliant, Emma! Tomorrow, a new week."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"No new words — twelve of week three's best, back on the table. If a card feels soft, that's your homework.","items":[{"en":"где","ru":"where","ex":"[gdye] — Где ты?"},{"en":"куда́","ru":"where to","ex":"[koo-DA] — Куда́ ты идёшь?"},{"en":"до́ма","ru":"at home","ex":"[DO-ma] — Я до́ма."},{"en":"домо́й","ru":"home(ward)","ex":"[da-MOY] — Я иду́ домо́й."},{"en":"мне нра́вится","ru":"I like (it)","ex":"[mnye NRA-veet-sa] — Мне нра́вится Москва́."},{"en":"идти́","ru":"to go (on foot)","ex":"[ee-TEE] — Я иду́ в парк."},{"en":"е́хать","ru":"to go (by transport)","ex":"[YE-khat] — Я е́ду домо́й."},{"en":"хочу́","ru":"I want","ex":"[kha-CHOO] — Я хочу́ ко́фе."},{"en":"Да́йте, пожа́луйста","ru":"give (me), please","ex":"[DAY-tye pa-ZHA-loo-sta] — the polite order"},{"en":"когда́","ru":"when","ex":"[kag-DA] — Когда́ фильм?"},{"en":"почему́","ru":"why","ex":"[pa-chee-MOO] — Почему́ ты до́ма?"},{"en":"ка́ждый день","ru":"every day","ex":"[KA-zhdy dyen] — Я хожу́ в парк ка́ждый день."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning on the right — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Мне нра́вится Москва́.","I like Moscow."],["Я иду́ в парк.","I'm going to the park."],["Да́йте, пожа́луйста, ко́фе.","A coffee, please."],["Куда́ ты идёшь?","Where are you going?"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Eight questions — the whole of week three on one tray. If one bites, the rule is one scroll up.","items":[{"q":"«Ле́на ___.» (Lena is at work.)","opts":["на рабо́те","на рабо́ту","в рабо́те"],"answer":0},{"q":"“I like the parks”:","opts":["Мне нра́вится па́рки.","Мне нра́вятся па́рки.","Мне нра́вится па́рке."],"answer":1},{"q":"“On Wednesday” is:","opts":["в среда́","в сре́ду","на сре́ду"],"answer":1},{"q":"Emma is walking to the café right now:","opts":["Я е́ду в кафе́.","Я хожу́ в кафе́.","Я иду́ в кафе́."],"answer":2},{"q":"«Я иду́ в ___.» — to the park:","opts":["парк","па́рке","па́рка"],"answer":0},{"q":"The polite way to order water:","opts":["Я во́ду.","Да́йте, пожа́луйста, во́ду.","Да́йте во́да."],"answer":1},{"q":"«Магази́н» means:","opts":["a magazine","a shop","a menu"],"answer":1},{"q":"How do you ask “Do you like coffee?”","opts":["Ты лю́бишь ко́фе?","До ты лю́бишь ко́фе?","Ты ко́фе до лю́бишь?"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's end-of-week-three postcard home — the fullest one yet. Read it aloud; you own every line.","title":"Три неде́ли в Москве́","sentences":["Я в Москве́ уже́ три неде́ли.","Мне о́чень нра́вится го́род: па́рки, метро́ и кафе́.","Ка́ждый день я хожу́ в университе́т, а пото́м иду́ в кафе́.","В суббо́ту я е́ду в центр на метро́.","Ле́на в кафе́ говори́т: «Что ты хо́чешь?» — «Да́йте, пожа́луйста, ко́фе и моро́женое!»","Почему́ мне нра́вится Москва́? Потому́ что здесь мои́ друзья́."],"translation":"I've been in Moscow for three weeks already. I really like the city: the parks, the metro and the cafés. Every day I go to the university, and then I go to a café. On Saturday I go to the centre by metro. At the café Lena says: “What would you like?” — “Coffee and ice cream, please!” Why do I like Moscow? Because my friends are here. (уже́ = already, пото́м = then, здесь = here)"},"essay":{"intro":"Your week-three victory lap, in writing.","prompt":"Write 4–5 sentences about your Moscow week: where you are (в / на + -е), what you like (мне нра́вится / нра́вятся), where you go and how (иду́ / е́ду / хожу́ + куда́), and one café order (Да́йте, пожа́луйста…). Slip in one question if you can.","hint":"Lean on your frames: Я в… · Мне нра́вится / нра́вятся… · Ка́ждый день я хожу́ в… · В суббо́ту я е́ду в… · Да́йте, пожа́луйста… Motion pulls the accusative: в парк, not в па́рке 🙂","example":"Я в Москве́ уже́ три неде́ли. Мне нра́вятся па́рки и метро́. Ка́ждый день я хожу́ в университе́т, а в суббо́ту я е́ду в центр. В кафе́ я говорю́: «Да́йте, пожа́луйста, ко́фе». Почему́ мне здесь хорошо́? Потому́ что я люблю́ Москву́!"}},"22":{"day":22,"week":"04","themeRu":"My home","themeEn":"Мой дом","intro":"Ива́н has been promising Emma a tour of the family flat all week, and today's the day. You'll walk through it with her — the rooms, the furniture, and one small grammar trap hiding on every surface: Russian's <b>на</b> for “on”. You already own the prepositional case from week three; today it furnishes a whole home.","introAudio":"Ivan has been promising Emma a tour of the family flat all week, and today's the day. You'll walk through it with her — the rooms, the furniture, and one small grammar trap hiding on every surface: Russian's na, for on. You already own the prepositional case from week three; today it furnishes a whole home.","goals":["Name the rooms of a home: <b>ко́мната, ку́хня, спа́льня</b>","Say where things are with <b>на</b> for surfaces: <b>на столе́, на дива́не</b>","Furnish a room and make it agree: <b>мой стол, моя́ крова́ть, моё окно́</b>","Describe your own room in a few warm sentences"],"learned":["Named the rooms of a home: <b>ко́мната, ку́хня, спа́льня</b>","Said where things are with <b>на</b> for surfaces: <b>на столе́, на дива́не</b>","Furnished a room with agreement: <b>мой стол, моя́ крова́ть, моё окно́</b>","Described your own room in Russian"],"review":{"intro":"Three quick throwbacks before we open the front door — gender, a beloved city, and a plan for the weekend.","introAudio":"Three quick throwbacks before we open the front door — gender, a beloved city, and a plan for the weekend.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the situation and tap the right answer. A wrong tap costs nothing — that's how memory sets.","q":"Pick the right “my” for кни́га (book):","opts":["мой","моя́","моё"],"answer":1,"explain":"Кни́га ends in -а — she's feminine, so <b>моя́</b>. Agreement follows the thing's gender, never the owner's."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — no stress marks needed.","q":"Type the Russian for “I love Moscow” — aim the ending:","accept":["я люблю москву","люблю москву","ya lyublyu moskvu","ya lublu moskvu"],"placeholder":"Я люблю́ …","explain":"Я люблю́ Москву́ — the -а of Москва́ bends to -у under the verb."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“On Sunday I'm in the park.”</b>","want":"В воскресенье я в парке","answer":"В воскресе́нье я в па́рке"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Rooms","title":"<em>Кварти́ра</em> and its rooms — the prepositional, again","paras":["Every home is a set of little boxes, and Russian has a word for each. A <b>кварти́ра</b> is a flat — most Muscovites live in one, not in a house. Inside are the <b>ко́мнаты</b> (rooms): the <b>спа́льня</b> (bedroom), the <b>ку́хня</b> (kitchen), the <b>ва́нная</b> (bathroom).","To say where you are, reach for the case you built in week three: <b>в</b> + the place with an <b>-е</b> ending. Ко́мната → <b>в ко́мнате</b>, спа́льня → <b>в спа́льне</b>. No new grammar — just new walls to hang it on.","One quiet member breaks ranks: the kitchen takes <b>на</b>, not в. You'll almost always hear <b>на ку́хне</b> — file it next to на рабо́те from week three, a set phrase you simply learn."],"audio":"Every home is a set of little boxes, and Russian has a word for each. A kvartira is a flat — most Muscovites live in one, not in a house. Inside are the komnaty, the rooms: the spalnya, bedroom; the kukhnya, kitchen; the vannaya, bathroom. To say where you are, reach for the case you built in week three: v plus the place with an ye ending. Komnata becomes v komnate, spalnya becomes v spalnye. No new grammar — just new walls to hang it on. One quiet member breaks ranks: the kitchen takes na, not v. You'll almost always hear na kukhnye — file it next to na rabotye, a set phrase you simply learn.","table":{"rows":[["кварти́ра → в кварти́ре <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"квартира — в квартире\">🔊</button>","flat → in the flat"],["ко́мната → в ко́мнате <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"комната — в комнате\">🔊</button>","room → in the room"],["спа́льня → в спа́льне <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"спальня — в спальне\">🔊</button>","bedroom → in the bedroom"],["ку́хня → на ку́хне <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"кухня — на кухне\">🔊</button>","kitchen → in the kitchen (на!)"],["ва́нная → в ва́нной <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ванная — в ванной\">🔊</button>","bathroom → in the bathroom"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"Emma is in the room.","en":"Э́мма <b>в ко́мнате</b>.","say":"Эмма в комнате."},{"ru":"Lena is in the kitchen.","en":"Ле́на <b>на ку́хне</b>.","say":"Лена на кухне.","gloss":"kitchen joins рабо́та on the на-list"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Крова́ть в ___.» — the bed is in the bedroom. Which form?","opts":["спа́льня","спа́льне","спа́льню"],"answer":1,"explain":"Place after <b>в</b> takes the -е ending: <b>в спа́льне</b>. Спа́льня is the naming form; спа́льню is the object form."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“In the room there's a sofa and a table.”</b>","want":"В комнате диван и стол","answer":"В ко́мнате дива́н и стол"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · On the surface","title":"<em>на столе́</em> — в is inside, на is on top","paras":["Now lower your eyes from the walls to the surfaces. English uses one word — <em>on</em> the table, <em>on</em> the sofa. Russian uses <b>на</b> plus the same -е ending: <b>на столе́</b> (on the table), <b>на дива́не</b> (on the sofa), <b>на стене́</b> (on the wall).","Keep <b>в</b> and <b>на</b> honest: <b>в</b> is inside something with walls, <b>на</b> is on top of a surface. Кни́га <b>в столе́</b> means the book is shut inside the table; кни́га <b>на столе́</b> means it's lying on top. One letter, a whole different picture.","This is the day's trap. English “on” and “at” cover both в and на, so nothing warns you which to pick. The habit that saves you: learn each place word together with its little preposition, the way you learned на рабо́те."],"audio":"Now lower your eyes from the walls to the surfaces. English uses one word — on the table, on the sofa. Russian uses na plus the same ye ending: na stolye, on the table; na divanye, on the sofa; na stenye, on the wall. Keep v and na honest: v is inside something with walls, na is on top of a surface. Kniga v stolye means the book is shut inside the table; kniga na stolye means it's lying on top. One letter, a whole different picture. This is the day's trap. English on and at cover both, so nothing warns you which to pick. The habit that saves you: learn each place word together with its little preposition, the way you learned na rabotye.","table":{"rows":[["стол → на столе́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"стол — на столе\">🔊</button>","table → on the table"],["дива́н → на дива́не <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"диван — на диване\">🔊</button>","sofa → on the sofa"],["стена́ → на стене́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"стена — на стене\">🔊</button>","wall → on the wall"],["по́лка → на по́лке <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"полка — на полке\">🔊</button>","shelf → on the shelf"]],"star":0},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Кни́га в столе́.","right":"Кни́га на столе́.","why":"На is the surface: the book lies on top. В столе́ shuts it inside the table — a magician's trick, not a normal sentence."}],"examples":[{"ru":"The lamp is on the table.","en":"Ла́мпа <b>на столе́</b>.","say":"Лампа на столе."},{"ru":"My book is on the sofa.","en":"Моя́ кни́га <b>на дива́не</b>.","say":"Моя книга на диване.","gloss":"surface → на + the -е ending"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Where's the book? Lying on top of the table:","opts":["Кни́га на стол.","Кни́га на столе́.","Кни́га в столе́."],"answer":1,"explain":"On a surface → <b>на</b> + the -е ending: <b>на столе́</b>. На стол would be motion (onto the table); в столе́ hides it inside."},{"type":"type","q":"Type: “Lena is in the kitchen.” — remember the stubborn preposition:","accept":["лена на кухне","lena na kuhne","lena na kukhne","она на кухне"],"placeholder":"Ле́на …","explain":"Ку́хня takes <b>на</b>, like рабо́та: <b>на ку́хне</b>. В ку́хне is possible but you'll rarely hear it."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Furniture has gender","title":"<em>мой стол, моя́ крова́ть, моё окно́</em>","paras":["Every piece of furniture has a gender, and it decides how the words around it dress. <b>Стол</b> and <b>дива́н</b> end in a consonant — masculine, «он»: <b>мой стол</b>, <b>но́вый дива́н</b>. <b>Окно́</b> ends in -о — neuter, «оно́»: <b>моё окно́</b>.","The tricky ones end in a soft sign <b>-ь</b>. You can't tell their gender by looking, so you simply learn it. <b>Крова́ть</b> (bed) and <b>дверь</b> (door) are both feminine: <b>моя́ крова́ть</b>, <b>но́вая дверь</b>.","Your English ear hears no gender in “bed” or “table”, so nothing warns you. Pair every new furniture word with its «мой / моя́ / моё» from the very start, and agreement takes care of itself."],"audio":"Every piece of furniture has a gender, and it decides how the words around it dress. Stol and divan end in a consonant — masculine, on: moy stol, novy divan. Okno ends in o — neuter, ono: moyo okno. The tricky ones end in a soft sign. You can't tell their gender by looking, so you simply learn it. Krovat, bed, and dver, door, are both feminine: moya krovat, novaya dver. Your English ear hears no gender in bed or table, so nothing warns you. Pair every new furniture word with its moy, moya or moyo from the very start, and agreement takes care of itself.","table":{"rows":[["стол — мой стол <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"стол — мой стол\">🔊</button>","table → masculine (он)"],["крова́ть — моя́ крова́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"кровать — моя кровать\">🔊</button>","bed → feminine (она́)"],["окно́ — моё окно́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"окно — моё окно\">🔊</button>","window → neuter (оно́)"],["дверь — моя́ дверь <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дверь — моя дверь\">🔊</button>","door → feminine (она́)"]],"star":1},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"мой крова́ть","right":"моя́ крова́ть","why":"Крова́ть ends in a soft sign and happens to be feminine — so <b>моя́</b>, and но́вая крова́ть. The soft sign hides the gender; you memorise it."}],"examples":[{"ru":"This is my new table.","en":"Э́то <b>мой но́вый</b> стол.","say":"Это мой новый стол."},{"ru":"My bed is new.","en":"Моя́ крова́ть <b>но́вая</b>.","say":"Моя кровать новая.","gloss":"feminine all the way through"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which “my” goes with крова́ть (bed)? It's feminine.","opts":["моя́","мой","моё"],"answer":0,"explain":"Крова́ть is feminine despite the soft sign → <b>моя́</b>. Мой is for masculine things, моё for neuter."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“This is my new sofa.”</b>","want":"Это мой новый диван","answer":"Э́то мой но́вый дива́н"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Furnish a room","title":"Putting it together: rooms with <em>в</em>, surfaces with <em>на</em>","paras":["Time to move the furniture in. A description just stacks what you own: name the room with <b>в</b>, then list what's inside — no verb needed, exactly like Чай на столе́. <b>В ко́мнате дива́н, стол и ла́мпа.</b>","Want to point out that something exists? Add <b>есть</b> — the same “there is” from у меня́ есть: <b>В спа́льне есть окно́.</b> In a plain list Russians usually drop it; reach for есть when you're answering “is there a…?”.","Now you can walk anyone through your home: room by room with в, surface by surface with на. That's a whole flat built from week-three grammar."],"audio":"Time to move the furniture in. A description just stacks what you own: name the room with v, then list what's inside — no verb needed, exactly like chai na stolye. V komnate divan, stol i lampa. Want to point out that something exists? Add yest — the same there is from u menya yest: v spalnye yest okno. In a plain list Russians usually drop it; reach for yest when you're answering is there a. Now you can walk anyone through your home: room by room with v, surface by surface with na. That's a whole flat built from week-three grammar.","examples":[{"ru":"In the room there's a sofa, a table and a lamp.","en":"<b>В ко́мнате</b> дива́н, стол и ла́мпа.","say":"В комнате диван, стол и лампа."},{"ru":"Is there a window in the bedroom? — Yes.","en":"<b>В спа́льне есть</b> окно́? — Да.","say":"В спальне есть окно? — Да."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Emma describes her room. Which sentence is right?","opts":["В ко́мнате дива́н и стол.","В ко́мнату дива́н и стол.","На ко́мнате дива́н и стол."],"answer":0,"explain":"A location → <b>в</b> + the -е ending: <b>в ко́мнате</b>. В ко́мнату is the motion form; на is for surfaces, not rooms."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say the phrase out loud. Listen to the sample first if you like.","target":"На столе́ ла́мпа и кни́ги.","sub":"one calm breath — everything sitting on the table","want":"На столе лампа и книги"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ива́н finally shows Emma the family flat. She walks from room to room, naming everything she sees. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, э́то моя́ кварти́ра.","ru":"Emma, this is my flat."},{"who":"A","en":"О́чень хоро́шая кварти́ра! Где ку́хня?","ru":"A very nice flat! Where's the kitchen?"},{"who":"B","en":"Ку́хня там. Ле́на на ку́хне — чай и блины́!","ru":"The kitchen's there. Lena's in the kitchen — tea and blini! (там = there)"},{"who":"A","en":"А э́то спа́льня?","ru":"And is this the bedroom?"},{"who":"B","en":"Да. В спа́льне крова́ть, стол и ла́мпа.","ru":"Yes. In the bedroom there's a bed, a table and a lamp."},{"who":"A","en":"Кака́я больша́я крова́ть!","ru":"What a big bed! (больша́я = big)"},{"who":"B","en":"А на столе́ — моя́ но́вая кни́га.","ru":"And on the table is my new book."},{"who":"A","en":"Мне о́чень нра́вится кварти́ра!","ru":"I really like the flat!"},{"who":"B","en":"Спаси́бо! До́ма хорошо́.","ru":"Thanks! It's nice at home."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words to furnish a home. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"кварти́ра","ru":"flat, apartment","ex":"Моя́ кварти́ра в це́нтре."},{"en":"ко́мната","ru":"room","ex":"В ко́мнате дива́н и стол."},{"en":"ку́хня","ru":"kitchen","ex":"Ле́на на ку́хне."},{"en":"спа́льня","ru":"bedroom","ex":"Крова́ть в спа́льне."},{"en":"ва́нная","ru":"bathroom","ex":"Э́то ва́нная."},{"en":"стол","ru":"table (masculine)","ex":"Ла́мпа на столе́."},{"en":"стул","ru":"chair (masculine)","ex":"Э́то мой но́вый стул."},{"en":"крова́ть","ru":"bed (feminine!)","ex":"Моя́ крова́ть но́вая."},{"en":"дива́н","ru":"sofa (masculine)","ex":"Я до́ма, на дива́не."},{"en":"окно́","ru":"window (neuter)","ex":"Окно́ в ко́мнате."},{"en":"дверь","ru":"door (feminine!)","ex":"Э́то дверь."},{"en":"ла́мпа","ru":"lamp","ex":"На столе́ ла́мпа."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning on the right — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Ла́мпа на столе́.","The lamp is on the table."],["Ле́на на ку́хне.","Lena is in the kitchen."],["Дива́н в ко́мнате.","The sofa is in the room."],["Моя́ крова́ть но́вая.","My bed is new."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five quick questions — today's home and nothing else. If one bites, the rule above is one scroll away.","items":[{"q":"“The lamp is on the table.”","opts":["Ла́мпа на стол.","Ла́мпа на столе́.","Ла́мпа в столе́."],"answer":1},{"q":"Which “my” fits крова́ть (bed)?","opts":["моя́","мой","моё"],"answer":0},{"q":"“The book is on the sofa.”","opts":["Кни́га на дива́н.","Кни́га на дива́не.","Кни́га в дива́не."],"answer":1},{"q":"“In the bedroom” is:","opts":["в спа́льня","на спа́льне","в спа́льне"],"answer":2},{"q":"What does «на столе́» mean?","opts":["in the table","on the table","under the table"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note about her room in Moscow — read it aloud, slowly. You know every word.","title":"Моя́ ко́мната","sentences":["Э́то моя́ ко́мната в Москве́.","В ко́мнате крова́ть, стол и дива́н.","На столе́ ла́мпа, ка́рта и но́вые кни́ги.","На ку́хне — Ле́на: чай и блины́!","Мне о́чень нра́вится моя́ ко́мната."],"translation":"This is my room in Moscow. In the room there's a bed, a table and a sofa. On the table are a lamp, a map and new books. In the kitchen — Lena: tea and blini! I really like my room."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to give someone a tour.","prompt":"Describe your room in 3–4 sentences: name the room, list two or three pieces of furniture in it (в …), and say what's on the table (на столе́). Make at least one «мой / моя́ / моё» agree.","hint":"Frames: В ко́мнате дива́н, стол и … · На столе́ … · Моя́ крова́ть / мой стол. Rooms take в + -е; surfaces take на + -е 🙂","example":"Э́то моя́ ко́мната. В ко́мнате крова́ть, стол и дива́н. На столе́ ла́мпа и кни́ги. Моя́ крова́ть но́вая, и мне о́чень нра́вится моя́ ко́мната."}},"23":{"day":23,"week":"04","themeRu":"In the city","themeEn":"В го́роде","intro":"Three weeks ago, «Ты где?» left Emma speechless. Today she crosses Moscow on her own — and answers a lost tourist who stops her for directions. The key is one contrast English keeps hidden: <b>где</b> asks where you <em>are</em> (в па́рке), <b>куда́</b> asks where you're <em>going</em> (в парк). One little “to”, two Russian cases. Let's make this one stick.","introAudio":"Three weeks ago, ty gdye — where are you — left Emma speechless. Today she crosses Moscow on her own, and answers a lost tourist who stops her for directions. The key is one contrast English keeps hidden: gdye asks where you are — v parkye — and kuda asks where you're going — v park. One little to, two Russian cases. Let's make this one stick.","goals":["Tell <b>где</b> (where you are) from <b>куда́</b> (where you're going)","Answer both: <b>в па́рке</b> for place, <b>в парк</b> for motion","Give directions: <b>Иди́те пря́мо, поверни́те напра́во / нале́во</b>","Stop a stranger politely and ask the way: <b>Извини́те, где…?</b>"],"learned":["Told <b>где</b> (where you are) from <b>куда́</b> (where you're going)","Answered both: <b>в па́рке</b> for place, <b>в парк</b> for motion","Gave directions: <b>Иди́те пря́мо, поверни́те напра́во / нале́во</b>","Stopped a stranger and asked the way: <b>Извини́те, где…?</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Three warm-ups before you hit the streets — yesterday's furniture, and two old cases that never really leave.","introAudio":"Three warm-ups before you hit the streets — yesterday's furniture, and two old cases that never really leave.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the right answer. Wrong taps teach too.","q":"Pick the right “my” for дива́н (sofa) — it's masculine:","opts":["мой","моя́","моё"],"answer":0,"explain":"Дива́н ends in a consonant — masculine, so <b>мой</b>. Yesterday's furniture, still agreeing."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — no stress marks needed.","q":"Type: “I'm going to the park.” (on foot)","accept":["я иду в парк","иду в парк","ya idu v park"],"placeholder":"Я иду́ в …"},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“On Saturday I'm at the café.”</b>","want":"В субботу я в кафе","answer":"В суббо́ту я в кафе́"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Where vs where-to","title":"<em>где</em> (в па́рке) or <em>куда́</em> (в парк)?","paras":["Here is the contrast that trips up every English speaker, because English hides it. “I'm <em>in</em> the park” and “I'm going <em>to</em> the park” — two little words, in and to. Russian asks two different questions and answers with two different cases.","<b>Где?</b> — where? — asks about a location. The answer sits still: <b>в па́рке</b>, the prepositional case with -е. <b>Куда́?</b> — where to? — asks about a destination, a direction of motion. The answer moves: <b>в парк</b>, the accusative, the plain dictionary form for these words.","So one English “the park” splits in two: <b>Я в па́рке</b> — I'm in the park (standing there). <b>Я иду́ в парк</b> — I'm going to the park (heading there). Same в, same word — but motion strips the ending off. Where you <em>are</em> dresses up (-е); where you're <em>going</em> travels light (plain form)."],"audio":"Here is the contrast that trips up every English speaker, because English hides it. I'm in the park, and I'm going to the park — two little words, in and to. Russian asks two different questions and answers with two different cases. Gdye — where — asks about a location. The answer sits still: v parkye, the prepositional with ye. Kuda — where to — asks about a destination. The answer moves: v park, the accusative, the plain dictionary form. So one English the park splits in two: ya v parkye, I'm in the park; ya idu v park, I'm going to the park. Same v, same word — but motion strips the ending off. Where you are dresses up; where you're going travels light.","mnemonic":"<b>Где</b> = staying → the word dresses up (в па́рке). <b>Куда́</b> = moving → the word travels light (в парк).","table":{"rows":[["Где ты? — в па́рке <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Где ты? — в парке\">🔊</button>","Where are you? — in the park (place)"],["Куда́ ты? — в парк <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Куда ты? — в парк\">🔊</button>","Where to? — to the park (motion)"],["Я в шко́ле. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я в школе.\">🔊</button>","I'm at school. (где — prepositional)"],["Я иду́ в шко́лу. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я иду в школу.\">🔊</button>","I'm going to school. (куда — accusative)"]],"star":1},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я иду́ в па́рке.","right":"Я иду́ в парк.","why":"Motion answers куда́, not где — so the accusative: <b>в парк</b>. В па́рке is for standing still. English “to the park” gives you no warning."},{"wrong":"Я в парк.","right":"Я в па́рке.","why":"No motion here — you're simply located. Где → the prepositional: <b>в па́рке</b>. Save в парк for when you're on your way."}],"examples":[{"ru":"I'm at school. — I'm going to school.","en":"Я <b>в шко́ле</b>. — Я иду́ <b>в шко́лу</b>.","say":"Я в школе. — Я иду в школу.","gloss":"place: -е · motion: -у"},{"ru":"Emma's going to the centre.","en":"Э́мма е́дет <b>в центр</b>.","say":"Эмма едет в центр.","gloss":"е́дет — she's going (by metro); центр doesn't change"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Ива́н texts «Куда́ ты?» — you're heading to the park. Reply:","opts":["Я в па́рке.","Я иду́ в парк.","Я иду́ в па́рке."],"answer":1,"explain":"Куда́ = motion → the accusative: <b>в парк</b>. Я в па́рке answers где (standing); Я иду́ в па́рке crosses the wires."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I'm at school.”</b> (standing there)","want":"Я в школе","answer":"Я в шко́ле"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Getting around","title":"City places — and <em>иду́ / е́ду</em> take куда́","paras":["Moscow is bigger than a flat, so here are the places you'll aim for: <b>магази́н</b> (shop — not “magazine”!), <b>апте́ка</b> (pharmacy), <b>банк</b> (bank), <b>пло́щадь</b> (square), <b>вокза́л</b> (train station).","To say you're heading somewhere, dust off week three's motion verbs: <b>иду́</b> on foot, <b>е́ду</b> by transport — both answer куда́ with the accusative. <b>Я иду́ в магази́н. Я е́ду в центр.</b>","Feminine -а places bend to -у for the destination: апте́ка → <b>в апте́ку</b>. Masculine ones don't change: <b>в магази́н, в банк, в центр</b>. Same rule as reading a book — the target of your motion takes the accusative."],"audio":"Moscow is bigger than a flat, so here are the places you'll aim for: magazin, shop — not magazine — apteka, pharmacy; bank; ploshchad, square; vokzal, train station. To say you're heading somewhere, dust off week three's motion verbs: idu on foot, yedu by transport — both answer kuda with the accusative. Ya idu v magazin. Ya yedu v tsentr. Feminine a-places bend to u for the destination: apteka becomes v apteku. Masculine ones don't change: v magazin, v bank, v tsentr. Same rule as reading a book — the target of your motion takes the accusative.","table":{"rows":[["магази́н → в магази́н <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"магазин — в магазин\">🔊</button>","shop → to the shop"],["апте́ка → в апте́ку <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"аптека — в аптеку\">🔊</button>","pharmacy → to the pharmacy"],["банк → в банк <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"банк — в банк\">🔊</button>","bank → to the bank"],["центр → в центр <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"центр — в центр\">🔊</button>","centre → to the centre"]],"star":1},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Я иду́ в магази́не.","right":"Я иду́ в магази́н.","why":"You're on your way, not there yet — куда́ takes the accusative: <b>в магази́н</b>. В магази́не is where you'd say you already are."}],"examples":[{"ru":"I'm going to the pharmacy.","en":"Я иду́ <b>в апте́ку</b>.","say":"Я иду в аптеку.","gloss":"апте́ка → в апте́ку (accusative -у)"},{"ru":"Ivan's going to the shop.","en":"Ива́н идёт <b>в магази́н</b>.","say":"Иван идёт в магазин.","gloss":"магази́н = shop, a classic false friend"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Я е́ду в ___.» — you're taking the metro to the centre.","opts":["центр","це́нтре","це́нтру"],"answer":0,"explain":"Motion → the accusative, and центр (masculine) doesn't change: <b>в центр</b>. Це́нтре is the standing-still ending."},{"type":"type","q":"Type: “I'm going to the shop.” (on foot)","accept":["я иду в магазин","иду в магазин","ya idu v magazin"],"placeholder":"Я иду́ в …","explain":"Куда́ + accusative, and магази́н doesn't change: <b>Я иду́ в магази́н</b>."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Directions","title":"<em>Иди́те пря́мо, поверни́те напра́во / нале́во</em>","paras":["When you ask a Russian the way, you'll hear three magic words. <b>Иди́те пря́мо</b> — go straight. <b>Поверни́те напра́во</b> — turn right. <b>Поверни́те нале́во</b> — turn left. These are polite commands, the -ите form you'd use with a stranger.","The directions themselves are worth memorising as a set: <b>пря́мо</b> (straight ahead), <b>напра́во</b> (to the right), <b>нале́во</b> (to the left). And to say where something already sits, switch to <b>спра́ва</b> (on the right) and <b>сле́ва</b> (on the left).","Notice the echo of today's lesson: the <em>-о</em> words point where to move (напра́во, нале́во), the <em>-а</em> words say where things rest (спра́ва, сле́ва). Motion versus place, one more time — Russian never stops making that distinction."],"audio":"When you ask a Russian the way, you'll hear three magic words. Idite pryamo — go straight. Povernite napravo — turn right. Povernite nalevo — turn left. These are polite commands, the ite form you'd use with a stranger. The directions themselves are worth memorising as a set: pryamo, straight ahead; napravo, to the right; nalevo, to the left. And to say where something already sits, switch to sprava, on the right, and sleva, on the left. Notice the echo of today's lesson: the o words point where to move, the a words say where things rest. Motion versus place, one more time.","table":{"rows":[["Иди́те пря́мо. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Идите прямо.\">🔊</button>","Go straight."],["Поверни́те напра́во. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Поверните направо.\">🔊</button>","Turn right."],["Поверни́те нале́во. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Поверните налево.\">🔊</button>","Turn left."],["Магази́н спра́ва. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Магазин справа.\">🔊</button>","The shop is on the right."]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"Go straight, then turn left.","en":"<b>Иди́те пря́мо</b>, пото́м <b>нале́во</b>.","say":"Идите прямо, потом налево.","gloss":"пото́м = then"},{"ru":"The pharmacy is on the left.","en":"Апте́ка <b>сле́ва</b>.","say":"Аптека слева."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A tourist should turn right. You tell them:","opts":["Иди́те пря́мо.","Поверни́те напра́во.","Поверни́те нале́во."],"answer":1,"explain":"Right = <b>напра́во</b>. Пря́мо is straight on; нале́во is left."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Turn left.”</b>","want":"Поверните налево","answer":"Поверни́те нале́во"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Asking the way","title":"<em>Извини́те, где метро́?</em> — and Emma answers","paras":["To stop a stranger politely, open with <b>Извини́те</b> — excuse me — then ask your где-question: <b>Извини́те, где метро́?</b> No “is”, as always.","You already know how to understand the reply. Someone points and says <b>Иди́те пря́мо, пото́м напра́во — метро́ спра́ва.</b> Go straight, then right — the metro's on your right. Every word is yours now.","And here's the full circle: a month ago Emma was the lost one. Today a tourist stops <em>her</em>, and she answers in Russian. That's what these three weeks were for."],"audio":"To stop a stranger politely, open with izvinite — excuse me — then ask your gdye-question: izvinite, gdye metro? No is, as always. You already know how to understand the reply. Someone points and says: idite pryamo, potom napravo — metro sprava. Go straight, then right — the metro's on your right. Every word is yours now. And here's the full circle: a month ago Emma was the lost one. Today a tourist stops her, and she answers in Russian. That's what these three weeks were for.","examples":[{"ru":"Excuse me, where's the pharmacy?","en":"<b>Извини́те, где</b> апте́ка?","say":"Извините, где аптека?"},{"ru":"Excuse me, where's the metro? — Straight on, then left.","en":"Извини́те, где метро́? — <b>Пря́мо, пото́м нале́во.</b>","say":"Извините, где метро? — Прямо, потом налево."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"You want to stop someone and ask where the shop is. Open with:","opts":["Извини́те, где магази́н?","Здра́вствуйте, куда́ магази́н?","Извини́те, куда́ магази́н?"],"answer":0,"explain":"You're asking a location, so <b>где</b> — and “excuse me” is <b>извини́те</b>. Куда́ would ask which way it's moving, and a shop stays put."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say the phrase out loud. Listen to the sample first if you like.","target":"Извини́те, где метро́?","sub":"excuse me, where's the metro — polite and clear","want":"Извините где метро"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma is crossing the centre on her own when a tourist with a map stops her — and for the first time, she's the one who knows the way. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Извини́те! Вы говори́те по-англи́йски?","ru":"Excuse me! Do you speak English?"},{"who":"A","en":"Немно́го. А по-ру́сски — хорошо́!","ru":"A little. But in Russian — well! (немно́го = a little)"},{"who":"B","en":"О́чень хорошо́! Где метро́?","ru":"Very good! Where's the metro?"},{"who":"A","en":"Метро́? Иди́те пря́мо, пото́м напра́во.","ru":"The metro? Go straight, then right."},{"who":"B","en":"Пря́мо и напра́во… А где апте́ка?","ru":"Straight and right… And where's a pharmacy?"},{"who":"A","en":"Апте́ка сле́ва, на у́лице Арба́т.","ru":"The pharmacy's on the left, on Arbat Street."},{"who":"B","en":"Вы о́чень хорошо́ зна́ете го́род! Спаси́бо большо́е!","ru":"You know the city very well! Thank you very much! (большо́е = very much)"},{"who":"A","en":"Пожа́луйста! До свида́ния!","ru":"You're welcome! Goodbye!"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words to move through a city. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"го́род","ru":"city","ex":"Я в го́роде."},{"en":"у́лица","ru":"street","ex":"Кафе́ на у́лице Арба́т."},{"en":"пло́щадь","ru":"square (feminine)","ex":"Кра́сная пло́щадь — в це́нтре."},{"en":"магази́н","ru":"shop (not “magazine”!)","ex":"Я иду́ в магази́н."},{"en":"апте́ка","ru":"pharmacy","ex":"Апте́ка спра́ва."},{"en":"банк","ru":"bank","ex":"Банк нале́во."},{"en":"метро́","ru":"metro, underground","ex":"Где метро́?"},{"en":"пря́мо","ru":"straight ahead","ex":"Иди́те пря́мо."},{"en":"напра́во","ru":"to the right","ex":"Поверни́те напра́во."},{"en":"нале́во","ru":"to the left","ex":"Поверни́те нале́во."},{"en":"спра́ва / сле́ва","ru":"on the right / on the left","ex":"Магази́н спра́ва, апте́ка сле́ва."},{"en":"Извини́те","ru":"excuse me","ex":"Извини́те, где метро́?"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning on the right — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Где метро́?","Where's the metro?"],["Иди́те пря́мо.","Go straight."],["Поверни́те напра́во.","Turn right."],["Я иду́ в парк.","I'm going to the park."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions — place versus motion, and the words that walk you across town.","items":[{"q":"«Куда́ ты?» — you're going to the shop. Reply:","opts":["Я в магази́не.","Я иду́ в магази́н.","Я иду́ в магази́не."],"answer":1},{"q":"«Где ты?» — you're in the park. Reply:","opts":["Я иду́ в парк.","Я в парк.","Я в па́рке."],"answer":2},{"q":"“Turn left” is:","opts":["Поверни́те напра́во.","Иди́те пря́мо.","Поверни́те нале́во."],"answer":2},{"q":"Which case answers «куда́?» (where to)","opts":["the prepositional (в па́рке)","the accusative (в парк)","the dative (мне)"],"answer":1},{"q":"“Excuse me, where's the pharmacy?”","opts":["Извини́те, где апте́ка?","Извини́те, куда́ апте́ка?","Здра́вствуйте, где апте́ку?"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note after a day of finding her way — and helping someone else find theirs. Read it aloud.","title":"Я зна́ю го́род!","sentences":["Сего́дня я в це́нтре, в Москве́.","Я иду́ в магази́н, пото́м в апте́ку.","Магази́н пря́мо, а апте́ка спра́ва.","Тури́ст спра́шивает: «Где метро́?»","Я говорю́: «Иди́те пря́мо, пото́м нале́во.»","И я о́чень хорошо́ зна́ю го́род!"],"translation":"Today I'm in the centre, in Moscow. I'm going to the shop, then to the pharmacy. The shop is straight ahead, and the pharmacy is on the right. A tourist asks: “Where's the metro?” I say: “Go straight, then left.” And I know the city very well! (тури́ст спра́шивает = a tourist asks)"},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to be the one who knows the way.","prompt":"Give directions from your home to one nearby place (a shop, a café, the metro) in 3–4 sentences. Use at least one command (Иди́те / Поверни́те) and one direction word (пря́мо / напра́во / нале́во).","hint":"Frame: Иди́те пря́мо, пото́м напра́во / нале́во. Something already there? Use спра́ва / сле́ва 🙂","example":"Иди́те пря́мо, пото́м нале́во. Магази́н спра́ва. Метро́ то́же нале́во, пото́м пря́мо. Кафе́ в це́нтре, на у́лице Арба́т."}},"24":{"day":24,"week":"04","themeRu":"How many?","themeEn":"Ско́лько?","intro":"Yesterday Emma found her way across the city; today she has to pay for things once she gets there. Numbers are about to earn their keep — the tens up to a hundred, and the little grammar surprise English never prepares you for: in Russian, <b>the number reshapes the noun</b>. One book, but “two books” and “five books” bend it two different ways. Good news — you already do this with <b>год · го́да · лет</b>.","introAudio":"Yesterday Emma found her way across the city; today she has to pay for things once she gets there. Numbers are about to earn their keep — the tens up to a hundred, and the little grammar surprise English never prepares you for: in Russian, the number reshapes the noun. One book, but two books and five books bend it two different ways. Good news — you already do this with god, goda, lyet.","goals":["Count in tens to a hundred: <b>два́дцать, со́рок, сто</b>","Match noun to number: <b>одна́ кни́га, две кни́ги, пять книг</b>","Pick <b>оди́н / одна́</b> and <b>два / две</b> by gender","Shop and count roubles: <b>Ско́лько сто́ит? — Сто рубле́й</b>"],"learned":["Counted in tens to a hundred: <b>два́дцать, со́рок, сто</b>","Matched noun to number: <b>одна́ кни́га, две кни́ги, пять книг</b>","Picked <b>оди́н / одна́</b> and <b>два / две</b> by gender","Shopped and counted roubles: <b>Ско́лько сто́ит? — Сто рубле́й</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Three quick ones before the market — age, an object, and a car that has to agree.","introAudio":"Three quick ones before the market — age, an object, and a car that has to agree.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the situation and tap the right answer. A wrong tap costs nothing.","q":"«Ско́лько тебе́ лет?» — you're twenty (два́дцать). Your answer:","opts":["Я два́дцать лет.","Мне два́дцать лет.","У меня́ два́дцать лет."],"answer":1,"explain":"Age arrives <em>to</em> you: <b>Мне… лет</b> — the dative. And note лет, the after-5 form you'll meet again today."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — no stress marks needed.","q":"Type: “I'm reading a book.” — aim the object:","accept":["я читаю книгу","читаю книгу","ya chitayu knigu"],"placeholder":"Я чита́ю …"},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“My new car is at home.”</b>","want":"Моя новая машина дома","answer":"Моя́ но́вая маши́на до́ма"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Bigger numbers","title":"The tens: <em>два́дцать … сто</em>","paras":["You count to twenty already — let's stretch to a hundred. The tens have a tidy rhythm: <b>два́дцать</b> (20), <b>три́дцать</b> (30), then <b>со́рок</b> (40) breaks the pattern, <b>пятьдеся́т</b> (50), <b>шестьдеся́т</b> (60)… up to <b>сто</b> (100).","Building the in-between numbers is just addition, said aloud: <b>два́дцать оди́н</b> (21), <b>два́дцать пять</b> (25), <b>три́дцать два</b> (32). No “and”, no hyphen — you say the ten, then the one.","So a price tag reads exactly how it looks: <b>со́рок пять</b> — forty-five, <b>сто</b> — one hundred. You now have every number you need at a Moscow market."],"audio":"You count to twenty already — let's stretch to a hundred. The tens have a tidy rhythm: dvadtsat, twenty; tridtsat, thirty; then sorok, forty, breaks the pattern; pyatdesyat, fifty; shestdesyat, sixty; up to sto, a hundred. Building the in-between numbers is just addition, said aloud: dvadtsat odin, twenty-one; dvadtsat pyat, twenty-five; tridtsat dva, thirty-two. No and, no hyphen — you say the ten, then the one. So a price tag reads exactly how it looks: sorok pyat, forty-five; sto, a hundred. You now have every number you need at a Moscow market.","table":{"rows":[["два́дцать <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"двадцать\">🔊</button>","20"],["три́дцать <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"тридцать\">🔊</button>","30"],["со́рок <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"сорок\">🔊</button>","40 (the odd one)"],["пятьдеся́т <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пятьдесят\">🔊</button>","50"],["сто <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"сто\">🔊</button>","100"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Twenty-five.","en":"<b>Два́дцать пять</b>.","say":"Двадцать пять."},{"ru":"Forty-five roubles.","en":"<b>Со́рок пять</b> рубле́й.","say":"Сорок пять рублей.","gloss":"рубле́й — the after-5 form of рубль"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which is 40?","opts":["со́рок","четы́ре","четы́рнадцать"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Со́рок</b> is the odd-one-out ten. Четы́ре is 4, четы́рнадцать is 14."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“twenty-five”</b>","want":"Двадцать пять","answer":"Два́дцать пять"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Number + noun","title":"<em>одна́ кни́га, две кни́ги, пять книг</em> — three shelves","paras":["Here's the day's real lesson — and you've secretly done it before. In Russian, the number decides what shape the noun takes. Three shelves: after <b>1</b>, the plain form (одна́ кни́га); after <b>2, 3, 4</b>, a form that usually looks like -и/-ы (две кни́ги); after <b>5 and up</b>, a shorter “of many” form (пять книг).","You already ran this exact dance twice: <b>год / го́да / лет</b> for age, <b>час / часа́ / часо́в</b> for the clock. Кни́га just joins in: <b>одна́ кни́га, две кни́ги, пять книг</b>. Same three shelves, new word.","The trap for an English speaker: English keeps the noun still (“one book, five book<em>s</em>”) and only ever adds -s. Russian re-shapes it twice. Don't fight it — lean on the год / часо́в rhythm you already trust."],"audio":"Here's the day's real lesson — and you've secretly done it before. In Russian, the number decides what shape the noun takes. Three shelves: after one, the plain form — odna kniga; after two, three, four, a form that usually looks like ee — dvye knigi; after five and up, a shorter of-many form — pyat knig. You already ran this dance twice: god, goda, lyet for age; chas, chasa, chasov for the clock. Kniga just joins in: odna kniga, dvye knigi, pyat knig. Same three shelves, new word. The trap for an English speaker: English keeps the noun still and only adds s. Russian re-shapes it twice. Lean on the god and chasov rhythm you already trust.","mnemonic":"Three shelves, always: <b>1</b> → one shape, <b>2–4</b> → second shape, <b>5+</b> → third. Just like <b>год · го́да · лет</b>.","table":{"rows":[["одна́ кни́га <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"одна книга\">🔊</button>","1 book (plain form)"],["две кни́ги <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"две книги\">🔊</button>","2 books (after 2–4)"],["три кни́ги <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"три книги\">🔊</button>","3 books (still shelf 2)"],["пять книг <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пять книг\">🔊</button>","5 books (after 5+)"]],"star":3},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"пять кни́ги","right":"пять книг","why":"After 5 and up, the noun drops to the “of many” form: <b>книг</b>. Кни́ги belongs to 2–4 (две кни́ги). Same jump as пять лет, not пять го́да."}],"examples":[{"ru":"Two books, five books.","en":"<b>Две кни́ги</b>, <b>пять книг</b>.","say":"Две книги, пять книг."},{"ru":"I have one book and two maps.","en":"У меня́ <b>одна́ кни́га</b> и <b>две ка́рты</b>.","say":"У меня одна книга и две карты.","gloss":"ка́рта → две ка́рты"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«У меня́ пять ___.» (I have five books.)","opts":["кни́га","кни́ги","книг"],"answer":2,"explain":"After 5+, the “of many” form: <b>пять книг</b>. Кни́ги is the 2–4 form; кни́га is just one."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I have two books.”</b>","want":"У меня две книги","answer":"У меня́ две кни́ги"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · One and two have gender","title":"<em>оди́н / одна́</em>, <em>два / две</em>","paras":["Two little numbers change with the gender of what they count. <b>Оди́н</b> follows the noun's gender like a possessive: <b>оди́н рубль</b> (masc), <b>одна́ кни́га</b> (fem), <b>одно́ окно́</b> (neut).","And <b>2</b> has two faces: <b>два</b> for masculine and neuter (<b>два рубля́, два окна́</b>), <b>две</b> for feminine (<b>две кни́ги, две ка́рты</b>). Three and up don't bother with gender — only 1 and 2 do.","So gender, your old week-one companion, reaches all the way into counting. One more place where knowing «он / она́ / оно́» quietly pays off."],"audio":"Two little numbers change with the gender of what they count. Odin follows the noun's gender like a possessive: odin rubl, masculine; odna kniga, feminine; odno okno, neuter. And two has two faces: dva for masculine and neuter — dva rublya, dva okna — dvye for feminine — dvye knigi, dvye karty. Three and up don't bother with gender; only one and two do. So gender, your old week-one companion, reaches all the way into counting. One more place where knowing on, ona, ono quietly pays off.","table":{"rows":[["оди́н рубль <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"один рубль\">🔊</button>","1 rouble (masc)"],["одна́ кни́га <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"одна книга\">🔊</button>","1 book (fem)"],["два рубля́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"два рубля\">🔊</button>","2 roubles (masc → два)"],["две кни́ги <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"две книги\">🔊</button>","2 books (fem → две)"]],"star":3},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"два кни́ги","right":"две кни́ги","why":"Кни́га is feminine, so two is <b>две</b>, not два. Два is for masculine and neuter: два рубля́, два окна́."}],"examples":[{"ru":"One window, two windows.","en":"<b>Одно́ окно́</b>, <b>два окна́</b>.","say":"Одно окно, два окна.","gloss":"neuter takes два"},{"ru":"I have one map and two books.","en":"У меня́ <b>одна́ ка́рта</b> и <b>две кни́ги</b>.","say":"У меня одна карта и две книги."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«В ко́мнате ___ окна́.» (two windows — окно́ is neuter)","opts":["две","два","одна́"],"answer":1,"explain":"Окно́ is neuter → <b>два окна́</b>. Две is only for feminine things (две кни́ги)."},{"type":"type","q":"Type: “two books” (кни́га is feminine):","accept":["две книги","dve knigi"],"placeholder":"… кни́ги","explain":"Feminine → <b>две</b>, and 2–4 take the кни́ги form: две кни́ги."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · At the market","title":"<em>Ско́лько сто́ит?</em> — counting roubles","paras":["Time to spend. The rouble counts on the same three shelves: <b>оди́н рубль</b>, <b>два рубля́</b>, <b>пять рубле́й</b> — год / го́да / лет wearing a price tag.","To ask a price: <b>Ско́лько сто́ит?</b> — how much is it? (literally “how much does it cost”). The same <b>ско́лько</b> from Ско́лько тебе́ лет. The answer is just a number plus рубле́й: <b>Сто рубле́й.</b>","Hand over your order with <b>Да́йте, пожа́луйста</b> — the polite “give me, please” from the café. A market runs on three phrases: Ско́лько сто́ит? · Да́йте, пожа́луйста · Спаси́бо."],"audio":"Time to spend. The rouble counts on the same three shelves: odin rubl, dva rublya, pyat rublyey — god, goda, lyet wearing a price tag. To ask a price: skolka stoit — how much is it, literally how much does it cost. The same skolka from skolka tebye lyet. The answer is just a number plus rublyey: sto rublyey, a hundred roubles. Hand over your order with dayte pozhaluysta — the polite give me please from the café. A market runs on three phrases: skolka stoit, dayte pozhaluysta, spasibo.","table":{"rows":[["Ско́лько сто́ит? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Сколько стоит?\">🔊</button>","How much is it?"],["оди́н рубль <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"один рубль\">🔊</button>","1 rouble"],["два рубля́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"два рубля\">🔊</button>","2 roubles"],["пять рубле́й <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"пять рублей\">🔊</button>","5 roubles"]],"star":0},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Сто рубля́.","right":"Сто рубле́й.","why":"Сто is over 5, so the “of many” form: <b>рубле́й</b>. Рубля́ stays with 2–4 (два рубля́). Same shelf as пять часо́в."}],"examples":[{"ru":"How much is the tea? — Fifty roubles.","en":"Ско́лько сто́ит чай? — <b>Пятьдеся́т рубле́й</b>.","say":"Сколько стоит чай? — Пятьдесят рублей."},{"ru":"Two teas, please.","en":"<b>Два ча́я</b>, пожа́луйста.","say":"Два чая, пожалуйста.","gloss":"два ча́я — two teas"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Ско́лько сто́ит?» — it's 100 roubles. The answer:","opts":["Сто рубль.","Сто рубле́й.","Сто рубля́."],"answer":1,"explain":"100 sits on the 5+ shelf → <b>рубле́й</b>. Рубль is for one; рубля́ for 2–4."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and ask the price out loud. Listen to the sample first if you like.","target":"Ско́лько сто́ит?","sub":"the market's magic question — how much is it?","want":"Сколько стоит"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Saturday at a Moscow market. Emma wants tea and apples, and it's all counting and roubles now. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Здра́вствуйте! Чай, ко́фе, я́блоки?","ru":"Hello! Tea, coffee, apples? (я́блоки = apples)"},{"who":"A","en":"Здра́вствуйте! Ско́лько сто́ит чай?","ru":"Hello! How much is the tea?"},{"who":"B","en":"Пятьдеся́т рубле́й.","ru":"Fifty roubles."},{"who":"A","en":"А я́блоки?","ru":"And the apples?"},{"who":"B","en":"То́же пятьдеся́т.","ru":"Also fifty."},{"who":"A","en":"Да́йте, пожа́луйста, чай и два я́блока.","ru":"Give me tea and two apples, please."},{"who":"B","en":"Хорошо́. Сто рубле́й.","ru":"Good. A hundred roubles."},{"who":"A","en":"Вот, пожа́луйста. Спаси́бо!","ru":"Here you go. Thanks! (вот = here you are)"},{"who":"B","en":"Спаси́бо! До свида́ния!","ru":"Thank you! Goodbye!"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for numbers, money and the market. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"ско́лько","ru":"how much, how many","ex":"Ско́лько сто́ит?"},{"en":"сто́ит","ru":"costs","ex":"Ско́лько сто́ит чай?"},{"en":"рубль","ru":"rouble","ex":"оди́н рубль, два рубля́"},{"en":"три́дцать","ru":"thirty","ex":"три́дцать рубле́й"},{"en":"со́рок","ru":"forty","ex":"со́рок рубле́й"},{"en":"пятьдеся́т","ru":"fifty","ex":"пятьдеся́т рубле́й"},{"en":"сто","ru":"one hundred","ex":"сто рубле́й"},{"en":"оди́н / одна́","ru":"one (m / f)","ex":"оди́н рубль, одна́ кни́га"},{"en":"два / две","ru":"two (m / f)","ex":"два рубля́, две кни́ги"},{"en":"де́ньги","ru":"money","ex":"У меня́ есть де́ньги."},{"en":"ры́нок","ru":"market","ex":"Я иду́ на ры́нок."},{"en":"Да́йте, пожа́луйста","ru":"give me, please","ex":"Да́йте чай, пожа́луйста."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning on the right — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Ско́лько сто́ит?","How much is it?"],["две кни́ги","two books"],["пять рубле́й","five roubles"],["сто рубле́й","a hundred roubles"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions — the number bends the noun. Hold the три shelves and you're through.","items":[{"q":"«У меня́ две ___.» (two books)","opts":["кни́ги","кни́га","книг"],"answer":0},{"q":"«пять ___» (five roubles)","opts":["рубль","рубля́","рубле́й"],"answer":2},{"q":"Two books — кни́га is feminine. Which “two”?","opts":["две","два","одна́"],"answer":0},{"q":"Which is 50?","opts":["пятна́дцать","пятьдеся́т","пять"],"answer":1},{"q":"“How much is the tea?”","opts":["Ско́лько сто́ит чай?","Ско́лько лет чай?","Ско́лько чай?"],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's note from the market — a hundred roubles, well spent. Read it aloud.","title":"На ры́нке","sentences":["Сего́дня суббо́та, и я на ры́нке.","Ско́лько сто́ит чай? — Пятьдеся́т рубле́й.","А я́блоки? — То́же пятьдеся́т.","«Да́йте, пожа́луйста, чай и два я́блока.»","Э́то сто рубле́й. У меня́ есть сто рубле́й!","Я люблю́ ры́нок!"],"translation":"Today is Saturday, and I'm at the market. How much is the tea? — Fifty roubles. And the apples? — Also fifty. “Give me tea and two apples, please.” That's a hundred roubles. I have a hundred roubles! I love the market! (я́блоки = apples)"},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn at the market, with a hundred roubles in your pocket.","prompt":"Write 3–4 sentences at the market: ask a price (Ско́лько сто́ит…?), name what you buy and how many, and add up the roubles.","hint":"Frames: Ско́лько сто́ит …? · Да́йте, пожа́луйста, … · Remember the three shelves — оди́н рубль, два рубля́, пять рубле́й 🙂","example":"Я на ры́нке. Ско́лько сто́ит чай? — Пятьдеся́т рубле́й. Да́йте, пожа́луйста, два ча́я. Э́то сто рубле́й!"}},"25":{"day":25,"week":"04","themeRu":"Weather","themeEn":"Пого́да","intro":"Emma has been in Moscow a month, and this morning the city changes colour. Today you'll talk about the weather — and meet Russian's most minimalist grammar yet: <b>хо́лодно</b> (“it's cold”) is a whole sentence with no subject and no “is”. Rain and snow don't even fall here — they <em>walk</em>: <b>идёт дождь</b>, the rain goes. And by the end, the first snow.","introAudio":"Emma has been in Moscow a month, and this morning the city changes colour. Today you'll talk about the weather — and meet Russian's most minimalist grammar yet: kholodno, it's cold, is a whole sentence with no subject and no is. Rain and snow don't even fall here — they walk: idyot dozhd, the rain goes. And by the end, the first snow.","goals":["Say how it feels with one word: <b>Хо́лодно, Тепло́, Жа́рко</b>","Report rain and snow: <b>Идёт дождь, Идёт снег</b>","Name the seasons: <b>зима́, весна́, ле́то, о́сень</b>","Ask and answer <b>Кака́я сего́дня пого́да?</b>"],"learned":["Said how it feels with one word: <b>Хо́лодно, Тепло́, Жа́рко</b>","Reported rain and snow: <b>Идёт дождь, Идёт снег</b>","Named the seasons: <b>зима́, весна́, ле́то, о́сень</b>","Asked and answered <b>Кака́я сего́дня пого́да?</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Three warm-ups before the cold sets in — where-to, counting, and the city you've fallen for.","introAudio":"Three warm-ups before the cold sets in — where-to, counting, and the city you've fallen for.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the situation and tap the right answer. A wrong tap costs nothing.","q":"«Куда́ ты?» — you're going to the shop. Reply:","opts":["Я в магази́не.","Я иду́ в магази́н.","Я в магази́н."],"answer":1,"explain":"Куда́ = motion → the accusative: <b>в магази́н</b>. Я в магази́не answers где (already there)."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — no stress marks needed.","q":"Type: “two books” (кни́га is feminine):","accept":["две книги","dve knigi"],"placeholder":"… кни́ги"},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“I like Moscow.”</b>","want":"Мне нравится Москва","answer":"Мне нра́вится Москва́"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · No subject","title":"<em>Хо́лодно</em> — a whole sentence in one word","paras":["Russian weather is the ultimate no-“to-be” sentence. To say “it's cold”, you need exactly one word: <b>Хо́лодно.</b> No “it”, no “is” — just the state, hanging in the air. <b>Тепло́</b> — it's warm. <b>Жа́рко</b> — it's hot.","English props these up with scaffolding: <em>it is cold</em>, three words. Russian knocks the scaffolding away and one word stands alone. You've felt this before — Хорошо́! (“it's good”) has been doing the same thing since day one.","Add <b>сего́дня</b> to place it in time: <b>Сего́дня хо́лодно.</b> Today it's cold. Still no subject, still no verb — сего́дня just tells you when."],"audio":"Russian weather is the ultimate no-to-be sentence. To say it's cold, you need exactly one word: kholodno. No it, no is — just the state, hanging in the air. Teplo, it's warm. Zharko, it's hot. English props these up with scaffolding: it is cold, three words. Russian knocks the scaffolding away and one word stands alone. You've felt this before — khorosho, it's good, has been doing the same thing since day one. Add sevodnya to place it in time: sevodnya kholodno, today it's cold. Still no subject, still no verb — sevodnya just tells you when.","mnemonic":"“It's cold” has three English words and no real subject. Russian agrees there's no subject — and drops to one word: <b>Хо́лодно.</b>","table":{"rows":[["Хо́лодно. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Холодно.\">🔊</button>","It's cold."],["Тепло́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Тепло.\">🔊</button>","It's warm."],["Жа́рко. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Жарко.\">🔊</button>","It's hot."],["Сего́дня хо́лодно. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Сегодня холодно.\">🔊</button>","Today it's cold."]],"star":0},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Э́то хо́лодно.","right":"Хо́лодно.","why":"No “it”, no “is” — weather stands alone: <b>Хо́лодно.</b> Adding э́то reaches for an English subject that Russian doesn't want here."}],"examples":[{"ru":"It's cold today. — Yes, very.","en":"Сего́дня <b>хо́лодно</b>. — Да, о́чень.","say":"Сегодня холодно. — Да, очень."},{"ru":"It's warm in Moscow today.","en":"В Москве́ сего́дня <b>тепло́</b>.","say":"В Москве сегодня тепло."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"How do you say “it's cold” in Russian?","opts":["Э́то хо́лодно.","Хо́лодно.","Я хо́лодно."],"answer":1,"explain":"One word, no subject: <b>Хо́лодно.</b> Weather in Russian never needs “it” or “is”."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Today it's warm.”</b>","want":"Сегодня тепло","answer":"Сего́дня тепло́"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Rain and snow","title":"<em>Идёт дождь, идёт снег</em> — the rain “goes”","paras":["Rain and snow don't “fall” in Russian — they <em>walk</em>. <b>Идёт дождь</b> — literally “the rain goes”. <b>Идёт снег</b> — “the snow goes”. That <b>идёт</b> is your old friend from Я иду́ (to go on foot); here it's the “he / she / it” form.","The word order you'll hear most puts the verb first: <b>Идёт дождь. Идёт снег.</b> But <b>Дождь идёт</b> is equally fine — Russian's flexible order lets the weather lead or follow.","So a grey Moscow morning is two words: <b>Идёт дождь.</b> And when winter arrives — <b>Идёт снег.</b>"],"audio":"Rain and snow don't fall in Russian — they walk. Idyot dozhd — literally, the rain goes. Idyot sneg — the snow goes. That idyot is your old friend from ya idu, to go on foot; here it's the he-she-it form. The word order you'll hear most puts the verb first: idyot dozhd, idyot sneg. But dozhd idyot is equally fine — Russian's flexible order lets the weather lead or follow. So a grey Moscow morning is two words: idyot dozhd. And when winter arrives — idyot sneg.","table":{"rows":[["Идёт дождь. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Идёт дождь.\">🔊</button>","It's raining. (the rain “goes”)"],["Идёт снег. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Идёт снег.\">🔊</button>","It's snowing. (the snow “goes”)"],["Сего́дня идёт дождь. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Сегодня идёт дождь.\">🔊</button>","It's raining today."],["Дождь идёт. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Дождь идёт.\">🔊</button>","It's raining. (order flipped)"]],"star":1},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Дождь есть.","right":"Идёт дождь.","why":"No “to be” and no “there is” for weather — the rain simply <b>идёт</b> (goes). Есть doesn't belong here."}],"examples":[{"ru":"Look — it's snowing!","en":"Смотри́ — <b>идёт снег</b>!","say":"Смотри — идёт снег!","gloss":"смотри́ = look (to a friend)"},{"ru":"It's raining in Moscow today.","en":"В Москве́ сего́дня <b>идёт дождь</b>.","say":"В Москве сегодня идёт дождь."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"It's snowing. In Russian:","opts":["Идёт снег.","Э́то снег.","Снег хо́лодно."],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Идёт снег</b> — the snow “goes”. Э́то снег just means “this is snow”; the weather needs идёт."},{"type":"type","q":"Type: “It's raining.” (the rain “goes”)","accept":["идёт дождь","идет дождь","дождь идёт","дождь идет","idyot dozhd"],"placeholder":"Идёт …","explain":"<b>Идёт дождь</b> — verb first is the everyday order, but Дождь идёт is fine too."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Seasons","title":"<em>зима́, весна́, ле́то, о́сень</em>","paras":["Four seasons, four words: <b>зима́</b> (winter), <b>весна́</b> (spring), <b>ле́то</b> (summer), <b>о́сень</b> (autumn). To say which one it is now, no verb needed: <b>Сейча́с зима́.</b> — “It's winter now.”","Each season keeps its own weather company: <b>Зимо́й хо́лодно, идёт снег. Ле́том жа́рко.</b> Winter is cold with snow; summer is hot. (Зимо́й, ле́том — “in winter, in summer” — take them as ready phrases for now.)","Notice the genders you can hear: зима́, весна́, о́сень feel feminine; ле́то is neuter (-о). Your old week-one gender, still quietly working."],"audio":"Four seasons, four words: zima, winter; vesna, spring; lyeto, summer; osen, autumn. To say which one it is now, no verb needed: seychas zima, it's winter now. Each season keeps its own weather company: zimoy kholodno, idyot sneg; lyetom zharko. Winter is cold with snow; summer is hot. Zimoy and lyetom — in winter, in summer — take them as ready phrases for now. Notice the genders you can hear: zima, vesna, osen feel feminine; lyeto is neuter. Your old week-one gender, still quietly working.","table":{"rows":[["зима́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"зима\">🔊</button>","winter"],["весна́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"весна\">🔊</button>","spring"],["ле́то <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"лето\">🔊</button>","summer"],["о́сень <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"осень\">🔊</button>","autumn"]],"star":0},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Сейча́с зимо́й.","right":"Сейча́с зима́.","why":"To name the season, use the plain form: <b>зима́</b>. Зимо́й means “in winter” — a when, not a what."}],"examples":[{"ru":"It's winter now, and it's cold.","en":"Сейча́с <b>зима́</b>, и хо́лодно.","say":"Сейчас зима, и холодно."},{"ru":"I like autumn in Moscow.","en":"Мне нра́вится <b>о́сень</b> в Москве́.","say":"Мне нравится осень в Москве.","gloss":"о́сень — feminine, ends in a soft sign"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Сейча́с ___.» — it's summer now.","opts":["ле́то","зима́","о́сень"],"answer":0,"explain":"Summer = <b>ле́то</b> (neuter, -о). Зима́ is winter; о́сень is autumn."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“It's winter now, and it's cold.”</b>","want":"Сейчас зима и холодно","answer":"Сейча́с зима́ и хо́лодно"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · What's the weather?","title":"<em>Кака́я сего́дня пого́да?</em> — and the first snow","paras":["To ask, there's one warm question: <b>Кака́я сего́дня пого́да?</b> — “what's the weather like today?” (literally “what kind of weather today”). Кака́я is the feminine “what kind” — пого́да is feminine, so they match.","The answer stacks what you now own: <b>Сего́дня хо́лодно. Идёт снег. Зима́!</b> Three tiny sentences, no subject or “to be” between them — pure Russian weather-talk.","And today it really happens: Emma looks out of Ива́н's window and sees the first snow of her Moscow winter. <b>Идёт снег.</b> Two words she couldn't say a month ago."],"audio":"To ask, there's one warm question: kakaya sevodnya pogoda — what's the weather like today, literally what kind of weather today. Kakaya is the feminine what-kind — pogoda is feminine, so they match. The answer stacks what you now own: sevodnya kholodno, idyot sneg, zima. Three tiny sentences, no subject or to-be between them — pure Russian weather-talk. And today it really happens: Emma looks out of Ivan's window and sees the first snow of her Moscow winter. Idyot sneg. Two words she couldn't say a month ago.","table":{"rows":[["Кака́я сего́дня пого́да? <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Какая сегодня погода?\">🔊</button>","What's the weather like today?"],["Сего́дня хо́лодно. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Сегодня холодно.\">🔊</button>","Today it's cold."],["Идёт снег. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Идёт снег.\">🔊</button>","It's snowing."],["Сейча́с зима́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Сейчас зима.\">🔊</button>","It's winter now."]],"star":0},"examples":[{"ru":"What's the weather like today? — Cold, and it's snowing.","en":"Кака́я сего́дня пого́да? — <b>Хо́лодно, идёт снег.</b>","say":"Какая сегодня погода? — Холодно, идёт снег."},{"ru":"It's warm in spring.","en":"Весно́й <b>тепло́</b>.","say":"Весной тепло.","gloss":"весно́й = in spring"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Ива́н asks «Кака́я сего́дня пого́да?» — it's cold and snowing. Best answer:","opts":["Хо́лодно, идёт снег.","Я хо́лодно, снег идёт есть.","Э́то хо́лодно снег."],"answer":0,"explain":"Two clean subjectless sentences: <b>Хо́лодно. Идёт снег.</b> No “I”, no “is”, no есть."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say the phrase out loud. Listen to the sample first if you like.","target":"Идёт снег.","sub":"two words you couldn't say a month ago","want":"Идёт снег"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"November in Moscow. Emma is at Ива́н's window with a cup of tea when the first snow of the year begins to fall. Tap any line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, смотри́ в окно́!","ru":"Emma, look out of the window! (смотри́ = look)"},{"who":"A","en":"О! Идёт снег!","ru":"Oh! It's snowing!"},{"who":"B","en":"Да, снег! Сейча́с зима́.","ru":"Yes, snow! It's winter now."},{"who":"A","en":"Как краси́во! А хо́лодно?","ru":"How lovely! Is it cold? (краси́во = lovely)"},{"who":"B","en":"Да, о́чень хо́лодно. Ми́нус де́сять!","ru":"Yes, very cold. Minus ten!"},{"who":"A","en":"В Ло́ндоне тепло́, а в Москве́ зима́!","ru":"In London it's warm, but in Moscow it's winter!"},{"who":"B","en":"Тебе́ нра́вится снег?","ru":"Do you like the snow?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да! Я люблю́ снег и люблю́ Москву́!","ru":"Yes! I love the snow and I love Moscow!"},{"who":"B","en":"Чай и снег — э́то хорошо́.","ru":"Tea and snow — that's nice."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve words for a Moscow sky. Tap a card to flip it, tap 🔊 to hear it.","items":[{"en":"пого́да","ru":"weather","ex":"Кака́я сего́дня пого́да?"},{"en":"хо́лодно","ru":"(it's) cold","ex":"Сего́дня хо́лодно."},{"en":"тепло́","ru":"(it's) warm","ex":"Весно́й тепло́."},{"en":"жа́рко","ru":"(it's) hot","ex":"Ле́том жа́рко."},{"en":"дождь","ru":"rain","ex":"Идёт дождь."},{"en":"снег","ru":"snow","ex":"Идёт снег."},{"en":"идёт","ru":"goes (of rain / snow)","ex":"Идёт снег, идёт дождь."},{"en":"зима́","ru":"winter","ex":"Зимо́й хо́лодно."},{"en":"весна́","ru":"spring","ex":"Весно́й тепло́."},{"en":"ле́то","ru":"summer","ex":"Ле́том жа́рко."},{"en":"о́сень","ru":"autumn","ex":"Сейча́с о́сень."},{"en":"со́лнце","ru":"sun","ex":"Сего́дня со́лнце!"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning on the right — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Идёт снег.","It's snowing."],["Сего́дня хо́лодно.","Today it's cold."],["Сейча́с зима́.","It's winter now."],["Ле́том жа́рко.","In summer it's hot."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions — weather with no subject and no “is”. Hold that and you're through.","items":[{"q":"“It's cold” in Russian:","opts":["Э́то хо́лодно.","Я хо́лодно.","Хо́лодно."],"answer":2},{"q":"“It's snowing”:","opts":["Идёт снег.","Снег есть.","Э́то снег."],"answer":0},{"q":"“It's winter now”:","opts":["Сейча́с зимо́й.","Сейча́с зима́.","Сейча́с ле́то."],"answer":1},{"q":"What does «Идёт дождь» literally say?","opts":["the rain is here","the rain goes","it is rain"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Кака́я сего́дня пого́да?» — cold and raining. Best answer:","opts":["Хо́лодно, идёт дождь.","Я хо́лодный, дождь.","Э́то хо́лодно дождь."],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's postcard from the first snow of her Moscow winter. Read it aloud — with a little wonder.","title":"Зима́ в Москве́","sentences":["Сего́дня о́чень хо́лодно.","В Москве́ идёт снег — пе́рвый снег!","Сейча́с зима́. Ми́нус де́сять!","В Ло́ндоне сейча́с тепло́, а в Москве́ снег.","Ива́н говори́т: «Тебе́ нра́вится зима́?»","Да! Мне о́чень нра́вится Москва́ — и зима́, и снег!"],"translation":"Today it's very cold. In Moscow it's snowing — the first snow! It's winter now. Minus ten! In London it's warm right now, but in Moscow there's snow. Ivan says: “Do you like winter?” Yes! I really like Moscow — winter and snow too! (пе́рвый = first)"},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to describe the sky.","prompt":"Describe today's weather where you are in 3–4 sentences: is it cold or warm? Is it raining or snowing? What season is it? End with one thing you like about it.","hint":"Weather needs no subject: Хо́лодно. / Тепло́. / Идёт дождь. / Идёт снег. Name the season with Сейча́с… and add Мне нра́вится… 🙂","example":"Сего́дня хо́лодно, и идёт снег. Сейча́с зима́. В Москве́ зимо́й хо́лодно. Мне о́чень нра́вится снег!"}},"26":{"day":26,"week":"04","themeRu":"The past","themeEn":"Про́шлое","intro":"Remember day one, when the verb “to be” quietly vanished? Today it comes back — but only in the past. And here's your reward for a hard month of cases: the Russian past tense is <b>easier</b> than the present. It doesn't care whether you're <b>я</b>, <b>ты</b> or <b>он</b> — it asks one thing only: are you a he, a she, or a group? Emma's month in Moscow is nearly over, so let's look back on it — in the past tense.","introAudio":"Remember day one, when the verb to be quietly vanished? Today it comes back — but only in the past. And here's your reward for a hard month of cases: the Russian past tense is easier than the present. It doesn't care whether you're ya, ty or on — it asks one thing only: are you a he, a she, or a group? Emma's month in Moscow is nearly over, so let's look back on it — in the past tense.","goals":["Put any verb into the past: drop <b>-ть</b>, add <b>-л / -ла / -ли</b>","Choose your own form: a man says <b>чита́л</b>, a woman <b>чита́ла</b>","Bring back “was”: <b>Я был / Я была́ в Москве́</b>","Tell where you were and what you did across a whole month"],"learned":["Put verbs into the past: dropped <b>-ть</b>, added <b>-л / -ла / -ли</b>","Chose the right form by gender: <b>чита́л / чита́ла</b>","Brought back “was”: <b>Я был / Я была́</b>","Told where you were and what you did"],"review":{"intro":"Three quick throwbacks before the past walks in — and yes, gender and cases are on the tray again.","introAudio":"Three quick throwbacks before the past walks in — and yes, gender and cases are on the tray again.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the sentence and tap the answer that fits. A wrong tap is just practice.","q":"«У меня́ ___ дом.» — pick the “new” that fits (дом is masculine):","opts":["но́вая","но́вое","но́вый"],"answer":2,"explain":"Дом is masculine → <b>но́вый</b>. Agreement follows the thing's gender — the always-on rule of this whole course."},{"type":"choice","q":"«У меня́ ___.» — you have two books:","opts":["две кни́ги","два кни́ги","две кни́га"],"answer":0,"explain":"For a feminine thing, “two” is <b>две</b>, and after 2 the noun takes -и: <b>две кни́ги</b>."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“The book is on the table.”</b>","want":"Книга на столе","answer":"Кни́га на столе́"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · The past tense","title":"<em>-л, -ла, -ли</em> — drop the <em>-ть</em>, add the ending","paras":["Here is the easy win the month has been promising. To put a verb in the past, take its dictionary form, drop the <b>-ть</b>, and add one small ending. <b>чита́ть → чита́л</b>, <b>рабо́тать → рабо́тал</b>, <b>знать → знал</b>. That's the whole machine.","And now the gift: the ending does <b>not</b> care who you are — я, ты, он all take the same form. It cares about one thing only: <b>gender and number</b>. A man says <b>чита́л</b>, a woman says <b>чита́ла</b> (add -а), any group says <b>чита́ли</b> (add -и). English changes the verb for tense; the Russian past changes it for gender. Fewer forms than the present you already run.","So «я чита́л», «ты чита́л» and «он чита́л» are identical — the pronoun tells you the person, the ending tells you the gender. One rule covers three pronouns."],"audio":"Here is the easy win the month has been promising. To put a verb in the past, take its dictionary form, drop the -t, and add one small ending. Chitat becomes chital, rabotat becomes rabotal, znat becomes znal. And now the gift: the ending does not care who you are — ya, ty, on all take the same form. It cares about one thing only: gender and number. A man says chital, a woman says chitala, a group says chitali. English changes the verb for tense; the Russian past changes it for gender.","table":{"rows":[["чита́л <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читал\">🔊</button>","masculine (я / ты / он)"],["чита́ла <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читала\">🔊</button>","feminine (я / ты / она́)"],["чита́ло <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читало\">🔊</button>","neuter (оно́ — a neuter thing)"],["чита́ли <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читали\">🔊</button>","plural (мы / вы / они́)"]],"star":0},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Вчера́ я чита́ю кни́гу.","right":"Вчера́ я чита́л кни́гу.","why":"«Вчера́» is yesterday — the verb must step into the past: <b>чита́л / чита́ла</b>. Чита́ю is happening right now."},{"wrong":"Э́мма чита́л.","right":"Э́мма чита́ла.","why":"Emma is a woman, so the past ending is <b>-ла</b>. The past agrees with gender, not with the person — that's the whole trick."}],"examples":[{"ru":"Yesterday I worked a lot. (a man)","en":"Вчера́ я мно́го <b>рабо́тал</b>.","say":"Вчера я много работал."},{"ru":"Yesterday I worked a lot. (a woman)","en":"Вчера́ я мно́го <b>рабо́тала</b>.","say":"Вчера я много работала."},{"ru":"We read and watched films.","en":"Мы <b>чита́ли</b> и <b>смотре́ли</b> фи́льмы.","say":"Мы читали и смотрели фильмы.","gloss":"-ли covers we, you and they"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Emma looks back: «Вчера́ я ___ кни́гу.» (Emma is a woman.)","opts":["чита́ю","чита́л","чита́ла"],"answer":2,"explain":"Emma is a woman and it's the past → <b>чита́ла</b> (-ла). Чита́ю is the present; чита́л is a man."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Мы ___ фи́льмы вчера́.» (We watched films yesterday.)","opts":["смотре́ли","смотре́л","смотре́ла"],"answer":0,"explain":"A group takes <b>-ли</b>: <b>смотре́ли</b>. Gender retires in the plural, exactly like adjectives."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Yesterday I worked a lot.”</b> (a man)","want":"Вчера я много работал","answer":"Вчера́ я мно́го рабо́тал"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Was and were","title":"<em>Я был, я была́</em> — “to be” comes back","paras":["In the present, “to be” is invisible: <b>Я до́ма</b> — I'm home, no verb. But the past needs a verb, so it returns: <b>был</b>. “I was home” is <b>Я был до́ма</b> (a man) or <b>Я была́ до́ма</b> (a woman).","It has the same four dresses as any past verb: <b>был</b> (masculine), <b>была́</b> (feminine), <b>бы́ло</b> (neuter), <b>бы́ли</b> (plural). Watch the stress jump: it sits quietly at the front — был, бы́ло, бы́ли — then leaps to the very end for the feminine, <b>была́</b> [by-LA]. That runaway stress is the trap of the day.","Use был to say where someone was: <b>Я был в теа́тре. Мы бы́ли в па́рке.</b> And it opens a memory: <b>Э́то был хоро́ший ме́сяц</b> — it was a good month."],"audio":"In the present, to be is invisible: ya doma — I'm home, no verb. But the past needs a verb, so it comes back: byl. I was home is ya byl doma for a man, ya byla doma for a woman. It has four dresses: byl, byla, bylo, byli. Watch the stress jump: it sits at the front — byl, bylo, byli — then leaps to the very end for the feminine: byLA. That runaway stress is the trap of the day.","table":{"rows":[["был <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"был\">🔊</button>","masculine: he / I / you (m) was"],["была́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"была\">🔊</button>","feminine: she / I / you (f) was"],["бы́ло <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"было\">🔊</button>","neuter: it was"],["бы́ли <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"были\">🔊</button>","plural: we / you / they were"]],"star":1},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Э́мма был в кафе́.","right":"Э́мма была́ в кафе́.","why":"Emma is a woman → <b>была́</b>, with the stress flung to the end. «Был» would make her a man."},{"wrong":"Вчера́ я до́ма.","right":"Вчера́ я был / была́ до́ма.","why":"The present drops “to be”, but the past can't: yesterday needs <b>был / была́</b>. Without it the sentence floats with no time."}],"examples":[{"ru":"I was at the theatre yesterday. (a woman)","en":"Вчера́ я <b>была́</b> в теа́тре.","say":"Вчера я была в театре."},{"ru":"We were in the park.","en":"Мы <b>бы́ли</b> в па́рке.","say":"Мы были в парке."},{"ru":"It was a good month.","en":"Э́то <b>был</b> хоро́ший ме́сяц.","say":"Это был хороший месяц.","gloss":"ме́сяц is masculine → был"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Ле́на tells you about her day: «Вчера́ я ___ на рабо́те.» (Lena is a woman.)","opts":["был","была́","бы́ло"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ле́на is a woman → <b>была́</b>, stress flung to the end. Был is for a man, бы́ло for a neuter thing."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — no stress marks needed.","q":"Type: “We were in Moscow.”","accept":["мы были в москве","my byli v moskve","мы бы́ли в москве́","my bili v moskve"],"placeholder":"Мы … в …","explain":"Мы бы́ли в Москве́ — the plural “was” is <b>бы́ли</b>, plus the place ending -е."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud. Listen to the sample first if you like.","target":"Мы бы́ли в па́рке.","sub":"the plural “were” — one -ли covers everyone","want":"Мы были в парке"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · A whole month, in the past","title":"<em>Я чита́л, я смотре́л</em> — retelling what you did","paras":["Now put your familiar verbs to work in the past. <b>Я чита́л(а) кни́ги. Я смотре́л(а) фи́льмы. Я говори́л(а) по-ру́сски.</b> Same verbs as always — just wearing the -л / -ла ending.","Two English habits to drop. First, there's no “<b>did</b>”: a question is just the past verb with a rising voice — <b>Ты чита́л?</b>, never «Did you чита́л». Second, “didn't” is simply <b>не</b> before the verb: <b>Я не знал(а).</b>","Because the ending already shows gender, a whole story flows without repeating yourself: <b>Я была́ в па́рке, чита́ла и говори́ла по-ру́сски.</b> One subject, three verbs, all in step."],"audio":"Now put your familiar verbs to work in the past. Ya chital knigi, ya smotrel filmy, ya govoril po-russki — same verbs, just wearing the L ending. Two English habits to drop. First, there's no did: a question is just the past verb with a rising voice — ty chital? — never did you chital. Second, didn't is simply nye before the verb: ya nye znal.","mistakes":[{"wrong":"Ты чита́ешь кни́гу вчера́?","right":"Ты чита́л кни́гу вчера́?","why":"«Вчера́» pins it to the past — so the verb is <b>чита́л / чита́ла</b>, not the present чита́ешь. And no “did” is needed: the past form plus a rising voice makes the question."}],"examples":[{"ru":"Did you watch the film? (to a friend)","en":"Ты <b>смотре́л</b> фильм?","say":"Ты смотрел фильм?","gloss":"no “did” — just the past verb and a rising voice"},{"ru":"I didn't know Moscow before. (a woman)","en":"Ра́ньше я не <b>зна́ла</b> Москву́.","say":"Раньше я не знала Москву.","gloss":"Москву́ — the accusative is still on duty in the past"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Ра́ньше Ива́н не ___ по-англи́йски.» (Ivan didn't use to speak English.)","opts":["говори́ла","говори́л","говори́ли"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ива́н is a man → <b>говори́л</b>. «Не» before the verb is the whole “didn't” — no “did” anywhere."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Yesterday I read and watched a film.”</b> (a man)","want":"Вчера я читал и смотрел фильм","answer":"Вчера́ я чита́л и смотре́л фильм"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · When it happened","title":"<em>вчера́, ра́ньше, ме́сяц наза́д</em>","paras":["A past sentence loves a time word. <b>Вчера́</b> [fchee-RA] — yesterday — is the past-tense sister of сего́дня and за́втра: <b>Вчера́ я был(а) в теа́тре.</b>","For “before / earlier” use <b>ра́ньше</b>, and pair it with сейча́с for a proud before-and-after: <b>Ра́ньше я не говори́л(а) по-ру́сски — сейча́с говорю́!</b>","And “ago” is <b>наза́д</b>, standing <em>after</em> the time: <b>ме́сяц наза́д</b> — a month ago, <b>год наза́д</b> — a year ago. Mind the order: Russian says «month ago», the mirror of English."],"audio":"A past sentence loves a time word. Fchera — yesterday — is the past-tense sister of sevodnya and zaftra. For before or earlier use ranshe, and pair it with seychas for a proud before-and-after: ranshe ya nye govoril po-russki — seychas govoryu! And ago is nazad, standing after the time: mesyats nazad — a month ago, god nazad — a year ago. Mind the order: Russian says month ago, the mirror of English.","examples":[{"ru":"A month ago I was in London. (a woman)","en":"<b>Ме́сяц наза́д</b> я была́ в Ло́ндоне.","say":"Месяц назад я была в Лондоне."},{"ru":"Before, I didn't understand Russian — now I do!","en":"<b>Ра́ньше</b> я не понима́ла по-ру́сски — сейча́с понима́ю!","say":"Раньше я не понимала по-русски — сейчас понимаю!"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which word puts the sentence in the past? «___ я был в теа́тре.»","opts":["За́втра","Сейча́с","Вчера́"],"answer":2,"explain":"<b>Вчера́</b> — yesterday. За́втра (tomorrow) and сейча́с (now) both quarrel with был."},{"type":"type","q":"Type: “a month ago”","accept":["месяц назад","mesyats nazad","ме́сяц наза́д","mesac nazad"],"placeholder":"… наза́д","explain":"Ме́сяц наза́д — and наза́д comes <em>after</em> the time, the mirror of English “ago”."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Before, I didn't speak Russian.”</b> (a woman)","want":"Раньше я не говорила по-русски","answer":"Ра́ньше я не говори́ла по-ру́сски"}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma and Ива́н look back at her month over the last of the tea. A month ago she knew two words; tonight she can tell the whole story — in the past tense. Tap a line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, ме́сяц наза́д ты не говори́ла по-ру́сски.","ru":"Emma, a month ago you didn't speak Russian."},{"who":"A","en":"Да! Ра́ньше я зна́ла то́лько «приве́т» и «спаси́бо».","ru":"Yes! Before, I only knew ‘privet’ and ‘spasibo’. (то́лько = only)"},{"who":"B","en":"А сейча́с ты чита́ешь ру́сские кни́ги!","ru":"And now you read Russian books!"},{"who":"A","en":"Да! Вчера́ я чита́ла ру́сские кни́ги.","ru":"Yes! Yesterday I read Russian books."},{"who":"B","en":"Где ты была́ в Москве́?","ru":"Where were you in Moscow?"},{"who":"A","en":"Я была́ в па́рке, в теа́тре, в кафе́… Я была́ везде́!","ru":"I was in the park, at the theatre, at the café… I was everywhere! (везде́ = everywhere)"},{"who":"B","en":"И тебе́ нра́вится Москва́?","ru":"And do you like Moscow?"},{"who":"A","en":"Я люблю́ Москву́! Э́то был о́чень хоро́ший ме́сяц.","ru":"I love Moscow! It was a very good month."},{"who":"B","en":"Хоро́ший ме́сяц — и хоро́ший друг.","ru":"A good month — and a good friend."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve past-tense bricks: verbs in their -л / -ла clothes, and the time words that anchor them. Tap to flip, tap 🔊 to hear.","items":[{"en":"был / была́","ru":"was (m / f)","ex":"Я был / была́ в Москве́."},{"en":"бы́ли","ru":"were (plural)","ex":"Мы бы́ли в па́рке."},{"en":"чита́л / чита́ла","ru":"read (past)","ex":"Вчера́ я чита́л(а) кни́гу."},{"en":"рабо́тал / рабо́тала","ru":"worked","ex":"Ива́н мно́го рабо́тал."},{"en":"смотре́л / смотре́ла","ru":"watched","ex":"Я смотре́л(а) фильм."},{"en":"говори́л / говори́ла","ru":"spoke, said","ex":"Я говори́л(а) по-ру́сски."},{"en":"знал / зна́ла","ru":"knew","ex":"Ра́ньше я не знал(а) Москву́."},{"en":"люби́л / люби́ла","ru":"loved","ex":"Я всегда́ люби́л(а) чай."},{"en":"вчера́","ru":"yesterday","ex":"Вчера́ была́ пя́тница."},{"en":"ра́ньше","ru":"before, earlier","ex":"Ра́ньше я не говори́л(а) по-ру́сски."},{"en":"наза́д","ru":"ago","ex":"Ме́сяц наза́д я был(а) в Ло́ндоне."},{"en":"ме́сяц","ru":"month","ex":"Я был(а) в Москве́ ме́сяц."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning on the right — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Я была́ до́ма.","I was home. (a woman)"],["Мы бы́ли в па́рке.","We were in the park."],["Вчера́ я мно́го чита́л.","Yesterday I read a lot. (a man)"],["Ме́сяц наза́д","a month ago"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions — the past tense, start to finish. Remember: the ending marks gender, not person.","items":[{"q":"“I was in Moscow,” says Emma (a woman):","opts":["Я был в Москве́.","Я была́ в Москве́.","Я в Москве́."],"answer":1},{"q":"Put «чита́ть» into the past for a group of friends:","opts":["чита́л","чита́ла","чита́ли"],"answer":2},{"q":"«Вчера́ Ива́н ___ до́ма.» (Ivan was home.)","opts":["был","была́","бы́ли"],"answer":0},{"q":"The Russian past tense changes for…","opts":["gender and number","the person (I / you / he)","nothing at all"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Ра́ньше я не ___ по-ру́сски.» (a woman speaking)","opts":["говорю́","говори́ла","говори́л"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma looks back at her whole month — every verb in the past. Read it aloud; you were there for all of it.","title":"Мой ме́сяц","sentences":["Ме́сяц наза́д я была́ в Ло́ндоне.","Ра́ньше я не говори́ла по-ру́сски.","В Москве́ я мно́го чита́ла и говори́ла.","Я была́ в па́рке, в теа́тре и в кафе́.","Вчера́ я чита́ла кни́гу и хорошо́ понима́ла.","Э́то был о́чень хоро́ший ме́сяц."],"translation":"A month ago I was in London. Before, I didn't speak Russian. In Moscow I read and spoke a lot. I was in the park, at the theatre and at the café. Yesterday I read a book and understood it well. It was a very good month."},"essay":{"intro":"Your turn to look back.","prompt":"Write 3–4 sentences about yesterday or last week in the past tense: where you were (Я был / была́…), and two things you did (чита́л/чита́ла, рабо́тал/рабо́тала, смотре́л/смотре́ла…). Pick the -л or -ла form that fits you.","hint":"Drop the -ть, add your ending: a man чита́л, a woman чита́ла, a group чита́ли. And “was” comes back as был / была́ — the verb that hides in the present 🙂","example":"Вчера́ я была́ до́ма. Я мно́го чита́ла и смотре́ла фильм. Ра́ньше я не говори́ла по-ру́сски, а сейча́с говорю́!"}},"27":{"day":27,"week":"04","themeRu":"Aspect (a peek)","themeEn":"Ви́ды глаго́ла","intro":"Emma is halfway through a postcard home, stuck on one word. In English, “I wrote a postcard” and “I was writing a postcard” lean on a single verb — write. Russian keeps two: <b>писа́ть</b> for the writing-in-progress, <b>написа́ть</b> for the finished, sealed, done. Today you'll just <em>meet</em> these twins — a gentle peek at the biggest idea in Russian verbs. No system to master; only a new thing to notice.","introAudio":"Emma is halfway through a postcard home, stuck on one word. In English, I wrote a postcard and I was writing a postcard lean on a single verb — write. Russian keeps two: pisat for the writing-in-progress, napisat for the finished, sealed, done. Today you'll just meet these twins — a gentle peek at the biggest idea in Russian verbs. No system to master; only a new thing to notice.","goals":["Meet the Russian verb <b>pair</b>: one twin for the process, one for the result","Tell doing from done: <b>Я писа́л(а)</b> vs <b>Я написа́л(а)</b>","Spot the result twin — often just a prefix: <b>чита́ть → прочита́ть</b>","Choose the twin that says what you really mean"],"learned":["Met the verb pair: a process twin and a result twin","Told doing from done: <b>писа́л(а)</b> vs <b>написа́л(а)</b>","Spotted the result twin behind a prefix: <b>прочита́ть, написа́ть</b>","Chose the twin that says what you mean"],"review":{"intro":"Three quick ones before the twins arrive — yesterday's past tense, plus a case that never sleeps.","introAudio":"Three quick ones before the twins arrive — yesterday's past tense, plus a case that never sleeps.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer that fits. A wrong tap is just practice.","q":"Ива́н looks back (a man): «Вчера́ я ___ до́ма.»","opts":["была́","был","бы́ло"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ива́н is a man → <b>был</b>. Yesterday's rule: the past marks gender, not person."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Я люблю́ ___.» (I love Moscow.)","opts":["Москва́","Москву́","Москве́"],"answer":1,"explain":"Object of люблю́ → the accusative <b>Москву́</b>. Case is always-on in this course."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“Lena was at work.”</b>","want":"Лена была на работе","answer":"Ле́на была́ на рабо́те"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Verbs come in twos","title":"One English verb, <em>two</em> Russian twins","paras":["Here is the biggest idea in Russian verbs — and today you only need to <em>notice</em> it. Most verbs travel in pairs of twins. One twin is about the <b>process</b> — the doing, the being-busy: <b>де́лать</b> (to be doing). The other is about the <b>result</b> — the finished, done, complete: <b>сде́лать</b> (to get done).","English leans on one verb, “to do”, and lets the rest of the sentence fill in whether it's finished. Russian bakes that difference into the verb itself, by choosing the twin.","You've already met one twin of several pairs — чита́ть, писа́ть, смотре́ть are all process twins. Today you meet their result partners. Don't panic: this is a peek, not a system to master in a day."],"audio":"Here's the biggest idea in Russian verbs, and today you only need to notice it. Most verbs travel in pairs of twins. One twin is about the process — the doing, the being-busy: delat, to be doing. The other is about the result — the finished, done, complete: sdelat, to get done. English leans on one verb, to do, and lets context fill the rest. Russian picks the twin that fits.","examples":[{"ru":"What are you doing? (the process)","en":"Что ты <b>де́лаешь</b>?","say":"Что ты делаешь?"},{"ru":"I did it — done! (the result)","en":"Я <b>сде́лал(а)</b> — гото́во!","say":"Я сделал — готово!","gloss":"гото́во = ready, done"},{"ru":"“to read” has a twin — process and result","en":"чита́ть / прочита́ть","say":"читать, прочитать"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I'm doing my work right now” — the process. Which twin?","opts":["сде́лать","де́лать"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ongoing, in progress → the process twin <b>де́лать</b>. Сде́лать is the finished result."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which pair is “to read / to read to the end”?","opts":["чита́ть / прочита́ть","чита́ть / чита́ла","де́лать / чита́ть"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>чита́ть / прочита́ть</b> — process twin and result twin. The others mix in a past form or two unrelated verbs."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Doing vs done","title":"<em>Я писа́л</em> vs <em>Я написа́л</em> — in the past","paras":["The twins shine brightest in the past. <b>Я чита́л кни́гу</b> — I was reading a book, busy with it, maybe not to the end. <b>Я прочита́л кни́гу</b> — I read the book, all of it, finished.","Emma's postcard tells the same story. <b>Я писа́ла откры́тку</b> — I was writing a postcard (process). <b>Я написа́ла откры́тку</b> — I wrote it, done and sealed (result).","Same past-tense endings you learned yesterday — <b>-л / -ла / -ли</b> — sit on both twins. The only new choice is <em>which</em> twin: were you busy, or did you finish?"],"audio":"The twins shine brightest in the past. Ya chital knigu — I was reading a book, busy with it, maybe not to the end. Ya prochital knigu — I read the book, all of it, finished. Emma's postcard is the same story: ya pisala otkrytku — I was writing a postcard — versus ya napisala otkrytku — I wrote it, done and sealed.","table":{"rows":[["Я писа́л откры́тку. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я писал открытку.\">🔊</button>","I was writing a postcard. — process"],["Я написа́л откры́тку. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я написал открытку.\">🔊</button>","I wrote the postcard. — result, done"],["Я чита́л кни́гу. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я читал книгу.\">🔊</button>","I was reading a book. — process"],["Я прочита́л кни́гу. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я прочитал книгу.\">🔊</button>","I read the book (all of it). — result"]],"star":1},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Вот откры́тка! Я писа́ла её.","right":"Вот откры́тка! Я написа́ла её.","why":"It's finished and in your hand → the result twin <b>написа́ла</b>. Писа́ла only tells that you were busy with it — one English “wrote” hides both."}],"examples":[{"ru":"I was reading a book (busy with it). (a woman)","en":"Я <b>чита́ла</b> кни́гу.","say":"Я читала книгу.","gloss":"process — maybe not to the end"},{"ru":"I read the book (all of it, finished). (a woman)","en":"Я <b>прочита́ла</b> кни́гу.","say":"Я прочитала книгу.","gloss":"result — done and dusted"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"The postcard is finished at last. Emma (a woman) says:","opts":["Я писа́ла откры́тку.","Я написа́ла откры́тку.","Я пишу́ откры́тку."],"answer":1,"explain":"Finished result → the result twin <b>написа́ла</b>. Писа́ла is the process; пишу́ is right now."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I was reading a book.”</b> (a man — the process)","want":"Я читал книгу","answer":"Я чита́л кни́гу"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud. Listen to the sample first if you like.","target":"Я прочита́л кни́гу.","sub":"finished, cover to cover — the result verb","want":"Я прочитал книгу"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Spotting the twin","title":"A little prefix makes the result twin","paras":["How do you recognise the result twin? Very often it's the process verb with a small piece stuck on the front. <b>чита́ть → про</b>чита́ть, <b>писа́ть → на</b>писа́ть, <b>де́лать → с</b>де́лать, <b>смотре́ть → по</b>смотре́ть.","Different pairs take different prefixes, and there's no tidy rule to predict which — not at this level. So don't hunt for a system; learn each pair <em>as a pair</em>, the way you learned у меня́ есть next to у меня́ нет.","The prefix usually adds the idea of “all the way, to completion”: про- reads it through, на- writes it out, с- gets it done."],"audio":"How do you spot the result twin? Very often it's the process verb with a little piece stuck on the front: chitat becomes prochitat with pro; pisat becomes napisat with na; delat becomes sdelat with s; smotret becomes posmotret with po. Don't memorise a system — there isn't a tidy one at this level. Just learn each pair as a pair, the way you met u menya yest and u menya nyet.","table":{"rows":[["чита́ть → прочита́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"читать, прочитать\">🔊</button>","read → read (to the end) · про-"],["писа́ть → написа́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"писать, написать\">🔊</button>","write → write (finish) · на-"],["де́лать → сде́лать <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"делать, сделать\">🔊</button>","do → do (get done) · с-"],["смотре́ть → посмотре́ть <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"смотреть, посмотреть\">🔊</button>","watch → watch (to the end) · по-"]],"star":0},"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which prefix turns писа́ть into its result twin?","opts":["про-","на-","с-"],"answer":1,"explain":"писа́ть → <b>на</b>писа́ть. Each pair keeps its own prefix — that's why you learn them two-by-two."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — no stress marks needed.","q":"Type the result twin of чита́ть (read to the end):","accept":["прочитать","prochitat","прочита́ть"],"placeholder":"про…","explain":"чита́ть → <b>прочита́ть</b> — the prefix про- carries it to the finish line."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I watched the film (to the end).”</b> (a woman)","want":"Я посмотрела фильм","answer":"Я посмотре́ла фильм"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · Which twin, when","title":"Process for the doing, result for the done","paras":["So which twin do you reach for? Use the <b>process</b> twin for something ongoing, repeated or unfinished — I was reading, I read for hours. Use the <b>result</b> twin for one finished thing — I read it, I wrote it, done.","This is the honest edge of A1. Aspect is a whole country; today we just stand at the border and wave. You'll cross it properly later — for now, notice it.","A quick self-test: if you could add “…and finished it”, you want the result twin. If you could add “…for a while”, you want the process twin."],"audio":"So which twin? Reach for the process twin for something ongoing, repeated, or unfinished — I was reading, I read for a while. Reach for the result twin for one finished thing — I read it, I wrote it, done. This is the honest edge of A1: aspect is a big country and today we only stand at the border and wave. Notice it now; you'll cross it later.","examples":[{"ru":"I read for a long time (process). (a woman)","en":"Я до́лго <b>чита́ла</b>.","say":"Я долго читала.","gloss":"до́лго = for a long time → process"},{"ru":"And I finished the book! (a woman)","en":"И я <b>прочита́ла</b> кни́гу!","say":"И я прочитала книгу!","gloss":"one finished result → result twin"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Я до́лго ___ письмо́.» — you spent ages on it, the process. (a man)","opts":["написа́л","писа́л","пишу́"],"answer":1,"explain":"“For a long time / in progress” → the process twin <b>писа́л</b>. Написа́л would mean you finished it."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I did the work (finished it).”</b> (a woman)","want":"Я сделала работу","answer":"Я сде́лала рабо́ту"},{"type":"type","q":"Type: “I wrote a letter (and finished it).” (a man)","accept":["я написал письмо","ya napisal pismo","я написа́л письмо́"],"placeholder":"Я … письмо́","explain":"Finished → the result twin <b>написа́л</b>. The process twin писа́л would leave it half-done."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Emma is halfway through a postcard home. Ле́на watches the process — and, at last, its result. Tap a line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, что ты де́лаешь?","ru":"Emma, what are you doing?"},{"who":"A","en":"Я пишу́ откры́тку — из Москвы́!","ru":"I'm writing a postcard — from Moscow! (пишу́ = I'm writing)"},{"who":"B","en":"Ты уже́ написа́ла?","ru":"Have you already finished it? (уже́ = already)"},{"who":"A","en":"Нет! Я писа́ла, писа́ла — но не написа́ла.","ru":"No! I was writing and writing — but I haven't finished it."},{"who":"B","en":"Проце́сс есть, а результа́та нет!","ru":"There's a process, but no result!"},{"who":"A","en":"Оди́н моме́нт…","ru":"One moment…"},{"who":"A","en":"Всё! Написа́ла!","ru":"Done! I've finished it! (Всё = that's it, done)"},{"who":"B","en":"Хорошо́! Э́то результа́т.","ru":"Good! That's a result."}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve cards — four verb pairs and the words that name the idea. Each twin next to its partner: process, then result.","items":[{"en":"де́лать","ru":"to do, make (process)","ex":"Что ты де́лаешь?"},{"en":"сде́лать","ru":"to do, finish (result)","ex":"Я сде́лал(а) рабо́ту."},{"en":"чита́ть","ru":"to read (process)","ex":"Я чита́л(а) кни́гу."},{"en":"прочита́ть","ru":"to read to the end (result)","ex":"Я прочита́л(а) кни́гу — всю!"},{"en":"писа́ть","ru":"to write (process)","ex":"Я писа́л(а) письмо́."},{"en":"написа́ть","ru":"to write, finish (result)","ex":"Я написа́л(а) откры́тку."},{"en":"смотре́ть","ru":"to watch (process)","ex":"Я смотре́л(а) фильм."},{"en":"посмотре́ть","ru":"to watch to the end (result)","ex":"Я посмотре́л(а) весь фильм."},{"en":"письмо́","ru":"letter","ex":"Я чита́ю письмо́."},{"en":"откры́тка","ru":"postcard","ex":"Откры́тка из Москвы́."},{"en":"проце́сс","ru":"process","ex":"де́лать — э́то проце́сс."},{"en":"результа́т","ru":"result","ex":"сде́лать — э́то результа́т."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning on the right — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Я писа́л письмо́.","I was writing a letter."],["Я написа́л письмо́.","I wrote the letter (finished)."],["Я прочита́ла кни́гу.","I read the whole book. (a woman)"],["де́лать / сде́лать","to do (process / result)"]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions on the twins. One English verb, two Russian — process or result?","items":[{"q":"Which pair means “to read (process) / to read to the end (result)”?","opts":["чита́ть / прочита́ть","чита́ть / чита́ла","де́лать / сде́лать"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Вот письмо́ — я ___!» (Here's the letter — I've written it!)","opts":["писа́л","пишу́","написа́л"],"answer":2},{"q":"English has one verb “to write”. Russian has…","opts":["a pair: a process twin and a result twin","one verb, just like English","no verb at all"],"answer":0},{"q":"«Я до́лго ___ кни́гу.» (I read the book for a long time — the process.)","opts":["чита́ю","чита́л","прочита́л"],"answer":1},{"q":"The postcard is finished. Emma (a woman) says:","opts":["Я написа́ла откры́тку.","Я писа́ла откры́тку.","Я пишу́ откры́тку."],"answer":0}]},"reading":{"intro":"The postcard Emma finally finished — read it aloud. Notice the process verbs, then the result that seals it.","title":"Откры́тка из Москвы́","sentences":["Приве́т из Москвы́!","Я здесь ме́сяц — и говорю́ по-ру́сски!","Я мно́го чита́ла, писа́ла и смотре́ла фи́льмы.","Вчера́ я прочита́ла кни́гу — всю!","И написа́ла откры́тку — вот она́!","Спаси́бо, Москва́! Я люблю́ тебя́!"],"translation":"Hi from Moscow! I've been here a month — and I speak Russian! I read, wrote and watched films a lot. Yesterday I read a book — all of it! And I wrote this postcard — here it is! Thank you, Moscow! I love you! (здесь = here, всю = the whole thing, вот она́ = here it is)"},"essay":{"intro":"Two twins, one choice.","prompt":"Write 3–4 sentences about something you were doing and then finished. Use a process verb (чита́л/чита́ла, писа́л/писа́ла) for the doing, and its result twin (прочита́л(а), написа́л(а)) for the finish.","hint":"Process twin for the activity, result twin for the done-and-dusted. «Я писа́л(а) письмо́…» → «…и написа́л(а)!» One little prefix turns the process into the result 🙂","example":"Вчера́ я писа́ла письмо́. Я писа́ла и писа́ла. И написа́ла! Ещё я прочита́ла кни́гу — всю."}},"28":{"day":28,"week":"04","themeRu":"Family and people","themeEn":"Семья́ и лю́ди","intro":"Emma props her phone on the café table — it's a video call home, and suddenly her two worlds meet. Today you'll name the people who matter: <b>ма́ма, па́па, брат, сестра́, ба́бушка, де́душка</b>. You'll say who you have, describe them, and dodge one sneaky false friend — <b>семья́</b> looks like “family”, but its cousin <b>фами́лия</b> means your <em>surname</em>. Let's meet everyone.","introAudio":"Emma props her phone on the café table — it's a video call home, and suddenly her two worlds meet. Today you'll name the people who matter: mama, papa, brat, sestra, babushka, dedushka. You'll say who you have, describe them, and dodge one sneaky false friend — semya looks like family, but its cousin familiya means your surname. Let's meet everyone.","goals":["Name your people: <b>ма́ма, па́па, брат, сестра́, ба́бушка, де́душка</b>","Say who you have: <b>У меня́ есть брат и сестра́</b>","Describe them so every word agrees: <b>Мой ста́рший брат — студе́нт</b>","Never mix up <b>семья́</b> (family) and <b>фами́лия</b> (surname)"],"learned":["Named your people: <b>ма́ма, па́па, брат, сестра́, ба́бушка, де́душка</b>","Said who you have: <b>У меня́ есть брат и сестра́</b>","Described them with everything agreeing: <b>Мой ста́рший брат — студе́нт</b>","Kept <b>семья́</b> and <b>фами́лия</b> apart"],"review":{"intro":"Three warm-ups before the family arrives — the twins from yesterday, the past, and an old agreement habit.","introAudio":"Three warm-ups before the family arrives — the twins from yesterday, the past, and an old agreement habit.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer that fits. A wrong tap is just practice.","q":"The letter is finished. «Я ___ письмо́.»","opts":["писа́л","написа́л","пишу́"],"answer":1,"explain":"Finished result → the twin <b>написа́л</b>. Писа́л is the process, пишу́ is right now."},{"type":"choice","q":"Emma (a woman): «Ме́сяц наза́д я ___ в Ло́ндоне.»","opts":["был","была́","бы́ли"],"answer":1,"explain":"Emma is a woman → <b>была́</b>, with the stress on the end. The past marks gender."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“My new friend is at the university.”</b>","want":"Мой новый друг в университете","answer":"Мой но́вый друг в университе́те"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Your people","title":"<em>ма́ма, па́па, брат, сестра́</em> — and the -а surprise","paras":["Meet the family. <b>ма́ма</b> — mum, <b>па́па</b> — dad, <b>брат</b> — brother, <b>сестра́</b> — sister, <b>ба́бушка</b> — grandmother, <b>де́душка</b> — grandfather. Warm, everyday, first words a Russian child says.","Now a surprise. <b>па́па</b> and <b>де́душка</b> end in -а — which usually flags feminine — but they're men, so they're <b>masculine</b>: мой па́па, мой де́душка, never «моя́». With <em>people</em>, gender follows the person, not the ending.","That's the opposite of things, where the ending rules (стол is “он”, кни́га is “она́”). A person's own gender always wins."],"audio":"Meet the family. Mama, mum. Papa, dad. Brat, brother. Sestra, sister. Babushka, grandmother. Dedushka, grandfather. Now a surprise. Papa and dedushka end in the letter a, which usually means feminine — but these are men, so they are masculine: moy papa, moy dedushka, never moya. With people, gender follows the person, not the ending. That is the opposite of things, where the ending rules.","table":{"rows":[["ма́ма <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"мама\">🔊</button>","mum (feminine)"],["па́па <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"папа\">🔊</button>","dad (masculine, though it ends in -а!)"],["брат <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"брат\">🔊</button>","brother"],["сестра́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"сестра\">🔊</button>","sister"],["ба́бушка <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"бабушка\">🔊</button>","grandmother"],["де́душка <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"дедушка\">🔊</button>","grandfather (masculine, -а)"]],"star":1},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"моя́ па́па","right":"мой па́па","why":"Па́па is a man → <b>мой</b>, even though it ends in -а. With people the person's gender wins, not the letter."}],"examples":[{"ru":"This is my mum and my dad.","en":"Э́то моя́ <b>ма́ма</b> и мой <b>па́па</b>.","say":"Это моя мама и мой папа.","gloss":"ма́ма → моя́, but па́па → мой"},{"ru":"My grandfather loves tea.","en":"Мой <b>де́душка</b> лю́бит чай.","say":"Мой дедушка любит чай."},{"ru":"I have a brother and a sister.","en":"У меня́ есть <b>брат</b> и <b>сестра́</b>.","say":"У меня есть брат и сестра."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«___ па́па до́ма.» (My dad is home — careful!)","opts":["Моя́","Мой","Моё"],"answer":1,"explain":"Па́па is a man → <b>мой</b>, despite the -а ending. People go by the person, not the letter."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — no stress marks needed.","q":"Type the Russian for “grandmother”:","accept":["бабушка","babushka","ба́бушка"],"placeholder":"grandma…","explain":"<b>ба́бушка</b> — and it's feminine, so моя́ ба́бушка."},{"type":"choice","q":"Which is Emma's <b>sister</b>, agreeing correctly?","opts":["мой сестра́","моё сестра́","моя́ сестра́"],"answer":2,"explain":"Сестра́ is feminine → <b>моя́ сестра́</b>. The possessive matches the person's gender."}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Who you have","title":"<em>У меня́ есть брат</em> — and <em>нет бра́та</em>","paras":["To say who you have, use the frame you already own: <b>У меня́ есть</b> — I have. <b>У меня́ есть брат и сестра́</b> — I have a brother and a sister. Есть means “there is”, not the missing “to be”.","And to say you don't have someone, <b>нет</b> takes the genitive — exactly as it did for things: <b>У меня́ нет бра́та</b> (no brother), <b>У меня́ нет сестры́</b> (no sister). People bend just like objects did after нет.","Ask the same way with <b>у тебя́ есть…?</b> — do you have…? <b>У тебя́ есть сестра́?</b> — melody up at the end, no “do” needed."],"audio":"To say who you have, use the frame you already own: u menya yest — I have. U menya yest brat i sestra — I have a brother and a sister. And to say you don't have someone, nyet takes the genitive, exactly as it did for things: u menya nyet brata, no brother; u menya nyet sestry, no sister. The people bend just like the objects did. Ask the same way with u tebya yest — do you have?","table":{"rows":[["У меня́ есть брат. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"У меня есть брат.\">🔊</button>","I have a brother."],["У меня́ есть сестра́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"У меня есть сестра.\">🔊</button>","I have a sister."],["У меня́ нет бра́та. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"У меня нет брата.\">🔊</button>","I have no brother. — нет + genitive"],["У меня́ нет сестры́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"У меня нет сестры.\">🔊</button>","I have no sister. — нет + genitive"]],"star":2},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"У меня́ нет брат.","right":"У меня́ нет бра́та.","why":"After нет the genitive is compulsory — <b>бра́та</b>. Same rule as нет ка́рты for things; people obey it too."}],"examples":[{"ru":"I have a big family.","en":"У меня́ <b>больша́я семья́</b>.","say":"У меня большая семья.","gloss":"больша́я = big"},{"ru":"Do you have a brother? — No, I have a sister.","en":"<b>У тебя́ есть</b> брат? — Нет, у меня́ сестра́.","say":"У тебя есть брат? — Нет, у меня сестра.","gloss":"у тебя́ есть = do you have"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“I have a sister” in Russian:","opts":["У меня́ есть сестра́.","У меня́ нет сестра́.","Я есть сестра́."],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>У меня́ есть сестра́</b> — the have-frame plus the dictionary form. Есть means “there is”, not the missing “to be”."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I don't have a brother.”</b>","want":"У меня нет брата","answer":"У меня́ нет бра́та"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Everything agrees","title":"Describing people — the whole chain matches","paras":["Now describe your people — and here's the trap of the day. Every word leaning on a person must match that person's gender, right across the sentence. <b>Мой ста́рший брат — студе́нт</b>: my, older, brother, student — all masculine. <b>Моя́ мла́дшая сестра́ — студе́нтка</b>: all feminine. (ста́рший = older, мла́дший = younger.)","One gender, echoed by every word. English changes nothing — “my older brother is a student” has no gender in it. Russian tunes each word to agree, like instruments to one note.","Lean on the tools you already own: possessives (мой / моя́), adjectives (но́вый / но́вая, хоро́ший / хоро́шая), even nationalities (ру́сский / ру́сская). Same agreement, now pointed at people."],"audio":"Now describe your people — and here's the trap of the day. Every word that leans on a person must match that person's gender, all the way across the sentence. Moy starshy brat — student: my, elder, brother, student — all masculine. Moya mladshaya sestra — studentka: my, younger, sister, student — all feminine. One gender, echoed by every word. English changes nothing; Russian changes everything to agree.","table":{"rows":[["Мой ста́рший брат — студе́нт. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мой старший брат студент.\">🔊</button>","My older brother is a student. (all masculine)"],["Моя́ мла́дшая сестра́ — студе́нтка. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Моя младшая сестра студентка.\">🔊</button>","My younger sister is a student. (all feminine)"],["Мой па́па — ру́сский. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мой папа русский.\">🔊</button>","My dad is Russian."],["Моя́ ба́бушка — до́брая. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Моя бабушка добрая.\">🔊</button>","My grandmother is kind. (до́брая = kind)"]],"star":0},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Моя́ брат — хоро́шая студе́нтка.","right":"Мой брат — хоро́ший студе́нт.","why":"Брат is a man, so the whole chain goes masculine: <b>мой хоро́ший студе́нт</b>. Mixing genders mid-sentence is the classic slip."}],"examples":[{"ru":"My older brother is a good student.","en":"Мой <b>ста́рший</b> брат — <b>хоро́ший</b> студе́нт.","say":"Мой старший брат хороший студент."},{"ru":"My younger sister is a Russian student.","en":"Моя́ <b>мла́дшая</b> сестра́ — <b>ру́сская</b> студе́нтка.","say":"Моя младшая сестра русская студентка."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Pick the sentence where everything agrees:","opts":["Мой сестра́ — хоро́шая студе́нтка.","Моя́ сестра́ — хоро́шая студе́нтка.","Моя́ сестра́ — хоро́ший студе́нт."],"answer":1,"explain":"Сестра́ is feminine, so the whole line is feminine: <b>моя́ хоро́шая студе́нтка</b>. Agreement must hold end to end."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“My older brother is a student.”</b>","want":"Мой старший брат студент","answer":"Мой ста́рший брат студе́нт"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud. Match the stress you see.","target":"У меня́ есть ста́ршая сестра́.","sub":"older sister — ста́ршая agrees with her","want":"У меня есть старшая сестра"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · A false friend","title":"<em>семья́</em> is family — <em>фами́лия</em> is your surname","paras":["One last thing — a false friend wearing a familiar face. <b>семья́</b> looks like “family”, and it <em>is</em> family. But its cousin <b>фами́лия</b> does not mean family — it means your <b>surname</b>, your last name.","So: <b>Моя́ семья́ в А́нглии</b> — my family is in England. <b>Моя́ фами́лия — Смит</b> — my surname is Smith. Two look-alike words, two different jobs.","The trap is automatic for English speakers — “familia, family, of course”. Pause on it once and it's yours."],"audio":"One last thing — a false friend wearing a familiar face. Semya looks like family, and it means family. But its cousin familiya does not mean family — it means your surname, your last name. Moya semya v Anglii — my family is in England. Moya familiya Smit — my surname is Smith. Two look-alike words, two different jobs.","table":{"rows":[["семья́ <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"семья\">🔊</button>","family"],["фами́лия <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"фамилия\">🔊</button>","surname, last name (NOT family!)"],["Моя́ семья́ в А́нглии. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Моя семья в Англии.\">🔊</button>","My family is in England."],["Моя́ фами́лия — Смит. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Моя фамилия Смит.\">🔊</button>","My surname is Smith."]],"star":1},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Моя́ фами́лия в А́нглии.","right":"Моя́ семья́ в А́нглии.","why":"Your <em>surname</em> can't live in England — you mean <b>семья́</b>, family. Фами́лия is the name you write on forms."}],"examples":[{"ru":"My family is big and kind.","en":"Моя́ <b>семья́</b> больша́я и до́брая.","say":"Моя семья большая и добрая.","gloss":"больша́я = big, до́брая = kind"},{"ru":"My surname is Ivanov.","en":"Моя́ <b>фами́лия</b> — Ивано́в.","say":"Моя фамилия Иванов."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A form asks for your <b>surname</b>. The word is:","opts":["семья́","фами́лия","ма́ма"],"answer":1,"explain":"<b>фами́лия</b> = surname. Семья́ (family) is the false friend that traps English speakers."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“My family is in England.”</b>","want":"Моя семья в Англии","answer":"Моя́ семья́ в А́нглии"},{"type":"type","q":"Type the Russian for “family” (not surname!):","accept":["семья","semya","семья́","semia"],"placeholder":"семь…","explain":"<b>семья́</b> = family. Its look-alike фами́лия means your surname."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"A video call home — then Ле́на turns the screen to her own family. Two families, one evening. Tap a line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, э́то моя́ семья́!","ru":"Emma, this is my family!"},{"who":"A","en":"О́чень прия́тно! А э́то кто?","ru":"Nice to meet you! And who's this?"},{"who":"B","en":"Э́то моя́ ма́ма и мой па́па.","ru":"This is my mum and my dad."},{"who":"A","en":"А э́то ба́бушка и де́душка?","ru":"And are these grandma and grandpa?"},{"who":"B","en":"Да! Ба́бушка лю́бит чай, а де́душка — кни́ги.","ru":"Yes! Grandma loves tea, and grandpa loves books."},{"who":"A","en":"У тебя́ есть брат?","ru":"Do you have a brother? (у тебя́ есть = do you have)"},{"who":"B","en":"Да! Ива́н — мой ста́рший брат.","ru":"Yes! Ivan is my older brother. (ста́рший = older)"},{"who":"A","en":"Ха-ха! Ива́н — мой хоро́ший друг!","ru":"Ha ha! Ivan is my good friend!"},{"who":"B","en":"Тепе́рь ты то́же семья́.","ru":"Now you're family too. (тепе́рь = now)"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve people worth naming. Tap to flip, tap 🔊 to hear — and watch which ones take мой despite the -а.","items":[{"en":"семья́","ru":"family","ex":"Моя́ семья́ в А́нглии."},{"en":"ма́ма","ru":"mum","ex":"Моя́ ма́ма до́ма."},{"en":"па́па","ru":"dad","ex":"Мой па́па до́ма (masculine, though -а!)."},{"en":"брат","ru":"brother","ex":"У меня́ есть брат."},{"en":"сестра́","ru":"sister","ex":"Моя́ сестра́ — студе́нтка."},{"en":"ба́бушка","ru":"grandmother","ex":"Моя́ ба́бушка лю́бит чай."},{"en":"де́душка","ru":"grandfather","ex":"Мой де́душка чита́ет кни́гу (masculine, -а)."},{"en":"сын","ru":"son","ex":"Э́то мой сын."},{"en":"дочь","ru":"daughter","ex":"Э́то моя́ дочь."},{"en":"роди́тели","ru":"parents","ex":"Мои́ роди́тели в Ло́ндоне."},{"en":"муж / жена́","ru":"husband / wife","ex":"муж и жена́ — a husband and wife."},{"en":"фами́лия","ru":"surname (not family!)","ex":"Моя́ фами́лия — Смит."}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning on the right — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Э́то моя́ ма́ма.","This is my mum."],["У меня́ есть ста́рший брат.","I have an older brother."],["Моя́ фами́лия — Смит.","My surname is Smith."],["У меня́ нет сестры́.","I have no sister."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions — your people, all agreeing, and one false friend to sidestep.","items":[{"q":"Which word is your <b>surname</b>, not your family?","opts":["семья́","фами́лия","ма́ма"],"answer":1},{"q":"Emma's grandmother — pick the agreeing possessive:","opts":["мой ба́бушка","моё ба́бушка","моя́ ба́бушка"],"answer":2},{"q":"«___ па́па до́ма.» (My dad — careful, па́па is masculine!)","opts":["Моя́","Мой","Моё"],"answer":1},{"q":"«У меня́ ___ брат.» (I have a brother.)","opts":["есть","нет","не"],"answer":0},{"q":"«У меня́ нет ___.» (I don't have a brother — genitive!)","opts":["брат","бра́та","бра́ту"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma introduces her family — read it aloud, and watch every word line up by gender.","title":"Моя́ семья́","sentences":["Э́то моя́ семья́.","Мой па́па и моя́ ма́ма — в А́нглии.","У меня́ есть ста́рший брат и мла́дшая сестра́.","Мой брат — студе́нт, и моя́ сестра́ то́же студе́нтка.","Моя́ ба́бушка и мой де́душка лю́бят чай и кни́ги.","Я о́чень люблю́ мою́ семью́!"],"translation":"This is my family. My dad and my mum are in England. I have an older brother and a younger sister. My brother is a student, and my sister is also a student. My grandmother and grandfather love tea and books. I love my family very much! (ста́рший = older, мла́дшая = younger)"},"essay":{"intro":"Introduce your people.","prompt":"Write 3–4 sentences about your family: say who you have (У меня́ есть…), and describe two of them with a possessive and an adjective (Мой брат — …, Моя́ сестра́ — …). Make every word agree.","hint":"Match the possessive to the person: <b>мой</b> брат, <b>моя́</b> сестра́ — and remember <b>па́па</b> and <b>де́душка</b> take <b>мой</b> even though they end in -а 🙂","example":"У меня́ есть ма́ма, па́па и сестра́. Мой па́па и моя́ ма́ма — в А́нглии. Моя́ сестра́ — хоро́шая студе́нтка. Я о́чень люблю́ мою́ семью́!"}},"29":{"day":29,"week":"04","themeRu":"My day","themeEn":"Мой день","intro":"By now Emma has a rhythm: a morning, a café, a park, an evening. Today you'll tell a whole day from start to finish — using only words you already own, tied together with three small connectors: <b>обы́чно</b> (usually), <b>пото́м</b> (then), <b>ка́ждый день</b> (every day). No new grammar today, just the art of stringing your Russian into a story.","introAudio":"By now Emma has a rhythm: a morning, a café, a park, an evening. Today you'll tell a whole day from start to finish — using only words you already own, tied together with three small connectors: obychno — usually, patom — then, and kazhdy den — every day. No new grammar today, just the art of stringing your Russian into a story.","goals":["Tell your routine in the present: <b>Ка́ждый день я…</b>, <b>Обы́чно я…</b>","Place things in the day: <b>у́тром, днём, ве́чером, но́чью</b>","Chain events with <b>пото́м</b> — and keep your pronoun for clarity","Fold in a past moment: <b>Обы́чно…, но вчера́ я был(а)…</b>"],"learned":["Told your routine: <b>Ка́ждый день я…</b>, <b>Обы́чно я…</b>","Placed things in the day: <b>у́тром, днём, ве́чером, но́чью</b>","Chained events with <b>пото́м</b>, keeping the pronoun","Mixed a past moment into the routine"],"review":{"intro":"Three warm-ups before the day begins — the past, the twins, and a case that never clocks off.","introAudio":"Three warm-ups before the day begins — the past, the twins, and a case that never clocks off.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the answer that fits. A wrong tap is just practice.","q":"Ива́н (a man): «Вчера́ я ___ до́ма.»","opts":["была́","был","бы́ло"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ива́н is a man → <b>был</b>. The past marks gender, not person."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Я ___ кни́гу — всю!» (I read the whole book.)","opts":["чита́л","прочита́л","чита́ю"],"answer":1,"explain":"The whole book, finished → the result twin <b>прочита́л</b>. Чита́л is just the process."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“I like the parks.”</b>","want":"Мне нравятся парки","answer":"Мне нра́вятся па́рки"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Rule 1 · Your routine","title":"<em>Ка́ждый день, обы́чно</em> — a habit in the present","paras":["To tell your routine, stay in the present and add two small words. <b>ка́ждый день</b> — every day — and <b>обы́чно</b> — usually. <b>Ка́ждый день я рабо́таю и чита́ю.</b> <b>Обы́чно я до́ма ве́чером.</b>","No new tense: your everyday life is just the present tense you already run, with these two signposts pinned to the front.","One fixed detail: день is masculine, so it's <b>ка́ждый</b> день — never «ка́ждая». Learn the phrase as a single block."],"audio":"To tell your routine, stay in the present and add two small words. Kazhdy den — every day — and obychno — usually. Kazhdy den ya rabotayu i chitayu — every day I work and read. Obychno ya doma vecherom — I'm usually home in the evening. No new tense: your everyday life is just the present tense you already run, with these two signposts.","table":{"rows":[["ка́ждый день <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"каждый день\">🔊</button>","every day"],["обы́чно <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"обычно\">🔊</button>","usually"],["Ка́ждый день я чита́ю. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Каждый день я читаю.\">🔊</button>","Every day I read."],["Обы́чно я до́ма. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Обычно я дома.\">🔊</button>","I'm usually home."]],"star":0},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"Ка́ждая день я рабо́таю.","right":"Ка́ждый день я рабо́таю.","why":"День is masculine → <b>ка́ждый</b> день. It's a fixed phrase — learn it as one block."}],"examples":[{"ru":"Every day I speak Russian.","en":"<b>Ка́ждый день</b> я говорю́ по-ру́сски.","say":"Каждый день я говорю по-русски."},{"ru":"I usually work in the afternoon.","en":"<b>Обы́чно</b> я рабо́таю днём.","say":"Обычно я работаю днём.","gloss":"днём = in the afternoon (rule 2!)"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"“Every day I read” — pick it:","opts":["Ка́ждая день я чита́ю.","Ка́ждый день я чита́ю.","Ка́ждый день я чита́л."],"answer":1,"explain":"День is masculine → <b>ка́ждый день</b>, and a routine stays in the present: чита́ю."},{"type":"choice","q":"«___ я до́ма ве́чером.» (I'm usually home in the evening.)","opts":["Обы́чно","Вчера́","Пото́м"],"answer":0,"explain":"<b>Обы́чно</b> = usually — the word for a habit. Вчера́ is yesterday, пото́м is “then”."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Every day I speak Russian.”</b>","want":"Каждый день я говорю по-русски","answer":"Ка́ждый день я говорю́ по-ру́сски"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 2 · Parts of the day","title":"<em>у́тром, днём, ве́чером, но́чью</em>","paras":["Russian slices the day into four ready-made adverbs. <b>у́тром</b> — in the morning. <b>днём</b> — in the daytime, the afternoon. <b>ве́чером</b> — in the evening. <b>но́чью</b> — at night.","Just drop one in front and add a place: <b>У́тром я до́ма. Днём я в университе́те. Ве́чером я в па́рке.</b> No preposition to choose, no ending to build — these words come pre-assembled.","They pair beautifully with your places: у́тром + до́ма, днём + на рабо́те, ве́чером + в кафе́. Time, then place — the shape of every plan."],"audio":"Russian splits the day into four adverbs. Utrom — in the morning. Dnyom — in the daytime, the afternoon. Vecherom — in the evening. Nochyu — at night. Just drop one in front and add a place: utrom ya doma, dnyom ya v universitete, vecherom ya v parke. No preposition, no case to build — these are ready-made words.","table":{"rows":[["у́тром <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"утром\">🔊</button>","in the morning"],["днём <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"днём\">🔊</button>","in the afternoon / daytime"],["ве́чером <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"вечером\">🔊</button>","in the evening"],["но́чью <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"ночью\">🔊</button>","at night"]],"star":0},"mistakes":[{"wrong":"В у́тро я до́ма.","right":"У́тром я до́ма.","why":"“In the morning” is one ready word — <b>у́тром</b>. No preposition, no ending to bolt on."}],"examples":[{"ru":"In the morning I'm home, in the afternoon at the university.","en":"<b>У́тром</b> я до́ма, <b>днём</b> я в университе́те.","say":"Утром я дома, днём я в университете."},{"ru":"In the evening I watch a film.","en":"<b>Ве́чером</b> я смотрю́ фильм.","say":"Вечером я смотрю фильм."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«___ я за́втракаю до́ма.» (In the morning I have breakfast at home.)","opts":["Ве́чером","У́тром","Но́чью"],"answer":1,"explain":"Breakfast belongs to <b>у́тром</b> — the morning. Ве́чером is evening, но́чью is night."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — no stress marks needed.","q":"Type: “in the evening”","accept":["вечером","vecherom","ве́чером"],"placeholder":"in the evening…","explain":"<b>ве́чером</b> — one ready word, no preposition."},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud. Listen to the sample first if you like.","target":"У́тром я за́втракаю до́ма.","sub":"your morning in one sentence","want":"Утром я завтракаю дома"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 3 · Then, and then","title":"<em>пото́м</em> chains it — keep your <em>я</em>","paras":["To chain events, use <b>пото́м</b> — then, after that. <b>У́тром я за́втракаю, пото́м я е́ду в университе́т.</b> One event, then the next, on a single thread.","Now the trap of the day. Russian word order is flexible: <b>У́тром я чита́ю</b> and <b>Я чита́ю у́тром</b> are both fine. But keep your pronoun — the <b>я</b>. The ending hints at the person, yet in a fast story the pronoun keeps who-does-what crystal clear.","Drop the я in a long chain and your listener works harder than they should. Flexible order, steady pronoun — that's the recipe for clear Russian.","One more thing: Russian happily drops <b>я</b> — the ending already says who acts, so «Пото́м иду́ домо́й» is fine Russian. In a long chain, though, keeping <b>я</b> keeps the thread easy to follow. At this stage, keep it."],"audio":"To chain events, use patom — then, after that. Utrom ya zavtrakayu, patom ya yedu v universitet — in the morning I have breakfast, then I go to the university. Now the trap. Russian word order is flexible: utrom ya chitayu and ya chitayu utrom both work. But keep your pronoun — the ya. The ending hints at the person, yet in a fast story the pronoun keeps who-does-what crystal clear. Drop it and your listener works harder than they should. One more thing: Russian happily drops ya — the ending already says who acts. In a long chain, though, keeping ya keeps the thread easy to follow. At this stage, keep it.","examples":[{"ru":"First I stroll in the park, then I watch a film.","en":"Снача́ла я гуля́ю в па́рке, <b>пото́м</b> смотрю́ фильм.","say":"Сначала я гуляю в парке, потом смотрю фильм.","gloss":"снача́ла = first"},{"ru":"In the morning I read — or: I read in the morning.","en":"<b>У́тром</b> я чита́ю. = Я чита́ю <b>у́тром</b>.","say":"Утром я читаю. Я читаю утром.","gloss":"both orders are fine — keep the я"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Emma keeps the <b>я</b> in “Пото́м я иду́ в парк” because…","opts":["Russian never drops pronouns","it keeps who-does-what clear","the verb makes no sense alone"],"answer":1,"explain":"Russian <em>can</em> drop pronouns, but keeping <b>я</b> makes a fast story clear. That's the day's trap: flexible order, steady pronoun."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Then I go to the park.”</b>","want":"Потом я иду в парк","answer":"Пото́м я иду́ в парк"}]},{"eyebrow":"Rule 4 · A day, with a past moment","title":"<em>Обы́чно…, но вчера́…</em> — the routine and the exception","paras":["Now put it all together — a whole day in the present, with one past moment folded in. <b>Ка́ждый день я рабо́таю. Но вчера́ я был(а) в па́рке.</b>","Usually the present; for one day that broke the pattern, slip into the past you learned this week. That contrast — <b>обы́чно</b> versus <b>вчера́</b> — is how real stories breathe.","You already own every piece: the present for habits, the past for what happened, the connectors to tie them. A whole day, told in your own Russian."],"audio":"Now put it all together — a whole day, morning to night, in the present, with one past moment folded in. Kazhdy den ya rabotayu. No vchera ya byl v parke — every day I work, but yesterday I was in the park. Usually the present; for one day that broke the pattern, slip into the past you learned this week. That contrast — obychno versus vchera — is how real stories breathe.","examples":[{"ru":"I usually work, but yesterday I rested. (a woman)","en":"Обы́чно я рабо́таю, но вчера́ я <b>отдыха́ла</b>.","say":"Обычно я работаю, но вчера я отдыхала.","gloss":"отдыха́ть = to rest"},{"ru":"Every day I'm at the university, but today I'm home.","en":"Ка́ждый день я в университе́те, но сего́дня я до́ма.","say":"Каждый день я в университете, но сегодня я дома."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"«Обы́чно я до́ма, но вчера́ я ___ в теа́тре.» (a woman)","opts":["была́","был","бу́ду"],"answer":0,"explain":"One past day → the past <b>была́</b> (a woman). Обы́чно keeps the present; вчера́ flips to the past."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“In the evening I stroll in the park.”</b>","want":"Вечером я гуляю в парке","answer":"Ве́чером я гуля́ю в па́рке"},{"type":"type","q":"Type: “Every day I work.”","accept":["каждый день я работаю","kazhdy den ya rabotayu","ка́ждый день я рабо́таю","kazhdiy den ya rabotayu"],"placeholder":"Ка́ждый день …","explain":"Ка́ждый день я рабо́таю — a habit stays in the present tense."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"Ива́н asks Emma about her days in Moscow — and she walks him through one, morning to evening. Tap a line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, что ты де́лаешь ка́ждый день?","ru":"Emma, what do you do every day?"},{"who":"A","en":"О, мой день о́чень хоро́ший! У́тром я за́втракаю до́ма.","ru":"Oh, my day is very good! In the morning I have breakfast at home."},{"who":"B","en":"А пото́м?","ru":"And then?"},{"who":"A","en":"Пото́м я е́ду в университе́т. Днём я рабо́таю и чита́ю.","ru":"Then I go to the university. In the afternoon I work and read."},{"who":"B","en":"Ты обы́чно в кафе́ днём?","ru":"Are you usually at the café in the afternoon?"},{"who":"A","en":"Да! Ко́фе и сы́рники. Пото́м я гуля́ю в па́рке.","ru":"Yes! Coffee and sirniki. Then I stroll in the park."},{"who":"B","en":"А ве́чером?","ru":"And in the evening?"},{"who":"A","en":"Ве́чером я до́ма. Я отдыха́ю и смотрю́ фильм.","ru":"In the evening I'm home. I rest and watch a film."},{"who":"B","en":"Хоро́ший день, Э́мма!","ru":"A good day, Emma!"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve cards for a whole day — the times, the links, and a few gentle routine verbs (all the -е- family you already conjugate).","items":[{"en":"день","ru":"day","ex":"Ка́ждый день я чита́ю."},{"en":"у́тром","ru":"in the morning","ex":"У́тром я до́ма."},{"en":"днём","ru":"in the afternoon, daytime","ex":"Днём я рабо́таю."},{"en":"ве́чером","ru":"in the evening","ex":"Ве́чером я смотрю́ фильм."},{"en":"но́чью","ru":"at night","ex":"Но́чью я до́ма."},{"en":"обы́чно","ru":"usually","ex":"Обы́чно я в университе́те."},{"en":"пото́м","ru":"then, after that","ex":"Пото́м я иду́ в парк."},{"en":"ка́ждый день","ru":"every day","ex":"Ка́ждый день я говорю́ по-ру́сски."},{"en":"за́втракать","ru":"to have breakfast","ex":"У́тром я за́втракаю."},{"en":"гуля́ть","ru":"to stroll, walk","ex":"Я гуля́ю в па́рке."},{"en":"отдыха́ть","ru":"to rest","ex":"Ве́чером я отдыха́ю."},{"en":"де́лать","ru":"to do","ex":"Что ты де́лаешь днём?"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning on the right — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["У́тром я за́втракаю.","In the morning I have breakfast."],["Пото́м я е́ду в университе́т.","Then I go to the university."],["Ве́чером я гуля́ю в па́рке.","In the evening I stroll in the park."],["Ка́ждый день хоро́ший.","Every day is good."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Five questions — a day in order, and the pronoun that keeps it clear.","items":[{"q":"«___ я за́втракаю до́ма.» (In the morning…)","opts":["Ве́чером","У́тром","Но́чью"],"answer":1},{"q":"“every day” in Russian:","opts":["ка́ждая день","ка́ждый дня","ка́ждый день"],"answer":2},{"q":"Which word chains one event to the next — “then”?","opts":["обы́чно","пото́м","у́тром"],"answer":1},{"q":"«Ве́чером я ___ в па́рке.» (I stroll in the park.)","opts":["гуля́ю","гуля́ешь","гуля́ем"],"answer":0},{"q":"Emma keeps the <b>я</b> in a chain of actions because it…","opts":["is grammatically required","keeps who-does-what clear","changes the meaning"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's perfect ordinary Moscow day, morning to night. Read it aloud — it's built from words you've owned for weeks.","title":"Мой день в Москве́","sentences":["Э́то мой день в Москве́.","У́тром я за́втракаю до́ма.","Пото́м я е́ду в университе́т и рабо́таю.","Днём я обы́чно в кафе́: ко́фе и сы́рники.","Ве́чером я гуля́ю в па́рке, а пото́м смотрю́ фильм.","Ка́ждый день хоро́ший — я люблю́ Москву́!"],"translation":"This is my day in Moscow. In the morning I have breakfast at home. Then I go to the university and work. In the afternoon I'm usually at the café: coffee and sirniki. In the evening I stroll in the park, and then I watch a film. Every day is good — I love Moscow!"},"essay":{"intro":"Write your day.","prompt":"Describe your ordinary day in 4–5 sentences, morning to night. Use у́тром / днём / ве́чером to place things in time, and пото́м or ка́ждый день to link them. Keep the pronoun so it stays clear.","hint":"Frame it by time of day: У́тром я… Пото́м я… Ве́чером я… — and обы́чно (usually) turns one day into your every day 🙂","example":"У́тром я за́втракаю до́ма и чита́ю. Пото́м я е́ду в университе́т и рабо́таю. Днём я обы́чно в кафе́. Ве́чером я гуля́ю в па́рке, а пото́м смотрю́ фильм. Хоро́ший день!"}},"30":{"day":30,"week":"04","themeRu":"About me","themeEn":"О себе́","intro":"Thirty days ago, Cyrillic was a wall of unfamiliar shapes. Look at you now. Emma's month in Moscow ends today, and so does your climb through A1 — so we'll spend this last lesson the best way there is: gathering everything you can do into one warm paragraph about yourself. No new grammar today. Just you, thirty days stronger, telling Russia who you are.","introAudio":"Thirty days ago, Cyrillic was a wall of unfamiliar shapes. Look at you now. Emma's month in Moscow ends today, and so does your climb through A1 — so we'll spend this last lesson the best way there is: gathering everything you can do into one warm paragraph about yourself. No new grammar today. Just you, thirty days stronger, telling Russia who you are.","goals":["Say who you are, start to finish: name, age, where you're from","Paint your world: what you have, where you are, what you like","Reach into the past: <b>Ме́сяц наза́д я был(а)…</b>","Put it all into one paragraph about yourself — <b>О себе́</b>"],"learned":["Said who you are: name, age, where you're from","Painted your world: what you have, where you are, what you like","Reached into the past: <b>Ме́сяц наза́д я был(а)…</b>","Wrote a whole paragraph about yourself — <b>О себе́</b>"],"review":{"intro":"Five throwbacks — one from each corner of the level. Gender and cases, as ever, are on the tray.","introAudio":"Five throwbacks — one from each corner of the level. Gender and cases, as ever, are on the tray.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> read the sentence and tap the answer that fits. A wrong tap is just practice.","q":"“I am a student” (a woman) — no “to be”, no “a”:","opts":["Я студе́нтка.","Я есть студе́нтка.","Я ам студе́нтка."],"answer":0,"explain":"Day one's gift: <b>Я студе́нтка</b> — drop “am”, drop “a”. The others smuggle English scaffolding back in."},{"type":"choice","q":"«___ кни́га» — my book:","opts":["Мой","Моя́","Моё"],"answer":1,"explain":"Кни́га is feminine → <b>моя́</b>. The possessive matches the thing's gender, not the owner's."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Я чита́ю ___.» (I'm reading a book.)","opts":["кни́га","кни́гу","кни́ги"],"answer":1,"explain":"The object of чита́ю takes the accusative: feminine -а → -у, <b>кни́гу</b>."},{"type":"choice","q":"«Я в ___.» — in Moscow:","opts":["Москва́","Москву́","Москве́"],"answer":2,"explain":"Place after в → the prepositional -е: <b>в Москве́</b>. Москву́ is the object form, Москва́ just names her."},{"type":"build","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the words in order to build the sentence.","q":"Build: <b>“Yesterday I was home.”</b> (a woman)","want":"Вчера я была дома","answer":"Вчера́ я была́ до́ма"}]},"rules":[{"eyebrow":"Recap 1 · Who I am","title":"Name, age, where you're from — the opening lines","paras":["Everything a stranger needs, in five bricks. Greet: <b>Здра́вствуйте</b> to be safe, <b>приве́т</b> to a friend. Then day one's gift, the vanished verb: <b>Я студе́нтка</b> — no “am”, no “a”.","Your name is a ready phrase: <b>Меня́ зову́т Э́мма</b>. Where you're from uses из + the genitive: <b>Я из А́нглии</b>. And age lands in the dative: <b>Мне два́дцать лет</b> — to me, twenty years, never «я два́дцать».","Say those five and you already exist in Russian. Everything after is detail."],"audio":"Everything a stranger needs, in five bricks. Greet: zdravstvuyte to be safe, privet to a friend. Then the zero copula, day one's gift — ya studentka, no am, no a. Your name: menya zovut Emma. Where from: ya iz Anglii. And your age through the dative: mnye dvadtsat let — to me, twenty years, never ya dvadtsat. Say those five and you already exist in Russian.","table":{"rows":[["Меня́ зову́т Э́мма. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Меня зовут Эмма.\">🔊</button>","My name is Emma."],["Я студе́нтка из А́нглии. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я студентка из Англии.\">🔊</button>","I'm a student from England."],["Мне два́дцать лет. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мне двадцать лет.\">🔊</button>","I'm twenty years old."],["Здра́вствуйте! О́чень прия́тно! <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Здравствуйте! Очень приятно!\">🔊</button>","Hello! Nice to meet you!"]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"Hello! My name is Emma.","en":"Здра́вствуйте! Меня́ зову́т Э́мма.","say":"Здравствуйте! Меня зовут Эмма."},{"ru":"I'm a student, and I'm from England.","en":"Я студе́нтка, и я из А́нглии.","say":"Я студентка, и я из Англии."},{"ru":"I'm twenty years old.","en":"Мне два́дцать лет.","say":"Мне двадцать лет.","gloss":"age lands in the dative — мне, not я"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"A woman introduces herself. Pick the natural, correct line:","opts":["Я есть студе́нтка из А́нглия.","Я студе́нтка из А́нглии.","Я студе́нтка из А́нглию."],"answer":1,"explain":"No “to be” (я студе́нтка), and из takes the genitive: <b>из А́нглии</b>. The distractors smuggle in есть or the wrong case."},{"type":"type","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> type the Russian. Plain letters are fine — no stress marks needed.","q":"Type: “My name is …” (use your own name, or Emma)","accept":["меня зовут эмма","menya zovut emma","меня́ зову́т Э́мма","меня зовут"],"placeholder":"Меня́ зову́т …","explain":"<b>Меня́ зову́т</b> + a name — one ready-made brick from day one."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I'm twenty years old.”</b>","want":"Мне двадцать лет","answer":"Мне два́дцать лет"}]},{"eyebrow":"Recap 2 · My world & what I like","title":"Have, place, like — the map of your life","paras":["Your world in three frames. What you have: <b>У меня́ есть</b> — <b>У меня́ есть брат и друзья́</b>. Where you are: the prepositional — <b>Я в Москве́, на рабо́те, до́ма</b>.","What you like runs on two rails you keep apart. <b>Мне нра́вится</b> — the thing pleases me, and stays nominative: Мне нра́вится метро́. <b>Я люблю́</b> + the accusative — you act on it: Я люблю́ <b>Москву́</b>.","And every describing word still agrees: <b>мой брат, моя́ сестра́, но́вые друзья́</b>. Have, place, like — with everything in gender-step."],"audio":"Your world in three frames. What you have: u menya yest — u menya yest brat i druzya. Where you are: the prepositional — ya v Moskve, na rabote, doma. What you like, on two rails: mnye nravitsa — the thing pleases me, and stays nominative — and ya lyublyu plus the accusative — ya lyublyu Moskvu. And every describing word still agrees: moy brat, moya sestra, novye druzya. Have, place, like — with everything in gender-step.","table":{"rows":[["У меня́ есть друзья́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"У меня есть друзья.\">🔊</button>","I have friends."],["Я в Москве́, в кафе́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я в Москве, в кафе.\">🔊</button>","I'm in Moscow, in a café."],["Мне нра́вится метро́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Мне нравится метро.\">🔊</button>","I like the metro. — нра́вится + nominative"],["Я люблю́ Москву́. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Я люблю Москву.\">🔊</button>","I love Moscow. — люблю́ + accusative"]],"star":3},"examples":[{"ru":"I have a family and good friends.","en":"У меня́ есть семья́ и <b>хоро́шие</b> друзья́.","say":"У меня есть семья и хорошие друзья."},{"ru":"I like the parks, and I love the metro.","en":"Мне <b>нра́вятся</b> па́рки, и я <b>люблю́</b> метро́.","say":"Мне нравятся парки, и я люблю метро.","gloss":"нра́вятся for plural; люблю́ + accusative"}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence keeps both “like” rails correct?","opts":["Мне нра́вится Москву́.","Я люблю́ Москва́.","Я люблю́ Москву́, и мне нра́вится метро́."],"answer":2,"explain":"люблю́ + accusative (Москву́), нра́вится + nominative (метро́). The other two cross the rails."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“I have a brother and a sister.”</b>","want":"У меня есть брат и сестра","answer":"У меня́ есть брат и сестра́"},{"type":"speak","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> tap the mic and say it out loud. Listen to the sample first if you like.","target":"Мне нра́вится Москва́.","sub":"you have earned this sentence","want":"Мне нравится Москва"}]},{"eyebrow":"Recap 3 · My past & my days","title":"Was, finished, every day — your story in time","paras":["Your story now moves through time. The past you unlocked this week: <b>Ме́сяц наза́д я был(а) в Ло́ндоне</b> — the ending marking gender, not person. The aspect twins: <b>Я чита́ла</b>, and finally <b>Я прочита́ла</b> — I read it, done.","And your days, tied with connectors: <b>Ка́ждый день я говорю́ по-ру́сски. У́тром я за́втракаю, пото́м рабо́таю, ве́чером гуля́ю.</b>","Past, present, routine — you can walk through all three now, and that's the whole of A1. One last time: put them together into you."],"audio":"Your story now moves through time. The past you unlocked this week: mesyats nazad ya byl or byla — a month ago I was — the ending marking gender, not person. The aspect twins: ya chitala, and finally ya prochitala — I read it, done. And your days, tied with connectors: kazhdy den ya govoryu po-russki, utrom ya zavtrakayu, patom rabotayu, vecherom gulyayu. Past, present, routine — you can walk through all three now.","table":{"rows":[["Ме́сяц наза́д я была́ в Ло́ндоне. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Месяц назад я была в Лондоне.\">🔊</button>","A month ago I was in London."],["Вчера́ я прочита́ла кни́гу. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Вчера я прочитала книгу.\">🔊</button>","Yesterday I read a book (all of it)."],["Ка́ждый день я говорю́ по-ру́сски. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Каждый день я говорю по-русски.\">🔊</button>","Every day I speak Russian."],["У́тром я за́втракаю, пото́м рабо́таю. <button class=\"play\" data-say=\"Утром я завтракаю, потом работаю.\">🔊</button>","In the morning I have breakfast, then I work."]],"star":2},"examples":[{"ru":"A month ago I didn't speak Russian — now I do! (a woman)","en":"Ме́сяц наза́д я не <b>говори́ла</b> по-ру́сски — сейча́с говорю́!","say":"Месяц назад я не говорила по-русски — сейчас говорю!"},{"ru":"Every day I read, and yesterday I finished a book. (a woman)","en":"Ка́ждый день я чита́ю, а вчера́ я <b>прочита́ла</b> кни́гу.","say":"Каждый день я читаю, а вчера я прочитала книгу."}],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","q":"Emma (a woman): «Ме́сяц наза́д я ___ в Ло́ндоне.»","opts":["был","была́","бы́ло"],"answer":1,"explain":"A woman in the past → <b>была́</b>. The past marks gender — the crown jewel of week four."},{"type":"build","q":"Build: <b>“Every day I speak Russian.”</b>","want":"Каждый день я говорю по-русски","answer":"Ка́ждый день я говорю́ по-ру́сски"},{"type":"type","q":"Type: “Yesterday I was home.” (a woman)","accept":["вчера я была дома","vchera ya byla doma","вчера́ я была́ до́ма"],"placeholder":"Вчера́ я …","explain":"Вчера́ я была́ до́ма — the past “was” comes back as была́ for a woman."}]}],"scene":{"intro":"The last evening. Ива́н and Ле́на walk Emma to the metro. A month ago she couldn't say a word; tonight, goodbye is the hardest sentence of all. Tap a line to hear it.","lines":[{"who":"B","en":"Э́мма, за́втра ты е́дешь домо́й.","ru":"Emma, tomorrow you go home. (домо́й = home[ward])"},{"who":"A","en":"Да. Э́то был о́чень хоро́ший ме́сяц.","ru":"Yes. It was a very good month."},{"who":"B","en":"Ме́сяц наза́д ты не говори́ла по-ру́сски. А сейча́с!","ru":"A month ago you didn't speak Russian. And now!"},{"who":"A","en":"Я зна́ю! Спаси́бо, Ива́н. Ты и Ле́на — моя́ семья́ здесь.","ru":"I know! Thank you, Ivan. You and Lena are my family here. (здесь = here)"},{"who":"B","en":"Мы лю́бим тебя́, Э́мма.","ru":"We love you, Emma."},{"who":"A","en":"Я люблю́ Москву́. И я ещё верну́сь!","ru":"I love Moscow. And I'll be back! (я ещё верну́сь = I'll come back)"},{"who":"B","en":"Коне́чно! До свида́ния, Э́мма.","ru":"Of course! Goodbye, Emma."},{"who":"A","en":"Не «до свида́ния». Пока́!","ru":"Not ‘goodbye’. See you!"}]},"vocab":{"intro":"Twelve signature phrases from the whole level — the words that make you a person in Russian. Say each one and mean it.","items":[{"en":"Меня́ зову́т…","ru":"My name is…","ex":"Меня́ зову́т Э́мма."},{"en":"Я студе́нт / студе́нтка","ru":"I'm a student (m / f)","ex":"Я студе́нтка из А́нглии."},{"en":"Мне … лет","ru":"I'm … years old","ex":"Мне два́дцать лет."},{"en":"Я из…","ru":"I'm from…","ex":"Я из Ло́ндона."},{"en":"У меня́ есть…","ru":"I have…","ex":"У меня́ есть друзья́."},{"en":"Мне нра́вится…","ru":"I like…","ex":"Мне нра́вится Москва́."},{"en":"Я люблю́…","ru":"I love…","ex":"Я люблю́ сы́рники."},{"en":"Я был / была́…","ru":"I was…","ex":"Ме́сяц наза́д я была́ в Ло́ндоне."},{"en":"ка́ждый день","ru":"every day","ex":"Ка́ждый день я говорю́ по-ру́сски."},{"en":"о себе́","ru":"about myself","ex":"Я пишу́ о себе́."},{"en":"до свида́ния / пока́","ru":"goodbye / bye","ex":"До свида́ния, Москва́! Пока́!"},{"en":"спаси́бо","ru":"thank you","ex":"Спаси́бо, Москва́!"}]},"drag":{"intro":"Match the Russian to the English — one last time.","howto":"<b>What to do:</b> drag each card on the left onto its meaning on the right — or tap one, then the other.","pairs":[["Меня́ зову́т Э́мма.","My name is Emma."],["Мне два́дцать лет.","I'm twenty years old."],["Мне нра́вится Москва́.","I like Moscow."],["Вчера́ я была́ до́ма.","Yesterday I was home."]]},"quiz":{"intro":"Eight questions — one last walk through the whole level. Take your time; you know all of this now.","items":[{"q":"Your friend's grandmother opens the door. You say:","opts":["Приве́т!","Здра́вствуйте!","Пока́!"],"answer":1},{"q":"“This is a café”:","opts":["Э́то кафе́.","Э́то есть кафе́.","Э́то ест кафе́."],"answer":0},{"q":"You're twenty. «___ два́дцать лет.»","opts":["Я","Мне","Меня́"],"answer":1},{"q":"“I love Moscow”:","opts":["Я люблю́ Москва́.","Я люблю́ Москве́.","Я люблю́ Москву́."],"answer":2},{"q":"“Lena is at work”:","opts":["Ле́на на рабо́те.","Ле́на в рабо́те.","Ле́на на рабо́ту."],"answer":0},{"q":"“I like the parks”:","opts":["Я нра́вятся па́рки.","Мне нра́вится па́рки.","Мне нра́вятся па́рки."],"answer":2},{"q":"Emma (a woman): “I was in the park.”","opts":["Я был в па́рке.","Я была́ в па́рке.","Я в па́рке."],"answer":1},{"q":"The letter is finished: «Я ___ письмо́.»","opts":["пишу́","написа́л","писа́л"],"answer":1}]},"reading":{"intro":"Emma's goodbye note, written the night before she flies home. Read it aloud — it's the whole level in six lines.","title":"До свида́ния, Москва́","sentences":["За́втра я е́ду в Ло́ндон.","Ме́сяц наза́д я зна́ла то́лько «приве́т» и «спаси́бо».","Сейча́с я говорю́, чита́ю и понима́ю по-ру́сски!","У меня́ есть хоро́шие друзья́ здесь — Ива́н и Ле́на.","Мне о́чень нра́вится Москва́: па́рки, метро́ и сы́рники.","До свида́ния, Москва́! Я ещё верну́сь."],"translation":"Tomorrow I go to London. A month ago I only knew ‘privet’ and ‘spasibo’. Now I speak, read and understand Russian! I have good friends here — Ivan and Lena. I really like Moscow: the parks, the metro and the sirniki. Goodbye, Moscow! I'll be back. (то́лько = only, здесь = here, я ещё верну́сь = I'll return)"},"essay":{"intro":"One last page — and it's the one that matters most: you, in Russian. Everything you've built over thirty days comes together here. Take your time, and write it the way you'd say it to a new friend on the Arbat.","prompt":"Write about yourself — «О себе́» — in 6–8 short sentences: greet, give your name, say who and how old you are and where you're from; name something you have and something you like; and add one thing from your past or your day. This is your whole A1 in one paragraph.","hint":"You already own every brick: Здра́вствуйте! Меня́ зову́т… Мне … лет. Я из… У меня́ есть… Мне нра́вится… Вчера́ я был(а)… Ка́ждый день я… Sign it with a smile 🙂","example":"Здра́вствуйте! Меня́ зову́т Э́мма. Я студе́нтка из А́нглии, и мне два́дцать лет. Ме́сяц наза́д я не говори́ла по-ру́сски — а сейча́с говорю́! У меня́ есть но́вые друзья́: Ива́н и Ле́на. Мне о́чень нра́вится Москва́ — па́рки, метро́ и сы́рники. Ка́ждый день я чита́ю, гуля́ю и говорю́ по-ру́сски. Вчера́ я написа́ла откры́тку из Москвы́. До свида́ния, Москва́ — я ещё верну́сь!"}}};
